window.EXAM_BANK={MC:[{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"A table gives values of \\(f\\) near \\(x=1\\): \\(f(0.9)=1.9\\), \\(f(0.99)=1.99\\), \\(f(1.01)=2.01\\), \\(f(1.1)=2.1\\). The table best supports which estimate of \\(\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"2.01"}],"explain":"<b>Both sides close in on the same height:</b> from the left \\(f\\to 2\\), from the right \\(f\\to 2\\). The limit is the common value, \\(2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> reads the \\(x\\)-value being approached instead of the \\(y\\)-value the outputs approach.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> wrongly assumes the sides disagree — here they match.<br>• <b>2.01</b> grabs a single nearby table entry rather than the value being approached.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a limit is the height the outputs head toward, read from <i>both</i> sides, not a value the function must actually take.","unit":1,"id":"u1m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(g(x)=\\dfrac{|x-2|}{x-2}\\), what is \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}g(x)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"does not exist","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"-1"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>The one-sided limits disagree.</b> For \\(x>2\\), \\(|x-2|=x-2\\) so \\(g=1\\); for \\(x<2\\), \\(|x-2|=-(x-2)\\) so \\(g=-1\\). Right limit \\(=1\\), left limit \\(=-1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> uses only the right-hand limit.<br>• <b>-1</b> uses only the left-hand limit.<br>• <b>0</b> averages the two one-sided limits — limits are not averaged.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a two-sided limit exists only when both one-sided limits agree.","unit":1,"id":"u1m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"The position of a particle is \\(s(t)=t^2\\) meters. The average velocity on \\([3,\\,3+h]\\) is \\(\\dfrac{(3+h)^2-9}{h}\\). As \\(h\\to 0\\), this approaches:","opts":[{"t":"6","correct":true},{"t":"9"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>Expand and simplify before taking the limit.</b> \\(\\frac{(3+h)^2-9}{h}=\\frac{6h+h^2}{h}=6+h\\to 6\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>9</b> is \\(s(3)\\), the position, not the rate.<br>• <b>0</b> comes from plugging \\(h=0\\) into the original \\(\\frac00\\) form without simplifying.<br>• <b>3</b> is the time value, not the velocity.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> instantaneous velocity is the limit of average velocity as the interval shrinks — the indeterminate \\(\\frac00\\) resolves after algebra.","unit":1,"id":"u1m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"A function is defined piecewise with \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3^-}f(x)=5\\) and \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3^+}f(x)=5\\), but \\(f(3)=1\\). What is \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"5","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>The limit ignores the function's value at the point.</b> Both one-sided limits equal \\(5\\), so \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=5\\) — regardless of the fact that \\(f(3)=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> reports \\(f(3)\\), the actual value, which the limit does not use.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> confuses \"limit \\(\\ne\\) value\" with \"limit fails to exist\" — the limit is fine; the function is just discontinuous.<br>• <b>3</b> reports the \\(x\\)-value.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a limit describes where \\(f\\) is <i>heading</i>, which can differ from where it actually <i>is</i>.","unit":1,"id":"u1m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"A table shows \\(\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}\\) for \\(x=0.1,0.01,0.001\\) giving \\(0.99833,\\ 0.99998,\\ 0.9999998\\). The table suggests \\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"0.998"}],"explain":"<b>The values march toward 1.</b> As \\(x\\to 0\\) the ratio climbs \\(0.998\\to 0.99998\\to 0.9999998\\), approaching \\(1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> plugs in \\(x=0\\) to get \\(\\frac00\\) and wrongly calls it zero.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> wrongly treats \\(\\frac00\\) as automatically undefined — the limit is a clean \\(1\\).<br>• <b>0.998</b> stops at the first table row instead of following the trend.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a numerical table reveals the value being approached; \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\to 1\\) is the foundational trig limit.","unit":1,"id":"u1m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f\\) has a hole at \\((2,4)\\) and a filled point at \\((2,1)\\). Which statement is correct?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=4\\) and \\(f(2)=1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1\\) and \\(f(2)=4\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1\\)"},{"t":"the limit does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>The hole marks the limit; the filled dot marks the value.</b> The curve approaches height \\(4\\) (the hole), so \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=4\\). The solid point at \\((2,1)\\) is the actual value \\(f(2)=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>limit\\(=1\\), \\(f(2)=4\\)</b> swaps the roles of the hole and the dot.<br>• <b>limit\\(=1\\)</b> reads the limit off the filled point instead of the approaching curve.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> ignores that the curve approaches a single height from both sides.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> an open circle shows where the function heads; a filled circle shows the defined value.","unit":1,"id":"u1m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x-3}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"6","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>Factor the difference of squares and cancel.</b> \\(\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\to 6\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> stops at the \\(\\frac00\\) form and calls it zero without factoring.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> wrongly treats the removable hole as a fatal discontinuity — the limit exists.<br>• <b>3</b> substitutes into \\(x\\) rather than \\(x+3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac00\\) signals a common factor; cancel it, then substitute.","unit":1,"id":"u1m6","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac{x^3-8}{x-2}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"12","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"4"},{"t":"6"}],"explain":"<b>Factor the difference of cubes.</b> \\(x^3-8=(x-2)(x^2+2x+4)\\), so the ratio is \\(x^2+2x+4\\to 4+4+4=12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> reads the \\(\\frac00\\) form as zero.<br>• <b>4</b> keeps only the constant term \\(x^2+2x+4\\) evaluated as just \\(4\\), dropping \\(x^2+2x\\).<br>• <b>6</b> mistakenly factors as if it were \\(x^2-8\\) or miscomputes \\(2^2+2(2)\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)\\) clears the common factor cleanly.","unit":1,"id":"u1m7","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac14\\)","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Multiply by the conjugate.</b> \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}=\\frac{x}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}\\to\\frac{1}{4}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> reads the \\(\\frac00\\) form as zero.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> abandons the problem at \\(\\frac00\\) instead of rationalizing.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> forgets the \\(+2\\), using \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt4}=\\frac12\\) instead of \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt4+2}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> radicals in a \\(\\frac00\\) limit call for the conjugate, which cancels the troublesome factor.","unit":1,"id":"u1m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 9}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}-3}{x-9}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac16\\)","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac13\\)"},{"t":"does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>Recognize the denominator as a difference of squares in \\(\\sqrt{x}\\):</b> \\(x-9=(\\sqrt{x}-3)(\\sqrt{x}+3)\\). Cancel to get \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+3}\\to\\frac{1}{6}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> reads \\(\\frac00\\) as zero.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> uses \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt9}\\) and forgets the \\(+3\\) in the denominator.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> stops at \\(\\frac00\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> writing \\(x-9=(\\sqrt x-3)(\\sqrt x+3)\\) is the conjugate trick in disguise.","unit":1,"id":"u1m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}}{x-3}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac19\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac19\\)"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>Combine the complex fraction first.</b> \\(\\frac1x-\\frac13=\\frac{3-x}{3x}\\), so the expression is \\(\\frac{3-x}{3x(x-3)}=\\frac{-(x-3)}{3x(x-3)}=\\frac{-1}{3x}\\to\\frac{-1}{9}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac19\\)</b> drops the sign from \\(3-x=-(x-3)\\).<br>• <b>0</b> reads the \\(\\frac00\\) form as zero.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> stops at \\(\\frac00\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> clear the inner fractions, then cancel the \\((x-3)\\) factor; watch the sign on \\(3-x\\).","unit":1,"id":"u1m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"computing","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac{3}{x-2}\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"does not exist (infinite)","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac32\\)"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>This is NOT a \\(\\frac00\\) form.</b> The numerator approaches \\(3\\ne 0\\) while the denominator \\(\\to 0\\), so the magnitude blows up: from the right \\(\\to+\\infty\\), from the left \\(\\to-\\infty\\). The two-sided limit does not exist.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> confuses a large denominator (\\(\\to 0\\)) with a large value going to zero.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac32\\)</b> wrongly plugs in as if continuous.<br>• <b>3</b> just reads the numerator.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a nonzero-over-zero form is an infinite discontinuity (DNE), fundamentally different from \\(\\frac00\\), which may have a finite limit.","unit":1,"id":"u1m11","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin 5x}{x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"5","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac15\\)"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Match the angle.</b> \\(\\frac{\\sin 5x}{x}=5\\cdot\\frac{\\sin 5x}{5x}\\to 5\\cdot 1=5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> applies \\(\\frac{\\sin u}{u}\\to 1\\) without accounting for the mismatch between \\(5x\\) and \\(x\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac15\\)</b> divides by 5 instead of multiplying.<br>• <b>0</b> reads the \\(\\frac00\\) form as zero.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac{\\sin(kx)}{x}\\to k\\) — force the denominator to match the angle, then compensate.","unit":1,"id":"u1m12","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin 2x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac32\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac23\\)"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Split into two known limits.</b> \\(\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin 2x}=\\frac{\\sin 3x}{3x}\\cdot\\frac{2x}{\\sin 2x}\\cdot\\frac{3x}{2x}\\to 1\\cdot 1\\cdot\\frac32=\\frac32\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac23\\)</b> inverts the ratio of the angles.<br>• <b>1</b> cancels the sines as if \\(\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin 2x}=1\\).<br>• <b>0</b> reads \\(\\frac00\\) as zero.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the limit equals the ratio of the angle coefficients, \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\).","unit":1,"id":"u1m13","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"0","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)"},{"t":"does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>A standard trig limit.</b> \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x}\\to 0\\) — confirm by the conjugate: \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x}\\cdot\\frac{1+\\cos x}{1+\\cos x}=\\frac{\\sin^2 x}{x(1+\\cos x)}=\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos x}\\to 1\\cdot\\frac{0}{2}=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> confuses this with \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\to 1\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> is the limit of \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x^2}\\) — wrong denominator power.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> stops at \\(\\frac00\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x}\\to 0\\) but \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x^2}\\to\\frac12\\) — the power of \\(x\\) decides.","unit":1,"id":"u1m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\tan x}{x}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"\\(\\infty\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Rewrite tangent.</b> \\(\\frac{\\tan x}{x}=\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\to 1\\cdot\\frac{1}{1}=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> reads the \\(\\frac00\\) form as zero.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> stops at \\(\\frac00\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> wrongly assumes tangent blows up near \\(0\\) — it doesn't; \\(\\tan 0=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> split \\(\\tan x\\) into \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\); both factors have clean limits.","unit":1,"id":"u1m15","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"By the Squeeze Theorem, \\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}x^2\\cos\\!\\left(\\tfrac1x\\right)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"0","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"does not exist"},{"t":"\\(\\infty\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Trap it between known bounds.</b> Since \\(-1\\le\\cos(\\frac1x)\\le 1\\), we have \\(-x^2\\le x^2\\cos(\\frac1x)\\le x^2\\). Both \\(\\pm x^2\\to 0\\), so the middle is squeezed to \\(0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> evaluates \\(\\cos(\\frac1x)\\) as if it settled to a value — it oscillates, but the \\(x^2\\) crushes it.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> notes the oscillation but misses that the amplitude \\(\\to 0\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> ignores that \\(x^2\\to 0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a bounded factor times something \\(\\to 0\\) gives \\(0\\), even when the bounded factor never settles.","unit":1,"id":"u1m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x^2}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>Conjugate, then use \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\to 1\\).</b> \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x^2}\\cdot\\frac{1+\\cos x}{1+\\cos x}=\\frac{\\sin^2 x}{x^2(1+\\cos x)}=\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right)^2\\cdot\\frac{1}{1+\\cos x}\\to 1\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> is the limit of \\(\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x}\\) — one power of \\(x\\) too few in the denominator.<br>• <b>1</b> forgets the \\(\\frac{1}{1+\\cos x}=\\frac12\\) factor.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> stops at \\(\\frac00\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the conjugate converts \\(1-\\cos x\\) into \\(\\sin^2 x\\), unlocking the \\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\) limit.","unit":1,"id":"u1m17","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x=a\\) exactly when:","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=f(a)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(a)\\) is defined"},{"t":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)\\) exists"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is increasing at \\(a\\)"}],"explain":"<b>All three conditions in one equation.</b> Continuity requires \\(f(a)\\) to exist, the limit to exist, AND the two to be equal — captured exactly by \\(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=f(a)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f(a)\\) defined</b> is necessary but not sufficient — the limit could still disagree.<br>• <b>limit exists</b> alone allows a hole where \\(f(a)\\) is missing or wrong.<br>• <b>increasing</b> has nothing to do with continuity.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the single equation \\(\\lim=f(a)\\) bundles all three parts of the continuity test.","unit":1,"id":"u1m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}kx+1 & x\\le 2\\\\ x^2-1 & x>2\\end{cases}\\) to be continuous at \\(x=2\\), \\(k=\\)","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac32\\)"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>Match the two pieces at the seam.</b> Left value: \\(k(2)+1=2k+1\\). Right limit: \\(2^2-1=3\\). Set equal: \\(2k+1=3\\Rightarrow k=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac32\\)</b> solves \\(2k=3\\), forgetting the \\(+1\\).<br>• <b>2</b> sets \\(2k+1=5\\) using \\(2^2+1\\) instead of \\(2^2-1\\).<br>• <b>3</b> reports the right-hand value rather than solving for \\(k\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> continuity at a junction means left value \\(=\\) right value; solve the resulting equation for the parameter.","unit":1,"id":"u1m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}\\) has what kind of discontinuity at \\(x=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"removable","correct":true},{"t":"jump"},{"t":"infinite"},{"t":"none — it is continuous"}],"explain":"<b>The factor cancels, leaving a hole.</b> \\(\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}=x+2\\), so the limit at \\(2\\) is \\(4\\), but \\(f(2)\\) is undefined. The limit exists — a removable discontinuity.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>jump</b> would need disagreeing one-sided limits; here both sides \\(\\to 4\\).<br>• <b>infinite</b> would need the denominator to survive (nonzero numerator over zero); it cancels instead.<br>• <b>none</b> ignores that \\(f(2)\\) is undefined.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when a \\(\\frac00\\) factor cancels, the limit exists and the break is a patchable hole.","unit":1,"id":"u1m20","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-9}\\) at \\(x=-3\\) has what kind of discontinuity?","opts":[{"t":"infinite","correct":true},{"t":"removable"},{"t":"jump"},{"t":"none"}],"explain":"<b>Factor and see which zero survives.</b> \\(\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}\\). At \\(x=3\\) the factor cancels (removable), but at \\(x=-3\\) the denominator still vanishes while the numerator doesn't — the function blows up.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>removable</b> describes \\(x=3\\), not \\(x=-3\\) — only one of the two breaks is a hole.<br>• <b>jump</b> would need finite disagreeing sides.<br>• <b>none</b> ignores the surviving zero in the denominator.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a zero that cancels is removable; a zero that survives is an infinite discontinuity (vertical asymptote).","unit":1,"id":"u1m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"A function has \\(\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=7\\) but \\(f(4)=2\\). The discontinuity at \\(x=4\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"removable","correct":true},{"t":"jump"},{"t":"infinite"},{"t":"there is none"}],"explain":"<b>The limit exists but disagrees with the value.</b> Both one-sided limits give \\(7\\), so the limit exists; the value \\(f(4)=2\\) is simply wrong. Redefining \\(f(4)=7\\) would fix it — removable.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>jump</b> requires the one-sided limits to disagree; here the limit is a single value \\(7\\).<br>• <b>infinite</b> requires unbounded behavior.<br>• <b>none</b> ignores that \\(\\lim\\ne f(4)\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> limit exists but \\(\\ne f(a)\\) is the \"wrong value\" flavor of removable discontinuity.","unit":1,"id":"u1m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"On which interval is \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-x-6}\\) continuous?","opts":[{"t":"all \\(x\\) except \\(x=3\\) and \\(x=-2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"all real numbers"},{"t":"all \\(x\\) except \\(x=-1\\)"},{"t":"all \\(x>3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>A rational function breaks where its denominator is zero.</b> \\(x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)\\), zero at \\(x=3,-2\\). The numerator is nonzero there, so both are genuine discontinuities; \\(f\\) is continuous everywhere else.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>all reals</b> ignores the denominator's zeros.<br>• <b>except \\(x=-1\\)</b> sets the numerator to zero — but a zero numerator gives a root, not a discontinuity.<br>• <b>all \\(x>3\\)</b> arbitrarily restricts to one side.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> rational functions are continuous on their domain — everywhere except where the denominator vanishes.","unit":1,"id":"u1m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{2x^2+1}{x^2-3}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\(\\infty\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Equal degrees — a tie.</b> The horizontal asymptote is the ratio of leading coefficients, \\(\\frac{2}{1}=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> applies the \"bottom wins\" rule, which is only for a larger denominator degree.<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> applies the \"top wins\" rule, only for a larger numerator degree.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> inverts the coefficient ratio.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> equal degrees \\(\\Rightarrow\\) HA at the ratio of leading coefficients.","unit":1,"id":"u1m24","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{3x+2}{x^2+1}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"0","correct":true},{"t":"3"},{"t":"\\(\\infty\\)"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>Bottom wins.</b> Denominator degree (2) exceeds numerator degree (1), so the fraction \\(\\to 0\\) — horizontal asymptote \\(y=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3</b> grabs the leading coefficient as if degrees were equal.<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> reverses the race — that's for a bigger numerator.<br>• <b>1</b> wrongly cancels as if both leading terms matched.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> larger denominator degree \\(\\Rightarrow\\) the ratio collapses to \\(0\\).","unit":1,"id":"u1m25","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2+1}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\infty\\) (no horizontal asymptote)","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>Top wins.</b> Numerator degree (3) exceeds denominator degree (2), so the function grows without bound — no horizontal asymptote.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> applies the \"bottom wins\" rule backward.<br>• <b>1</b> treats it as a tie.<br>• <b>3</b> invents a coefficient ratio that doesn't apply.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> larger numerator degree \\(\\Rightarrow\\) unbounded growth; no HA (though a slant/polynomial asymptote may exist).","unit":1,"id":"u1m26","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}\\) has a vertical asymptote at:","opts":[{"t":"\\(x=2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x=-1\\)"},{"t":"\\(y=1\\)"},{"t":"\\(x=0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Vertical asymptotes occur where the denominator is zero and the numerator isn't.</b> \\(x-2=0\\) at \\(x=2\\), and the numerator \\(x+1=3\\ne 0\\) there — a vertical asymptote.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x=-1\\)</b> sets the numerator to zero — that gives an \\(x\\)-intercept, not an asymptote.<br>• <b>\\(y=1\\)</b> is the <i>horizontal</i> asymptote (equal degrees), not vertical.<br>• <b>\\(x=0\\)</b> is the \\(y\\)-axis, unrelated.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> set the denominator to zero for vertical asymptotes; check the numerator isn't also zero (which would signal a hole).","unit":1,"id":"u1m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous on \\([0,1]\\) with \\(f(0)=-1\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\). The Intermediate Value Theorem guarantees:","opts":[{"t":"a value \\(c\\) in \\((0,1)\\) with \\(f(c)=0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"exactly one root in \\((0,1)\\)"},{"t":"the root is at \\(c=0.5\\)"},{"t":"nothing without a formula for \\(f\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Sign change across a continuous function forces a crossing.</b> Since \\(0\\) lies between \\(f(0)=-1\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\) and \\(f\\) is continuous, some \\(c\\in(0,1)\\) has \\(f(c)=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>exactly one root</b> overclaims — the IVT guarantees <i>at least</i> one, never the count.<br>• <b>\\(c=0.5\\)</b> invents a location; the IVT never tells you <i>where</i>.<br>• <b>nothing without a formula</b> forgets that continuity plus a sign change is already enough.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the IVT is an existence theorem — it promises a root exists, not where or how many.","unit":1,"id":"u1m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"A continuous function \\(g\\) satisfies \\(g(2)=3\\) and \\(g(5)=8\\). The IVT guarantees \\(g(c)=6\\) for some \\(c\\) because:","opts":[{"t":"6 lies between 3 and 8","correct":true},{"t":"6 is greater than 3"},{"t":"\\(g\\) is increasing"},{"t":"6 is an even number"}],"explain":"<b>The target must lie between the endpoint values.</b> Since \\(3<6<8\\) and \\(g\\) is continuous on \\([2,5]\\), the IVT guarantees some \\(c\\in(2,5)\\) with \\(g(c)=6\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>6 greater than 3</b> is incomplete — it must also be below \\(8\\), i.e. strictly between.<br>• <b>\\(g\\) increasing</b> is never given and isn't required by the IVT.<br>• <b>even number</b> is irrelevant.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the IVT applies to any value strictly between \\(f(a)\\) and \\(f(b)\\) — not just roots.","unit":1,"id":"u1m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"whatisalimit","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of \\(f\\) near \\(x=2\\). What does the table suggest about \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>1.9</th><th>1.99</th><th>2.01</th><th>2.1</th></tr><tr><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><td>3.90</td><td>3.99</td><td>5.01</td><td>5.10</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"it does not exist, because the one-sided limits differ","correct":true},{"t":"it is \\(4.5\\), the average of the two sides"},{"t":"it is \\(4\\)"},{"t":"it is \\(5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Read each side separately.</b> From the left the values climb toward \\(4\\); from the right they fall toward \\(5\\). A two-sided limit exists only when both sides agree, and \\(4\\ne5\\), so the limit does not exist.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>4.5</b> averages the two one-sided limits — but a limit is not a compromise between the sides; if they disagree there is simply no single value the function approaches. <b>4</b> and <b>5</b> each report one side and ignore the other, which would be correct only if the question had asked for \\(\\lim_{x\\to2^-}\\) or \\(\\lim_{x\\to2^+}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> with a table, cover the right-hand columns and read the left, then do the reverse. The two-sided limit exists only if the two readings match.","unit":1,"id":"u1m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"asymptotes","calc":false,"prompt":"The line \\(y=\\dfrac32\\) is a horizontal asymptote of which function?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{3x^{2}-1}{2x^{2}+5}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{3x-1}{2x^{2}+5}\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{3x^{3}-1}{2x^{2}+5}\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{3x^{2}-1}{2x+5}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Horizontal asymptotes are decided by comparing degrees</b>, because as \\(|x|\\) grows only the leading terms matter.<br><br>Equal degrees give the ratio of the leading coefficients: \\(\\dfrac{3x^{2}}{2x^{2}}\\to\\dfrac32\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second has a <i>smaller</i> degree on top, so it approaches \\(y=0\\) — bottom-heavy always flattens to zero. The third and fourth are top-heavy, so they have no horizontal asymptote at all; the third grows without bound and the fourth has a slant asymptote.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> compare degrees first. Bigger on the bottom → \\(y=0\\); equal → ratio of leading coefficients; bigger on top → no horizontal asymptote.","unit":1,"id":"u1m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous everywhere, \\(f(0)=-4\\) and \\(f(3)=6\\). Which of the following must be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(c)=0\\) for at least one \\(c\\) in \\((0,3)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\) is increasing on \\([0,3]\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(c)=0\\) for exactly one \\(c\\) in \\((0,3)\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(1.5)=1\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The Intermediate Value Theorem</b> says a continuous function takes every value between its endpoint values. \\(0\\) lies between \\(-4\\) and \\(6\\), so some \\(c\\) gives \\(f(c)=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>increasing</b> reads far more into continuity than it provides — \\(f\\) may wander up and down repeatedly and still finish higher. <b>exactly one</b> is the subtle one: the theorem guarantees <i>at least</i> one crossing and never uniqueness, and a wiggly \\(f\\) could cross zero three times. <b>\\(f(1.5)=1\\)</b> assumes \\(f\\) is linear.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> existence theorems promise existence. Any option upgrading \"at least one\" to \"exactly one\", or continuity to monotonicity, is claiming something nobody proved.","unit":1,"id":"u1m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"squeeze","calc":false,"prompt":"Suppose \\(-x^{2}\\le g(x)\\le x^{2}\\) for all \\(x\\). What can be concluded about \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}g(x)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"it is \\(0\\), by the Squeeze Theorem","correct":true},{"t":"it is \\(0\\), because \\(g(0)=0\\)"},{"t":"nothing, since \\(g\\) is not given explicitly"},{"t":"it does not exist, because \\(g\\) may oscillate"}],"explain":"<b>The Squeeze Theorem:</b> if \\(g\\) is trapped between two functions that share a limit, \\(g\\) must share it too. Here both \\(-x^{2}\\) and \\(x^{2}\\) approach \\(0\\) as \\(x\\to0\\), so the gap closes and \\(g\\) has nowhere else to go.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"because \\(g(0)=0\\)\"</b> reaches the right answer for the wrong reason, and the reason is not even established — the inequality forces \\(g(0)=0\\), but a limit never depends on the value <i>at</i> the point. <b>\"nothing\"</b> misses that the bounds alone are enough; you do not need a formula. <b>\"does not exist\"</b> is precisely what the theorem rules out — \\(g\\) may oscillate wildly, like \\(x^{2}\\sin\\frac1x\\), and the limit is still \\(0\\) because the amplitude is crushed to nothing.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> squeeze arguments give you a limit without ever knowing the function. Check that both bounds share the same limit, and that is the whole proof.","unit":1,"id":"u1m33","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"continuity","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x^{2}-9}{x-3}\\) for \\(x\\ne3\\). What kind of discontinuity does \\(f\\) have at \\(x=3\\), and can it be removed?","opts":[{"t":"removable; define \\(f(3)=6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"removable; define \\(f(3)=0\\)"},{"t":"infinite; it cannot be removed"},{"t":"jump; it cannot be removed"}],"explain":"<b>Factor before judging.</b> \\(\\dfrac{x^{2}-9}{x-3}=\\dfrac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\) for \\(x\\ne3\\), so the graph is the line \\(y=x+3\\) with a single point punched out.<br><br>The limit exists — \\(\\lim_{x\\to3}f(x)=6\\) — so the hole can be filled by defining \\(f(3)=6\\). That is exactly what \"removable\" means.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(f(3)=0\\)</b> plugs in the value that made the denominator vanish rather than the value of the limit. <b>infinite</b> would need the numerator to survive while the denominator dies; here the \\((x-3)\\) cancels, so nothing blows up. <b>jump</b> would need the one-sided limits to differ, and they do not.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a zero denominator is not automatically an asymptote. Factor first — if the offending factor cancels, the discontinuity is a hole and the limit tells you how to fill it.","unit":1,"id":"u1m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"The expression \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{(2+h)^3-8}{h}\\) is equal to which of the following?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f'(2)\\) where \\(f(x)=x^3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'(0)\\) where \\(f(x)=x^3\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(2)\\) where \\(f(x)=x^3\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'(8)\\) where \\(f(x)=x^3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Match the limit to the derivative template.</b> The definition \\(f'(a)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\). Here \\(f(a+h)=(2+h)^3\\) and \\(f(a)=8=2^3\\), so \\(f(x)=x^3\\) and \\(a=2\\). The limit is \\(f'(2)=3(2)^2=12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f'(0)\\)</b> mistakes the \\(h\\) (which \\(\\to0\\)) for the point \\(a\\). The point is \\(2\\), not \\(0\\).<br>• <b>\\(f(2)\\)</b> forgets this is a <i>rate of change</i> (a derivative), not a function value.<br>• <b>\\(f'(8)\\)</b> reads the constant \\(8\\) as the point, but \\(8\\) is \\(f(2)\\), not \\(a\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> in \\(\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\), the number added to \\(h\\) inside is \\(a\\); the standalone constant subtracted is \\(f(a)\\).","unit":2,"id":"u2m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x-3}\\) can be interpreted as the derivative of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at \\(x=3\\). Its value is","opts":[{"t":"6","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>This is the alternate form of the derivative,</b> \\(f'(a)=\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\\), with \\(f(x)=x^2\\), \\(a=3\\). So the value is \\(f'(3)=2(3)=6\\). (You can also factor: \\(\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\to6\\).)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> sees the indeterminate \\(\\frac00\\) form and stops, concluding the limit is \\(0\\) — but \\(\\frac00\\) means &ldquo;simplify further,&rdquo; not &ldquo;zero.&rdquo;<br>• <b>3</b> reports the \\(x\\)-value being approached instead of evaluating.<br>• <b>does not exist</b> assumes the hole makes the limit fail, but the limit ignores the single missing point.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac00\\) at a point is the signature of a derivative in disguise — factor and cancel.","unit":2,"id":"u2m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"A differentiable function \\(f\\) has values \\(f(4)=10\\) and \\(f(4.2)=10.7\\). The best estimate of \\(f'(4)\\) from this data is","opts":[{"t":"3.5","correct":true},{"t":"0.7"},{"t":"0.2"},{"t":"53.5"}],"explain":"<b>Estimate the derivative with the difference quotient.</b> \\(f'(4)\\approx\\frac{f(4.2)-f(4)}{4.2-4}=\\frac{10.7-10}{0.2}=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0.7</b> computes only the change in \\(f\\) (the numerator) and forgets to divide by the change in \\(x\\).<br>• <b>0.2</b> reports the change in \\(x\\) instead of the rate.<br>• <b>53.5</b> divides by \\(0.2\\) but also keeps the raw \\(10.7\\) — an arithmetic slip from not subtracting first.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a derivative is a <i>ratio</i> — change in output over change in input — never just one of the two.","unit":2,"id":"u2m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(s(t)\\) gives position, which notation represents the object's instantaneous velocity at time \\(t=5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(s'(5)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(s(5)\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{s(5)}{5}\\)"},{"t":"\\(s(5)-s(0)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Velocity is the derivative of position.</b> Instantaneous velocity at \\(t=5\\) is the rate of change of position there: \\(s'(5)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(s(5)\\)</b> is the position at \\(t=5\\), not how fast it's changing.<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{s(5)}{5}\\)</b> is average velocity from \\(t=0\\) only if \\(s(0)=0\\) — and it's still an <i>average</i>, not instantaneous.<br>• <b>\\(s(5)-s(0)\\)</b> is total displacement over \\([0,5]\\), again not a rate.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> &ldquo;instantaneous rate&rdquo; always means the derivative; averages are differences over intervals.","unit":2,"id":"u2m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\) with a sharp <b>corner</b> there. Which statement is true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x=1\\) but \\(f'(1)\\) does not exist","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'(1)=0\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is not continuous at \\(x=1\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'(1)\\) exists and equals the average of the two slopes"}],"explain":"<b>A corner breaks differentiability but not continuity.</b> The graph connects (you can draw through the point without lifting your pen), so \\(f\\) is continuous. But the left and right slopes disagree at a corner, so no single tangent slope exists — \\(f'(1)\\) is undefined.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f'(1)=0\\)</b> confuses a corner with a flat spot; the slope isn't zero, it's undefined.<br>• <b>not continuous</b> confuses a corner (continuous) with a jump (discontinuous).<br>• <b>average of the two slopes</b> invents a value; the derivative simply doesn't exist when the one-sided slopes differ.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiable \\(\\Rightarrow\\) continuous, but continuous does <i>not</i> \\(\\Rightarrow\\) differentiable.","unit":2,"id":"u2m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the limit definition, the derivative of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) is found by simplifying \\(\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\\). After expanding, the expression inside the limit becomes","opts":[{"t":"\\(2x+h\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2x\\)"},{"t":"\\(2x+h^2\\)"},{"t":"\\(x+h\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Expand, cancel, then simplify — but before taking the limit.</b> \\((x+h)^2-x^2=2xh+h^2\\). Divide by \\(h\\): \\(\\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}=2x+h\\). <i>Then</i> the limit as \\(h\\to0\\) gives \\(2x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2x\\)</b> is the final answer <i>after</i> the limit — the question asks for the expression still inside the limit, before \\(h\\to0\\).<br>• <b>\\(2x+h^2\\)</b> divides only the first term by \\(h\\), forgetting \\(\\frac{h^2}{h}=h\\).<br>• <b>\\(x+h\\)</b> drops the factor of \\(2\\) from \\(2xh\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the algebra must cancel the \\(h\\) in the denominator <i>before</i> substituting \\(h=0\\), or you get \\(\\frac00\\).","unit":2,"id":"u2m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}ax^2 & x\\le 1\\\\ bx+1 & x>1\\end{cases}\\). For \\(f\\) to be <b>differentiable</b> at \\(x=1\\), the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must satisfy","opts":[{"t":"\\(a=-1,\\ b=-2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(a=2,\\ b=1\\)"},{"t":"\\(a=1,\\ b=2\\)"},{"t":"any \\(a,b\\) with \\(a=b\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiable means two conditions: continuous AND equal slopes.</b><br><b>Continuity:</b> left value \\(a(1)^2=a\\) must equal right value \\(b(1)+1=b+1\\), so \\(a=b+1\\).<br><b>Equal slopes:</b> left derivative \\(2ax\\big|_{1}=2a\\) must equal right derivative \\(b\\), so \\(2a=b\\).<br>Substituting: \\(2a=b\\) and \\(a=b+1\\Rightarrow a=2a+1\\Rightarrow a=-1,\\ b=-2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(a=2,b=1\\)</b> satisfies only continuity (\\(2=1+1\\)) but not the slope match (\\(2(2)\\ne1\\)).<br>• <b>\\(a=1,b=2\\)</b> matches slopes (\\(2(1)=2\\)) but fails continuity (\\(1\\ne3\\)).<br>• <b>any \\(a=b\\)</b> ignores both conditions entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> piecewise differentiability is a <i>two-test</i> problem — the pieces must meet <i>and</i> have matching slopes.","unit":2,"id":"u2m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"Which function is continuous at \\(x=0\\) but <b>not</b> differentiable there?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(x)=|x|\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(x)=x^2\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}1&x\\ge0\\\\0&x<0\\end{cases}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Look for a corner — connected but not smooth.</b> \\(|x|\\) is continuous everywhere, but at \\(x=0\\) the left slope is \\(-1\\) and the right slope is \\(+1\\): a corner, so \\(f'(0)\\) doesn't exist.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x^2\\)</b> is smooth everywhere, including \\(x=0\\) (slope \\(0\\)) — differentiable.<br>• <b>\\(1/x\\)</b> isn't even <i>continuous</i> at \\(0\\) (vertical asymptote), so it fails the wrong condition.<br>• <b>the step function</b> has a jump at \\(0\\) — also not continuous.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the question wants continuous-but-not-differentiable, which is exactly a corner, cusp, or vertical tangent — not a discontinuity.","unit":2,"id":"u2m7","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f\\) is differentiable at \\(x=a\\), which of the following <b>must</b> be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x=a\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'(a)=0\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a maximum or minimum at \\(x=a\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is a polynomial"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiability forces continuity.</b> If a function has a well-defined slope at a point, it can't have a break there — this is the one-way implication: differentiable \\(\\Rightarrow\\) continuous.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f'(a)=0\\)</b> would mean a horizontal tangent, which is just one special case, not a requirement.<br>• <b>max or min</b> describes a critical point, unrelated to merely being differentiable.<br>• <b>polynomial</b> — plenty of differentiable functions (like \\(\\sin x\\), \\(e^x\\)) aren't polynomials.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the only thing differentiability guarantees is continuity; the converse and the other claims don't follow.","unit":2,"id":"u2m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(f(x)=x^{2/3}\\) at \\(x=0\\) has","opts":[{"t":"a cusp — continuous but not differentiable","correct":true},{"t":"a corner with slopes \\(\\pm1\\)"},{"t":"a removable discontinuity"},{"t":"a horizontal tangent"}],"explain":"<b>\\(x^{2/3}\\) has a cusp at the origin.</b> It's continuous (\\(f(0)=0\\)), but the derivative \\(f'(x)=\\frac23x^{-1/3}\\) blows up to \\(+\\infty\\) on one side and \\(-\\infty\\) on the other as \\(x\\to0\\). The graph comes to a sharp point with vertical tangent lines — a cusp.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>corner with slopes \\(\\pm1\\)</b> describes \\(|x|\\), where slopes are finite; a cusp has infinite slopes.<br>• <b>removable discontinuity</b> is wrong — the function is perfectly continuous there.<br>• <b>horizontal tangent</b> is the opposite; the tangents are vertical, not horizontal.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a cusp is a failure mode where the one-sided slopes run off to \\(\\pm\\infty\\), unlike a corner's finite mismatch.","unit":2,"id":"u2m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(g(x)=\\begin{cases}x^2 & x\\le 2\\\\ kx-4 & x>2\\end{cases}\\) to be <b>continuous</b> at \\(x=2\\), \\(k\\) must equal","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"8"}],"explain":"<b>Continuity just needs the pieces to meet.</b> Left value: \\(2^2=4\\). Right value: \\(k(2)-4=2k-4\\). Set equal: \\(2k-4=4\\Rightarrow 2k=8\\Rightarrow k=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> solves \\(2k-4=0\\) (sets the right piece to zero) instead of matching the left value of \\(4\\).<br>• <b>0</b> forgets the \\(-4\\) and sets \\(2k=4\\) wrong, or matches to the wrong value.<br>• <b>8</b> matches \\(2k=8\\) but forgets to bring the \\(-4\\) across, leaving \\(k\\) twice as large as needed... actually solves \\(2k-4=4\\) as \\(2k=8,k=4\\); choosing \\(8\\) means stopping at \\(2k=8\\) and reporting \\(2k\\) instead of \\(k\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> continuity is the <i>first</i> test (values meet); it's weaker than differentiability, which would also need the slopes to match.","unit":2,"id":"u2m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"At a point where a continuous function has a <b>vertical tangent</b>, the derivative","opts":[{"t":"does not exist (the slope is infinite)","correct":true},{"t":"equals zero"},{"t":"equals one"},{"t":"equals the \\(y\\)-value at that point"}],"explain":"<b>A vertical tangent means an infinite slope, so the derivative is undefined.</b> The function can be perfectly continuous (like \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\) at \\(0\\)), but a vertical line has no finite slope, so \\(f'\\) does not exist there.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>zero</b> describes a <i>horizontal</i> tangent — the opposite situation.<br>• <b>one</b> is an arbitrary finite value; vertical tangents have no finite slope at all.<br>• <b>the \\(y\\)-value</b> confuses the function's height with its slope.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> &ldquo;derivative exists&rdquo; requires a <i>finite</i> slope; vertical tangents (infinite slope) are one of the four ways differentiability fails.","unit":2,"id":"u2m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x}\\), then \\(f'(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\sqrt{x}-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\ln x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Rewrite as powers, then apply the power rule to each term.</b> \\(\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\\Rightarrow \\frac12x^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}\\). And \\(\\frac1x=x^{-1}\\Rightarrow -x^{-2}=-\\frac{1}{x^2}\\). So \\(f'=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}-\\frac{1}{x^2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(+\\frac{1}{x^2}\\)</b> forgets that \\(x^{-1}\\) differentiates to \\(-x^{-2}\\) — the negative exponent brings a minus sign.<br>• <b>\\(2\\sqrt x\\)</b> handles \\(x^{1/2}\\) backwards (integrating instead of differentiating).<br>• <b>\\(-\\ln x\\)</b> wrongly recalls \\(\\frac1x\\) as the <i>derivative</i> of \\(\\ln x\\) and runs it in reverse.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> rewrite roots and reciprocals as exponents <i>first</i>, then &ldquo;bring down, subtract one&rdquo; — watching the sign on negative exponents.","unit":2,"id":"u2m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"The equation of the tangent line to \\(f(x)=x^3-4x\\) at \\(x=2\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(y=8(x-2)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(y=8x\\)"},{"t":"\\(y=0\\)"},{"t":"\\(y-2=8(x-0)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Tangent line = point + slope.</b> Point: \\(f(2)=8-8=0\\), so \\((2,0)\\). Slope: \\(f'(x)=3x^2-4\\), \\(f'(2)=12-4=8\\). Point-slope: \\(y-0=8(x-2)\\), i.e. \\(y=8(x-2)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=8x\\)</b> uses the slope but forgets to anchor at the point \\((2,0)\\) — this line passes through the origin, not \\((2,0)\\).<br>• <b>\\(y=0\\)</b> uses only the point's height and forgets the slope entirely.<br>• <b>\\(y-2=8(x-0)\\)</b> swaps the coordinates of the point, using \\(2\\) as the \\(y\\)-value and \\(0\\) as the \\(x\\)-value.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a tangent line needs <i>both</i> \\(f(a)\\) for the point and \\(f'(a)\\) for the slope, assembled as \\(y-f(a)=f'(a)(x-a)\\).","unit":2,"id":"u2m13","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(f(x)=x^3-3x\\) has a horizontal tangent at","opts":[{"t":"\\(x=1\\) and \\(x=-1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x=0\\) only"},{"t":"\\(x=3\\) and \\(x=-3\\)"},{"t":"\\(x=\\sqrt3\\) and \\(x=-\\sqrt3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Horizontal tangent means \\(f'(x)=0\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-3=0\\Rightarrow x^2=1\\Rightarrow x=\\pm1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x=0\\)</b> finds where \\(f=0\\) or where the inflection is, not where the slope is zero.<br>• <b>\\(x=\\pm3\\)</b> drops the factor of \\(3\\) wrong, solving \\(x^2=9\\).<br>• <b>\\(x=\\pm\\sqrt3\\)</b> solves \\(3x^2-3=0\\) as \\(x^2=3\\) — forgetting to divide the \\(3\\) out (\\(3x^2=3\\Rightarrow x^2=1\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> horizontal tangents are the zeros of \\(f'\\), not the zeros of \\(f\\).","unit":2,"id":"u2m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"The slope of the line <b>normal</b> to \\(f(x)=x^2\\) at the point \\((1,1)\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac12\\)"},{"t":"\\(-2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The normal is perpendicular to the tangent.</b> Tangent slope: \\(f'(1)=2(1)=2\\). The normal slope is the negative reciprocal: \\(-\\frac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2\\)</b> gives the <i>tangent</i> slope, not the normal.<br>• <b>\\(\\frac12\\)</b> takes the reciprocal but forgets the negative sign.<br>• <b>\\(-2\\)</b> negates but forgets to take the reciprocal.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> perpendicular slopes are <i>negative reciprocals</i> — both flip the fraction <i>and</i> change the sign.","unit":2,"id":"u2m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=2x^3-9x^2+12x\\), on which interval is \\(f\\) <b>decreasing</b>?","opts":[{"t":"\\((1,2)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,1)\\)"},{"t":"\\((2,\\infty)\\)"},{"t":"\\((0,3)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Decreasing means \\(f'(x)<0\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=6x^2-18x+12=6(x^2-3x+2)=6(x-1)(x-2)\\). This is negative between the roots, on \\((1,2)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\((-\\infty,1)\\)</b> and <b>\\((2,\\infty)\\)</b> are where \\(f'>0\\) (increasing) — outside the roots.<br>• <b>\\((0,3)\\)</b> grabs convenient round numbers but isn't tied to the sign of \\(f'\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a product like \\(6(x-1)(x-2)\\) is negative only <i>between</i> its roots, where one factor is positive and the other negative.","unit":2,"id":"u2m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"powerrule","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{x^2+3x}{x}\\right)=\\) (simplify first)","opts":[{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2x+3\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2x+3}{x}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2x+3}{1}-1\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Simplify before differentiating.</b> \\(\\frac{x^2+3x}{x}=x+3\\). Its derivative is just \\(1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2x+3\\)</b> differentiates the <i>numerator</i> \\(x^2+3x\\) alone, ignoring the division by \\(x\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{2x+3}{x}\\)</b> differentiates top and bottom separately — that's never a valid rule.<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{2x+3}{1}-1\\)</b> is a garbled quotient-rule attempt on something that didn't need it.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> always check whether a quotient <i>simplifies</i> first — here it collapses to a line, no quotient rule required.","unit":2,"id":"u2m17","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(3\\sin x-2\\cos x\\right)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(3\\cos x+2\\sin x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(3\\cos x-2\\sin x\\)"},{"t":"\\(-3\\cos x+2\\sin x\\)"},{"t":"\\(3\\cos x+2\\sin x+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate term by term with the trig rules.</b> \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x=\\cos x\\) gives \\(3\\cos x\\). \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos x=-\\sin x\\), so \\(-2\\cos x\\) becomes \\(-2(-\\sin x)=+2\\sin x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(3\\cos x-2\\sin x\\)</b> forgets that the derivative of \\(\\cos\\) is <i>negative</i> \\(\\sin\\); the two minus signs should make a plus.<br>• <b>\\(-3\\cos x+2\\sin x\\)</b> wrongly makes \\(\\sin\\) differentiate to \\(-\\cos\\).<br>• <b>\\(+C\\)</b> adds a constant of integration — but this is differentiation, not an integral.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the minus sign lives on \\(\\cos\\)'s derivative; track how it interacts with the coefficient's sign.","unit":2,"id":"u2m18","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(e^x+\\ln x\\right)\\) evaluated at \\(x=1\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(e+1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(e\\)"},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"\\(e+0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Use the two self-evident derivatives.</b> \\(\\frac{d}{dx}e^x=e^x\\) and \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln x=\\frac1x\\). So the derivative is \\(e^x+\\frac1x\\); at \\(x=1\\): \\(e^1+\\frac11=e+1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(e\\)</b> differentiates \\(e^x\\) correctly but forgets the \\(\\ln x\\) term contributes \\(\\frac11=1\\).<br>• <b>\\(1\\)</b> keeps only the \\(\\ln\\) term's contribution and drops \\(e^x\\).<br>• <b>\\(e+0\\)</b> wrongly treats \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln x\\) as \\(0\\) (as if \\(\\ln x\\) were constant).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(e^x\\) is its own derivative; \\(\\ln x\\) becomes \\(\\frac1x\\) — two of the most-tested derivatives in the unit.","unit":2,"id":"u2m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"The fourth derivative of \\(f(x)=\\cos x\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\cos x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-\\cos x\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sin x\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\sin x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Follow the cycle.</b> \\(\\cos x\\to-\\sin x\\to-\\cos x\\to\\sin x\\to\\cos x\\). Four derivatives bring you back to the start: \\(\\cos x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-\\cos x\\)</b> is the <i>second</i> derivative (two steps in), not the fourth.<br>• <b>\\(\\sin x\\)</b> is the third derivative.<br>• <b>\\(-\\sin x\\)</b> is the first derivative.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> derivatives of \\(\\sin\\) and \\(\\cos\\) cycle with period \\(4\\); reduce the order mod \\(4\\) to find where you land.","unit":2,"id":"u2m20","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan x\\) at \\(x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\) equals","opts":[{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sqrt2\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\sec^2x\\).</b> At \\(x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), \\(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\), so \\(\\sec\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\sec^2\\frac{\\pi}{4}=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(1\\)</b> reports \\(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{4}=1\\) (the function value) instead of its derivative.<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt2\\)</b> stops at \\(\\sec\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and forgets to <i>square</i> it.<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)</b> reports \\(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) instead.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the derivative of \\(\\tan\\) is \\(\\sec^2\\) — evaluate the secant, then square it.","unit":2,"id":"u2m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"Which of the six basic trig functions has the derivative \\(-\\csc^2 x\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\cot x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\csc x\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tan x\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sec x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\cot x=-\\csc^2x\\).</b> It's the &ldquo;co-&rdquo; partner of \\(\\tan\\) (whose derivative is \\(+\\sec^2x\\)), and the co-functions carry the negative sign.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\csc x\\)</b> has derivative \\(-\\csc x\\cot x\\), a product, not \\(-\\csc^2x\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tan x\\)</b> gives \\(+\\sec^2x\\) — same structure but secant, and positive.<br>• <b>\\(\\sec x\\)</b> gives \\(\\sec x\\tan x\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tan/\\cot\\) give squared functions (\\(\\sec^2,-\\csc^2\\)); \\(\\sec/\\csc\\) give products. The co-functions (\\(\\cot,\\csc\\)) take the minus sign.","unit":2,"id":"u2m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f(x)=e^x\\), at what point does the tangent line have slope equal to the function's value \\(e^2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(x=2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x=e^2\\)"},{"t":"\\(x=\\ln 2\\)"},{"t":"there is no such point"}],"explain":"<b>\\(e^x\\) is its own derivative, so slope equals height at every point.</b> The slope at \\(x=a\\) is \\(e^a\\), which equals the value \\(e^a\\). For this to be \\(e^2\\), take \\(a=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x=e^2\\)</b> confuses the input with the output value.<br>• <b>\\(x=\\ln2\\)</b> would give slope \\(e^{\\ln2}=2\\), not \\(e^2\\).<br>• <b>no such point</b> misses the defining feature of \\(e^x\\): slope <i>always</i> equals height.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(e^x\\), \\(f'(a)=f(a)=e^a\\) — the slope and the height are the same number everywhere.","unit":2,"id":"u2m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(x^2 e^x\\right)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(2xe^x+x^2e^x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2xe^x\\)"},{"t":"\\(x^2e^x\\)"},{"t":"\\(2xe^{x}\\cdot 1\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Product rule: \\(u'v+uv'\\).</b> With \\(u=x^2,\\ v=e^x\\): \\(u'=2x,\\ v'=e^x\\). So \\(f'=(2x)(e^x)+(x^2)(e^x)=2xe^x+x^2e^x=xe^x(2+x)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2xe^x\\)</b> only differentiates the \\(x^2\\) and keeps \\(e^x\\) along for the ride — the classic &ldquo;\\(u'v'\\)&rdquo; trap that forgets the second term.<br>• <b>\\(x^2e^x\\)</b> differentiates only the \\(e^x\\) factor.<br>• <b>\\(2xe^x\\cdot1\\)</b> mistakenly uses \\(v'=1\\) (as if \\(e^x\\) differentiated to \\(1\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a product changes when <i>either</i> factor changes — you need both \\(u'v\\) and \\(uv'\\).","unit":2,"id":"u2m24","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\ln x-1}{x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1/x}{1}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Quotient rule: \\(\\frac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}\\).</b> With \\(u=\\ln x,\\ v=x\\): \\(u'=\\frac1x,\\ v'=1\\). Numerator: \\(\\frac1x\\cdot x-\\ln x\\cdot1=1-\\ln x\\). Denominator: \\(x^2\\). So \\(\\frac{1-\\ln x}{x^2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\frac{\\ln x-1}{x^2}\\)</b> reverses the numerator order — &ldquo;low d-high minus high d-low,&rdquo; not the other way; the sign flips.<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{1}{x^2}\\)</b> keeps only the \\(u'v\\) term and drops \\(-uv'\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\frac{1/x}{1}\\)</b> differentiates top and bottom separately — not a real rule.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> order matters in the quotient rule; the derivative of the <i>top</i> comes first.","unit":2,"id":"u2m25","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(x\\ln x\\right)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\ln x+1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac1x\\)"},{"t":"\\(1+\\dfrac1x\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\ln x+x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Product rule with \\(u=x,\\ v=\\ln x\\).</b> \\(u'=1,\\ v'=\\frac1x\\). So \\(f'=(1)(\\ln x)+(x)\\left(\\frac1x\\right)=\\ln x+1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\frac1x\\)</b> differentiates only \\(\\ln x\\) and ignores the \\(x\\) factor.<br>• <b>\\(1+\\frac1x\\)</b> uses \\(u'=1\\) and \\(v'=\\frac1x\\) but forgets to <i>multiply</i> each by the other factor.<br>• <b>\\(\\ln x+x\\)</b> computes \\(uv'\\) as \\(x\\cdot\\frac1x\\) wrong, leaving \\(x\\) instead of \\(1\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> in \\(uv'\\), the \\(x\\) and \\(\\frac1x\\) cancel to \\(1\\) — a clean, very common result.","unit":2,"id":"u2m26","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values at \\(x=3\\): \\(f(3)=5,\\ f'(3)=-1\\). If \\(g(x)=2x\\,f(x)\\), then \\(g'(3)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-6\\)"},{"t":"\\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(-2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Product rule with the given table values.</b> \\(g=2x\\cdot f(x)\\), so \\(g'=2f(x)+2x\\,f'(x)\\). At \\(x=3\\): \\(g'(3)=2(5)+2(3)(-1)=10-6=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-6\\)</b> keeps only the \\(2x\\,f'(x)=6(-1)\\) term and drops \\(2f(x)\\).<br>• <b>\\(10\\)</b> keeps only \\(2f(x)=2(5)\\) and drops the second term.<br>• <b>\\(-2\\)</b> uses \\(2\\cdot f'(3)=2(-1)\\) but forgets the \\(x\\) in the second term and the first term entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> table-based product/quotient problems are pure FRQ bread-and-butter — plug the given values into both terms of the rule.","unit":2,"id":"u2m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{x}\\) and \\(f(2)=6,\\ f'(2)=3\\), then \\(h'(2)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac32\\)"},{"t":"\\(3\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac32\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Quotient rule with \\(u=f(x),\\ v=x\\).</b> \\(h'=\\frac{f'(x)\\cdot x-f(x)\\cdot1}{x^2}\\). At \\(x=2\\): \\(\\frac{3(2)-6(1)}{4}=\\frac{6-6}{4}=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\frac32\\)</b> computes \\(\\frac{f'(2)}{2}=\\frac32\\), forgetting the \\(-f(x)\\) term — i.e. differentiating top over bottom.<br>• <b>\\(3\\)</b> reports \\(f'(2)\\) alone.<br>• <b>\\(-\\frac32\\)</b> gets the numerator order backwards.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> even when the answer is a clean \\(0\\), you must run the full quotient rule — both numerator terms.","unit":2,"id":"u2m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"To differentiate \\(y=(x^2+1)(x^3-x)\\), the <b>most efficient</b> first step on the AP exam is","opts":[{"t":"expand to a polynomial, then use the power rule","correct":true},{"t":"apply the quotient rule"},{"t":"rewrite as \\((x^2+1)\\div(x^3-x)^{-1}\\)"},{"t":"differentiate each factor and multiply"}],"explain":"<b>When both factors are polynomials, expanding is often cleanest.</b> \\((x^2+1)(x^3-x)=x^5-x^3+x^3-x=x^5-x\\), so \\(y'=5x^4-1\\) in one line — no product rule bookkeeping.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>quotient rule</b> doesn't apply — there's no division here.<br>• <b>rewriting as a quotient</b> manufactures a harder problem out of a simple product.<br>• <b>differentiate each factor and multiply</b> is the \\((uv)'=u'v'\\) trap — flat-out wrong.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the product rule always works, but for polynomial \\(\\times\\) polynomial, expanding first is usually faster and less error-prone.","unit":2,"id":"u2m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"derivdefined","calc":false,"prompt":"The limit \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{(2+h)^{5}-32}{h}\\) is the derivative of which function, at which point — and what is its value?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(x)=x^{5}\\) at \\(x=2\\); the value is \\(80\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(x)=x^{5}\\) at \\(x=2\\); the value is \\(32\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=(x+2)^{5}\\) at \\(x=0\\); the value is \\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=5x^{4}\\) at \\(x=2\\); the value is \\(80\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Match the limit against the definition \\(f'(a)=\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\\).</b> The pattern \\((2+h)^{5}\\) identifies \\(f(x)=x^{5}\\) and \\(a=2\\), and the \\(32\\) is \\(f(2)=2^{5}\\), which confirms it.<br><br>So the value is \\(f'(2)=5(2)^{4}=80\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second identifies the function correctly but reports \\(f(2)\\) instead of \\(f'(2)\\). The third is a valid re-parametrisation but its value is wrong. The fourth confuses the function with its derivative — if \\(f\\) were \\(5x^{4}\\), the limit would give \\(f'(2)=160\\); the answer \\(80\\) is right but the reasoning would then be inconsistent.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> in these recognition questions, the constant being subtracted is \\(f(a)\\). Use it to confirm your \\(f\\) and \\(a\\) before differentiating.","unit":2,"id":"u2m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of \\(f\\), \\(f'\\), \\(g\\) and \\(g'\\) at \\(x=2\\). If \\(h(x)=f(x)\\,g(x)\\), what is \\(h'(2)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g'(x)\\)</th></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>\\(-1\\)</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(11\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-5\\)"},{"t":"\\(19\\)"},{"t":"\\(-19\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The product rule is \\((fg)'=f'g+fg'\\)</b> — each factor takes a turn being differentiated.<br><br>\\(h'(2)=f'(2)g(2)+f(2)g'(2)=(-1)(4)+(3)(5)=-4+15=11\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>-5</b> is \\(f'g'=(-1)(5)\\), the belief that the derivative of a product is the product of the derivatives — the error the product rule exists to prevent. <b>19</b> is \\((+1)(4)+(3)(5)\\), losing the minus sign on \\(f'(2)\\). <b>-19</b> is \\(f'g-fg'\\), using the <i>quotient</i> rule's numerator by mistake.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> table questions test whether you can place four numbers in the right four slots. Write the rule out symbolically first, then substitute.","unit":2,"id":"u2m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"prodquot","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the same table, if \\(q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\), what is \\(q'(2)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g'(x)\\)</th></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>\\(-1\\)</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{19}{16}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{11}{16}\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{19}{4}\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{5}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The quotient rule is \\(\\left(\\dfrac fg\\right)'=\\dfrac{f'g-fg'}{g^{2}}\\)</b> — and unlike the product rule, the order of the two terms matters.<br><br>\\(q'(2)=\\dfrac{(-1)(4)-(3)(5)}{4^{2}}=\\dfrac{-4-15}{16}=-\\dfrac{19}{16}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{11}{16}\\)</b> adds the two terms in the numerator, i.e. uses the product rule on top. <b>\\(-\\tfrac{19}{4}\\)</b> divides by \\(g\\) instead of \\(g^{2}\\). <b>\\(-\\tfrac15\\)</b> is \\(f'/g'\\), the quotient of the derivatives.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> low d-high minus high d-low, over the square of the low. Getting the subtraction backwards flips the sign, which is why the numerator's order is worth saying out loud.","unit":2,"id":"u2m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"diffcont","calc":false,"prompt":"Which statement about differentiability and continuity is always true?","opts":[{"t":"if \\(f\\) is differentiable at \\(a\\), then \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(a\\)","correct":true},{"t":"if \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(a\\), then \\(f\\) is differentiable at \\(a\\)"},{"t":"if \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(a\\), then \\(f'(a)=0\\)"},{"t":"if \\(f\\) is not differentiable at \\(a\\), then \\(f\\) is not continuous at \\(a\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiability is the stronger condition</b>: having a tangent line requires the graph to be unbroken there, so differentiable \\(\\Rightarrow\\) continuous.<br><br>The converse fails, and the standard counterexample is \\(f(x)=|x|\\) at \\(x=0\\) — perfectly continuous, but the corner means the left and right difference quotients disagree, so no derivative exists.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second is that false converse. The third confuses continuity with having a horizontal tangent. The fourth is the contrapositive of the <i>false</i> converse, so it fails for the same reason — \\(|x|\\) is not differentiable at \\(0\\) yet is continuous there.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> one arrow only. Differentiable implies continuous; continuous implies nothing about differentiability. Corners, cusps and vertical tangents are all continuous and all non-differentiable.","unit":2,"id":"u2m33","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"keyfunc","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=e^{x}\\sin x\\), then \\(f'(0)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(0\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(-1\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Product rule with two \"key functions\".</b> \\(f'(x)=e^{x}\\sin x+e^{x}\\cos x\\).<br><br>At \\(x=0\\): \\(e^{0}\\sin 0+e^{0}\\cos 0=1\\cdot0+1\\cdot1=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>0</b> comes from evaluating only the first term, or from computing \\(f(0)\\) rather than \\(f'(0)\\). <b>2</b> adds \\(e^{0}\\) twice as if both trig factors were \\(1\\), forgetting \\(\\sin 0=0\\). <b>-1</b> uses \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x=-\\cos x\\); the minus sign belongs to the derivative of <i>cosine</i>, not sine.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> apply the rule symbolically, then substitute. Plugging numbers in early is how the \\(\\sin 0=0\\) simplification gets missed.","unit":2,"id":"u2m34","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\sin(x^2)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\cos(x^2)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2x\\cos(x^2)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2x\\cos(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-2x\\cos(x^2)\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Chain rule: the outer function is \\(\\sin u\\), the inner is \\(u=x^2\\). Differentiate the outer at the inner, then multiply by the inner&rsquo;s derivative: \\(\\cos(x^2)\\cdot 2x\\). Choice (A) is the classic miss &mdash; it stops at \\(\\cos(x^2)\\) and never multiplies by \\(u'=2x\\). (C) wrongly changes the argument to \\(2x\\), and (D) invents a sign that nothing in the computation produces.","unit":3,"id":"u3m0","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(y=(3x^2+1)^4\\), then \\(y'=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(4(3x^2+1)^3\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(24x(3x^2+1)^3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(8x(3x^2+1)^3\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(24x(3x^2+1)^4\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Power-of-an-inner is the chain rule: bring down the 4, drop the power to 3, then multiply by the inner derivative \\(\\frac{d}{dx}(3x^2+1)=6x\\). So \\(y'=4(3x^2+1)^3\\cdot 6x=24x(3x^2+1)^3\\). (A) forgets the inner derivative entirely; (C) uses \\(2x\\) instead of \\(6x\\) for the inner; (D) forgets to reduce the exponent.","unit":3,"id":"u3m1","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of \\(f\\), \\(f'\\), \\(g\\), and \\(g'\\). If \\(h(x)=f\\big(g(x)\\big)\\), what is \\(h'(1)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g'(x)\\)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>\\(-2\\)</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>\\(-1\\)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(20\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-10\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The chain rule in table form: \\(h'(1)=f'\\big(g(1)\\big)\\cdot g'(1)\\). Read \\(g(1)=2\\), so you need \\(f'(2)=4\\), then multiply by \\(g'(1)=5\\): \\(4\\cdot 5=20\\). (B) evaluates \\(f'\\) at \\(x=1\\) instead of at \\(g(1)\\) &mdash; the single most common table-question error. (C) forgets to multiply by \\(g'(1)\\), and (D) composes in the wrong order, computing \\(g'(f(1))\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m2","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\ln(\\cos x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tan x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tan x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\sec x\\tan x\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Chain rule with outer \\(\\ln u\\): the derivative is \\(\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot u'\\). Here \\(u=\\cos x\\), so \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot(-\\sin x)=-\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}=-\\tan x\\). (C) stops at \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) and never multiplies by the inner derivative; (A) drops the minus sign from \\((\\cos x)'=-\\sin x\\); (D) is the derivative of \\(\\sec x\\), a different function.","unit":3,"id":"u3m3","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\cos^{3}(2x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-3\\sin(2x)\\cos^{2}(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(6\\sin(2x)\\cos^{2}(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(3\\cos^{2}(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-6\\sin(2x)\\cos^{2}(2x)\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"A double chain: \\(\\cos^3(2x)=\\big(\\cos(2x)\\big)^3\\). Peel the layers outermost-first: the cube gives \\(3\\cos^2(2x)\\), times the derivative of \\(\\cos(2x)\\), which is \\(-\\sin(2x)\\cdot 2\\). Multiplying: \\(3\\cdot(-\\sin 2x)\\cdot 2=-6\\sin(2x)\\cos^2(2x)\\). (A) forgets the innermost factor of 2; (B) drops the negative from differentiating cosine; (C) stops after the outer power and skips both inner layers.","unit":3,"id":"u3m4","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"The slope of the line tangent to \\(y=\\sqrt{x^{2}+9}\\) at \\(x=4\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{10}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{8}{5}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{4}{5}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{5}{4}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Write the root as a power: \\(y=(x^2+9)^{1/2}\\), so \\(y'=\\tfrac12(x^2+9)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2x=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+9}}\\). At \\(x=4\\): \\(\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\). (A) forgets the inner derivative \\(2x\\), leaving \\(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{25}}=\\frac1{10}\\); (B) keeps \\(2x\\) but drops the \\(\\tfrac12\\), doubling the answer; (D) is the reciprocal &mdash; a sign you inverted rise and run.","unit":3,"id":"u3m5","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=g(x^{3})\\) and \\(g'(8)=5\\), then \\(f'(2)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(5\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(15\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(60\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(20\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Chain rule: \\(f'(x)=g'(x^3)\\cdot 3x^2\\). At \\(x=2\\) the inner value is \\(2^3=8\\), exactly where \\(g'\\) is known: \\(f'(2)=g'(8)\\cdot 3(2)^2=5\\cdot 12=60\\). (A) forgets the factor \\(3x^2\\) entirely; (B) uses \\(3x\\) instead of \\(3x^2\\); (D) uses \\(x^2\\) without the 3. The structure &ldquo;evaluate \\(g'\\) at the inner, multiply by the inner&rsquo;s derivative&rdquo; is the whole question.","unit":3,"id":"u3m6","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(2)=-1\\), \\(f'(2)=4\\), and \\(h(x)=\\big(f(x)\\big)^{3}\\), then \\(h'(2)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-12\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(48\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(12\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Chain rule on a power of \\(f\\): \\(h'(x)=3\\big(f(x)\\big)^2 f'(x)\\). At \\(x=2\\): \\(3(-1)^2\\cdot 4=3\\cdot 1\\cdot 4=12\\). The key move is squaring \\(-1\\) correctly &mdash; \\((-1)^2=+1\\). (A) treats \\((-1)^2\\) as \\(-1\\); (B) squares \\(f'(2)\\) instead of \\(f(2)\\); (C) forgets the outer power rule and just reports \\(f'(2)\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m7","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,2^{\\,x^{2}}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(x^{2}\\,2^{\\,x^{2}-1}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2x\\cdot 2^{\\,x^{2}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2x\\ln 2\\cdot 2^{\\,x^{2}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2^{\\,x^{2}}\\ln 2\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Exponential with a chained exponent: \\(\\frac{d}{dx}a^{u}=a^{u}\\ln a\\cdot u'\\). With \\(a=2\\) and \\(u=x^2\\): \\(2^{x^2}\\ln 2\\cdot 2x\\). (A) applies the <i>power</i> rule to an <i>exponential</i> &mdash; the variable is in the exponent, so the power rule does not apply. (B) forgets the \\(\\ln 2\\) that every base-\\(a\\) exponential carries; (D) forgets the inner derivative \\(2x\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m8","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(y=\\sqrt{u}\\) and \\(u=x^{2}-3x\\). Using \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy}{du}\\cdot\\dfrac{du}{dx}\\), the value of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) at \\(x=4\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{5}{4}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{5}{2}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{5}{8}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Evaluate each Leibniz factor at the right place. At \\(x=4\\), \\(u=16-12=4\\). Then \\(\\frac{dy}{du}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt 4}=\\frac14\\) &mdash; evaluated at \\(u=4\\), not \\(x=4\\) &mdash; and \\(\\frac{du}{dx}=2x-3=5\\). Product: \\(\\frac14\\cdot 5=\\frac54\\). (B) forgets \\(\\frac{du}{dx}\\); (D) uses \\(\\frac{1}{2u}\\) instead of \\(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\\); (C) uses \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) for \\(\\frac{dy}{du}\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m9","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=25\\), then \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{y}{x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-2x\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate both sides with respect to \\(x\\), remembering \\(y\\) is a function of \\(x\\): \\(2x+2y\\,y'=0\\). The \\(2y\\,y'\\) comes from the chain rule on \\(y^2\\). Solve: \\(y'=-x/y\\). (B) drops the sign when moving \\(2x\\) across; (C) inverts the ratio; (D) treats \\(y^2\\) as a constant and never produces a \\(y'\\) term at all &mdash; the defining implicit-differentiation error.","unit":3,"id":"u3m10","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"The curve \\(x^{3}+y^{3}=9\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\). The slope of the curve at that point is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-4\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Implicit differentiation: \\(3x^2+3y^2y'=0\\), so \\(y'=-\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\). At \\((1,2)\\): \\(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\). (A) loses the negative sign; (B) uses \\(-x/y\\) as if this were the circle formula &mdash; each curve generates its own \\(y'\\), so re-derive rather than recall; (D) is the reciprocal, from solving \\(3y^2y'=-3x^2\\) upside down.","unit":3,"id":"u3m11","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(e^{y}=x\\), then \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^{y}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\ln x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Differentiate both sides: \\(e^{y}\\,y'=1\\), so \\(y'=\\dfrac{1}{e^{y}}\\). Since the original equation says \\(e^{y}=x\\), this is \\(\\dfrac1x\\) &mdash; which is exactly \\(\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln x\\), as it must be, because \\(e^y=x\\) means \\(y=\\ln x\\). Implicit differentiation just re-derived a known fact. (A) forgets to solve for \\(y'\\); (C) confuses the function \\(y=\\ln x\\) with its derivative.","unit":3,"id":"u3m12","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(\\sin y + x = y\\), then \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos y}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos y-1}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1+\\cos y}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\cos y\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate term by term: \\(\\cos y\\cdot y' + 1 = y'\\). Collect every \\(y'\\) on one side: \\(1=y'-\\cos y\\,y'=y'(1-\\cos y)\\), so \\(y'=\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos y}\\). (B) flips the subtraction when factoring &mdash; watch which side each \\(y'\\) term lands on; (C) adds instead of subtracts; (D) forgets that the isolated \\(y\\) on the right also differentiates to \\(y'\\), not to \\(1\\) alone.","unit":3,"id":"u3m13","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"All four points below lie on the curve \\(x^{2}+xy+y^{2}=3\\). At which point is the tangent line horizontal?","opts":[{"t":"\\((1,1)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\((1,-2)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((2,-1)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\((0,\\sqrt{3})\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiating (product rule on \\(xy\\)): \\(2x+y+xy'+2y\\,y'=0\\), so \\(y'=-\\dfrac{2x+y}{x+2y}\\). Horizontal means the numerator vanishes: \\(2x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(y=-2x\\). Testing: at \\((1,-2)\\) the slope is \\(\\frac{0}{-3}=0\\) &mdash; horizontal. At \\((1,1)\\) the slope is \\(-1\\); at \\((0,\\sqrt3)\\) it is \\(-\\tfrac12\\); and at \\((2,-1)\\) the <i>denominator</i> vanishes instead, giving a vertical tangent &mdash; the mirror-image condition. Numerator zero \\(\\Rightarrow\\) horizontal; denominator zero \\(\\Rightarrow\\) vertical.","unit":3,"id":"u3m14","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=25\\), then \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{25}{y^{3}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{y}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}-y^{2}}{y^{3}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{25}{y^{3}}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"From \\(y'=-x/y\\), differentiate again with the quotient rule: \\(y''=-\\dfrac{y-xy'}{y^{2}}\\). Substitute \\(y'=-x/y\\): \\(y''=-\\dfrac{y+ x^{2}/y}{y^{2}}=-\\dfrac{y^{2}+x^{2}}{y^{3}}\\). Now use the original equation \\(x^2+y^2=25\\): \\(y''=-\\dfrac{25}{y^{3}}\\). The two moves everyone misses: substituting \\(y'\\) back in, and then simplifying with the <i>original curve equation</i>. (A) drops the sign; (B) stops before substituting; (C) has a sign error inside the numerator.","unit":3,"id":"u3m15","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"The line tangent to the curve \\(y^{2}=x^{3}-3x+4\\) at the point \\((0,2)\\) has equation","opts":[{"t":"\\(y=2-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(y=-\\tfrac{3}{4}x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(y=2-\\tfrac{3}{4}x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(y=2+\\tfrac{3}{4}x\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate implicitly: \\(2y\\,y'=3x^{2}-3\\), so \\(y'=\\dfrac{3x^{2}-3}{2y}\\). At \\((0,2)\\): \\(y'=\\dfrac{-3}{4}\\). Point&ndash;slope: \\(y=2-\\tfrac34 x\\). (A) forgets the 2 in \\(2y\\,y'\\), doubling the slope; (D) drops the negative from \\(3(0)^2-3=-3\\); (B) has the right slope but forgets the tangent line must pass through \\((0,2)\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m16","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(x=y^{3}-y\\), then at the point where \\(y=2\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(11\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{11}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{6}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(6\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate both sides with respect to \\(x\\): \\(1=(3y^{2}-1)\\,y'\\), so \\(y'=\\dfrac{1}{3y^{2}-1}\\). At \\(y=2\\): \\(\\dfrac{1}{12-1}=\\dfrac{1}{11}\\). Equivalently, \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=1\\big/\\frac{dx}{dy}\\) &mdash; the reciprocal relationship that powers inverse derivatives. (A) reports \\(\\frac{dx}{dy}\\) without taking the reciprocal; (C) and (D) evaluate \\(3y^2-1\\) incorrectly at \\(y=2\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m17","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(\\sin(x+y)=y\\), then \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\cos(x+y)}{1+\\cos(x+y)}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\cos(x+y)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos(x+y)}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\cos(x+y)}{1-\\cos(x+y)}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Chain rule on the left: \\(\\cos(x+y)\\cdot(1+y')=y'\\). Expand and collect \\(y'\\): \\(\\cos(x+y)=y'-\\cos(x+y)\\,y'=y'\\big(1-\\cos(x+y)\\big)\\), so \\(y'=\\dfrac{\\cos(x+y)}{1-\\cos(x+y)}\\). The inner derivative of \\(x+y\\) is \\(1+y'\\) &mdash; forgetting the \\(1\\) or the \\(y'\\) there is where this problem is lost. (A) has a sign slip in the collection step; (C) loses the \\(\\cos(x+y)\\) numerator when dividing.","unit":3,"id":"u3m18","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"The curve \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=25\\) has vertical tangent lines at","opts":[{"t":"\\((0,5)\\) and \\((0,-5)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\((5,0)\\) and \\((-5,0)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"no points","correct":false},{"t":"every point where \\(y=x\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"From \\(y'=-x/y\\), the tangent is vertical where the <i>denominator</i> is zero (and the numerator is not): \\(y=0\\), which on this circle happens at \\((\\pm 5,0)\\) &mdash; the far left and right edges, where the circle is momentarily straight up-and-down. (A) gives the points where \\(x=0\\), which are the <i>horizontal</i> tangents at the top and bottom. Keep the pairing straight: numerator \\(=0\\Rightarrow\\) horizontal; denominator \\(=0\\Rightarrow\\) vertical.","unit":3,"id":"u3m19","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(f(x)=x^{3}+x\\) and let \\(g\\) be the inverse function of \\(f\\). What is \\(g'(2)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{13}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(13\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"The inverse-derivative rule: \\(g'(2)=\\dfrac{1}{f'\\big(g(2)\\big)}\\). First find \\(g(2)\\) by solving \\(f(x)=2\\): \\(x^3+x=2\\) gives \\(x=1\\), so \\(g(2)=1\\). Then \\(f'(x)=3x^{2}+1\\), \\(f'(1)=4\\), and \\(g'(2)=\\tfrac14\\). (B) evaluates \\(f'\\) at \\(x=2\\) (getting 13) &mdash; the near-universal error: the reciprocal is taken at the <i>matched input</i> \\(g(2)=1\\), not at 2 itself. (A) and (C) forget the reciprocal.","unit":3,"id":"u3m20","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of a differentiable, invertible function \\(f\\) and its derivative. What is \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(7)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th></tr><tr><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{3}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{5}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{7}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Hunt for 7 in the <i>output</i> row: \\(f(2)=7\\), so \\(f^{-1}(7)=2\\). Then \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(7)=\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\). (B) finds the right cell but forgets the reciprocal; (C) reciprocates \\(f'(1)\\), the wrong row entry; (D) reciprocates the input 7 itself, which has no meaning here. Table version of the rule: locate the output, hop to its input, reciprocate \\(f'\\) there.","unit":3,"id":"u3m21","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f\\) is differentiable and invertible, then \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{f'(x)}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{f'\\big(f^{-1}(x)\\big)}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'\\big(f^{-1}(x)\\big)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{f'(x)}{\\big(f(x)\\big)^{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The formula in full: the reciprocal of \\(f'\\), evaluated at the mirror input \\(f^{-1}(x)\\). (A) is the most-chosen wrong answer &mdash; it reciprocates at \\(x\\) itself, but the tangent to \\(f^{-1}\\) at \\(x\\) reflects the tangent to \\(f\\) at the <i>matching point</i> \\(\\big(f^{-1}(x),x\\big)\\). (C) forgets the reciprocal. (D) is the derivative of \\(\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}\\) &mdash; a reciprocal <i>function</i>, which is a completely different object from an inverse function.","unit":3,"id":"u3m22","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(3)=5\\), \\(f'(3)=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\), and \\(g=f^{-1}\\), then \\(g'(5)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{5}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(5\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Since \\(f(3)=5\\), the inverse sends 5 back to 3: \\(g(5)=3\\). Then \\(g'(5)=\\dfrac{1}{f'(3)}=\\dfrac{1}{1/2}=2\\). A slope of \\(\\tfrac12\\) on \\(f\\) reflects across \\(y=x\\) into a slope of \\(2\\) on the inverse &mdash; rise and run trade places. (A) forgets the reciprocal entirely; (B) reciprocates the output value 5 rather than the derivative.","unit":3,"id":"u3m23","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(f(x)=e^{x}+x\\). The value of \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(1)\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{e+1}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(e+1\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Solve \\(f(x)=1\\) first: \\(e^{x}+x=1\\) at \\(x=0\\) (since \\(e^0+0=1\\)), so \\(f^{-1}(1)=0\\). Then \\(f'(x)=e^{x}+1\\), \\(f'(0)=2\\), and \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(1)=\\tfrac12\\). (B) is the trap: evaluating \\(f'\\) at \\(x=1\\) gives \\(e+1\\), then reciprocating &mdash; but the formula demands \\(f'\\) at the matched input \\(0\\). (C) and (D) skip the reciprocal at various stages.","unit":3,"id":"u3m24","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"The line tangent to the graph of \\(f\\) at \\((2,6)\\) has slope 4. The line tangent to the graph of \\(f^{-1}\\) at \\((6,2)\\) has slope","opts":[{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Reflecting a graph across \\(y=x\\) swaps rise and run, so every tangent slope becomes its reciprocal: \\(4\\to\\tfrac14\\). The point \\((2,6)\\) reflects to \\((6,2)\\), which is exactly where the question asks. No sign change occurs &mdash; reflection across \\(y=x\\) preserves the sign of the slope, so (B) and (D) can be eliminated immediately; a rising graph has a rising inverse.","unit":3,"id":"u3m25","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(g\\) be the inverse of a differentiable function \\(f\\). If \\(g(3)=2\\) and \\(f'(2)=6\\), then \\(g'(3)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(6\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{6}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{3}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Everything is already matched for you: \\(g(3)=2\\) says the mirror input is 2, and \\(f'(2)\\) is given. So \\(g'(3)=\\dfrac{1}{f'\\big(g(3)\\big)}=\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}=\\dfrac{1}{6}\\). This is the formula with zero algebra &mdash; the test is whether you plug into the right spot. (A) forgets the reciprocal; (C) reciprocates the input 3 instead of the derivative value.","unit":3,"id":"u3m26","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.1 · The Chain Rule","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of \\(f\\), \\(f'\\), \\(g\\) and \\(g'\\). If \\(h(x)=f\\big(g(x)\\big)\\), what is \\(h'(1)\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g(x)\\)</th><th>\\(g'(x)\\)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>\\(-2\\)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>7</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(-12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-6\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(18\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(42\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The chain rule in table form: \\(h'(1)=f'\\big(g(1)\\big)\\cdot g'(1)\\). Read \\(g(1)=4\\), so you need \\(f'(4)=6\\), then multiply by \\(g'(1)=-2\\): \\(6\\cdot(-2)=-12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> \\(-6\\) evaluates \\(f'\\) at \\(x=1\\) instead of at \\(g(1)\\) — the single most common table error, and the reason the table gives you two rows. \\(18\\) uses \\(f'(1)\\cdot g'(4)\\), mixing up which row each factor comes from. \\(42\\) computes \\(f'(4)\\cdot g'(4)\\), reading both values off the wrong row.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate the outer derivative at the <i>inner value</i>, not at \\(x\\). Write \\(g(1)\\) down first, then go looking for \\(f'\\) at that number.","unit":3,"id":"u3m27","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.3 · Differentiating Inverse Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is differentiable and invertible, with \\(f(1)=2\\) and \\(f'(1)=3\\). What is \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(2)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac13\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-3\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The inverse derivative rule is \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(b)=\\dfrac{1}{f'\\big(f^{-1}(b)\\big)}\\). Since \\(f(1)=2\\), the mirror input is \\(f^{-1}(2)=1\\), so \\(\\big(f^{-1}\\big)'(2)=\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)}=\\dfrac13\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> \\(3\\) forgets the reciprocal entirely. \\(\\tfrac12\\) reciprocates the <i>input</i> \\(2\\) rather than the derivative value. \\(-3\\) invents a sign — inverting a function reflects it across \\(y=x\\), which reciprocates slopes but never flips their sign.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> two steps, in this order. Find where the inverse sends your input, then take one over \\(f'\\) at that place.","unit":3,"id":"u3m28","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.2 · Implicit Differentiation","calc":false,"prompt":"The curve \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=25\\) passes through \\((3,4)\\). What is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) there?","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac34\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac34\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac43\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac43\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate both sides with respect to \\(x\\), remembering that \\(y\\) is a function of \\(x\\): \\(2x+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0\\), so \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\). At \\((3,4)\\) that is \\(-\\dfrac34\\).<br><br>A sanity check: the point is in the first quadrant of a circle, where the curve is falling — so a negative slope is exactly what the geometry demands.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> \\(\\tfrac34\\) drops the minus sign, which the picture immediately contradicts. \\(-\\tfrac43\\) inverts to \\(-\\dfrac{y}{x}\\), swapping the roles of the coordinates. \\(\\tfrac43\\) does both.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> implicit differentiation puts \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) wherever you differentiate a \\(y\\). On a circle the answer \\(-x/y\\) is worth remembering, and the sign is always checkable against the picture.","unit":3,"id":"u3m29","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\sin(2x)\\), then \\(f''(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-4\\sin(2x)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-\\sin(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(4\\cos(2x)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-2\\sin(2x)\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate twice, and the chain rule contributes a factor of \\(2\\) each time. \\(f'(x)=2\\cos(2x)\\), then \\(f''(x)=2\\cdot\\big(-\\sin(2x)\\big)\\cdot 2=-4\\sin(2x)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> \\(-\\sin(2x)\\) ignores the inner derivative on both passes — the answer you would get for \\(\\sin x\\). \\(4\\cos(2x)\\) keeps both factors of \\(2\\) but differentiates cosine as if it were sine, losing the sign. \\(-2\\sin(2x)\\) collects only one of the two factors of \\(2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> each differentiation of \\(\\sin(kx)\\) brings down another \\(k\\). After \\(n\\) derivatives the coefficient is \\(k^{n}\\), which is why higher-order chain rule problems grow so fast.","unit":3,"id":"u3m30","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"Which technique is needed to differentiate \\(y=x^{\\sin x}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"logarithmic differentiation","correct":true},{"t":"the power rule","correct":false},{"t":"the chain rule alone","correct":false},{"t":"the product rule alone","correct":false}],"explain":"Look at where the variable sits. The power rule needs a <i>constant</i> exponent, and the exponential rule needs a <i>constant</i> base — here \\(x\\) appears in both places, so neither applies.<br><br>Take logs first: \\(\\ln y=\\sin x\\,\\ln x\\), then differentiate implicitly. That turns the exponent into a product, which the product rule can handle.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the <b>power rule</b> would give \\(\\sin x\\cdot x^{\\sin x-1}\\), treating the exponent as constant. The <b>chain rule alone</b> cannot start, because there is no single inner function. The <b>product rule alone</b> misreads \\(x^{\\sin x}\\) as a product rather than a power.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> variable base <i>and</i> variable exponent means logarithmic differentiation. Spotting which of the three shapes you have — \\(x^{n}\\), \\(a^{x}\\), or \\(f(x)^{g(x)}\\) — is the whole decision.","unit":3,"id":"u3m31","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\arcsin(3x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9x^{2}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9x^{2}}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3x^{2}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{1+9x^{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Chain rule on \\(\\arcsin u\\): \\(\\dfrac{u'}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\) with \\(u=3x\\). The inner derivative is 3 and \\(u^{2}=9x^{2}\\), giving \\(\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9x^{2}}}\\). (A) forgets the inner derivative; (C) squares the inner incorrectly, writing \\(3x^2\\) instead of \\((3x)^2=9x^2\\); (D) is the \\(\\arctan\\) template &mdash; no radical belongs to \\(\\arctan\\), and no \\(1+u^2\\) belongs to \\(\\arcsin\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m32","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\arctan\\big(\\sqrt{x}\\big)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}\\,(1+x)}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1+x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\,(1+x)}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Two layers: \\(\\arctan u\\) gives \\(\\dfrac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\), and \\(u=\\sqrt x\\) gives \\(u'=\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt x}\\). Since \\(u^{2}=x\\), the product is \\(\\dfrac{1}{1+x}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt x}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt x\\,(1+x)}\\). (B) keeps only the outer layer; (C) keeps only the inner; (D) drops the \\(2\\) from differentiating the square root. Note how \\(u^2\\) simplifying to \\(x\\) makes the final form deceptively clean.","unit":3,"id":"u3m33","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"The slope of the graph of \\(y=\\arctan x\\) at \\(x=1\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"\\(y'=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^{2}}\\), so at \\(x=1\\) the slope is \\(\\dfrac{1}{1+1}=\\dfrac12\\). (A) is \\(\\arctan(1)\\) itself &mdash; the <i>height</i> of the graph at that point, not its slope; confusing a function value with a derivative value is the trap this question exists to catch. (D) sneaks in a radical that \\(\\arctan\\) never produces.","unit":3,"id":"u3m34","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\arccos\\big(x^{2}\\big)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"\\(\\arccos u\\) differentiates to \\(-\\dfrac{u'}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\) &mdash; the exact negative of \\(\\arcsin\\). With \\(u=x^{2}\\): \\(u'=2x\\) and \\(u^{2}=x^{4}\\), so \\(-\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\\). (B) loses the minus sign that distinguishes \\(\\arccos\\) from \\(\\arcsin\\); (C) fails to square the inner function under the radical; (D) forgets the inner derivative \\(2x\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m35","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(\\lvert x\\rvert \\gt 1\\), \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,\\operatorname{arcsec}x=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\lvert x\\rvert\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\lvert x\\rvert\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The \\(\\operatorname{arcsec}\\) derivative carries an absolute value: \\(\\dfrac{1}{\\lvert x\\rvert\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}}\\), which keeps the derivative positive on <i>both</i> branches &mdash; \\(\\operatorname{arcsec}\\) increases for \\(x\\gt 1\\) and for \\(x\\lt -1\\) alike. (A) without the absolute value goes negative for \\(x \\lt -1\\), contradicting the increasing graph; (C) is \\(\\operatorname{arccsc}\\), its negative twin; (D) forgets the \\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\) factor entirely.","unit":3,"id":"u3m36","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(g(x)=\\arctan\\big(e^{x}\\big)\\), then \\(g'(0)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1+e}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{e}{1+e^{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Chain rule: \\(g'(x)=\\dfrac{e^{x}}{1+\\big(e^{x}\\big)^{2}}=\\dfrac{e^{x}}{1+e^{2x}}\\). At \\(x=0\\): \\(\\dfrac{1}{1+1}=\\dfrac12\\). Note the denominator squares the whole inner function: \\(\\big(e^x\\big)^2=e^{2x}\\), not \\(e^{x^2}\\). (B) forgets the denominator at 0; (C) puts \\(e\\) (the value at \\(x=1\\)) into a formula being evaluated at 0; (D) is \\(g'(1)\\), a correct value at the wrong point.","unit":3,"id":"u3m37","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.4 · Inverse Trigonometric Functions","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(y=\\arcsin x+\\arccos x\\) for \\(-1 \\lt x \\lt 1\\), then \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\text{undefined}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The two derivatives are exact negatives: \\(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}+\\Big(-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\Big)=0\\). That must be right, because \\(\\arcsin x+\\arccos x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\) identically &mdash; the function is a constant, so its derivative is 0. (A) is the error of giving both terms the same sign. When a derivative comes out to zero, look for the hidden identity: constant functions hide in plain sight.","unit":3,"id":"u3m38","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(y=\\ln\\!\\big(x^{4}(x^{2}+1)\\big)\\) for \\(x \\gt 0\\), then \\(y'=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{x^{4}(x^{2}+1)}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(4x^{3}+2x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{4}{x}+\\dfrac{2x}{x^{2}+1}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{4}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}+1}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Rewrite before differentiating: \\(y=4\\ln x+\\ln(x^{2}+1)\\) by log laws. Now each term is one clean derivative: \\(\\dfrac4x+\\dfrac{2x}{x^{2}+1}\\). Attacking the original directly &mdash; chain rule through a product inside a log &mdash; gives the same answer with triple the algebra. (A) is \\(\\frac1u\\) with no \\(u'\\); (B) is only \\(u'\\) with no \\(\\frac1u\\); (D) expands the logs but forgets the chain factor \\(2x\\) on the second term.","unit":3,"id":"u3m39","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\,x^{x}=\\) (for \\(x \\gt 0\\))","opts":[{"t":"\\(x\\cdot x^{\\,x-1}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(x^{x}\\ln x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\ln x+1\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(x^{x}(\\ln x+1)\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Neither the power rule nor the exponential rule applies alone &mdash; the base <i>and</i> the exponent both vary. Take logs: \\(\\ln y=x\\ln x\\), differentiate implicitly: \\(\\dfrac{y'}{y}=\\ln x+1\\) (product rule on the right), so \\(y'=x^{x}(\\ln x+1)\\). (A) pretends the exponent is constant; (B) pretends the base is constant; (C) forgets to multiply back through by \\(y=x^{x}\\) at the end &mdash; the last step of every logarithmic differentiation.","unit":3,"id":"u3m40","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x^{2}+1}{x}\\), then \\(f'(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(1+\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}-1}{x^{2}+1}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(1-\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Split the fraction first: \\(f(x)=x+\\dfrac1x=x+x^{-1}\\), so \\(f'(x)=1-x^{-2}=1-\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\\). The quotient rule also works &mdash; \\(\\frac{2x\\cdot x-(x^2+1)}{x^2}=\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2}\\), the same thing &mdash; but splitting is faster and less error-prone. (A) drops the negative from the power rule on \\(x^{-1}\\); (C) differentiates only the numerator, ignoring the denominator entirely.","unit":3,"id":"u3m41","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"For which of the following is implicit differentiation <b>required</b> to find \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(y=x^{2}e^{x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(y=\\ln(x^{2}+1)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\sin(xy)=x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(y=\\arctan(3x)\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Implicit differentiation is for relations you cannot (or should not bother to) solve for \\(y\\). In \\(\\sin(xy)=x\\), the variable \\(y\\) is locked inside \\(\\sin(xy)\\) with \\(x\\) &mdash; no algebra frees it. The other three are already solved for \\(y\\): (A) is a product rule, (B) and (D) are chain rules. Seeing \\(y\\) tangled with \\(x\\) on the same side of the equation is the signal.","unit":3,"id":"u3m42","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{x^{3}-1}{x-1}\\) for \\(x\\neq 1\\), then \\(f'(2)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(12\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(7\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(5\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Factor first: \\(x^{3}-1=(x-1)(x^{2}+x+1)\\), so \\(f(x)=x^{2}+x+1\\) for \\(x\\neq1\\), and \\(f'(x)=2x+1\\), giving \\(f'(2)=5\\). The quotient rule produces the same value with far more work &mdash; recognizing the removable factor is the &ldquo;selecting procedures&rdquo; skill being tested. (A) is \\(3x^2\\) at 2, differentiating only the numerator; (B) adds wrong; (C) is \\(2x\\) at 2, dropping the \\(+1\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m43","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\big[x^{2}\\arctan x\\big]=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^{2}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2x\\arctan x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2x\\arctan x+\\dfrac{x^{2}}{1+x^{2}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}}{1+x^{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"The outermost structure is a <i>product</i>: \\(u=x^{2}\\), \\(v=\\arctan x\\). Product rule: \\(u'v+uv'=2x\\arctan x+x^{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{1+x^{2}}\\). Both terms survive &mdash; (B) and (D) each keep only one half of the product rule, the most common way this question is missed. (A) multiplies the two derivatives together, which is never the product rule.","unit":3,"id":"u3m44","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.5 · Selecting Procedures","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left[\\dfrac{\\sin(2x)}{e^{x}}\\right]=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^{-x}\\big(2\\cos 2x-\\sin 2x\\big)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(e^{-x}\\big(2\\cos 2x+\\sin 2x\\big)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(e^{-x}\\big(\\cos 2x-\\sin 2x\\big)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2\\cos 2x-\\sin 2x}{e^{2x}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Quotient rule with a chain inside: \\(\\dfrac{2\\cos(2x)\\,e^{x}-\\sin(2x)\\,e^{x}}{e^{2x}}\\). Factor \\(e^{x}\\) from the numerator and cancel one power: \\(e^{-x}\\big(2\\cos 2x-\\sin 2x\\big)\\). (B) has a sign error in the quotient rule&rsquo;s subtraction; (C) forgets the chain factor 2 on \\(\\sin(2x)\\); (D) does the algebra correctly until the last step, then fails to cancel \\(e^{x}\\) &mdash; leaving \\(e^{2x}\\) below makes it a different function.","unit":3,"id":"u3m45","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=x^{4}-3x^{2}\\), then \\(f''(1)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(12\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-2\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(18\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate twice: \\(f'(x)=4x^{3}-6x\\), then \\(f''(x)=12x^{2}-6\\). Evaluate last: \\(f''(1)=12-6=6\\). (C) is \\(f'(1)\\) &mdash; stopping one derivative short; (A) keeps only the first term of \\(f''\\); (D) evaluates \\(12x^2+6\\), a sign slip on the constant. Differentiate fully, <i>then</i> plug in &mdash; never the reverse.","unit":3,"id":"u3m46","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(y=\\sin(2x)\\), then \\(\\dfrac{d^{4}y}{dx^{4}}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\sin 2x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-16\\sin 2x\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(16\\sin 2x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(16\\cos 2x\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Each derivative multiplies by 2 (chain rule) and advances the sine cycle one step: \\(2\\cos 2x\\to-4\\sin 2x\\to-8\\cos 2x\\to 16\\sin 2x\\). After four steps the trig part returns to \\(\\sin 2x\\) and the accumulated factor is \\(2^{4}=16\\). (A) remembers the four-step cycle but forgets that every step also pays a factor of 2; (B) mismatches the sign pattern (the signs go \\(+,-,-,+\\)); (D) stops the cycle a quarter-turn early.","unit":3,"id":"u3m47","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=x\\,e^{x}\\), then \\(f''(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\((x+1)e^{x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(x\\,e^{x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(2e^{x}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\((x+2)e^{x}\\)","correct":true}],"explain":"Product rule twice. First: \\(f'(x)=e^{x}+x e^{x}=(x+1)e^{x}\\). Again: \\(f''(x)=e^{x}+(x+1)e^{x}=(x+2)e^{x}\\). The pattern continues &mdash; each derivative bumps the constant by 1, so \\(f^{(n)}(x)=(x+n)e^{x}\\). (A) is \\(f'\\), one derivative short; (C) differentiates \\((x+1)\\) and \\(e^x\\) separately and adds wrong; (B) pretends \\(xe^x\\) is its own derivative like \\(e^x\\) alone would be.","unit":3,"id":"u3m48","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"A curve satisfies \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{y}\\). In terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{y^{2}-x^{2}}{y^{3}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}-y^{2}}{y^{3}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{y}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{y-x}{y^{2}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Differentiate \\(y'=\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) with the quotient rule, remembering \\(y'\\) appears again: \\(y''=\\dfrac{y-x\\,y'}{y^{2}}\\). Substitute \\(y'=\\frac{x}{y}\\): \\(y''=\\dfrac{y-\\frac{x^{2}}{y}}{y^{2}}=\\dfrac{y^{2}-x^{2}}{y^{3}}\\). The two-step ritual for implicit second derivatives: differentiate, then <i>substitute the first derivative back in</i>. (C) stops before substituting; (B) flips the subtraction; (D) never substitutes and drops a power of \\(y\\).","unit":3,"id":"u3m49","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}\\), then \\(f^{(4)}(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{24}{x^{5}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{24}{x^{5}}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{24}{x^{4}}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{6}{x^{4}}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Write \\(f(x)=x^{-1}\\) and track both the sign and the factorial growth: \\(f'=-x^{-2}\\), \\(f''=2x^{-3}\\), \\(f'''=-6x^{-4}\\), \\(f^{(4)}=24x^{-5}\\). The signs alternate, so the 4th derivative (even step) is <i>positive</i>, and the coefficient is \\(4!=24\\). (A) misses that even-order derivatives of \\(1/x\\) are positive; (D) is \\(f'''\\), one step short; (C) has the wrong power &mdash; each derivative drops the exponent by exactly one.","unit":3,"id":"u3m50","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\ln x\\), then \\(f''(4)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{1}{16}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{16}\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"\\(f'(x)=\\dfrac1x=x^{-1}\\), so \\(f''(x)=-x^{-2}=-\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\\). At \\(x=4\\): \\(-\\dfrac{1}{16}\\). (A) is \\(f'(4)\\) &mdash; the first derivative, not the second; (D) drops the negative that the power rule produces on \\(x^{-1}\\); (B) applies the negative but forgets to square. The second derivative of \\(\\ln x\\) being negative everywhere is why the log curve is concave down.","unit":3,"id":"u3m51","calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"3.6 · Higher-Order Derivatives","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves along a line with position \\(s(t)=t^{3}-9t^{2}+24t\\) for \\(t\\ge 0\\). What is the particle&rsquo;s acceleration at the <b>first</b> time its velocity is zero?","opts":[{"t":"\\(-6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(6\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(-18\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\(0\\)","correct":false}],"explain":"Velocity is the first derivative: \\(v(t)=3t^{2}-18t+24=3(t-2)(t-4)\\), which is zero at \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=4\\) &mdash; the first is \\(t=2\\). Acceleration is the second derivative: \\(a(t)=6t-18\\), so \\(a(2)=-6\\). (B) evaluates at \\(t=4\\) instead (a(4)=+6); (C) is \\(a(0)\\), never solving for the rest time; (D) assumes zero velocity forces zero acceleration &mdash; it doesn&rsquo;t: the particle is momentarily still while actively decelerating.","unit":3,"id":"u3m52","calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"Water drains from a tank so that the volume remaining is \\(V(t)\\) liters after \\(t\\) minutes. If \\(V'(10)=-3\\), what is the best interpretation?","opts":[{"t":"At \\(t=10\\) min, the volume is decreasing at a rate of \\(3\\) liters per minute","correct":true},{"t":"At \\(t=10\\) min, there are \\(3\\) liters left in the tank"},{"t":"Over the first \\(10\\) minutes, \\(3\\) liters drained out"},{"t":"At \\(t=10\\) min, the volume is decreasing by \\(3\\) liters per minute per minute"}],"explain":"<b>A derivative is an instantaneous rate, carrying the units \"output per input.\"</b> Here output is liters, input is minutes, so \\(V'(10)=-3\\) means the volume is changing at \\(-3\\) liters/minute <i>at the instant</i> \\(t=10\\) — decreasing at \\(3\\) L/min.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"\\(3\\) liters left\" confuses the <i>rate</i> \\(V'\\) with the <i>amount</i> \\(V\\).<br>• \"\\(3\\) liters over 10 minutes\" reports an average change, not the instantaneous rate at a single instant.<br>• \"per minute per minute\" gives \\(V''\\) units (acceleration of volume), not \\(V'\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> read the units off the derivative — first derivative is output-per-input at an instant; the sign tells increasing vs decreasing.","unit":4,"id":"u4m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"A population is modeled by \\(P(t)\\), measured in thousands of people, where \\(t\\) is in years. What are the units of \\(P'(t)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"Thousands of people per year","correct":true},{"t":"Thousands of people"},{"t":"Years per thousand people"},{"t":"Thousands of people per year per year"}],"explain":"<b>The derivative's units are always (units of output) / (units of input).</b> Output is thousands of people, input is years, so \\(P'(t)\\) is in <b>thousands of people per year</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"Thousands of people\" are the units of \\(P\\) itself, not its rate of change.<br>• \"Years per thousand people\" inverts the ratio.<br>• \"per year per year\" is the units of \\(P''\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiating divides output units by input units — once.","unit":4,"id":"u4m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"A function \\(f\\) satisfies \\(f(2)=5\\) and \\(f(6)=17\\). What is the average rate of change of \\(f\\) on the interval \\([2,6]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"3","correct":true},{"t":"12"},{"t":"22"},{"t":"6"}],"explain":"<b>Average rate of change is the slope of the secant line:</b> \\(\\dfrac{f(6)-f(2)}{6-2}=\\dfrac{17-5}{4}=\\dfrac{12}{4}=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>12</b> is just \\(f(6)-f(2)\\) — the change in output, without dividing by the change in input.<br>• <b>22</b> adds the outputs \\(5+17\\).<br>• <b>6</b> divides by the wrong thing or uses \\(f(6)/... \\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> average rate of change is a difference quotient — rise over run — never just the rise.","unit":4,"id":"u4m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"At time \\(t=3\\), a quantity \\(H(t)\\) has \\(H'(3)>0\\) and \\(H''(3)<0\\). Which statement is true at \\(t=3\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(H\\) is increasing, but at a decreasing rate","correct":true},{"t":"\\(H\\) is increasing, and at an increasing rate"},{"t":"\\(H\\) is decreasing, but slowing its decrease"},{"t":"\\(H\\) has a local maximum"}],"explain":"<b>The first derivative controls direction; the second controls how the rate itself changes.</b> \\(H'(3)>0\\) means \\(H\\) is increasing. \\(H''(3)<0\\) means \\(H'\\) is decreasing, so the increase is slowing — <b>increasing at a decreasing rate</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"increasing at an increasing rate\" would need \\(H''>0\\).<br>• \"decreasing\" contradicts \\(H'>0\\).<br>• A local max needs \\(H'=0\\), not \\(H'>0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \"increasing at a decreasing rate\" = \\(H'>0\\) with \\(H''<0\\) — direction from \\(H'\\), curvature of the story from \\(H''\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"A pebble is dropped and its height is \\(h(t)=100-4.9t^{2}\\) meters after \\(t\\) seconds. What is the velocity of the pebble at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"-19.6\\text{ m/s}","correct":true},{"t":"19.6\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"-9.8\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"80.4\\text{ m/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Velocity is the derivative of position.</b> \\(h'(t)=-9.8t\\), so \\(h'(2)=-9.8(2)=-19.6\\) m/s. The negative sign means the pebble is moving downward.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(19.6\\)</b> drops the sign — that's the <i>speed</i>, but the question asks for velocity, which is signed.<br>• <b>\\(-9.8\\)</b> is the coefficient / the acceleration \\(h''(t)\\), not the velocity at \\(t=2\\).<br>• <b>\\(80.4\\)</b> is \\(h(2)=100-19.6\\), the <i>height</i>, not the rate.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> velocity = \\(h'\\) with its sign; height is \\(h\\); acceleration is \\(h''\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"The table gives values of a differentiable function \\(g\\). Using the table, which is the best estimate of \\(g'(4)\\)?","math":"\\begin{array}{c|cccc} x & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ \\hline g(x) & 10 & 18 & 30 \\end{array}","opts":[{"t":"5","correct":true},{"t":"8"},{"t":"4"},{"t":"6"}],"explain":"<b>With a value on each side, the symmetric difference gives the best estimate:</b> \\(g'(4)\\approx\\dfrac{g(6)-g(2)}{6-2}=\\dfrac{30-10}{4}=5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8</b> is a one-sided slope on \\([2,4]\\): \\(\\dfrac{18-10}{2}\\).<br>• <b>6</b> is the one-sided slope on \\([4,6]\\): \\(\\dfrac{30-18}{2}\\).<br>• <b>4</b> divides \\(g(6)-g(2)=20\\) by \\(5\\) instead of \\(4\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when the point has data on both sides, the symmetric (centered) difference quotient over the widest balanced interval is the most accurate estimate of the derivative.","unit":4,"id":"u4m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"A company's profit is \\(P(x)\\) dollars when \\(x\\) units are made. At \\(x=50\\), \\(P'(50)=0\\) and \\(P''(50)<0\\). What does this say?","opts":[{"t":"Profit is (locally) maximized at \\(x=50\\) units","correct":true},{"t":"Profit is (locally) minimized at \\(x=50\\) units"},{"t":"Profit is zero at \\(x=50\\) units"},{"t":"Profit is increasing at \\(x=50\\) units"}],"explain":"<b>\\(P'=0\\) marks a critical point; the sign of \\(P''\\) classifies it (second-derivative test).</b> \\(P''(50)<0\\) means concave down, so \\(x=50\\) is a <b>local maximum</b> of profit.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• A local <i>minimum</i> would require \\(P''>0\\).<br>• \"Profit is zero\" confuses \\(P'=0\\) (rate is zero) with \\(P=0\\) (amount is zero).<br>• \"Increasing\" contradicts \\(P'=0\\); at that instant the profit is momentarily not changing.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(P'=0\\) with \\(P''<0\\) ⇒ local max; \\(P'=0\\) with \\(P''>0\\) ⇒ local min. Rate zero ≠ value zero.","unit":4,"id":"u4m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":true,"prompt":"The temperature of a cooling cup of coffee is \\(T(t)=70+120e^{-0.05t}\\) degrees Fahrenheit after \\(t\\) minutes. At what rate is the temperature changing at \\(t=10\\) minutes?","opts":[{"t":"-3.639\\text{ °F/min}","correct":true},{"t":"-6.000\\text{ °F/min}"},{"t":"142.78\\text{ °F/min}"},{"t":"3.639\\text{ °F/min}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate and evaluate.</b> \\(T'(t)=120\\cdot(-0.05)e^{-0.05t}=-6e^{-0.05t}\\). At \\(t=10\\): \\(T'(10)=-6e^{-0.5}\\approx-6(0.6065)=-3.639\\) °F/min.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-6\\)</b> forgets the \\(e^{-0.05t}\\) factor — that's the coefficient, i.e. \\(T'(0)\\).<br>• <b>\\(142.78\\)</b> is \\(T(10)\\), the temperature, not its rate.<br>• <b>\\(+3.639\\)</b> drops the sign — the coffee is cooling, so the rate is negative.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a rate \"at a time\" is \\(T'\\) evaluated there — differentiate first (chain rule included), then plug in, and keep the sign.","unit":4,"id":"u4m7","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"Grain is added to a silo so that after \\(t\\) hours the amount is \\(A(t)\\) tons, with \\(A'(t)>0\\) and \\(A''(t)>0\\). Which describes the silo?","opts":[{"t":"The amount of grain is increasing at an increasing rate","correct":true},{"t":"The amount of grain is increasing at a decreasing rate"},{"t":"The amount of grain is decreasing"},{"t":"The amount of grain is constant"}],"explain":"<b>\\(A'>0\\) ⇒ increasing; \\(A''>0\\) ⇒ the rate itself is increasing.</b> So grain is piling up faster and faster — <b>increasing at an increasing rate</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"increasing at a decreasing rate\" needs \\(A''<0\\).<br>• \"decreasing\" contradicts \\(A'>0\\).<br>• \"constant\" needs \\(A'=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> both derivatives positive means the quantity and its speed are both growing — the classic \"accelerating growth.\"","unit":4,"id":"u4m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(R(t)\\) is the rate (in gallons per hour) at which water flows into a reservoir at time \\(t\\) hours. What does \\(R'(5)=2\\) mean?","opts":[{"t":"At \\(t=5\\) hr, the flow rate is increasing by \\(2\\) gallons per hour, per hour","correct":true},{"t":"At \\(t=5\\) hr, water is flowing in at \\(2\\) gallons per hour"},{"t":"In \\(5\\) hours, \\(2\\) gallons have flowed in"},{"t":"At \\(t=5\\) hr, there are \\(2\\) gallons in the reservoir"}],"explain":"<b>\\(R\\) is already a rate (gal/hr); its derivative \\(R'\\) is the rate at which that rate changes.</b> So \\(R'(5)=2\\) means the inflow rate is <i>increasing</i> at \\(2\\) gallons per hour, per hour (gal/hr²).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"flowing in at \\(2\\) gal/hr\" is \\(R(5)\\), the rate itself — not its derivative.<br>• \"\\(2\\) gallons in 5 hours\" and \"\\(2\\) gallons in the reservoir\" both describe amounts of water (\\(\\int R\\,dt\\)), not \\(R'\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when the quantity is <i>already</i> a rate, its derivative measures how fast that rate is changing — the units gain a second \"per hour.\"","unit":4,"id":"u4m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves along a line with position \\(x(t)=t^{3}-6t^{2}+9t\\). At which time(s) is the particle at rest?","opts":[{"t":"t=1\\text{ and }t=3","correct":true},{"t":"t=0\\text{ and }t=3"},{"t":"t=2"},{"t":"t=1\\text{ and }t=2"}],"explain":"<b>\"At rest\" means velocity is zero.</b> \\(v(t)=x'(t)=3t^2-12t+9=3(t^2-4t+3)=3(t-1)(t-3)\\). So \\(v=0\\) at \\(t=1\\) and \\(t=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t=0,3\\)</b> mis-factors, or reads a root of \\(x(t)\\) instead of \\(v(t)\\).<br>• <b>\\(t=2\\)</b> is where the <i>acceleration</i> \\(a=6t-12\\) is zero, not the velocity.<br>• <b>\\(t=1,2\\)</b> mixes a velocity root with an acceleration root.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> at rest ⇔ \\(v(t)=0\\); differentiate position first, then solve.","unit":4,"id":"u4m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"For the particle with \\(x(t)=t^{3}-6t^{2}+9t\\), is the particle speeding up or slowing down at \\(t=2.5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"Slowing down","correct":true},{"t":"Speeding up"},{"t":"Neither — it is at rest"},{"t":"Moving at constant speed"}],"explain":"<b>Speeding up ⇔ velocity and acceleration have the same sign; slowing down ⇔ opposite signs.</b><br>\\(v(t)=3(t-1)(t-3)\\): at \\(t=2.5\\), \\(v=3(1.5)(-0.5)=-2.25<0\\). \\(a(t)=6t-12\\): \\(a(2.5)=3>0\\). Opposite signs ⇒ <b>slowing down</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"Speeding up\" would need \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) with matching signs.<br>• \"At rest\" is false: \\(v(2.5)\\neq0\\).<br>• \"Constant speed\" needs \\(a=0\\) (or \\(|v|\\) not changing), but \\(a(2.5)=3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> compare the <i>signs</i> of \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) — same ⇒ speeding up, opposite ⇒ slowing down. Speeding-up is not the same as \"velocity positive.\"","unit":4,"id":"u4m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle on a line has velocity \\(v(t)=t^{2}-4\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 3\\). What is the total distance traveled?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{23}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"3"},{"t":"\\dfrac{16}{3}"},{"t":"-3"}],"explain":"<b>Total distance integrates speed \\(|v|\\); the particle reverses where \\(v=0\\).</b> \\(v=t^2-4=0\\) at \\(t=2\\), so split there: \\(v<0\\) on \\([0,2]\\), \\(v>0\\) on \\([2,3]\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2|v|\\,dt=-\\int_0^2(t^2-4)\\,dt=-\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-4t\\Big]_0^2=-\\big(\\tfrac83-8\\big)=\\tfrac{16}{3}\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^3(t^2-4)\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-4t\\Big]_2^3=(9-12)-\\big(\\tfrac83-8\\big)=-3+\\tfrac{16}{3}=\\tfrac73\\).<br>Total distance \\(=\\tfrac{16}{3}+\\tfrac{7}{3}=\\dfrac{23}{3}\\approx7.667\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3</b> is the magnitude of <i>displacement</i>, \\(\\big|\\int_0^3 v\\,dt\\big|=|-3|\\) — integrating \\(v\\) without splitting at the reversal.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> keeps only the first piece \\([0,2]\\).<br>• <b>\\(-3\\)</b> is the signed displacement — distance can't be negative.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> distance integrates \\(|v|\\); split at every sign change of \\(v\\), then add the absolute areas.","unit":4,"id":"u4m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(v(t)=2t-6\\) (m/s). What is the speed of the particle at \\(t=1\\)?","opts":[{"t":"4\\text{ m/s}","correct":true},{"t":"-4\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"2\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"6\\text{ m/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Speed is the absolute value of velocity.</b> \\(v(1)=2(1)-6=-4\\) m/s, so the speed is \\(|-4|=4\\) m/s.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-4\\)</b> is the velocity — speed is never negative.<br>• <b>\\(2\\)</b> is the acceleration \\(v'(t)=2\\).<br>• <b>\\(6\\)</b> reads off the constant term.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> speed \\(=|v|\\ge0\\); velocity keeps its sign, speed drops it.","unit":4,"id":"u4m13","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(v(t)=t^{2}-2t-3\\). For what values of \\(t\\ge0\\) is the particle moving in the positive direction?","opts":[{"t":"t>3","correct":true},{"t":"t<3"},{"t":"t>-1"},{"t":"1<t<3"}],"explain":"<b>Positive direction ⇔ \\(v(t)>0\\).</b> \\(v=t^2-2t-3=(t-3)(t+1)\\). For \\(t\\ge0\\), the factor \\(t+1>0\\), so the sign follows \\(t-3\\): \\(v>0\\) exactly when \\(t>3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t<3\\)</b> is where \\(v<0\\) (moving negative) on this domain.<br>• <b>\\(t>-1\\)</b> ignores the root \\(t=3\\) and the domain.<br>• <b>\\(1<t<3\\)</b> comes from mis-factoring the quadratic.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> direction is the sign of \\(v\\); factor, find the roots, and test — but respect the domain \\(t\\ge0\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"If a particle's position is \\(x(t)\\), which quantity equals its acceleration?","opts":[{"t":"x''(t)","correct":true},{"t":"x'(t)"},{"t":"|x'(t)|"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int x(t)\\,dt"}],"explain":"<b>Acceleration is the second derivative of position</b> (the derivative of velocity): \\(a(t)=v'(t)=x''(t)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x'(t)\\)</b> is the velocity.<br>• <b>\\(|x'(t)|\\)</b> is the speed.<br>• the integral of position has no standard physical meaning here.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> position \\(\\xrightarrow{\\,d/dt\\,}\\) velocity \\(\\xrightarrow{\\,d/dt\\,}\\) acceleration — two derivatives down.","unit":4,"id":"u4m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has \\(v(t)=3t^{2}\\) (ft/s) and is at position \\(x=5\\) ft when \\(t=1\\). What is its position at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"11\\text{ ft}","correct":true},{"t":"8\\text{ ft}"},{"t":"12\\text{ ft}"},{"t":"7\\text{ ft}"}],"explain":"<b>Recover position with the accumulation form and the initial condition:</b> \\(x(2)=x(1)+\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 3t^2\\,dt\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 3t^2\\,dt=\\big[t^3\\big]_1^2=8-1=7\\). So \\(x(2)=5+7=11\\) ft.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8</b> is \\(x(1)+t^3\\big|_{... }\\) mishandled, or the antiderivative \\(t^3\\) at \\(t=2\\) minus... losing the initial value.<br>• <b>12</b> is \\(v(2)=3(4)\\) — the velocity, not position.<br>• <b>7</b> is the displacement \\(\\int_1^2 v\\,dt\\) alone, forgetting to add \\(x(1)=5\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> new position = old position + \\(\\int v\\,dt\\); the initial position is not optional.","unit":4,"id":"u4m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(v(t)=t^{2}-4t+3\\). At \\(t=2\\), the velocity is \\(-1\\) and the acceleration is \\(0\\). What is happening to the particle's speed at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"Its speed is momentarily neither increasing nor decreasing","correct":true},{"t":"It is speeding up"},{"t":"It is slowing down"},{"t":"It is at rest"}],"explain":"<b>Whether speed increases depends on the sign of acceleration relative to velocity — and here \\(a=0\\).</b> With \\(a(2)=0\\), there is no push either way at that instant, so the speed is momentarily neither increasing nor decreasing. (Indeed \\(a(t)=2t-4\\) changes sign at \\(t=2\\).)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"speeding up\"/\"slowing down\" both require \\(a\\neq0\\) with a definite sign relation to \\(v\\).<br>• \"at rest\" needs \\(v=0\\), but \\(v(2)=-1\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(a=0\\) at an instant means the speed is at a momentary standstill in its change — often the boundary between speeding up and slowing down.","unit":4,"id":"u4m17","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(v(t)=\\sin(t^{2})\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 2\\). What is the acceleration at \\(t=1.5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"-1.885","correct":true},{"t":"0.778"},{"t":"-0.628"},{"t":"1.885"}],"explain":"<b>Acceleration is \\(v'(t)\\); use the chain rule.</b> \\(a(t)=\\cos(t^2)\\cdot 2t=2t\\cos(t^2)\\). At \\(t=1.5\\): \\(a=2(1.5)\\cos(2.25)=3\\cos(2.25)\\approx 3(-0.6282)=-1.885\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0.778</b> is \\(v(1.5)=\\sin(2.25)\\) — the velocity, not its derivative.<br>• <b>\\(-0.628\\)</b> is \\(\\cos(2.25)\\) — differentiating \\(\\sin(t^2)\\) to \\(\\cos(t^2)\\) but forgetting the chain-rule factor \\(2t\\).<br>• <b>\\(+1.885\\)</b> drops the sign (\\(\\cos(2.25)\\) is negative).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\sin(t^2)=2t\\cos(t^2)\\) — the inner derivative \\(2t\\) is essential, and keep the sign of the cosine.","unit":4,"id":"u4m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle's position is \\(x(t)=t^{3}-6t^{2}+9t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le4\\). What is the particle's displacement over \\([0,4]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"8"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"-4"}],"explain":"<b>Displacement is the net change in position:</b> \\(x(4)-x(0)\\). \\(x(4)=64-96+36=4\\) and \\(x(0)=0\\), so displacement \\(=4-0=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> assumes the particle returns to start; it does not (\\(x(4)=4\\)).<br>• <b>8</b> and <b>\\(-4\\)</b> come from arithmetic slips in \\(x(4)\\) (e.g. sign errors on \\(-6t^2\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> displacement \\(=x(b)-x(a)\\) (signed net change); it differs from total distance whenever the particle reverses direction.","unit":4,"id":"u4m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"The radius of a circle increases at \\(3\\) cm/s. How fast is the area increasing when the radius is \\(5\\) cm? (\\(A=\\pi r^{2}\\))","opts":[{"t":"30\\pi\\text{ cm}^2/\\text{s}","correct":true},{"t":"25\\pi\\text{ cm}^2/\\text{s}"},{"t":"6\\pi\\text{ cm}^2/\\text{s}"},{"t":"30\\text{ cm}^2/\\text{s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the relation with respect to time.</b> \\(A=\\pi r^2\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi r\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\). With \\(r=5\\), \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=3\\): \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi(5)(3)=30\\pi\\) cm²/s.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(25\\pi\\)</b> is the area \\(\\pi r^2\\) at \\(r=5\\) — plugging into \\(A\\), not \\(dA/dt\\).<br>• <b>\\(6\\pi\\)</b> forgets the \\(r\\): \\(2\\pi\\cdot\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\).<br>• <b>30</b> drops the \\(\\pi\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate implicitly in \\(t\\) first (chain rule brings out \\(dr/dt\\)), <i>then</i> substitute the instant's values — never plug in before differentiating.","unit":4,"id":"u4m20","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"A spherical balloon is inflated so its radius grows at \\(2\\) in/s. How fast is the volume increasing when \\(r=3\\) in? (\\(V=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^{3}\\))","opts":[{"t":"72\\pi\\text{ in}^3/\\text{s}","correct":true},{"t":"36\\pi\\text{ in}^3/\\text{s}"},{"t":"24\\pi\\text{ in}^3/\\text{s}"},{"t":"144\\pi\\text{ in}^3/\\text{s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the volume relation:</b> \\(V=\\tfrac43\\pi r^3\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=4\\pi r^2\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\). With \\(r=3\\), \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=2\\): \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=4\\pi(9)(2)=72\\pi\\) in³/s.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(36\\pi\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=2\\) factor (uses \\(4\\pi r^2\\) alone).<br>• <b>\\(24\\pi\\)</b> mishandles the power rule (e.g. \\(\\tfrac43\\pi\\cdot 3r^2\\) then a slip, or \\(4\\pi r\\)).<br>• <b>\\(144\\pi\\)</b> doubles — e.g. \\(8\\pi r^2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(r^3)=3r^2\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\); the \\(3\\) cancels the \\(\\tfrac13\\), leaving \\(4\\pi r^2\\,dr/dt\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"A 5-meter ladder leans on a wall. Its base slides away from the wall at \\(1\\) m/s. When the base is \\(3\\) m from the wall, how fast is the top sliding down? (Use \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=25\\).)","opts":[{"t":"-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\text{ m/s}","correct":true},{"t":"-\\dfrac{4}{3}\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"-1\\text{ m/s}"},{"t":"-\\dfrac{3}{5}\\text{ m/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate \\(x^2+y^2=25\\) in time:</b> \\(2x\\dfrac{dx}{dt}+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=0\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).<br>When \\(x=3\\), \\(y=\\sqrt{25-9}=4\\), and \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=1\\): \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{3}{4}(1)=-\\dfrac34\\) m/s (downward).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-\\tfrac43\\)</b> inverts the ratio \\(x/y\\).<br>• <b>\\(-1\\)</b> assumes the top drops as fast as the base moves — false except symmetrically.<br>• <b>\\(-\\tfrac35\\)</b> uses the hypotenuse \\(5\\) in place of \\(y=4\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> find the missing side with the Pythagorean relation at the instant, then use \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"Water fills a cone (vertex down) with radius always half its depth, so \\(r=\\tfrac{h}{2}\\). Which formula gives the volume in terms of \\(h\\) alone? (\\(V=\\tfrac13\\pi r^{2}h\\))","opts":[{"t":"V=\\dfrac{\\pi h^{3}}{12}","correct":true},{"t":"V=\\dfrac{\\pi h^{3}}{3}"},{"t":"V=\\dfrac{\\pi h^{2}}{12}"},{"t":"V=\\dfrac{\\pi h^{3}}{4}"}],"explain":"<b>Substitute the constraint \\(r=\\tfrac{h}{2}\\) before differentiating</b> so \\(V\\) has one variable: \\(V=\\tfrac13\\pi\\left(\\tfrac{h}{2}\\right)^2 h=\\tfrac13\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac{h^2}{4}\\cdot h=\\dfrac{\\pi h^3}{12}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{\\pi h^3}{3}\\)</b> forgot to substitute \\(r=\\tfrac h2\\) (left \\(r^2h\\) as \\(h^3\\) without the \\(\\tfrac14\\)).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{\\pi h^2}{12}\\)</b> dropped a factor of \\(h\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{\\pi h^3}{4}\\)</b> squared \\(\\tfrac h2\\) but forgot the \\(\\tfrac13\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> use the similar-triangle relationship to reduce to one variable <i>first</i>; then \\(dV/dt\\) won't carry an unwanted \\(dr/dt\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"Two quantities satisfy \\(xy=12\\). If \\(x\\) increases at \\(2\\) units/s, how fast is \\(y\\) changing when \\(x=3\\)?","opts":[{"t":"-\\dfrac{8}{3}\\text{ units/s}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{8}{3}\\text{ units/s}"},{"t":"-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\text{ units/s}"},{"t":"-8\\text{ units/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the product with the product rule:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(xy)=x\\dfrac{dy}{dt}+y\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=0\\).<br>When \\(x=3\\), \\(y=\\tfrac{12}{3}=4\\), \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2\\): \\(3\\dfrac{dy}{dt}+4(2)=0\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{8}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(+\\tfrac83\\)</b> drops the sign — but if \\(x\\) grows and \\(xy\\) is fixed, \\(y\\) must shrink.<br>• <b>\\(-\\tfrac23\\)</b> forgets the factor \\(y=4\\).<br>• <b>\\(-8\\)</b> forgets to divide by \\(x=3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a product constraint needs the product rule; solve for the unknown rate after substituting the instant's \\(x,y\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m24","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"The edge of a cube grows at \\(0.5\\) cm/s. How fast is the volume growing when the edge is \\(4\\) cm? (\\(V=s^{3}\\))","opts":[{"t":"24\\text{ cm}^3/\\text{s}","correct":true},{"t":"48\\text{ cm}^3/\\text{s}"},{"t":"8\\text{ cm}^3/\\text{s}"},{"t":"12\\text{ cm}^3/\\text{s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate:</b> \\(V=s^3\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=3s^2\\dfrac{ds}{dt}=3(4)^2(0.5)=3(16)(0.5)=24\\) cm³/s.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>48</b> forgets the \\(\\dfrac{ds}{dt}=0.5\\) and uses \\(3s^2=48\\).<br>• <b>8</b> uses \\(2s\\dfrac{ds}{dt}\\) (area rule) instead of \\(3s^2\\).<br>• <b>12</b> uses \\(3s\\) instead of \\(3s^2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> power rule in time: \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(s^3)=3s^2\\dfrac{ds}{dt}\\) — keep the exponent's power on \\(s\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m25","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":true,"prompt":"Sand pours onto a pile shaped so its volume is \\(V=\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}h^{3}\\). If \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=10\\) ft³/min, how fast is the height rising when \\(h=4\\) ft?","opts":[{"t":"0.796\\text{ ft/min}","correct":true},{"t":"2.500\\text{ ft/min}"},{"t":"3.183\\text{ ft/min}"},{"t":"0.265\\text{ ft/min}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate and solve for \\(dh/dt\\):</b> \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\cdot3h^2\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi h^2}{4}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\).<br>At \\(h=4\\): \\(10=\\dfrac{\\pi(16)}{4}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=4\\pi\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{10}{4\\pi}\\approx0.796\\) ft/min.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.5</b> is \\(\\dfrac{10}{4}\\), dropping the \\(\\pi\\).<br>• <b>3.183</b> is \\(\\dfrac{10}{\\pi}\\), dropping the \\(4\\).<br>• <b>0.265</b> divides by an extra factor of \\(3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate to relate the rates, substitute \\(h\\), then isolate the unknown rate — carry \\(\\pi\\) and all constants through.","unit":4,"id":"u4m26","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"In a related-rates problem, a person 6 ft tall walks away from a 15-ft lamppost, and \\(s\\) is the tip-of-shadow distance from the post. Setting up similar triangles gives \\(s=\\tfrac{5}{3}x\\), where \\(x\\) is the person's distance from the post. If the person walks at \\(4\\) ft/s, how fast does the shadow's tip move?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{20}{3}\\text{ ft/s}","correct":true},{"t":"4\\text{ ft/s}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{8}{3}\\text{ ft/s}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{5}{3}\\text{ ft/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the linear relation:</b> \\(s=\\tfrac53x\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{ds}{dt}=\\tfrac53\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=\\tfrac53(4)=\\dfrac{20}{3}\\) ft/s.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>4</b> is the person's speed \\(dx/dt\\) — the tip of the shadow moves faster than the person.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac83\\)</b> is the speed of the shadow's <i>length</i> \\((s-x)\\), \\(\\tfrac23\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\), not its tip.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac53\\)</b> forgets to multiply by \\(dx/dt=4\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> once the geometry gives a clean relation between the changing lengths, differentiate <i>that</i> relation; be sure you're tracking the quantity asked (tip position vs shadow length).","unit":4,"id":"u4m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":true,"prompt":"Two cars leave an intersection: one heads east at \\(30\\) mph, the other north at \\(40\\) mph. How fast is the distance between them increasing after any time (they started together)?","opts":[{"t":"50\\text{ mph}","correct":true},{"t":"70\\text{ mph}"},{"t":"35\\text{ mph}"},{"t":"10\\text{ mph}"}],"explain":"<b>With \\(D^2=x^2+y^2\\), differentiate:</b> \\(2D\\dfrac{dD}{dt}=2x\\dfrac{dx}{dt}+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\). Because both cars move at constant speed from the origin, \\(x=30t,\\ y=40t,\\ D=50t\\), giving \\(\\dfrac{dD}{dt}=\\dfrac{x(30)+y(40)}{D}=\\dfrac{30t(30)+40t(40)}{50t}=\\dfrac{2500t}{50t}=50\\) mph.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>70</b> just adds the speeds \\(30+40\\) — but they move at right angles, so distances add by Pythagoras, not linearly.<br>• <b>35</b> averages the speeds.<br>• <b>10</b> subtracts them.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> perpendicular motions combine through \\(D^2=x^2+y^2\\); here the separation grows at \\(\\sqrt{30^2+40^2}=50\\) mph.","unit":4,"id":"u4m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"Which is the correct <i>first step</i> for a related-rates problem where the volume of a sphere changes over time?","opts":[{"t":"Differentiate \\(V=\\tfrac43\\pi r^{3}\\) with respect to \\(t\\), keeping \\(r\\) a function of \\(t\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Plug the given radius into \\(V=\\tfrac43\\pi r^{3}\\) first, then differentiate"},{"t":"Differentiate \\(V=\\tfrac43\\pi r^{3}\\) with respect to \\(r\\) only"},{"t":"Set \\(\\tfrac{dV}{dt}=\\tfrac43\\pi r^{3}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the relation with respect to time while the variables are still symbolic</b>, so the chain rule produces the rate you want: \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=4\\pi r^2\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\). Only <i>after</i> differentiating do you substitute the instant's values.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• Plugging in the radius first turns \\(r\\) into a constant, so its derivative becomes \\(0\\) — you lose the relationship entirely.<br>• Differentiating with respect to \\(r\\) only gives \\(dV/dr\\), not \\(dV/dt\\).<br>• Setting \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=\\tfrac43\\pi r^3\\) confuses the quantity with its rate.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate symbolically in \\(t\\) first, substitute numbers last — the order is what makes related rates work.","unit":4,"id":"u4m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\). Using the tangent line at \\(a=4\\), estimate \\(\\sqrt{4.1}\\).","opts":[{"t":"2.025","correct":true},{"t":"2.050"},{"t":"2.100"},{"t":"2.005"}],"explain":"<b>Linearization:</b> \\(L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)\\). Here \\(f(4)=2\\), \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac{1}{2\\sqrt x}\\Rightarrow f'(4)=\\tfrac14\\). So \\(L(4.1)=2+\\tfrac14(0.1)=2+0.025=2.025\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.050</b> uses slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) instead of \\(\\tfrac14\\) (forgot the \\(2\\) in \\(2\\sqrt x\\)).<br>• <b>2.100</b> uses slope \\(1\\), or just adds \\(0.1\\).<br>• <b>2.005</b> uses slope \\(\\tfrac{1}{20}\\) or an arithmetic slip.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> plug \\(f(a)\\), \\(f'(a)\\), and \\((x-a)\\) into \\(L\\); the whole game is getting the slope \\(f'(a)\\) right.","unit":4,"id":"u4m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\), the tangent-line estimate of \\(\\sqrt{4.1}\\) is an over- or underestimate?","opts":[{"t":"Overestimate, because \\(f\\) is concave down","correct":true},{"t":"Underestimate, because \\(f\\) is concave up"},{"t":"Overestimate, because \\(f\\) is increasing"},{"t":"Exact, because the tangent line matches \\(f\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Concavity decides over/under: a tangent line lies above a concave-down curve.</b> \\(f''(x)=-\\tfrac14x^{-3/2}<0\\), so \\(\\sqrt x\\) is concave down and the tangent line sits <i>above</i> the curve — the estimate is an <b>overestimate</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"underestimate / concave up\" has the concavity backwards (\\(f''<0\\) here).<br>• \"increasing\" is true but irrelevant — <i>concavity</i>, not direction, controls over/under.<br>• the tangent matches \\(f\\) only <i>at</i> \\(a\\), not nearby.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(f''<0\\) (concave down) ⇒ tangent above ⇒ overestimate; \\(f''>0\\) (concave up) ⇒ tangent below ⇒ underestimate.","unit":4,"id":"u4m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"A function has \\(f(2)=3\\) and \\(f'(2)=-1\\). Use the local linearization to estimate \\(f(2.1)\\).","opts":[{"t":"2.9","correct":true},{"t":"3.1"},{"t":"2.0"},{"t":"3.0"}],"explain":"<b>\\(L(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)\\):</b> \\(L(2.1)=3+(-1)(0.1)=3-0.1=2.9\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3.1</b> uses slope \\(+1\\) — dropping the negative sign of \\(f'(2)\\).<br>• <b>2.0</b> multiplies by \\(1\\) instead of \\(0.1\\) (uses \\(x-2=1\\)).<br>• <b>3.0</b> ignores the correction term entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the linear estimate adds \\(f'(a)\\cdot(x-a)\\) to \\(f(a)\\); respect the sign of the slope and the small size of \\(x-a\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the linearization \\(L(x)\\) of \\(f(x)=e^{x}\\) at \\(a=0\\)?","opts":[{"t":"L(x)=1+x","correct":true},{"t":"L(x)=x"},{"t":"L(x)=e^{x}"},{"t":"L(x)=1+e x"}],"explain":"<b>\\(L(x)=f(0)+f'(0)(x-0)\\).</b> Since \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f'(x)=e^x\\Rightarrow f'(0)=1\\), we get \\(L(x)=1+1\\cdot x=1+x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x\\)</b> forgets \\(f(0)=1\\) (the \\(y\\)-intercept).<br>• <b>\\(e^x\\)</b> is the function itself, not its tangent line.<br>• <b>\\(1+ex\\)</b> uses \\(f'(0)=e\\) instead of \\(1\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the linearization at \\(0\\) is \\(f(0)+f'(0)x\\); for \\(e^x\\) both are \\(1\\), giving the famous \\(e^x\\approx1+x\\) near \\(0\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m33","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"Using a tangent-line approximation, estimate \\(\\ln(1.1)\\). (Use \\(f(x)=\\ln x\\) at \\(a=1\\).)","opts":[{"t":"0.1","correct":true},{"t":"1.1"},{"t":"0.0"},{"t":"0.05"}],"explain":"<b>At \\(a=1\\):</b> \\(f(1)=\\ln1=0\\), \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac1x\\Rightarrow f'(1)=1\\). So \\(L(1.1)=0+1(0.1)=0.1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1.1</b> reports the input, not \\(\\ln\\) of it.<br>• <b>0.0</b> forgets the correction term \\(f'(1)(x-1)\\).<br>• <b>0.05</b> uses slope \\(\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\ln(1+x)\\approx x\\) for small \\(x\\), straight from the tangent line at \\(1\\) (slope \\(1\\), passing through \\((1,0)\\)).","unit":4,"id":"u4m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f(x)=x^{3}\\), use differentials to estimate the change in \\(f\\) as \\(x\\) goes from \\(2\\) to \\(2.01\\).","opts":[{"t":"0.12","correct":true},{"t":"0.01"},{"t":"0.06"},{"t":"1.2"}],"explain":"<b>The differential \\(dy=f'(x)\\,dx\\) estimates the change.</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2\\Rightarrow f'(2)=12\\), and \\(dx=0.01\\), so \\(dy=12(0.01)=0.12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0.01</b> is just \\(dx\\) — forgetting to multiply by the slope.<br>• <b>0.06</b> uses \\(f'(2)=6\\) (mis-differentiating \\(x^3\\) as \\(3x\\)).<br>• <b>1.2</b> slips a decimal place.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(dy=f'(x)\\,dx\\) — the derivative times the small input change — is the linear (tangent-line) estimate of the output change.","unit":4,"id":"u4m35","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"The tangent-line approximation of \\(g(x)=x^{2}\\) near \\(a=3\\) will produce estimates that are:","opts":[{"t":"Underestimates, because \\(g\\) is concave up","correct":true},{"t":"Overestimates, because \\(g\\) is concave up"},{"t":"Underestimates, because \\(g\\) is increasing"},{"t":"Exact everywhere"}],"explain":"<b>\\(g''(x)=2>0\\), so \\(g\\) is concave up and its tangent line lies below the curve</b> — the linear estimates come out as <b>underestimates</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"overestimate / concave up\" states the concavity right but the conclusion backwards.<br>• \"increasing\" is irrelevant to over/under — concavity is what matters.<br>• a parabola is not linear, so the tangent is exact only at \\(x=3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> concave up ⇒ curve above its tangent ⇒ tangent-line values underestimate.","unit":4,"id":"u4m36","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":true,"prompt":"Use the linearization of \\(f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\) at \\(a=8\\) to approximate \\(\\sqrt[3]{8.5}\\). Which is closest?","opts":[{"t":"2.0417","correct":true},{"t":"2.5000"},{"t":"2.1667"},{"t":"2.0833"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f(8)=2\\), \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac13x^{-2/3}\\Rightarrow f'(8)=\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac{1}{12}\\).</b> So \\(L(8.5)=2+\\tfrac{1}{12}(0.5)=2+0.0417=2.0417\\). (True value \\(\\approx2.0408\\).)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.5</b> adds \\(0.5\\) directly — using slope \\(1\\).<br>• <b>2.1667</b> uses slope \\(\\tfrac13\\) (forgot the \\(x^{-2/3}=\\tfrac14\\)).<br>• <b>2.0833</b> uses slope \\(\\tfrac16\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(x^{1/3}\\), \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac13x^{-2/3}\\); at \\(x=8\\) that's \\(\\tfrac{1}{12}\\) — small slope, so \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\) barely moves.","unit":4,"id":"u4m37","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"Near \\(x=0\\), which is the best linear approximation of \\(\\sin x\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\sin x\\approx x","correct":true},{"t":"\\sin x\\approx 1"},{"t":"\\sin x\\approx 1-x"},{"t":"\\sin x\\approx x^{2}"}],"explain":"<b>At \\(a=0\\):</b> \\(\\sin0=0\\), \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin x=\\cos x\\Rightarrow\\) slope \\(=\\cos0=1\\). So \\(L(x)=0+1\\cdot x=x\\), i.e. \\(\\sin x\\approx x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(1\\)</b> is \\(\\cos0\\) — the slope value mistaken for the approximation.<br>• <b>\\(1-x\\)</b> is the linearization of \\(\\cos x\\), not \\(\\sin x\\).<br>• <b>\\(x^2\\)</b> isn't linear and doesn't match the slope.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\sin x\\approx x\\) near \\(0\\) is the tangent line through the origin with slope \\(1\\) — this is exactly why \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\tfrac{\\sin x}{x}=1\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m38","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"The linearization of \\(f\\) at \\(a\\) gives a good approximation of \\(f(x)\\) when:","opts":[{"t":"\\(x\\) is close to \\(a\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x\\) is far from \\(a\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is a straight line only"},{"t":"\\(f'(a)=0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The tangent line hugs the curve only near the point of tangency.</b> The approximation \\(f(x)\\approx L(x)\\) is good when \\(x\\) is <b>close to \\(a\\)</b>; the error grows with \\((x-a)^2\\)-scale as you move away.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"far from \\(a\\)\" is exactly when the estimate degrades.<br>• the method is <i>useful precisely because</i> \\(f\\) need not be linear — it works for curves.<br>• \\(f'(a)=0\\) just makes the tangent horizontal; it doesn't govern accuracy.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> local linearity is <i>local</i> — trust the tangent line only for small \\(|x-a|\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m39","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\infty"},{"t":"\\text{does not exist}"}],"explain":"<b>Substitution gives \\(\\tfrac00\\), an indeterminate form — apply L'Hôpital:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\cos x}{1}=\\cos0=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> assumes the numerator \"wins\" because \\(\\sin0=0\\) — but the denominator also \\(\\to0\\) at the same rate.<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> assumes the denominator wins.<br>• \"does not exist\" — the limit is perfectly well-defined.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> at \\(\\tfrac00\\), the answer is the ratio of the rates \\(\\tfrac{f'}{g'}\\); here \\(\\tfrac{\\cos0}{1}=1\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m40","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2}\\frac{x^{2}-4}{x-2}\\).","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"2"}],"explain":"<b>Form is \\(\\tfrac00\\).</b> By L'Hôpital: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to2}\\frac{2x}{1}=4\\). (Or factor: \\(\\tfrac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}=x+2\\to4\\).)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> differentiates numerator and denominator but forgets to evaluate, or cancels wrongly.<br>• <b>0</b> plugs into the numerator only.<br>• <b>2</b> is \\(x\\to2\\) itself, not the limit value.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tfrac00\\) ⇒ take \\(\\tfrac{f'}{g'}=\\tfrac{2x}{1}\\), then substitute — or equivalently cancel the shared \\((x-2)\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m41","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{x+1}{x+2}\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\text{apply L'Hôpital}"}],"explain":"<b>Check the form first!</b> Substituting gives \\(\\tfrac{0+1}{0+2}=\\tfrac12\\) — a real number, <b>not</b> indeterminate. So you are already done: the limit is \\(\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> is the trap: blindly applying L'Hôpital gives \\(\\tfrac{1}{1}=1\\) — but L'Hôpital is <i>invalid</i> here because the form isn't \\(\\tfrac00\\) or \\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\).<br>• <b>0</b> plugs into the numerator only.<br>• \"apply L'Hôpital\" is exactly the wrong instinct.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> always substitute first; L'Hôpital applies <i>only</i> to indeterminate forms. Using it on a determinate form gives wrong answers.","unit":4,"id":"u4m42","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1-\\cos x}{x^{2}}\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\infty"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac00\\); apply L'Hôpital, then check the form again:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{2x}\\) is still \\(\\tfrac00\\). Apply once more: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\cos x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> stops too early or mis-evaluates \\(\\tfrac{\\sin x}{2x}\\) as \\(0\\).<br>• <b>1</b> forgets the \\(2\\) in the denominator after differentiating \\(x^2\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> assumes the denominator dominates.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> re-check the form after each application; keep differentiating top and bottom while it stays \\(\\tfrac00\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m43","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{3x^{2}+2}{x^{2}-1}\\).","opts":[{"t":"3","correct":true},{"t":"\\infty"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\).</b> L'Hôpital twice (or compare leading terms): \\(\\dfrac{6x}{2x}=3\\), still valid, \\(\\to3\\). Equivalently the ratio of leading coefficients \\(\\tfrac{3}{1}=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> looks only at the numerator growing.<br>• <b>0</b> looks only at the denominator.<br>• <b>1</b> assumes same-degree polynomials always give \\(1\\) — it's the ratio of leading <i>coefficients</i>, here \\(3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\) with equal-degree polynomials ⇒ ratio of leading coefficients; L'Hôpital confirms it.","unit":4,"id":"u4m44","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\tan x}{x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\infty"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac00\\).</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\sec^2 x\\), so by L'Hôpital \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sec^2 x}{1}=\\sec^2 0=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> assumes \\(\\tan0=0\\) settles it, ignoring the denominator's zero.<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> confuses this with \\(\\tan x\\) near \\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> is unrelated.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> near \\(0\\), \\(\\tan x\\approx x\\) (slope \\(\\sec^2 0=1\\)), so the ratio \\(\\to1\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m45","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin 2x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{3}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\dfrac{2}{3}"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac00\\).</b> By L'Hôpital: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{3\\cos 3x}{2\\cos 2x}=\\frac{3\\cos0}{2\\cos0}=\\frac{3}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> forgets the chain-rule factors \\(3\\) and \\(2\\), differentiating to \\(\\tfrac{\\cos3x}{\\cos2x}\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac23\\)</b> flips the ratio.<br>• <b>0</b> plugs into the numerator only.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the chain rule matters — \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin(kx)=k\\cos(kx)\\); the limit becomes the ratio of the inside coefficients, \\(\\tfrac32\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m46","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\ln x}{x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"0","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\infty"},{"t":"\\text{does not exist}"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\).</b> By L'Hôpital: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{1/x}{1}=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{x}=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> assumes \\(\\tfrac\\infty\\infty=1\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> assumes \\(\\ln x\\) keeps pace with \\(x\\) — it doesn't; logs grow far slower.<br>• \"does not exist\" — the limit is a clean \\(0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> polynomials (even just \\(x\\)) dominate logarithms at infinity, so \\(\\tfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\to0\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m47","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"To evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}} x\\ln x\\), which first step makes L'Hôpital applicable?","opts":[{"t":"Rewrite as \\(\\dfrac{\\ln x}{1/x}\\), an \\(\\tfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\\) form","correct":true},{"t":"Apply L'Hôpital directly to the product","correct":false},{"t":"Conclude the limit is \\(-\\infty\\) since \\(\\ln x\\to-\\infty\\)"},{"t":"Conclude the limit is \\(0\\) since \\(x\\to0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The form is \\(0\\cdot(-\\infty)\\), which L'Hôpital can't touch directly — convert it to a quotient first.</b> Writing \\(x\\ln x=\\dfrac{\\ln x}{1/x}\\) gives \\(\\tfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\\), now L'Hôpital-ready: \\(\\dfrac{1/x}{-1/x^2}=-x\\to0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"apply directly to the product\" — L'Hôpital requires a <i>quotient</i> of the form \\(\\tfrac00\\) or \\(\\tfrac\\infty\\infty\\).<br>• concluding \\(-\\infty\\) or \\(0\\) just picks one factor and ignores the tug-of-war.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(0\\cdot\\infty\\) must be rewritten as a fraction before L'Hôpital; the true limit here is \\(0\\).","unit":4,"id":"u4m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{x}-1}{x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"e"},{"t":"\\infty"}],"explain":"<b>Form \\(\\tfrac00\\).</b> By L'Hôpital: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{x}}{1}=e^{0}=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> uses \\(e^0-1=0\\) in the numerator only.<br>• <b>\\(e\\)</b> evaluates \\(e^x\\) at \\(x=1\\) instead of \\(x=0\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> assumes the denominator dominates.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tfrac{e^x-1}{x}\\to1\\) is the statement that \\(e^x\\) has slope \\(1\\) at \\(0\\) — the derivative of \\(e^x\\) at \\(0\\) by definition.","unit":4,"id":"u4m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"A \\(13\\)-foot ladder leans against a wall. The bottom slides away from the wall at \\(2\\) ft/sec. How fast is the top sliding down when the bottom is \\(5\\) feet from the wall?","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\) ft/sec","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\) ft/sec"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{6}{5}\\) ft/sec"},{"t":"\\(-2\\) ft/sec"}],"explain":"<b>Relate first, differentiate second, substitute last</b> — substituting numbers before differentiating is what kills these problems.<br><br>The relation is \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=169\\). Differentiating with respect to \\(t\\): \\(2x\\dfrac{dx}{dt}+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=0\\), so \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).<br><br>When \\(x=5\\), \\(y=\\sqrt{169-25}=12\\), giving \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-\\dfrac{5}{12}(2)=-\\dfrac56\\) ft/sec.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(-\\tfrac{24}{5}\\)</b> flips the ratio to \\(-\\frac{y}{x}\\frac{dx}{dt}\\). <b>\\(-\\tfrac65\\)</b> uses \\(y=13\\), confusing the ladder's length with the height on the wall — the length is the hypotenuse and is <i>constant</i>, which is exactly why it disappears when you differentiate. <b>\\(-2\\)</b> assumes the top falls as fast as the bottom slides.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the \\(13\\) is a constant and belongs in the equation, never as a value of \\(x\\) or \\(y\\). Find the missing side with Pythagoras at the given instant.","unit":4,"id":"u4m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"related","calc":false,"prompt":"A spherical balloon is inflated so that its volume increases at a constant rate. As the balloon grows, the rate at which the radius increases","opts":[{"t":"decreases, because the same volume is spread over a larger surface","correct":true},{"t":"stays constant, because \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}\\) is constant"},{"t":"increases, because the balloon is getting bigger"},{"t":"cannot be determined without the value of \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the relationship and look at what multiplies \\(\\frac{dr}{dt}\\).</b> From \\(V=\\tfrac43\\pi r^{3}\\), \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=4\\pi r^{2}\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\), so \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=\\dfrac{1}{4\\pi r^{2}}\\dfrac{dV}{dt}\\).<br><br>With \\(\\frac{dV}{dt}\\) fixed, \\(\\frac{dr}{dt}\\) is proportional to \\(\\frac{1}{r^{2}}\\) — so as \\(r\\) grows the radius creeps more and more slowly. Note \\(4\\pi r^{2}\\) is the surface area: the same volume per second has to spread over more skin.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"stays constant\"</b> assumes a constant \\(\\frac{dV}{dt}\\) forces a constant \\(\\frac{dr}{dt}\\), but they are linked by a factor that itself depends on \\(r\\). <b>\"increases\"</b> has the relationship backwards. <b>\"cannot be determined\"</b> misses that the <i>trend</i> follows from the algebra whatever the constant is.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> in related rates the two rates are joined by a factor that usually varies. Solve for the rate you want and read off what it depends on.","unit":4,"id":"u4m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"linear","calc":false,"prompt":"Use the tangent line to \\(f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\) at \\(x=8\\) to estimate \\(\\sqrt[3]{8.06}\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\(2.005\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2.06\\)"},{"t":"\\(2.02\\)"},{"t":"\\(2.0005\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Linearisation is \\(L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)\\)</b>, chosen at a nearby point where everything is exact.<br><br>\\(f(8)=2\\) and \\(f'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{3x^{2/3}}\\), so \\(f'(8)=\\dfrac{1}{3\\cdot4}=\\dfrac1{12}\\). Then \\(L(8.06)=2+\\dfrac{1}{12}(0.06)=2.005\\). (The true value is \\(2.004988\\ldots\\), so the estimate is excellent.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>2.06</b> adds the whole \\(\\Delta x\\) without multiplying by the derivative — treating the slope as \\(1\\). <b>2.02</b> uses \\(f'(8)=\\tfrac13\\), forgetting the \\(x^{2/3}=4\\) in the denominator. <b>2.0005</b> misplaces a decimal.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the change in \\(y\\) is the slope times the change in \\(x\\), never the change in \\(x\\) itself. Choosing \\(a\\) to be the nearby perfect cube is the whole art.","unit":4,"id":"u4m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"lhopital","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{x}-1-x}{x^{2}}=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(0\\)"},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"the limit does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>Check the form first:</b> substituting \\(0\\) gives \\(\\frac{1-1-0}{0}=\\frac00\\), so L'Hôpital's rule applies.<br><br>Differentiating top and bottom: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{x}-1}{2x}\\) — still \\(\\frac00\\), so apply it again: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{x}}{2}=\\frac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>0</b> stops after one application and evaluates \\(\\frac{e^{0}-1}{0}\\) as if the numerator alone decided it. <b>1</b> comes from applying the rule three times, past the point where the form stopped being indeterminate — once the expression is no longer \\(\\frac00\\) you must stop, or you will change the answer. <b>does not exist</b> gives up at the second indeterminate form.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> re-check the form after every application. Keep going while it stays \\(\\frac00\\) or \\(\\frac\\infty\\infty\\), and stop the moment it does not.","unit":4,"id":"u4m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moving along a line has velocity \\(v(t)<0\\) and acceleration \\(a(t)>0\\) at time \\(t\\). At that moment the particle is","opts":[{"t":"moving left and slowing down","correct":true},{"t":"moving left and speeding up"},{"t":"moving right and slowing down"},{"t":"momentarily at rest"}],"explain":"<b>Two separate readings.</b> The <i>sign</i> of \\(v\\) gives direction: \\(v<0\\) means moving in the negative direction, i.e. left.<br><br>Whether it is speeding up depends on whether \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) have the <b>same</b> sign. Here they are opposite, so the acceleration opposes the motion and the speed \\(|v|\\) is decreasing — the particle is slowing down.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"left and speeding up\"</b> reads \\(a>0\\) as \"speeding up\", but positive acceleration only means the velocity is <i>increasing</i> — and increasing a negative velocity moves it toward zero. <b>\"right\"</b> misreads the sign of \\(v\\). <b>\"at rest\"</b> would need \\(v=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> direction comes from the sign of \\(v\\); speeding up or slowing down comes from whether \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) agree in sign. Positive acceleration does not mean speeding up.","unit":4,"id":"u4m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"rates","calc":false,"prompt":"The temperature of an object is \\(T(t)\\) degrees at time \\(t\\) hours, and \\(T'(3)=-4\\). Which is the correct interpretation?","opts":[{"t":"at \\(t=3\\) hours the temperature is falling at about \\(4\\) degrees per hour","correct":true},{"t":"after 3 hours the object has cooled by \\(4\\) degrees"},{"t":"the temperature at \\(t=3\\) is \\(-4\\) degrees"},{"t":"the object cools by \\(4\\) degrees during every hour"}],"explain":"<b>A derivative is an instantaneous rate, and its units are the output's units per the input's units</b> — here degrees per hour. The negative sign means decreasing.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"has cooled by 4\"</b> reports a total change over three hours, which would be \\(T(3)-T(0)\\), not \\(T'(3)\\). <b>\"is \\(-4\\) degrees\"</b> confuses \\(T'(3)\\) with \\(T(3)\\) — the rate with the value. <b>\"every hour\"</b> extends an instantaneous rate to a whole interval, which is only true if \\(T\\) is linear; the rate at \\(t=3\\) says nothing about \\(t=7\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a good interpretation names the time, the direction, the size and the units, and says \"about\" — because an instantaneous rate only predicts the immediate future.","unit":4,"id":"u4m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(f(x)=x^2\\) on \\([1,5]\\). The Mean Value Theorem guarantees a number \\(c\\) in \\((1,5)\\) with \\(f'(c)\\) equal to the average rate of change. Find \\(c\\).","opts":[{"t":"c=3","correct":true},{"t":"c=6"},{"t":"c=2"},{"t":"c=\\sqrt{13}"}],"explain":"<b>MVT sets the instantaneous rate equal to the average rate.</b> Average rate \\(=\\dfrac{f(5)-f(1)}{5-1}=\\dfrac{25-1}{4}=6\\). Set \\(f'(c)=2c=6\\Rightarrow c=3\\), which lies in \\((1,5)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>6</b> is the average rate of change itself — the <i>value</i> of \\(f'(c)\\), not the location \\(c\\).<br>• <b>2</b> is \\(f'(1)\\), the slope at the left endpoint.<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{13}\\)</b> comes from setting \\(c^2=\\) (avg rate), confusing \\(f\\) with \\(f'\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> solve \\(f'(c)=\\dfrac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\\) for \\(c\\). For a parabola the \\(c\\) is always the midpoint of the interval.","unit":5,"id":"u5m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"Which hypotheses must hold for the Mean Value Theorem to apply to \\(f\\) on \\([a,b]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"Continuous on \\([a,b]\\) and differentiable on \\((a,b)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Continuous on \\((a,b)\\) only"},{"t":"Differentiable on \\([a,b]\\), endpoints included"},{"t":"\\(f(a)=f(b)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>MVT requires continuity on the closed interval and differentiability on the open one.</b> That exact split — closed \\([a,b]\\) for continuity, open \\((a,b)\\) for differentiability — is what lets the proof work.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"Continuous on \\((a,b)\\) only\" drops the endpoints and drops differentiability entirely.<br>• \"Differentiable on \\([a,b]\\)\" is too strong — the theorem never needs a derivative <i>at</i> the endpoints.<br>• \\(f(a)=f(b)\\) is the extra condition for <b>Rolle's</b> theorem, a special case of MVT.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> continuous on the closed interval, differentiable on the open interval — no more, no less.","unit":5,"id":"u5m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"Does the Mean Value Theorem apply to \\(f(x)=|x-2|\\) on the interval \\([0,5]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"No — \\(f\\) is not differentiable at \\(x=2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Yes — \\(f\\) is continuous, so MVT applies"},{"t":"No — \\(f\\) is not continuous on \\([0,5]\\)"},{"t":"Yes — and the guaranteed \\(c\\) is \\(x=2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>MVT needs differentiability on the whole open interval.</b> \\(f(x)=|x-2|\\) has a corner at \\(x=2\\), where the derivative does not exist — so a hypothesis fails and MVT is not guaranteed.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"continuous, so MVT applies\" forgets that continuity alone is not enough — differentiability is also required.<br>• \"not continuous\" is false: absolute value <i>is</i> continuous everywhere; it's the derivative that breaks.<br>• \"\\(c=2\\)\" is exactly where \\(f'\\) fails to exist, so it can't be the guaranteed point.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a single corner or cusp in \\((a,b)\\) voids MVT — check differentiability, not just continuity.","unit":5,"id":"u5m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f(x)=x^2-4x+3\\) on \\([1,3]\\), note \\(f(1)=f(3)=0\\). Rolle's Theorem guarantees a \\(c\\) in \\((1,3)\\) with \\(f'(c)=0\\). Find \\(c\\).","opts":[{"t":"c=2","correct":true},{"t":"c=0"},{"t":"c=1"},{"t":"c=4"}],"explain":"<b>Rolle's Theorem is MVT when the endpoints are level:</b> equal endpoint values force a horizontal tangent somewhere inside. \\(f'(x)=2x-4=0\\Rightarrow x=2\\), and \\(2\\in(1,3)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> and <b>4</b> are the <i>roots</i> of \\(f\\) shifted — \\(f(x)=0\\) at \\(x=1,3\\), not where \\(f'=0\\).<br>• <b>1</b> is an endpoint; Rolle's \\(c\\) must be strictly interior.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> Rolle gives \\(f'(c)=0\\) — solve the <i>derivative</i>, and the answer must lie strictly between the endpoints.","unit":5,"id":"u5m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"A car travels 150 miles in 3 hours along a straight highway. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","opts":[{"t":"At some instant the speedometer read exactly 50 mph","correct":true},{"t":"The car's speed was always 50 mph"},{"t":"The car's maximum speed was 50 mph"},{"t":"The car never exceeded 50 mph"}],"explain":"<b>Average speed \\(=\\dfrac{150}{3}=50\\) mph; MVT guarantees the instantaneous speed equalled the average at least once.</b> Position is continuous and differentiable in time, so some instant \\(c\\) has \\(x'(c)=50\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"always 50\" would require constant speed — MVT guarantees one instant, not all of them.<br>• \"maximum was 50\" / \"never exceeded 50\" are false — to average 50 with any variation, the car must go both above and below 50.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> MVT promises <i>at least one</i> instant matching the average — nothing about the max, min, or the rest of the trip.","unit":5,"id":"u5m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f\\) is continuous on the closed interval \\([a,b]\\), the Extreme Value Theorem guarantees that \\(f\\):","opts":[{"t":"attains an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum on \\([a,b]\\)","correct":true},{"t":"is differentiable on \\((a,b)\\)"},{"t":"has a critical point in \\((a,b)\\)"},{"t":"is increasing somewhere on \\([a,b]\\)"}],"explain":"<b>EVT: a continuous function on a <i>closed</i> interval attains both an absolute max and an absolute min.</b> That existence is what makes \"find the absolute maximum\" a fair question.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"differentiable\" — EVT says nothing about derivatives; continuity is all it needs (and a corner can still have extrema).<br>• \"has a critical point\" is false — the extrema might occur at the <b>endpoints</b>, which aren't critical points.<br>• \"increasing somewhere\" — a constant function is continuous with no increase.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> closed interval + continuous ⇒ absolute max and min <i>exist</i> (possibly at endpoints).","unit":5,"id":"u5m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"How many critical points does \\(f(x)=x^{2/3}\\) have, and where?","opts":[{"t":"One, at \\(x=0\\) (where \\(f'\\) does not exist)","correct":true},{"t":"None — \\(f'\\) is never zero"},{"t":"One, at \\(x=0\\) (where \\(f'=0\\))"},{"t":"Infinitely many"}],"explain":"<b>A critical point is where \\(f'=0\\) <i>or</i> \\(f'\\) does not exist</b> (and \\(x\\) is in the domain). Here \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac23 x^{-1/3}=\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\) is never zero, but is <b>undefined at \\(x=0\\)</b> — a cusp — which <i>is</i> in the domain. So there is one critical point, \\(x=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"None\" ignores the second half of the definition — \\(f'\\) not existing also counts.<br>• \"\\(f'=0\\) at \\(x=0\\)\" is wrong: \\(f'\\) is undefined there, not zero.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> hunt for <i>both</i> \\(f'=0\\) and \\(f'\\) DNE when listing critical points — cusps and corners are easy to miss.","unit":5,"id":"u5m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"Find the absolute maximum value of \\(f(x)=x^3-3x\\) on the closed interval \\([0,2]\\).","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"-2"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>Candidates test: check critical points and endpoints.</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-3=0\\Rightarrow x=\\pm1\\); only \\(x=1\\) is in \\([0,2]\\). Evaluate: \\(f(0)=0,\\ f(1)=-2,\\ f(2)=8-6=2\\). The largest is \\(f(2)=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>-2</b> is \\(f(1)\\), the absolute <i>minimum</i>, not the maximum.<br>• <b>0</b> is \\(f(0)\\), an endpoint value that isn't the largest.<br>• <b>1</b> is the location \\(x=1\\), not a value of \\(f\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the absolute max often sits at an <b>endpoint</b> — always evaluate \\(f\\) at every critical point <i>and</i> both endpoints, then pick the biggest output.","unit":5,"id":"u5m7","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"The differentiable function \\(f\\) has the values shown. Which is guaranteed on \\((0,8)\\)?","math":"\\begin{array}{c|cccc} x & 0 & 2 & 5 & 8 \\\\ \\hline f(x) & 4 & 4 & 10 & 4 \\end{array}","opts":[{"t":"There is a \\(c\\) with \\(f'(c)=0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'(x)>0\\) on all of \\((0,8)\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has an absolute max of \\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'(c)=\\tfrac34\\) for some \\(c\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Equal values force a horizontal tangent (Rolle).</b> Since \\(f(0)=f(2)=4\\), Rolle's Theorem on \\([0,2]\\) guarantees some \\(c\\) with \\(f'(c)=0\\) (and \\(f(2)=f(8)\\) gives another).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"\\(f'>0\\) throughout\" is impossible: \\(f\\) returns to \\(4\\), so it must decrease somewhere.<br>• \"absolute max of 10\" — the table only shows selected values; between them \\(f\\) could go higher, so \\(10\\) isn't guaranteed to be the max.<br>• \"\\(f'(c)=\\tfrac34\\)\" is the average rate on \\([0,8]\\): \\(\\frac{4-4}{8}=0\\), not \\(\\tfrac34\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> repeated \\(y\\)-values ⇒ a guaranteed \\(f'(c)=0\\) by Rolle; a table never guarantees a global extreme <i>between</i> its points.","unit":5,"id":"u5m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous on \\([2,6]\\) and differentiable on \\((2,6)\\), with \\(f(2)=3\\) and \\(f(6)=11\\). What value of \\(f'(c)\\) does MVT guarantee for some \\(c\\) in \\((2,6)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"8"},{"t":"14"},{"t":"4"}],"explain":"<b>MVT guarantees \\(f'(c)=\\dfrac{f(6)-f(2)}{6-2}=\\dfrac{11-3}{4}=\\dfrac{8}{4}=2\\).</b><br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8</b> is \\(f(6)-f(2)\\), the change in output without dividing by the change in input.<br>• <b>14</b> adds the outputs \\(3+11\\).<br>• <b>4</b> is the interval width \\(6-2\\), the denominator alone.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the guaranteed slope is the secant slope — rise over run — \\(\\frac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x}\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"On which interval(s) is \\(f(x)=x^3-3x^2+2\\) increasing?","opts":[{"t":"\\((-\\infty,0)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((0,2)\\)","correct":false},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,2)\\)"},{"t":"\\((0,\\infty)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f\\) increases where \\(f'>0\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-6x=3x(x-2)\\). This is positive when \\(x<0\\) or \\(x>2\\) (both factors same sign), so \\(f\\) increases on \\((-\\infty,0)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\((0,2)\\) is where \\(f'<0\\) — that's the <b>decreasing</b> interval.<br>• \\((-\\infty,2)\\) and \\((0,\\infty)\\) each keep half of a sign change, forgetting that \\(f'\\) flips sign at <i>both</i> \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> factor \\(f'\\), find its zeros, and test the sign on each interval — increasing exactly where \\(f'>0\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m10","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"At what \\(x\\) does \\(f(x)=x^3-3x^2+2\\) have a local minimum?","opts":[{"t":"x=2","correct":true},{"t":"x=0"},{"t":"x=1"},{"t":"x=-2"}],"explain":"<b>First-derivative test:</b> \\(f'(x)=3x(x-2)\\) changes from negative to positive at \\(x=2\\) (\\(f'<0\\) on \\((0,2)\\), \\(f'>0\\) after) — a local <b>minimum</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> is where \\(f'\\) goes \\(+\\to-\\) — that's the local <i>maximum</i>.<br>• <b>1</b> is where \\(f''=0\\) (the inflection point), not an extremum.<br>• <b>-2</b> isn't a critical point at all.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(-\\to+\\) is a min, \\(+\\to-\\) is a max — read the direction of the sign change, don't just find the zeros.","unit":5,"id":"u5m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"Given \\(f'(x)=x^2(x-4)\\), classify the critical point at \\(x=0\\).","opts":[{"t":"Neither — \\(f'\\) does not change sign at \\(x=0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Local maximum"},{"t":"Local minimum"},{"t":"Inflection point of \\(f'\\)"}],"explain":"<b>An extremum needs a sign change in \\(f'\\).</b> Near \\(x=0\\), the factor \\(x^2\\ge0\\) and \\((x-4)<0\\), so \\(f'\\le0\\) on <i>both</i> sides — no sign change. So \\(x=0\\) is a critical point but <b>not</b> an extremum (it's a shelf).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"max\"/\"min\" assume every \\(f'=0\\) is an extremum — the \\(x^2\\) factor (a double root) keeps the sign the same.<br>• \"inflection point of \\(f'\\)\" confuses the question — we're classifying \\(f\\), and inflections come from \\(f''\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a squared (even-power) factor in \\(f'\\) gives a critical point with <i>no</i> sign change — critical, but not an extremum.","unit":5,"id":"u5m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"The derivative of \\(f\\) is \\(f'(x)=e^{x}(x-2)\\). Where does \\(f\\) have a local minimum?","opts":[{"t":"x=2","correct":true},{"t":"x=0"},{"t":"x=-2"},{"t":"Nowhere — \\(f'\\) is never zero"}],"explain":"<b>\\(e^x>0\\) always, so the sign of \\(f'\\) is the sign of \\((x-2)\\).</b> That is negative for \\(x<2\\), positive for \\(x>2\\) — a \\(-\\to+\\) change at \\(x=2\\): a local minimum.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> is where \\(e^x=1\\), which has nothing to do with where \\(f'=0\\).<br>• <b>-2</b> misreads the factor \\((x-2)\\).<br>• \"never zero\" forgets that \\(f'=0\\) when the \\((x-2)\\) factor is zero.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> an always-positive factor (like \\(e^{x}\\)) never changes the sign — the critical points come entirely from the other factor.","unit":5,"id":"u5m13","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"What kind of extremum, if any, does \\(f(x)=x^{2/3}\\) have at \\(x=0\\)?","opts":[{"t":"A local (and absolute) minimum","correct":true},{"t":"A local maximum"},{"t":"No extremum — \\(f'\\) is undefined there"},{"t":"An inflection point"}],"explain":"<b>An extremum can occur where \\(f'\\) is undefined, as long as \\(f'\\) changes sign.</b> \\(f'(x)=\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\) is negative for \\(x<0\\) and positive for \\(x>0\\) — a \\(-\\to+\\) change — so the cusp at \\(x=0\\) is a local <b>minimum</b> (and \\(f\\ge0\\) makes it absolute).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"No extremum because \\(f'\\) is undefined\" — undefined derivatives are exactly the cusp/corner extrema you must still check.<br>• \"maximum\" reverses the sign change.<br>• \"inflection\" is a concavity idea; \\(x^{2/3}\\) is concave down on both sides, not an inflection.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the first-derivative test works even where \\(f'\\) doesn't exist — a sign change of \\(f'\\) still means an extremum.","unit":5,"id":"u5m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"On which interval is \\(f\\) decreasing if \\(f'(x)=(x+2)(x-1)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\((-2,1)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,-2)\\)"},{"t":"\\((1,\\infty)\\)"},{"t":"\\((-1,2)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f\\) decreases where \\(f'<0\\).</b> \\((x+2)(x-1)\\) is negative between its roots, i.e. on \\((-2,1)\\) (one factor positive, one negative).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\((-\\infty,-2)\\) and \\((1,\\infty)\\) are where <i>both</i> factors share a sign, so \\(f'>0\\): increasing.<br>• \\((-1,2)\\) uses the wrong roots — the zeros are \\(-2\\) and \\(1\\), not \\(-1\\) and \\(2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a product of two linear factors is negative <i>between</i> its roots — that's the decreasing interval.","unit":5,"id":"u5m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\) is negative on \\((0,3)\\) and positive on \\((3,5)\\), with \\(f'(3)=0\\). At \\(x=3\\), the function \\(f\\) has a:","opts":[{"t":"local minimum","correct":true},{"t":"local maximum"},{"t":"inflection point"},{"t":"vertical tangent"}],"explain":"<b>Read the sign change of \\(f'\\):</b> \\(f'\\) goes from negative to positive at \\(x=3\\), so \\(f\\) stops falling and starts rising — a local <b>minimum</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"maximum\" would need \\(f'\\) to change \\(+\\to-\\).<br>• \"inflection\" is about \\(f''\\) changing sign, not \\(f'\\).<br>• \"vertical tangent\" would need \\(f'\\) undefined (unbounded), but here \\(f'(3)=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> to classify \\(f\\), read the <i>sign change</i> of \\(f'\\) across the point — \\(-\\to+\\) is a valley.","unit":5,"id":"u5m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"How many relative extrema does \\(f(x)=x^4-2x^2\\) have?","opts":[{"t":"3","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"4"}],"explain":"<b>Count the sign changes of \\(f'\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=4x^3-4x=4x(x-1)(x+1)\\) has three simple roots \\(x=-1,0,1\\), and \\(f'\\) changes sign at each — so <b>3</b> relative extrema (min, max, min).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> counts only the two minima and forgets the local max at \\(x=0\\).<br>• <b>1</b> sees just the \"middle\" feature.<br>• <b>4</b> may count the degree, or an extra root that isn't there.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> each <i>simple</i> (odd-multiplicity) root of \\(f'\\) is a sign change and hence an extremum; count them.","unit":5,"id":"u5m17","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"fdt","calc":true,"prompt":"For \\(x>0\\), find the absolute minimum value of \\(f(x)=x+\\dfrac{4}{x}\\).","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"8"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Minimize with the first-derivative test.</b> \\(f'(x)=1-\\dfrac{4}{x^2}=0\\Rightarrow x^2=4\\Rightarrow x=2\\) (take \\(x>0\\)). Since \\(f'\\) goes \\(-\\to+\\) there, it's a minimum: \\(f(2)=2+\\dfrac42=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> is the location \\(x=2\\), not the value \\(f(2)\\).<br>• <b>8</b> may come from evaluating at a wrong \\(x\\) or adding incorrectly.<br>• <b>0</b> ignores that \\(f\\to\\infty\\) at both ends — the minimum is strictly positive.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> after finding the critical \\(x\\), plug it back into \\(f\\) — the question asks for the <i>value</i>, not the location.","unit":5,"id":"u5m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([a,b]\\) and \\(f'(x)>0\\) for all \\(x\\) in \\((a,b)\\), which is the absolute maximum of \\(f\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(b)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(a)\\)"},{"t":"a critical point inside \\((a,b)\\)"},{"t":"cannot be determined"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f'>0\\) throughout means \\(f\\) is strictly increasing on \\([a,b]\\)</b>, so the largest value is at the right endpoint: \\(f(b)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(f(a)\\) is the <i>minimum</i> of an increasing function.<br>• \"a critical point inside\" — there are no critical points, since \\(f'\\) is never zero.<br>• \"cannot be determined\" ignores that monotonicity pins the max at the endpoint.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a strictly increasing function on a closed interval takes its max at the right end, its min at the left end.","unit":5,"id":"u5m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"On which interval is \\(f(x)=x^3-3x^2\\) concave up?","opts":[{"t":"\\((1,\\infty)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,1)\\)"},{"t":"\\((0,2)\\)"},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,0)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Concave up where \\(f''>0\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-6x\\Rightarrow f''(x)=6x-6=6(x-1)>0\\) exactly when \\(x>1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\((-\\infty,1)\\) is where \\(f''<0\\) — concave <b>down</b>.<br>• \\((0,2)\\) is the <i>decreasing</i> interval (that's \\(f'\\), not \\(f''\\)).<br>• \\((-\\infty,0)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\) is where \\(f\\) is <i>increasing</i> — again \\(f'\\), not \\(f''\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> concavity is decided by \\(f''\\); don't confuse it with the increasing/decreasing behavior read off \\(f'\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m20","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Find the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the inflection point of \\(f(x)=x^3-6x^2+5\\).","opts":[{"t":"x=2","correct":true},{"t":"x=4"},{"t":"x=0"},{"t":"x=-2"}],"explain":"<b>Inflection: \\(f''\\) changes sign.</b> \\(f''(x)=6x-12=0\\Rightarrow x=2\\), and \\(f''\\) goes \\(-\\to+\\) there, so \\(x=2\\) is a genuine inflection point.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>4</b> is a critical point of \\(f\\): \\(f'(x)=3x^2-12x=3x(x-4)=0\\Rightarrow x=0,4\\) — those are extrema locations, not the inflection.<br>• <b>0</b> is the other critical point of \\(f\\).<br>• <b>-2</b> isn't a zero of \\(f''\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> inflection points come from \\(f''=0\\) <i>with a sign change</i> — a different computation from the \\(f'=0\\) that locates extrema.","unit":5,"id":"u5m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"A function has \\(f'(3)=0\\) and \\(f''(3)=-5\\). By the second-derivative test, \\(x=3\\) is a:","opts":[{"t":"local maximum","correct":true},{"t":"local minimum"},{"t":"inflection point"},{"t":"point where the test fails"}],"explain":"<b>Second-derivative test: at a critical point, the sign of \\(f''\\) classifies it.</b> \\(f'(3)=0\\) (critical) and \\(f''(3)<0\\) (concave down) ⇒ local <b>maximum</b> — the top of a dome.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"minimum\" needs \\(f''(3)>0\\).<br>• \"inflection\" needs \\(f''\\) to <i>change sign</i>, not just be negative.<br>• \"test fails\" only happens when \\(f''(c)=0\\); here it's a clear \\(-5\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(f'(c)=0\\) with \\(f''(c)<0\\) ⇒ max; with \\(f''(c)>0\\) ⇒ min; with \\(f''(c)=0\\) ⇒ inconclusive.","unit":5,"id":"u5m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Is \\(x=0\\) an inflection point of \\(f(x)=x^4\\)?","opts":[{"t":"No — \\(f''\\) does not change sign there","correct":true},{"t":"Yes, because \\(f''(0)=0\\)"},{"t":"Yes, because \\(f'(0)=0\\)"},{"t":"No, because \\(f'(0)\\ne0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f''=0\\) is only a <i>candidate</i> for an inflection — it must change sign.</b> \\(f''(x)=12x^2\\ge0\\) for all \\(x\\), so the curve is concave up on both sides of \\(0\\). No sign change ⇒ <b>not</b> an inflection point (in fact \\(x=0\\) is a minimum).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"Yes, because \\(f''(0)=0\\)\" is the classic trap — a zero of \\(f''\\) is necessary but not sufficient.<br>• \"\\(f'(0)=0\\)\" is irrelevant to concavity.<br>• \"\\(f'(0)\\ne0\\)\" is false: \\(f'(0)=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> an inflection needs \\(f''\\) to <i>switch signs</i>; \\(x^4\\) at \\(0\\) is the counterexample to memorize.","unit":5,"id":"u5m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"At a critical point \\(c\\), a student finds \\(f'(c)=0\\) and \\(f''(c)=0\\). What is the correct conclusion?","opts":[{"t":"The second-derivative test is inconclusive","correct":true},{"t":"\\(c\\) is a local maximum"},{"t":"\\(c\\) is a local minimum"},{"t":"\\(c\\) is an inflection point"}],"explain":"<b>When \\(f''(c)=0\\), the second-derivative test gives no information.</b> \\(c\\) could be a max, a min, or neither — you must fall back to the <b>first-derivative test</b> (sign of \\(f'\\) around \\(c\\)).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"max\" and \"min\" overstate — e.g. \\(x^4\\) (min) and \\(x^3\\) (neither) both have \\(f'=f''=0\\) at \\(0\\).<br>• \"inflection\" isn't guaranteed either; \\(x^4\\) has \\(f''(0)=0\\) but no inflection.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(f''(c)=0\\) ⇒ inconclusive; check the sign change of \\(f'\\) instead.","unit":5,"id":"u5m24","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"On which interval is \\(f(x)=x^3\\) concave down?","opts":[{"t":"\\((-\\infty,0)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((0,\\infty)\\)"},{"t":"nowhere"},{"t":"everywhere"}],"explain":"<b>Concave down where \\(f''<0\\).</b> \\(f''(x)=6x<0\\) exactly when \\(x<0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\((0,\\infty)\\) is where \\(f''>0\\) — concave <b>up</b>.<br>• \"nowhere\"/\"everywhere\" ignore that \\(f''=6x\\) changes sign at \\(0\\) (with an inflection there).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(f(x)=x^3\\) is the model inflection — concave down left of \\(0\\), concave up right of \\(0\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m25","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"How many inflection points does \\(f(x)=x^4-6x^2\\) have?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Find sign changes of \\(f''\\).</b> \\(f''(x)=12x^2-12=12(x^2-1)=12(x-1)(x+1)\\). It changes sign at both \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=1\\) — so <b>2</b> inflection points.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> counts only one of the two zeros.<br>• <b>3</b> may miscount by including \\(x=0\\) (where \\(f'=0\\), an extremum — not an inflection).<br>• <b>0</b> forgets \\(f''\\) has real roots.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> each simple root of \\(f''\\) is an inflection point; the extrema of \\(f\\) (from \\(f'\\)) are a separate set of points.","unit":5,"id":"u5m26","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Given \\(f''(x)=x(x-2)^2\\), how many inflection points does \\(f\\) have?","opts":[{"t":"1 (at \\(x=0\\) only)","correct":true},{"t":"2 (at \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=2\\))"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>An inflection needs \\(f''\\) to change sign.</b> \\(f''=x(x-2)^2\\): the factor \\((x-2)^2\\ge0\\) never changes sign, so at \\(x=2\\) there's <i>no</i> concavity change. Only \\(x=0\\) (a simple root) gives a sign change: <b>1</b> inflection point.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"2, at \\(0\\) and \\(2\\)\" falls for the trap — \\(x=2\\) is a <i>double</i> root, so \\(f''\\) touches zero without crossing.<br>• \"3\"/\"0\" miscount the roots or ignore \\(x=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> just like extrema from \\(f'\\), only <i>odd-multiplicity</i> roots of \\(f''\\) produce inflection points.","unit":5,"id":"u5m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f'\\) is increasing on an interval, then on that interval \\(f\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"concave up","correct":true},{"t":"concave down"},{"t":"increasing"},{"t":"decreasing"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f''=(f')'\\), so \\(f'\\) increasing means \\(f''>0\\) — concave up.</b><br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"concave down\" would need \\(f'\\) <i>decreasing</i>.<br>• \"increasing\"/\"decreasing\" describe \\(f\\)'s direction (the sign of \\(f'\\)), not whether \\(f'\\) itself is rising.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \"\\(f'\\) increasing\" and \"\\(f\\) concave up\" are the same statement — the slope of the slope is positive.","unit":5,"id":"u5m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"A cup of coffee is cooling: its temperature \\(T(t)\\) is decreasing, but the rate of cooling is slowing down. Which pair of signs is correct?","opts":[{"t":"\\(T'<0\\) and \\(T''>0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(T'<0\\) and \\(T''<0\\)"},{"t":"\\(T'>0\\) and \\(T''<0\\)"},{"t":"\\(T'>0\\) and \\(T''>0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Direction is \\(T'\\); \"slowing\" is about \\(T''\\).</b> Cooling ⇒ \\(T'<0\\). \"Slowing down\" means the (negative) rate is heading toward \\(0\\) — i.e. \\(T'\\) is <i>increasing</i> — so \\(T''>0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(T''<0\\) would mean the cooling is <i>speeding up</i>.<br>• \\(T'>0\\) contradicts \"cooling.\"<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a negative rate that's \"slowing\" is rising toward zero — decreasing quantity with \\(T''>0\\) (concave up).","unit":5,"id":"u5m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\) is positive on \\((-\\infty,2)\\), zero at \\(x=2\\), and negative on \\((2,\\infty)\\). At \\(x=2\\), the graph of \\(f\\) has a:","opts":[{"t":"local maximum","correct":true},{"t":"local minimum"},{"t":"inflection point"},{"t":"zero"}],"explain":"<b>Where \\(f'\\) changes \\(+\\to-\\), \\(f\\) tops out.</b> Positive slope then negative slope means \\(f\\) rises then falls — a local <b>maximum</b> at \\(x=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"minimum\" needs \\(f'\\) to change \\(-\\to+\\).<br>• \"inflection\" needs \\(f''\\) (the slope of \\(f'\\)) to change sign, not \\(f'\\) itself.<br>• \"zero\" confuses \\(f'=0\\) (flat tangent) with \\(f=0\\) (a root of \\(f\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> read \\(f\\)'s extrema from where the graph of \\(f'\\) <i>crosses</i> the axis, and the direction of the crossing.","unit":5,"id":"u5m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\) has a local maximum at \\(x=5\\). What does the graph of \\(f\\) have at \\(x=5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"an inflection point","correct":true},{"t":"a local maximum"},{"t":"a local minimum"},{"t":"a corner"}],"explain":"<b>A local max of \\(f'\\) is where \\(f''=0\\) (with a sign change) — an inflection of \\(f\\).</b> At a peak of \\(f'\\), the slope \\(f''\\) switches from positive to negative, so \\(f\\) switches concavity.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"local max of \\(f\\)\" happens where \\(f'=0\\) (crosses the axis), not where \\(f'\\) peaks.<br>• \"local min\" — same confusion, wrong feature of \\(f'\\).<br>• \"corner\" would need \\(f'\\) undefined, but a peak is a smooth point.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> extrema of \\(f'\\) ⇔ inflection points of \\(f\\); zeros of \\(f'\\) ⇔ extrema of \\(f\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"On an interval, \\(f'(x)>0\\) and \\(f''(x)<0\\). Which best describes the graph of \\(f\\) there?","opts":[{"t":"increasing and concave down","correct":true},{"t":"increasing and concave up"},{"t":"decreasing and concave down"},{"t":"decreasing and concave up"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f'>0\\) ⇒ increasing; \\(f''<0\\) ⇒ concave down.</b> The graph rises but bends downward — climbing at a decreasing rate.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"concave up\" contradicts \\(f''<0\\).<br>• the two \"decreasing\" options contradict \\(f'>0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(f'\\) gives direction, \\(f''\\) gives bend — read them independently and combine.","unit":5,"id":"u5m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\) lies <i>below</i> the \\(x\\)-axis but is <i>increasing</i> on \\((0,2)\\). On \\((0,2)\\), the graph of \\(f\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"decreasing and concave up","correct":true},{"t":"increasing and concave up"},{"t":"decreasing and concave down"},{"t":"increasing and concave down"}],"explain":"<b>Two facts, read separately.</b> \\(f'<0\\) (below the axis) ⇒ \\(f\\) is <b>decreasing</b>. \\(f'\\) increasing ⇒ \\(f''>0\\) ⇒ \\(f\\) is <b>concave up</b>. So \\(f\\) falls, but the fall is slowing.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• the \"increasing\" options misread \"\\(f'\\) below the axis\" (that's the value of \\(f'\\), which is negative).<br>• \"concave down\" misreads \"\\(f'\\) increasing\" (rising \\(f'\\) is \\(f''>0\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the <i>position</i> of the \\(f'\\) graph (above/below axis) gives direction; its <i>slope</i> (rising/falling) gives concavity.","unit":5,"id":"u5m33","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The signs of \\(f'\\) and \\(f''\\) on four intervals are given. On which interval is the graph of \\(f\\) falling and bending upward?","math":"\\begin{array}{c|cccc} \\text{interval} & \\text{I} & \\text{II} & \\text{III} & \\text{IV}\\\\ \\hline f' & + & + & - & - \\\\ f'' & + & - & + & - \\end{array}","opts":[{"t":"III","correct":true},{"t":"I"},{"t":"II"},{"t":"IV"}],"explain":"<b>Falling ⇒ \\(f'<0\\); bending upward ⇒ \\(f''>0\\).</b> That's the column with \\(f'=-\\) and \\(f''=+\\): interval <b>III</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• I has \\(f'+,f''+\\): rising and concave up.<br>• II has \\(f'+,f''-\\): rising and concave down.<br>• IV has \\(f'-,f''-\\): falling and concave down (bending the wrong way).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> match each requested behavior to a sign — direction to \\(f'\\), concavity to \\(f''\\) — then find the matching column.","unit":5,"id":"u5m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f''\\) changes sign at exactly two values \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=4\\), how many inflection points does \\(f\\) have?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"4"}],"explain":"<b>An inflection point is exactly a place where \\(f''\\) changes sign.</b> Two sign changes ⇒ <b>2</b> inflection points, at \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> would require \\(f''\\) to never switch sign.<br>• <b>1</b> undercounts.<br>• <b>4</b> perhaps counts zeros of both \\(f'\\) and \\(f''\\), but the question is only about concavity changes.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \"\\(f''\\) changes sign\" is the definition of an inflection point — count the changes directly.","unit":5,"id":"u5m35","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\) is a line with positive slope that crosses the \\(x\\)-axis at \\(x=1\\). Which statement about \\(f\\) is correct?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local minimum at \\(x=1\\) and is concave up everywhere","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local maximum at \\(x=1\\) and is concave up everywhere"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local minimum at \\(x=1\\) and is concave down everywhere"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has an inflection point at \\(x=1\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Read both features off the line.</b> \\(f'\\) crosses \\(-\\to+\\) at \\(x=1\\) ⇒ local <b>minimum</b> of \\(f\\). The line has positive slope everywhere ⇒ \\(f''>0\\) everywhere ⇒ \\(f\\) is <b>concave up</b> everywhere.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"local max\" reverses the crossing direction.<br>• \"concave down\" contradicts the positive slope of \\(f'\\).<br>• \"inflection at \\(x=1\\)\" — that's where \\(f'=0\\) (an extremum), not where \\(f''\\) changes sign; \\(f''\\) is a positive constant, never changing sign.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a straight-line \\(f'\\) means constant \\(f''\\) — one extremum where it crosses zero, and a single concavity throughout.","unit":5,"id":"u5m36","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"If the graph of \\(f\\) is increasing and concave down, which pair of signs must hold?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f'>0\\) and \\(f''<0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'>0\\) and \\(f''>0\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'<0\\) and \\(f''<0\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'<0\\) and \\(f''>0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Increasing ⇒ \\(f'>0\\); concave down ⇒ \\(f''<0\\).</b><br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(f''>0\\) is concave <i>up</i>.<br>• \\(f'<0\\) is <i>decreasing</i>.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> translate each English word to a derivative sign — \"increasing\" → \\(f'>0\\), \"concave down\" → \\(f''<0\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m37","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(f'(x)=(x-1)(x-3)\\), on what interval is the graph of \\(f\\) concave up?","opts":[{"t":"\\((2,\\infty)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,2)\\)"},{"t":"\\((1,3)\\)"},{"t":"\\((-\\infty,1)\\cup(3,\\infty)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Concavity of \\(f\\) is the sign of \\(f''=(f')'\\).</b> \\(f'(x)=x^2-4x+3\\Rightarrow f''(x)=2x-4>0\\) when \\(x>2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\((-\\infty,2)\\) is where \\(f''<0\\) — concave down.<br>• \\((1,3)\\) is where \\(f'<0\\) — that's \\(f\\) <i>decreasing</i>, not its concavity.<br>• \\((-\\infty,1)\\cup(3,\\infty)\\) is where \\(f'>0\\) — \\(f\\) increasing.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> to get concavity from \\(f'\\), differentiate once more — don't read the sign of \\(f'\\) itself.","unit":5,"id":"u5m38","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"The velocity of a particle is \\(v(t)\\) with \\(v(t)>0\\) and \\(v'(t)<0\\) on an interval. What is true of the position \\(s(t)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(s\\) is increasing and concave down","correct":true},{"t":"\\(s\\) is decreasing and concave down"},{"t":"\\(s\\) is increasing and concave up"},{"t":"\\(s\\) has a local maximum"}],"explain":"<b>Here \\(v=s'\\) and \\(v'=s''\\).</b> \\(v>0\\Rightarrow s'>0\\): position increasing. \\(v'<0\\Rightarrow s''<0\\): position concave down. (The particle moves forward but is slowing.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"decreasing\" contradicts \\(v>0\\).<br>• \"concave up\" contradicts \\(v'<0\\).<br>• \"local max\" of \\(s\\) needs \\(s'=v=0\\), but \\(v>0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> reading position from velocity is the same skill as reading \\(f\\) from \\(f'\\) — \\(v\\) is direction, \\(v'\\) is concavity.","unit":5,"id":"u5m39","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"An open box is formed from a \\(10\\times10\\) sheet by cutting squares of side \\(x\\) from the corners and folding. What value of \\(x\\) maximizes the volume?","opts":[{"t":"x=\\tfrac53","correct":true},{"t":"x=5"},{"t":"x=\\tfrac{10}{3}"},{"t":"x=2.5"}],"explain":"<b>\\(V(x)=x(10-2x)^2\\); differentiate and factor.</b> \\(V'(x)=(10-2x)^2+x\\cdot2(10-2x)(-2)=(10-2x)(10-6x)=0\\Rightarrow x=5\\text{ or }x=\\tfrac53\\). On \\(0<x<5\\), \\(x=5\\) gives a flat sheet (\\(V=0\\)), so the maximum is at \\(x=\\tfrac53\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>5</b> is the extraneous root — it's the <i>minimum</i> (zero volume), outside the useful range.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{10}{3}\\)</b> and <b>2.5</b> come from mis-factoring \\(V'\\) or halving the wrong quantity.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> factor \\(V'\\), find both roots, and reject the one that gives a degenerate box (check the domain \\(0<x<5\\)).","unit":5,"id":"u5m40","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"A rectangular field borders a straight river and needs fencing on only the three non-river sides. With \\(200\\) m of fence, what is the maximum area?","opts":[{"t":"5000\\text{ m}^2","correct":true},{"t":"2500\\text{ m}^2"},{"t":"10000\\text{ m}^2"},{"t":"50\\text{ m}"}],"explain":"<b>Let the two depths be \\(x\\) and the river-parallel side \\(L\\): \\(2x+L=200\\Rightarrow L=200-2x\\).</b> Area \\(A(x)=x(200-2x)\\), \\(A'(x)=200-4x=0\\Rightarrow x=50\\), \\(L=100\\), \\(A=50\\cdot100=5000\\) m².<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2500</b> forgets the river side is free and fences all four sides (a \\(50\\times50\\) square).<br>• <b>10000</b> ignores the constraint entirely.<br>• <b>50 m</b> is the value of \\(x\\), a length — not the area.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> build the constraint from which sides are actually fenced, then answer the quantity asked (area, with units).","unit":5,"id":"u5m41","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"Two positive numbers have a sum of \\(30\\). What is their maximum possible product?","opts":[{"t":"225","correct":true},{"t":"900"},{"t":"30"},{"t":"15"}],"explain":"<b>\\(P=x(30-x)=30x-x^2\\), \\(P'(x)=30-2x=0\\Rightarrow x=15\\), so both numbers are \\(15\\).</b> Product \\(=15\\cdot15=225\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>900</b> is \\(30^2\\) — squaring the sum, not the optimal product.<br>• <b>30</b> is the sum itself.<br>• <b>15</b> is one of the numbers, not the product.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the product of two numbers with a fixed sum is largest when they're <b>equal</b> — a pattern worth memorizing.","unit":5,"id":"u5m42","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":true,"prompt":"Two positive numbers have a product of \\(100\\). What is the minimum possible sum?","opts":[{"t":"20","correct":true},{"t":"10"},{"t":"100"},{"t":"200"}],"explain":"<b>\\(S=x+\\dfrac{100}{x}\\), \\(S'(x)=1-\\dfrac{100}{x^2}=0\\Rightarrow x^2=100\\Rightarrow x=10\\).</b> Then the other number is \\(\\tfrac{100}{10}=10\\), and \\(S=10+10=20\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>10</b> is one of the numbers (\\(x\\)), not the sum.<br>• <b>100</b> is the product.<br>• <b>200</b> uses a non-optimal split like \\(1\\times100\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the sum of two numbers with a fixed product is smallest when they're <b>equal</b> (\\(x=\\sqrt{100}\\)) — the mirror image of the fixed-sum problem.","unit":5,"id":"u5m43","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"An open box is cut from a \\(12\\times12\\) sheet by removing corner squares of side \\(x\\). What is the domain of \\(x\\) for a valid box?","opts":[{"t":"\\(0<x<6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(0<x<12\\)"},{"t":"\\(0<x<3\\)"},{"t":"\\(x>0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Each side of the base is \\(12-2x\\), which must stay positive:</b> \\(12-2x>0\\Rightarrow x<6\\), and \\(x>0\\) for a real cut. So \\(0<x<6\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(0<x<12\\) forgets that <i>two</i> cuts come off each side (\\(-2x\\), not \\(-x\\)).<br>• \\(0<x<3\\) over-restricts (perhaps using \\(12-4x>0\\)).<br>• \\(x>0\\) omits the upper bound entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> set every physical length \\(>0\\) to build the domain — here the base side \\(12-2x\\) caps \\(x\\) at \\(6\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m44","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"You have \\(60\\) m of fence to enclose a rectangular pen on all four sides. What is the maximum area?","opts":[{"t":"225\\text{ m}^2","correct":true},{"t":"900\\text{ m}^2"},{"t":"3600\\text{ m}^2"},{"t":"15\\text{ m}"}],"explain":"<b>Perimeter \\(2x+2y=60\\Rightarrow y=30-x\\).</b> Area \\(A=x(30-x)\\), maximized at \\(x=15\\) (so \\(y=15\\), a square). \\(A=15\\cdot15=225\\) m².<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>900</b> squares half the perimeter incorrectly or uses \\(30^2\\).<br>• <b>3600</b> is \\(60^2\\) — the whole perimeter squared.<br>• <b>15 m</b> is a side length, not the area.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for a fixed perimeter, the rectangle of maximum area is a <b>square</b> — and always report the area, with units.","unit":5,"id":"u5m45","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"To maximize \\(A=xy\\) subject to the constraint \\(x+2y=20\\), write \\(A\\) as a function of \\(y\\) alone.","opts":[{"t":"A(y)=20y-2y^2","correct":true},{"t":"A(y)=20y-y^2"},{"t":"A(y)=(20-y)y"},{"t":"A(y)=20-2y"}],"explain":"<b>Solve the constraint for \\(x\\) and substitute:</b> \\(x=20-2y\\), so \\(A=xy=(20-2y)y=20y-2y^2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(20y-y^2\\) uses \\(x=20-y\\), dropping the coefficient \\(2\\) on \\(y\\).<br>• \\((20-y)y\\) has the same error before expanding.<br>• \\(20-2y\\) is just \\(x\\), not the product \\(xy\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the constraint is substitution fuel — solve it exactly (keep the \\(2\\)) and plug into the objective before differentiating.","unit":5,"id":"u5m46","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"Solving the fence problem \\(A(x)=x(100-2x)\\) gives \\(x=25\\) for the maximum. The question asks for the <i>maximum area</i>. What is the answer?","opts":[{"t":"1250\\text{ m}^2","correct":true},{"t":"25\\text{ m}"},{"t":"50\\text{ m}"},{"t":"2500\\text{ m}^2"}],"explain":"<b>\\(x=25\\) is where the max occurs; plug it back in.</b> \\(A(25)=25(100-50)=25\\cdot50=1250\\) m².<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>25 m</b> is the location, not the area — the classic \"answered the wrong question\" error.<br>• <b>50 m</b> is the other side length \\(L=100-2x\\).<br>• <b>2500</b> squares \\(x\\) instead of computing \\(x\\cdot L\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> after finding the optimal \\(x\\), reread the prompt and report the <i>requested</i> quantity — with units.","unit":5,"id":"u5m47","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"A rectangle has its base on the \\(x\\)-axis and its top two corners on the parabola \\(y=12-x^2\\). Its area as a function of the half-width \\(x\\) is \\(A(x)=2x(12-x^2)\\). What \\(x\\) maximizes the area?","opts":[{"t":"x=2","correct":true},{"t":"x=\\sqrt{12}"},{"t":"x=\\sqrt{6}"},{"t":"x=\\tfrac{12}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the expanded area.</b> \\(A(x)=24x-2x^3\\), \\(A'(x)=24-6x^2=0\\Rightarrow x^2=4\\Rightarrow x=2\\) (take \\(x>0\\)). Then the height is \\(12-4=8\\) and \\(A=2\\cdot2\\cdot8=32\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(\\sqrt{12}\\) is where the parabola meets the axis (\\(y=0\\)) — a degenerate rectangle of zero area.<br>• \\(\\sqrt{6}\\) comes from \\(24-... \\) mis-solved (e.g. \\(24=... x^2\\) with a wrong coefficient).<br>• \\(\\tfrac{12}{3}=4\\) forgets to take the square root.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> expand before differentiating, solve \\(A'=0\\), and keep the root inside the domain \\(0<x<\\sqrt{12}\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"At a single critical point \\(c\\) in the domain, \\(f'(c)=0\\) and \\(f''(c)<0\\), and \\(c\\) is the only critical point. What can you conclude about \\(f(c)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"It is the absolute maximum on the domain","correct":true},{"t":"It is a local maximum but not necessarily absolute"},{"t":"It is the absolute minimum"},{"t":"Nothing without checking endpoints"}],"explain":"<b>Sole critical point + \\(f''(c)<0\\) ⇒ absolute maximum.</b> \\(f''(c)<0\\) makes \\(c\\) a local max; since it's the <i>only</i> critical point, \\(f\\) can't turn anywhere else, so no other point beats it — the local max is global.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \"local but not absolute\" is the correct default <i>only</i> when there are multiple critical points — the \"only critical point\" clause upgrades it to absolute.<br>• \"absolute minimum\" flips the concavity.<br>• \"nothing without endpoints\" — on an open/unbounded domain there are no endpoints to check, and the sole-critical-point argument settles it.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \"the only critical point, and it's a max\" is the cleanest absolute-extremum justification an AP reader can ask for.","unit":5,"id":"u5m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f'\\), the derivative of \\(f\\), is shown for \\(0\\le x\\le 5\\). At which \\(x\\) does \\(f\\) have a local <b>maximum</b>?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of f prime: it starts at negative 2, rises crossing zero at x=1, peaks at 3 when x=2.5, falls crossing zero at x=4, and ends at negative 2 when x=5\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 130 L88 100 L175 55 L262 100 L320 130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">1</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">2.5</text><text x=\"259\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">4</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">y = f '(x)</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(x=4\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x=1\\)"},{"t":"\\(x=2.5\\)"},{"t":"\\(x=5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>You are looking at \\(f'\\), so read SIGNS, not heights.</b> \\(f\\) has a local maximum where \\(f'\\) changes from positive to negative.<br><br>The graph crosses zero twice. At \\(x=1\\) it goes from negative up through zero — that is a local <i>minimum</i> of \\(f\\). At \\(x=4\\) it goes from positive down through zero — that is the local <b>maximum</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(x=1\\)</b> is the other crossing, right idea and wrong direction. <b>\\(x=2.5\\)</b> is where \\(f'\\) is <i>largest</i>, so \\(f\\) is climbing at its fastest there — the steepest point of \\(f\\), not a turning point. Confusing \"\\(f'\\) is at a maximum\" with \"\\(f\\) is at a maximum\" is the single most common error on this figure. <b>\\(x=5\\)</b> is an endpoint where \\(f'\\) is negative, so \\(f\\) is still falling.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> on a graph of \\(f'\\), zeros are candidates and the <i>sign change</i> decides which kind. The height of \\(f'\\) tells you about speed, never about extrema.","unit":5,"id":"u5m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the same graph of \\(f'\\), on which interval is \\(f\\) concave up?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same graph of f prime\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 130 L88 100 L175 55 L262 100 L320 130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">1</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">2.5</text><text x=\"259\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">4</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">y = f '(x)</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\((0,\\,2.5)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((1,\\,4)\\)"},{"t":"\\((2.5,\\,5)\\)"},{"t":"\\((0,\\,1)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Concavity of \\(f\\) is about whether \\(f'\\) is RISING</b>, since \\(f''=(f')'\\) is the slope of the curve you are looking at.<br><br>The graph of \\(f'\\) rises from \\(x=0\\) up to its peak at \\(x=2.5\\), then falls. So \\(f''>0\\) — and \\(f\\) is concave up — on \\((0,2.5)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\((1,4)\\)</b> is where \\(f'\\) is <i>positive</i>, which makes \\(f\\) increasing, not concave up — the classic swap of \"above the axis\" for \"going up\". <b>\\((2.5,5)\\)</b> is where \\(f'\\) falls, so \\(f\\) is concave <i>down</i>. <b>\\((0,1)\\)</b> is only part of the answer, cut off at the zero instead of at the peak.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> on a graph of \\(f'\\), the <i>sign</i> tells you whether \\(f\\) rises and the <i>slope</i> tells you how \\(f\\) bends. The peak of \\(f'\\) is an inflection point of \\(f\\), never an extremum.","unit":5,"id":"u5m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the same graph of \\(f'\\), on which interval is \\(f\\) increasing and concave down?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same graph of f prime\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 130 L88 100 L175 55 L262 100 L320 130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">1</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">2.5</text><text x=\"259\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">4</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">y = f '(x)</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\((2.5,\\,4)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((1,\\,2.5)\\)"},{"t":"\\((4,\\,5)\\)"},{"t":"\\((1,\\,4)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Two conditions, read off the same curve in two different ways.</b><br><br><i>Increasing</i> means \\(f'>0\\), which is the interval \\((1,4)\\) where the graph sits above the axis. <i>Concave down</i> means \\(f'\\) is falling, which happens after the peak, on \\((2.5,5)\\).<br><br>The overlap is \\((2.5,4)\\) — \\(f'\\) is still positive there but on its way down, so \\(f\\) is rising and levelling off.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\((1,2.5)\\)</b> is increasing but concave <i>up</i> — the rising half of the hump. <b>\\((4,5)\\)</b> is concave down but \\(f'\\) has gone negative, so \\(f\\) is decreasing. <b>\\((1,4)\\)</b> answers only the \"increasing\" half and ignores concavity entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when a question asks for two conditions, find each interval separately and intersect them. The answer is almost always narrower than either one alone.","unit":5,"id":"u5m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"concavity","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the same graph of \\(f'\\), the graph of \\(f\\) has a point of inflection at \\(x=\\)<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same graph of f prime\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 130 L88 100 L175 55 L262 100 L320 130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">1</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">2.5</text><text x=\"259\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">4</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">y = f '(x)</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(2.5\\) only","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\) and \\(4\\)"},{"t":"\\(1\\), \\(2.5\\) and \\(4\\)"},{"t":"there is no point of inflection"}],"explain":"<b>An inflection point of \\(f\\) is where concavity changes</b>, i.e. where \\(f''\\) changes sign — and \\(f''\\) is the <i>slope</i> of the graph you can see.<br><br>The graph of \\(f'\\) rises then falls, turning at \\(x=2.5\\). That is the only place its slope changes sign, so \\(x=2.5\\) is the only inflection point.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(1\\) and \\(4\\)</b> are the zeros of \\(f'\\) — those are the extrema of \\(f\\), not inflection points. This is the most common confusion on this figure: a zero of \\(f'\\) and a turning point of \\(f'\\) mean completely different things. <b>all three</b> throws in every interesting \\(x\\) without deciding what each one does. <b>none</b> misses that the slope of \\(f'\\) plainly flips from positive to negative at the peak.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> on a graph of \\(f'\\): crossings give extrema of \\(f\\); peaks and valleys give inflection points of \\(f\\).","unit":5,"id":"u5m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the absolute maximum value of \\(f(x)=x^{3}-3x\\) on the closed interval \\([0,3]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(18\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-2\\)"},{"t":"\\(0\\)"},{"t":"\\(3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The candidates test:</b> on a closed interval the absolute extrema occur either at a critical point or at an endpoint, so build the short list and evaluate \\(f\\) at every one.<br><br>\\(f'(x)=3x^{2}-3=0\\) gives \\(x=\\pm1\\), and only \\(x=1\\) lies in \\([0,3]\\). Candidates: \\(x=0,1,3\\).<br>\\(f(0)=0\\), \\(f(1)=-2\\), \\(f(3)=27-9=18\\). The largest is \\(\\mathbf{18}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>-2</b> is the absolute <i>minimum</i> — found the right candidates, picked the wrong end. <b>0</b> stops at the left endpoint. <b>3</b> reports the \\(x\\)-value where the maximum happens instead of the maximum <i>value</i>; the question asks \"what is the maximum value\", and mixing up input and output is worth a mark every time.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> endpoints are candidates too. A critical point can easily lose to an endpoint, and here it does — badly.","unit":5,"id":"u5m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"optim","calc":false,"prompt":"Two positive numbers have a sum of \\(20\\). What is their largest possible product?","opts":[{"t":"\\(100\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(400\\)"},{"t":"\\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(20\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Write one variable in terms of the other, then optimise.</b> If the numbers are \\(x\\) and \\(20-x\\), the product is \\(P(x)=x(20-x)=20x-x^{2}\\).<br><br>\\(P'(x)=20-2x=0\\Rightarrow x=10\\), and \\(P''=-2<0\\) confirms a maximum. The numbers are \\(10\\) and \\(10\\), and the product is \\(\\mathbf{100}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>400</b> is \\(20^{2}\\), from squaring the total rather than splitting it. <b>10</b> is the value of \\(x\\), not the product — the same input/output slip as before. <b>20</b> is the given sum.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> every optimisation is the same three moves — write the quantity to be optimised, use the constraint to reduce it to one variable, then set the derivative to zero. Finish by answering the question that was actually asked.","unit":5,"id":"u5m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"fdt","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f'(2)=0\\) and \\(f''(2)=-3\\), what does the second derivative test tell you about \\(f\\) at \\(x=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local maximum at \\(x=2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local minimum at \\(x=2\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a point of inflection at \\(x=2\\)"},{"t":"the test is inconclusive"}],"explain":"<b>The second derivative test:</b> at a critical point, \\(f''<0\\) means the curve is concave down there, so the graph sits below its horizontal tangent — a local maximum. \\(f''>0\\) would give a local minimum.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>local minimum</b> reverses the sign convention; remember concave down looks like a frown, which peaks. <b>inflection</b> would need \\(f''\\) to change sign at \\(x=2\\), and here it is a definite negative number. <b>inconclusive</b> is only correct when \\(f''(2)=0\\) — that is the case the test cannot decide, and it is worth knowing so you recognize when to fall back on the first derivative test.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the second derivative test needs two ingredients — a critical point and a <i>nonzero</i> second derivative. Given both, the sign of \\(f''\\) settles it in one step.","unit":5,"id":"u5m56","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"mvt","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous on \\([1,5]\\), differentiable on \\((1,5)\\), and \\(f(1)=f(5)=3\\). Which of the following must be true?","opts":[{"t":"there is some \\(c\\) in \\((1,5)\\) with \\(f'(c)=0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(x)=3\\) for all \\(x\\) in \\([1,5]\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) has a local maximum in \\((1,5)\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'(x)=0\\) for all \\(x\\) in \\((1,5)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>This is Rolle's theorem</b> — the Mean Value Theorem in the special case where the endpoints have equal heights. The average rate of change is \\(\\dfrac{3-3}{5-1}=0\\), so some \\(c\\) has \\(f'(c)=0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>constant</b> is one function that fits, not the only one — \\(f\\) could rise and come back down. <b>local maximum</b> assumes the flat spot is a peak, but \\(f\\) might dip instead, making it a minimum; the theorem promises a horizontal tangent without saying which kind. <b>\\(f'=0\\) everywhere</b> upgrades \"there exists\" to \"for all\", which is the same over-reading in a different costume.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> equal endpoint values guarantee at least one horizontal tangent somewhere between — no more, and no less.","unit":5,"id":"u5m57","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"connect","calc":false,"prompt":"On an interval where \\(f''>0\\), which of the following must be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(f'\\) is increasing","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\) is increasing"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is positive"},{"t":"\\(f'\\) is positive"}],"explain":"<b>\\(f''\\) is the derivative of \\(f'\\)</b>, so a positive \\(f''\\) says exactly one thing: \\(f'\\) is increasing. That is the definition of concave up.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(f\\) increasing</b> and <b>\\(f'\\) positive</b> are the same claim, and both are false in general — a concave-up function can be falling the whole way, like \\(f(x)=e^{-x}\\) or the left half of a parabola. Its slope is negative but rising toward zero. <b>\\(f\\) positive</b> confuses the sign of the function with the sign of its second derivative; \\(y=x^{2}-9\\) is concave up everywhere and negative near the origin.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> each derivative describes the one below it. \\(f''\\) governs \\(f'\\) directly, and \\(f\\) only indirectly — never assume a fact about \\(f''\\) transfers straight down two levels.","unit":5,"id":"u5m58","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"A pump's rate is recorded: at \\(t=0,2,5,7,10\\) minutes the rate \\(R(t)\\) is \\(4.2,\\ 6,\\ 7.4,\\ 5.6,\\ 3\\) gal/min. Using a <b>left</b> Riemann sum with the four subintervals of the table, the total gallons pumped over \\([0,10]\\) is approximately:","opts":[{"t":"58.0","correct":true},{"t":"54.4"},{"t":"56.2"},{"t":"52.6"}],"explain":"<b>Left sum uses each subinterval's LEFT value, with the table's unequal widths:</b> \\(4.2(2)+6(3)+7.4(2)+5.6(3)=8.4+18+14.8+16.8=58.0\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>54.4</b> is the <i>right</i> sum — heights read from the wrong end of each piece.<br>• <b>56.2</b> is the trapezoid sum (the average of left and right) — a different method.<br>• <b>52.6</b> uses equal widths of 2.5 — the table's spacing is not uniform.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> with a table, the widths come from the \\(t\\)-values, never from dividing the range evenly.","unit":6,"id":"u6m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous, positive, and <b>increasing</b> on \\([a,b]\\). Which statement about Riemann approximations of \\(\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\) must be true?","opts":[{"t":"The right sum overestimates the integral","correct":true},{"t":"The right sum underestimates the integral"},{"t":"The midpoint sum equals the integral"},{"t":"The trapezoid sum overestimates the integral"}],"explain":"<b>Increasing means each rectangle's right edge is its tallest point</b> — right rectangles poke above the curve, so the right sum overestimates (and the left sum underestimates).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"Right underestimates\"</b> is the rule for a <i>decreasing</i> function — direction flipped.<br>• <b>\"Midpoint is exact\"</b> is a myth; midpoint is merely usually <i>better</i>, not perfect.<br>• <b>\"Trapezoid overestimates\"</b> depends on <i>concavity</i>, not direction — increasing alone doesn't decide it.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> direction of \\(f\\) controls Left/Right over-or-under; concavity controls Midpoint/Trapezoid.","unit":6,"id":"u6m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous and <b>concave down</b> on \\([a,b]\\). The trapezoidal approximation of \\(\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\):","opts":[{"t":"underestimates, because each chord lies below the curve","correct":true},{"t":"overestimates, because each chord lies above the curve"},{"t":"is exact, because errors cancel"},{"t":"could be either — concavity is not enough information"}],"explain":"<b>Trapezoids connect endpoints with chords.</b> On a concave-down arc, every chord sags <i>below</i> the curve, so the trapezoid is missing the sliver between chord and curve: underestimate.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"Overestimates\"</b> is the concave-<i>up</i> rule, reversed.<br>• <b>\"Exact\"</b> would require straight-line pieces of \\(f\\).<br>• <b>\"Not enough information\"</b> — concavity alone IS the deciding fact for trapezoids (and midpoint, oppositely).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> concave down \\(\\Rightarrow\\) trapezoid under, midpoint over; concave up flips both.","unit":6,"id":"u6m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Which definite integral equals \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\frac{3}{n}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\,\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\int_1^4\\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\int_0^3\\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{3}\\int_1^4\\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\int_1^4\\sqrt{3x}\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Read the anatomy:</b> \\(\\Delta x=\\tfrac{3}{n}\\) means the interval has length 3; the sample points \\(1+\\tfrac{3k}{n}\\) start at 1 — so \\([1,4]\\), integrand \\(\\sqrt{x}\\): the limit is \\(\\int_1^4\\sqrt{x}\\,dx=\\tfrac{14}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\int_0^3\\sqrt x\\)</b> reads the start as 0, ignoring the \"+1\" inside the sample point — and computes a genuinely different number (\\(2\\sqrt3\\approx3.46\\), not \\(\\tfrac{14}{3}\\approx4.67\\)).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\int_1^4\\)</b> double-counts the width: the \\(3/n\\) IS the \\(dx\\); it doesn't also scale the integral.<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{3x}\\)</b> misreads the \\(3k/n\\) as multiplying \\(x\\) rather than building the partition.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(a\\) is the constant inside the sample point; \\(b=a+\\)(the coefficient of \\(k/n\\)); that same coefficient over \\(n\\) is \\(\\Delta x\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":true,"prompt":"Speeds are recorded at unequal times: \\(t=0,1,3,6\\) seconds give \\(v=10,12,15,9\\) m/s. The <b>trapezoidal</b> estimate of distance traveled on \\([0,6]\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"74 m","correct":true},{"t":"79 m"},{"t":"69 m"},{"t":"57 m"}],"explain":"<b>Trapezoid on each table piece, honest widths:</b> \\(\\tfrac{10+12}{2}(1)+\\tfrac{12+15}{2}(2)+\\tfrac{15+9}{2}(3)=11+27+36=74\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>79</b> is the left sum \\(10(1)+12(2)+15(3)\\).<br>• <b>69</b> is the right sum \\(12(1)+15(2)+9(3)\\).<br>• <b>57</b> averages only the first and last readings over the whole interval, \\(\\tfrac{10+9}{2}(6)\\) — throwing away the middle data.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> trapezoid = average the two edge heights <i>on each subinterval</i>, then multiply by that subinterval's own width.","unit":6,"id":"u6m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Water flows into a tank at rate \\(r(t)\\) gallons per minute for \\(0\\le t\\le 60\\). The quantity \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{60} r(t)\\,dt\\) represents:","opts":[{"t":"the total gallons added to the tank during the hour","correct":true},{"t":"the flow rate at \\(t=60\\)"},{"t":"the average flow rate over the hour"},{"t":"the rate of change of the flow rate"}],"explain":"<b>Integrating a rate accumulates its net change:</b> (gal/min)\\(\\times\\)(min) = gallons — total volume added.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Flow rate at 60</b> is \\(r(60)\\) — a value of the integrand, not its accumulation.<br>• <b>Average flow rate</b> would be \\(\\tfrac{1}{60}\\int_0^{60}r\\,dt\\) — the integral <i>divided by the width</i>.<br>• <b>Rate of change of the rate</b> is \\(r'(t)\\) — one derivative in the wrong direction.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> units multiply through the integral — the fastest distractor-killer on the exam.","unit":6,"id":"u6m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"For a smooth function, the error of a <b>left</b> Riemann sum behaves like \\(C/n\\). If doubling \\(n\\) is observed to cut a method's error roughly to one <b>quarter</b>, that method is most likely:","opts":[{"t":"midpoint or trapezoid","correct":true},{"t":"left endpoint"},{"t":"right endpoint"},{"t":"no method behaves this way"}],"explain":"<b>Error scaling is a fingerprint:</b> Left/Right errors fall like \\(1/n\\) (double \\(n\\) → halve); Midpoint/Trapezoid fall like \\(1/n^2\\) (double \\(n\\) → quarter).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Left</b> and <b>right</b> are the \\(1/n\\) methods — they only halve.<br>• <b>\"No method\"</b> — the curve-following methods do exactly this, which is why they dominate for the same work.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the order of convergence (\\(1/n\\) vs \\(1/n^2\\)) is the real difference between endpoint and curve-following methods.","unit":6,"id":"u6m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Which sum is the <b>right</b> Riemann approximation of \\(\\int_2^7 f(x)\\,dx\\) with \\(n\\) equal subintervals?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\Bigl(2+\\frac{5k}{n}\\Bigr)\\frac{5}{n}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f\\!\\Bigl(2+\\frac{5k}{n}\\Bigr)\\frac{5}{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\Bigl(2+\\frac{7k}{n}\\Bigr)\\frac{7}{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{5k}{n}\\Bigr)\\frac{5}{n}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Width \\(\\Delta x=\\tfrac{7-2}{n}=\\tfrac5n\\); right endpoints are \\(x_k=2+\\tfrac{5k}{n}\\), \\(k=1,\\dots,n\\).</b><br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(k=0\\) to \\(n-1\\)</b> samples the LEFT endpoints — same points, shifted one slot.<br>• <b>\\(7k/n\\) with \\(7/n\\)</b> uses the right endpoint 7 as if it were the interval <i>length</i>; the length is \\(b-a=5\\).<br>• <b>missing the \"+2\"</b> starts the partition at 0 instead of at \\(a\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\Delta x=(b-a)/n\\), and the sample points always carry the \"+a\".","unit":6,"id":"u6m7","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":true,"prompt":"A tank holds 20 gallons at \\(t=0\\). Water enters at \\(r(t)=7\\sin\\!\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\pi t}{6}\\bigr)\\) gal/min. The amount in the tank at \\(t=6\\) minutes is closest to:","opts":[{"t":"46.7 gal","correct":true},{"t":"26.7 gal"},{"t":"62.0 gal"},{"t":"33.4 gal"}],"explain":"<b>Amount = initial + accumulated inflow:</b> \\(20+\\int_0^6 7\\sin(\\pi t/6)\\,dt=20+\\tfrac{42}{\\pi}\\bigl[-\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{6}\\bigr]_0^6=20+\\tfrac{84}{\\pi}\\approx46.74\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>26.7</b> is the integral alone — the initial 20 gallons forgotten: the #1 rubric deduction on accumulation problems.<br>• <b>62.0</b> is \\(20+7(6)\\): rate × time, treating a varying rate as constant at its peak.<br>• <b>33.4</b> is \\(20+\\tfrac{42}{\\pi}\\) — the antiderivative's constant \\(\\tfrac{42}{\\pi}\\) applied to only one bound.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(A(t)=A(0)+\\int_0^t r\\) — the \"+ initial amount\" is a stated rubric point.","unit":6,"id":"u6m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous and <b>decreasing</b> on \\([a,b]\\). Which approximation of \\(\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\) must be an <b>overestimate</b>?","opts":[{"t":"the left Riemann sum","correct":true},{"t":"the right Riemann sum"},{"t":"both left and right"},{"t":"neither — it depends on concavity"}],"explain":"<b>Decreasing means each slice's LEFT edge is its tallest point</b> — left rectangles stick above the curve: overestimate. (Right rectangles tuck under.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Right</b> is the underestimate here — the increasing-function rule applied without flipping.<br>• <b>Both</b> is impossible: for monotone \\(f\\), left and right always bracket the integral.<br>• <b>\"Depends on concavity\"</b> confuses the Left/Right rule (direction-driven) with the Midpoint/Trapezoid rule (concavity-driven).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> monotone direction settles Left/Right immediately; concavity is for the other two.","unit":6,"id":"u6m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\) where \\(f\\) is continuous, \\(f(3)=0\\), and \\(f\\) changes sign from positive to negative at \\(x=3\\). Then \\(g\\) has:","opts":[{"t":"a local maximum at \\(x=3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"a local minimum at \\(x=3\\)"},{"t":"an inflection point at \\(x=3\\)"},{"t":"a discontinuity at \\(x=3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(g'=f\\) by FTC Part 1.</b> \\(g'\\) going \\(+\\) to \\(-\\) is the textbook first-derivative-test signature of a local max.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Local min</b> is the \\(-\\) to \\(+\\) crossing — direction reversed.<br>• <b>Inflection</b> of \\(g\\) comes from \\(f\\) changing <i>direction</i> (since \\(g''=f'\\)), not changing sign.<br>• <b>Discontinuity</b> — accumulation of a continuous function is not just continuous but differentiable.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> sign of \\(f\\) → direction of \\(g\\); sign <i>change</i> of \\(f\\) → extremum of \\(g\\); direction change of \\(f\\) → inflection of \\(g\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_2^{x^3}\\cos(t^2)\\,dt\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(3x^2\\cos(x^6)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\cos(x^6)\\)"},{"t":"\\(3x^2\\cos(x^3)\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\sin(x^6)\\cdot 3x^2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>FTC Part 1 with a chain:</b> substitute the upper bound into the integrand — \\((x^3)^2=x^6\\) — then multiply by the bound's derivative \\(3x^2\\). No antidifferentiation happens at all.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\cos(x^6)\\)</b> forgets the chain factor \\(3x^2\\) — the most common point lost on this question type.<br>• <b>\\(\\cos(x^3)\\)</b> substitutes the bound but forgets to square it inside \\(t^2\\).<br>• <b>\\(-\\sin(x^6)\\cdot3x^2\\)</b> differentiates the integrand — but FTC says evaluate, not differentiate.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tfrac{d}{dx}\\int_a^{u(x)}f(t)\\,dt=f(u(x))\\,u'(x)\\). Designed to take ten seconds.","unit":6,"id":"u6m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_x^{5}\\ln(1+t^2)\\,dt\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\ln(1+x^2)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\ln(1+x^2)\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\ln(26)-\\ln(1+x^2)\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The variable bound is on the BOTTOM — flip it first:</b> \\(\\int_x^5=-\\int_5^x\\), then FTC gives \\(-\\ln(1+x^2)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(+\\ln(1+x^2)\\)</b> applies FTC without paying the flip's minus sign.<br>• <b>\\(\\ln26-\\ln(1+x^2)\\)</b> \"evaluates at both bounds\" — FTC <i>Part 2</i> thinking; a constant bound contributes nothing to a derivative.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\)</b> differentiates the integrand — wrong operation entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a variable lower bound costs exactly one minus sign.","unit":6,"id":"u6m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\sec^2 x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\sqrt{3}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The antiderivative is a deck card:</b> \\([\\tan x]_0^{\\pi/3}=\\tan\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}-0=\\sqrt3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> evaluates \\(\\sec x\\) at the bounds, \\(2-1\\) — that antiderivative belongs to \\(\\sec x\\tan x\\), not \\(\\sec^2x\\).<br>• <b>\\(1/\\sqrt3\\)</b> is \\(\\tan(\\pi/6)\\) — the wrong special angle.<br>• <b>\\(4\\pi/3\\)</b> is \\(\\sec^2(\\pi/3)\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\) — endpoint value times width, a rectangle masquerading as an integral.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\int\\sec^2=\\tan\\) is one of the twelve cards; a definite integral is two evaluations and a subtraction.","unit":6,"id":"u6m13","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 220' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><line x1='20' y1='132.5' x2='280' y2='132.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='38.6' y1='18' x2='38.6' y2='202' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='112.9' y1='128.5' x2='112.9' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='112.9' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>2</text><line x1='187.1' y1='128.5' x2='187.1' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='187.1' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>4</text><line x1='261.4' y1='128.5' x2='261.4' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='261.4' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>6</text><polyline points='38.6,132.5 112.9,42.5 187.1,132.5 261.4,177.5' fill='none' stroke='#5f7ff2' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'/><text x='112.9' y='101.0' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>area 8</text><text x='236.3' y='152.8' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>area 2</text><text x='268' y='30' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='12' text-anchor='end' font-family='monospace'>y = f(x)</text></svg></div>The graph of \\(f\\) consists of three line segments, with the labeled region areas. If \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\), then \\(g(6)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(8\\)"},{"t":"\\(-2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Signed areas, glued:</b> \\(g(6)=\\underbrace{8}_{[0,4],\\ \\text{above}}+\\underbrace{(-2)}_{[4,6],\\ \\text{below}}=6\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>10</b> adds \\(8+2\\) — treating the below-axis region as positive. Position gives the sign.<br>• <b>8</b> stops at \\(x=4\\), ignoring the final region entirely.<br>• <b>−2</b> is only the last region — the accumulated history forgotten.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(g\\) accumulates <i>signed</i> area from 0 — below the axis counts against the total.","unit":6,"id":"u6m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 220' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><line x1='20' y1='132.5' x2='280' y2='132.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='38.6' y1='18' x2='38.6' y2='202' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='112.9' y1='128.5' x2='112.9' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='112.9' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>2</text><line x1='187.1' y1='128.5' x2='187.1' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='187.1' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>4</text><line x1='261.4' y1='128.5' x2='261.4' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='261.4' y='150.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>6</text><polyline points='38.6,132.5 112.9,42.5 187.1,132.5 261.4,177.5' fill='none' stroke='#5f7ff2' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'/><text x='112.9' y='101.0' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>area 8</text><text x='236.3' y='152.8' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>area 2</text><text x='268' y='30' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='12' text-anchor='end' font-family='monospace'>y = f(x)</text></svg></div>With the same figure and \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\), the absolute maximum of \\(g\\) on \\([0,6]\\) occurs at \\(x=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(6\\)"},{"t":"\\(0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(g'=f\\):</b> \\(f>0\\) on \\((0,4)\\) so \\(g\\) climbs to \\(g(4)=8\\); \\(f<0\\) on \\((4,6)\\) so \\(g\\) falls. The \\(+\\to-\\) sign change at \\(x=4\\) is the max, and it beats both endpoints (\\(g(0)=0,\\ g(6)=6\\)).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> is where \\(f\\) itself peaks — where \\(g\\) climbs <i>fastest</i>, not where it's biggest. Slope vs value, the classic confusion.<br>• <b>6</b> would need \\(f\\ge0\\) throughout; the below-axis region drags \\(g\\) back down.<br>• <b>0</b> is where \\(g\\) is smallest.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(g\\) is largest where \\(f\\) finishes crossing \\(+\\) to \\(-\\) — confirm with a candidates table.","unit":6,"id":"u6m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"Given \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^5 f(x)\\,dx=10\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^5 g(x)\\,dx=-2\\), find \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^5\\bigl(3f(x)-2g(x)\\bigr)dx\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\(34\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(26\\)"},{"t":"\\(8\\)"},{"t":"\\(24\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Linearity:</b> \\(3(10)-2(-2)=30+4=34\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>26</b> computes \\(30-4\\): the minus sign of \\(\\int g\\) lost in the double negative.<br>• <b>8</b> is \\(10-2\\) — the constants never applied.<br>• <b>24</b> is arithmetic slippage on the same double negative, \\(30-6\\)-style.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> integrals split over sums and constants slide through — handle the signs like a ledger.","unit":6,"id":"u6m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(\\displaystyle\\int_3^8 f(x)\\,dx=12\\), then \\(\\displaystyle\\int_8^3 2f(x)\\,dx=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-24\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(24\\)"},{"t":"\\(-12\\)"},{"t":"\\(12\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Two properties at once:</b> swapping bounds flips the sign, and the constant slides out: \\(\\int_8^3 2f=-2\\int_3^8 f=-24\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>24</b> scales but never pays the flip.<br>• <b>−12</b> flips but never scales.<br>• <b>12</b> does neither.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\int_b^a=-\\int_a^b\\) — bounds are oriented, and each property applies independently.","unit":6,"id":"u6m17","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\) with \\(f\\) continuous. \\(g\\) is <b>concave down</b> exactly on intervals where:","opts":[{"t":"\\(f\\) is decreasing","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\) is negative"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is concave down"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is positive"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate twice:</b> \\(g''=f'\\), so \\(g\\) concave down \\(\\iff f'<0\\iff f\\) decreasing.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f\\) negative</b> controls where \\(g\\) <i>decreases</i> — direction, not bend. One derivative off.<br>• <b>\\(f\\) concave down</b> is two derivatives too deep — that is \\(g\\)-third-derivative territory.<br>• <b>\\(f\\) positive</b> is where \\(g\\) increases.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> everything about \\(g\\) reads one derivative DOWN on \\(f\\): sign→direction, direction→bend.","unit":6,"id":"u6m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(h(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_2^x f(t)\\,dt\\) with \\(f\\) continuous and \\(f(2)=7\\). Which pair of statements is true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(h(2)=0\\) and \\(h'(2)=7\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(h(2)=7\\) and \\(h'(2)=0\\)"},{"t":"\\(h(2)=0\\) and \\(h'(2)=0\\)"},{"t":"\\(h(2)\\) cannot be determined"}],"explain":"<b>Two freebies:</b> \\(h(2)=\\int_2^2f=0\\) (no width, no area), and \\(h'(x)=f(x)\\) so \\(h'(2)=f(2)=7\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(h(2)=7\\)</b> confuses the function's value with the accumulation's value — \\(f(2)\\) is \\(h\\)'s <i>slope</i> there, not its height.<br>• <b>both zero</b> erases FTC Part 1.<br>• <b>\"cannot be determined\"</b> — \\(\\int_a^a=0\\) needs no information at all.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(h(a)=0\\) is the free entry in every candidates table, and \\(h'=f\\) is the theorem.","unit":6,"id":"u6m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\Bigl(4x^3-\\frac{2}{x^2}+5\\Bigr)dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(x^4+\\dfrac{2}{x}+5x+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x^4-\\dfrac{2}{x}+5x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(x^4+\\dfrac{2}{x^3}+5x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(12x^2+\\dfrac{4}{x^3}+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Power rule up, term by term:</b> \\(-2x^{-2}\\) lifts to \\(-2\\cdot\\tfrac{x^{-1}}{-1}=+\\tfrac{2}{x}\\) — the double negative is the whole question. Check: \\(\\tfrac{d}{dx}\\tfrac2x=-\\tfrac{2}{x^2}\\). \\(\\checkmark\\)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-2/x\\)</b> drops the sign flip from dividing by \\(-1\\).<br>• <b>\\(+2/x^3\\)</b> moves the exponent the WRONG direction — differentiation's \\(-3\\), not integration's \\(-1\\).<br>• <b>\\(12x^2+\\cdots\\)</b> differentiates instead of integrating.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> verify by differentiating — ten seconds converts \"probably right\" into \"certain.\"","unit":6,"id":"u6m20","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x^2\\sqrt{x^3+8}\\;dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{2}{9}(x^3+8)^{3/2}+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{2}{3}(x^3+8)^{3/2}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{1}{9}(x^3+8)^{3/2}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{2}{9}x^3(x^3+8)^{3/2}+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>u-sub with constant adjust:</b> \\(u=x^3+8\\), \\(du=3x^2dx\\) so \\(x^2dx=\\tfrac{du}{3}\\): \\(\\tfrac13\\int\\sqrt u\\,du=\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac23u^{3/2}=\\tfrac29(x^3+8)^{3/2}+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac23\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac13\\) from \\(du\\) — the missing-3 bookkeeping.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac19\\)</b> applies a \\(\\tfrac13\\) twice.<br>• <b>the \\(x^3\\)-carrying option</b> \"back-substitutes\" a factor that was already consumed by \\(du\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> MC options differing only by constants are a du-bookkeeping check — differentiate the candidates and one survives.","unit":6,"id":"u6m21","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\tan x\\,\\sec^2x\\;dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"\\(0\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The fingerprint pair:</b> \\(u=\\tan x\\), \\(du=\\sec^2x\\,dx\\); the bounds convert \\(0\\to0\\), \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\to1\\): \\(\\int_0^1u\\,du=\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> is \\(\\tan^2(\\pi/4)\\) — the \\(\\tfrac{u^2}{2}\\)'s half dropped.<br>• <b>0</b> mixes worlds — old \\(x\\)-bounds used on the \\(u\\)-antiderivative.<br>• <b>\\(\\pi/4\\)</b> treats the integral as width times something — no integration performed.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> definite u-sub converts the bounds through \\(u(x)\\) and finishes entirely in u-world.","unit":6,"id":"u6m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^4\\frac{dx}{1-x}\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\ln 3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\ln 3\\)"},{"t":"undefined — the integrand is discontinuous on \\([2,4]\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\ln 3-\\ln(-1)\\), which does not exist"}],"explain":"<b>\\(u=1-x\\), \\(du=-dx\\):</b> \\(\\int\\tfrac{dx}{1-x}=-\\ln|1-x|+C\\), so the value is \\(-\\ln|-3|+\\ln|-1|=-\\ln3\\). The asymptote sits at \\(x=1\\), safely OUTSIDE \\([2,4]\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(+\\ln3\\)</b> misses the minus emitted by \\(du=-dx\\).<br>• <b>\"discontinuous\"</b> fails the scan: \\(1-x=0\\) at \\(x=1\\notin[2,4]\\) — the interval is clean.<br>• <b>\\(\\ln(-1)\\)</b> forgets the absolute value that makes \\(\\ln|1-x|\\) perfectly defined for \\(x>1\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the \\(|\\cdot|\\) in \\(\\ln|u|\\) lets antiderivatives live on the negative side — and the asymptote scan must check the actual interval, not just exist.","unit":6,"id":"u6m23","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int 2^x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2}+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2^x\\ln 2+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2^{x+1}}{x+1}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(x\\cdot 2^{x-1}+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Deck card:</b> \\(\\tfrac{d}{dx}2^x=2^x\\ln2\\), so integrating must pre-divide: \\(\\tfrac{2^x}{\\ln2}+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2^x\\ln2\\)</b> is the DERIVATIVE — the \\(\\ln2\\) goes downstairs when integrating.<br>• <b>\\(2^{x+1}/(x+1)\\)</b> applies the power rule to an exponential — but the base is constant and the exponent is the variable.<br>• <b>\\(x\\,2^{x-1}\\)</b> is the same confusion in differentiated form.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(x^n\\) (variable base) takes the power rule; \\(a^x\\) (variable exponent) takes \\(\\ln a\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m24","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{dx}{x^2-4x+8}\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\arctan\\!\\Bigl(\\dfrac{x-2}{2}\\Bigr)+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\arctan(x-2)+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\ln\\bigl|x^2-4x+8\\bigr|+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\ln\\Bigl|\\dfrac{x-2}{x+2}\\Bigr|+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Discriminant \\(16-32<0\\): won't factor — complete the square:</b> \\((x-2)^2+4\\), then the arctan card with \\(a=2\\): \\(\\tfrac1a\\arctan\\tfrac ua=\\tfrac12\\arctan\\tfrac{x-2}{2}+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>no \\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> drops the \\(1/a\\) — the most-lost constant in the unit.<br>• <b>the \\(\\ln\\) option</b> would need the numerator to be the derivative \\(2x-4\\); a constant numerator means arctan.<br>• <b>the two-log option</b> is partial fractions — illegal here, because this quadratic has no real factors.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> constant top + irreducible quadratic bottom \\(\\Rightarrow\\) complete the square \\(\\Rightarrow\\) \\(\\tfrac1a\\arctan\\tfrac ua\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m25","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{x^2+1}{x-1}\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+x+2\\ln|x-1|+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^3/3+x}{x^2/2-x}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\ln\\bigl|x^2+1\\bigr|\\cdot\\ln|x-1|+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+x-2\\ln|x-1|+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Top-heavy — divide first:</b> \\(\\tfrac{x^2+1}{x-1}=x+1+\\tfrac{2}{x-1}\\) (check: \\((x-1)(x+1)+2=x^2+1\\)). Integrate: \\(\\tfrac{x^2}2+x+2\\ln|x-1|+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>the stacked-fraction option</b> integrates top and bottom separately — integrals NEVER distribute over quotients.<br>• <b>the product-of-logs option</b> invents a \"log both pieces\" rule that doesn't exist.<br>• <b>\\(-2\\ln\\)</b> divides correctly but flips the remainder's sign.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> degree(top) \\(\\ge\\) degree(bottom) \\(\\Rightarrow\\) long division before anything else.","unit":6,"id":"u6m26","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"Which integral <b>cannot</b> be evaluated with a single u-substitution?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\,e^{x}\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\,e^{x^2}dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int \\cos x\\,\\sin^4x\\;dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Run the fingerprint check on each:</b> \\(xe^{x^2}\\) pairs \\(x\\) with \\(d(x^2)\\); \\(\\cos x\\sin^4x\\) pairs \\(\\cos x\\) with \\(d(\\sin x)\\); \\(\\tfrac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\) pairs \\(x^2\\) with \\(d(x^3+1)\\). But in \\(xe^x\\) nothing is the derivative of anything else — \\(e^x\\) has no inside. That one needs integration by parts.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> each of the other three has its inside function's derivative standing right there, up to a constant — they are one-line u-subs, which is precisely why they are wrong answers here.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> u-sub needs a composition WITH its inside's derivative on duty. A genuine product of unrelated types is parts territory — the boundary between Lessons 3 and 4 in one question.","unit":6,"id":"u6m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(F(x)=x\\arctan x-\\tfrac12\\ln(1+x^2)\\). Then \\(F'(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\arctan x\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\arctan x+\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(x\\arctan x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate honestly:</b> the product rule gives \\(\\arctan x+\\tfrac{x}{1+x^2}\\); the log term contributes \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{2x}{1+x^2}=-\\tfrac{x}{1+x^2}\\). The fractions cancel exactly, leaving \\(\\arctan x\\) — so \\(F\\) is the antiderivative of \\(\\arctan x\\), verified the cheap way.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac1{1+x^2}\\)</b> differentiates only the \\(\\arctan\\) and forgets it sits inside a product.<br>• <b>the uncancelled option</b> drops the \\(-\\tfrac12\\) coefficient on the log's chain rule.<br>• <b>\\(x\\arctan x\\)</b> differentiates nothing.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> verification by differentiation also TEACHES — the cancellation you witness is exactly why parts produced that second term.","unit":6,"id":"u6m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\sec^2(2x)\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\tan(2x)+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tan(2x)+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\tan(2x)+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac13\\sec^3(2x)+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Deck card + constant adjust:</b> \\(u=2x\\), \\(dx=\\tfrac{du}2\\): \\(\\tfrac12\\tan(2x)+C\\). Check: the chain rule's 2 meets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) and cancels.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tan(2x)\\)</b> differentiates back to \\(2\\sec^2(2x)\\) — twice too big.<br>• <b>\\(2\\tan(2x)\\)</b> adjusts in the WRONG direction — four times too big.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\sec^3\\)</b> power-rules the \\(\\sec^2\\) as if \\(\\sec\\) were the variable — but \\(d(\\sec)\\ne dx\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> inner constants cost a reciprocal, never a copy — and the options can always be arbitrated by differentiating them.","unit":6,"id":"u6m29","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\sin x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(-x\\cos x+\\sin x+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x\\cos x-\\sin x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(-x\\cos x-\\sin x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\cos x+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Parts with LIATE:</b> \\(u=x,\\ dv=\\sin x\\,dx\\Rightarrow v=-\\cos x\\): \\(-x\\cos x+\\int\\cos x\\,dx=-x\\cos x+\\sin x+C\\). Verify: \\(-\\cos x+x\\sin x+\\cos x=x\\sin x\\). \\(\\checkmark\\)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x\\cos x-\\sin x\\)</b> is the global sign flip — \\(v=+\\cos x\\) used.<br>• <b>\\(-x\\cos x-\\sin x\\)</b> botches the double negative in \\(-\\int(-\\cos x)dx\\).<br>• <b>the \\(\\tfrac{x^2}2\\) option</b> antidifferentiates both factors separately — \\(\\int fg\\ne(\\int f)(\\int g)\\), ever.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(v=-\\cos x\\) plants two sign traps in one problem; differentiate your answer and both disarm.","unit":6,"id":"u6m30","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^{e^2}\\ln x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^2+1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(e^2-1\\)"},{"t":"\\(2e^2\\)"},{"t":"\\(e^2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The invisible product:</b> \\(\\int\\ln x\\,dx=x\\ln x-x\\) (parts with \\(dv=dx\\)). Evaluate: \\((e^2\\cdot2-e^2)-(0-1)=e^2+1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(e^2-1\\)</b> drops the sign on \\(-(0-1)\\) — the lower bound contributes \\(+1\\).<br>• <b>\\(2e^2\\)</b> evaluates \\(x\\ln x\\) alone, forgetting the \\(-x\\).<br>• <b>\\(e^2\\)</b> forgets the lower bound entirely — \\(F(b)\\) without \\(F(a)\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\ln(e^2)=2\\), both bounds always, and the \\(-x\\) is half the antiderivative.","unit":6,"id":"u6m31","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the tabular method, \\(\\displaystyle\\int x^2e^x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^x\\bigl(x^2-2x+2\\bigr)+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(e^x\\bigl(x^2+2x+2\\bigr)+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(e^x\\bigl(x^2-2x-2\\bigr)+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac{x^3}{3}e^x+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The table:</b> derivatives \\(x^2,2x,2,0\\); antiderivatives \\(e^x\\) all the way; signs \\(+,-,+\\). Diagonals: \\(x^2e^x-2xe^x+2e^x=e^x(x^2-2x+2)+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>all-plus</b> never alternates the signs — multiplying straight across instead of along signed diagonals.<br>• <b>\\(-2\\) at the end</b> alternates one slot too far: the third diagonal carries \\(+\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{x^3}3e^x\\)</b> integrates the factors independently — the product-rule myth again.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> signs start at \\(+\\) and attach to the DIAGONAL products; run the column all the way to 0.","unit":6,"id":"u6m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int e^x\\cos x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{e^x(\\sin x+\\cos x)}{2}+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{e^x(\\sin x-\\cos x)}{2}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(e^x\\sin x+C\\)"},{"t":"the integral cannot be expressed in elementary terms"}],"explain":"<b>The cyclic case:</b> parts twice returns the original integral; naming it \\(I\\) gives \\(I=e^x\\sin x+e^x\\cos x-I\\), so \\(2I=e^x(\\sin x+\\cos x)\\). Verify: differentiating, the \\(\\sin\\) terms cancel and \\(e^x\\cos x\\) returns. \\(\\checkmark\\)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\((\\sin-\\cos)\\)</b> is the answer to \\(\\int e^x\\sin x\\,dx\\) — the sibling integral.<br>• <b>\\(e^x\\sin x\\)</b> stops after round one, ignoring the leftover \\(-\\int e^x\\sin x\\,dx\\).<br>• <b>\"not elementary\"</b> is panic at the cycle — the integral returning IS the solving mechanism.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when the original reappears, name it \\(I\\) and solve algebraically. Two rounds, one equation.","unit":6,"id":"u6m33","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(\\dfrac{5x-7}{(x-2)(x-1)}=\\dfrac{A}{x-2}+\\dfrac{B}{x-1}\\), then \\(A=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(-3\\)"},{"t":"\\(5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Cover-up:</b> cover \\((x-2)\\), evaluate the rest at its root \\(x=2\\): \\(A=\\dfrac{5(2)-7}{2-1}=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> is \\(B\\) (evaluated at \\(x=1\\): \\(\\tfrac{-2}{-1}\\)) — right method, wrong factor.<br>• <b>−3</b> evaluates the surviving denominator as \\(1-2\\) instead of \\(2-1\\).<br>• <b>5</b> just reads the numerator's leading coefficient.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> cover the factor, evaluate everything that survives at THAT factor's root — one line per constant.","unit":6,"id":"u6m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{x+4}{x^2+3x+2}\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(3\\ln|x+1|-2\\ln|x+2|+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\ln\\bigl|x^2+3x+2\\bigr|+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\ln|x+1|+\\ln|x+2|+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\arctan(2x+3)+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Factor first:</b> \\(x^2+3x+2=(x+1)(x+2)\\). Cover-up: at \\(x=-1\\), \\(A=\\tfrac31=3\\); at \\(x=-2\\), \\(B=\\tfrac{2}{-1}=-2\\). Two log cards.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>the single-log option</b> pretends the numerator is the denominator's derivative — but \\(d(x^2+3x+2)=2x+3\\ne x+4\\). Close is not equal.<br>• <b>\\(\\ln+\\ln\\)</b> uses \\(A=B=1\\) without computing anything.<br>• <b>the arctan option</b> aims at an irreducible quadratic — but the discriminant is \\(9-8>0\\): it FACTORS, so PF was the branch.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> run the rational checklist in order — top-heavy? numerator \\(\\approx\\) derivative? factors? — then pick the machine.","unit":6,"id":"u6m35","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"Before integrating \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{x^2+5}{x+2}\\,dx\\), the required first step is:","opts":[{"t":"long division, giving \\(x-2+\\dfrac{9}{x+2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"partial fractions with \\(\\dfrac{A}{x+2}\\)"},{"t":"the substitution \\(u=x^2+5\\)"},{"t":"completing the square in the numerator"}],"explain":"<b>Degree 2 over degree 1: top-heavy.</b> Divide: \\((x+2)(x-2)=x^2-4\\), remainder 9 — so \\(x-2+\\tfrac9{x+2}\\), each piece a one-step integral.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>PF with one constant</b> can only produce a proper fraction — it cannot represent the polynomial hiding inside an improper one.<br>• <b>\\(u=x^2+5\\)</b> needs \\(du=2x\\,dx\\) as a factor; there is no lone \\(x\\) upstairs.<br>• <b>completing the square in the NUMERATOR</b> isn't a technique — that move lives in denominators, aiming at arctan.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> improper fraction \\(\\Rightarrow\\) divide first, reflexively — exactly like rewriting \\(\\tfrac{17}5\\) as \\(3+\\tfrac25\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m36","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"By the tabular method, \\(\\displaystyle\\int x^3\\cos x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(x^3\\sin x+3x^2\\cos x-6x\\sin x-6\\cos x+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(x^3\\sin x-3x^2\\cos x+6x\\sin x-6\\cos x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(x^3\\sin x+3x^2\\cos x+6x\\sin x+6\\cos x+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(x^3\\sin x+3x^2\\cos x-6x\\sin x+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The table:</b> derivatives \\(x^3,3x^2,6x,6,0\\); antiderivatives of \\(\\cos\\): \\(\\sin,-\\cos,-\\sin,\\cos\\); signs \\(+,-,+,-\\). Diagonals: \\(+x^3\\sin x-3x^2(-\\cos x)+6x(-\\sin x)-6(\\cos x)\\) — the chain's own minuses interleave with the sign column.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>option 2</b> applies the sign column but ignores the minuses already living in the trig antiderivative chain.<br>• <b>all-plus</b> ignores both sign sources.<br>• <b>the three-term option</b> stops before the derivative column reaches 0 — the silently dropped last term.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> trig chains carry their OWN signs (\\(\\sin\\to-\\cos\\to-\\sin\\to\\cos\\)); the alternating column multiplies on top of them.","unit":6,"id":"u6m37","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x^5\\ln x\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^6}{6}\\ln x-\\dfrac{x^6}{36}+C\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^6}{6}\\ln x-\\dfrac{x^6}{6}+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(5x^4\\ln x+x^4+C\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{x^6}{6}\\bigl(x\\ln x-x\\bigr)+C\\)"}],"explain":"<b>LIATE: the log is u.</b> \\(u=\\ln x,\\ dv=x^5dx\\Rightarrow v=\\tfrac{x^6}6\\): \\(\\tfrac{x^6}6\\ln x-\\int\\tfrac{x^6}6\\cdot\\tfrac1x\\,dx=\\tfrac{x^6}6\\ln x-\\tfrac16\\int x^5dx=\\tfrac{x^6}6\\ln x-\\tfrac{x^6}{36}+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-x^6/6\\)</b> forgets the new integral carries its own extra \\(\\tfrac16\\).<br>• <b>the derivative-looking option</b> differentiated the product instead.<br>• <b>multiplying by \\((x\\ln x-x)\\)</b> treats \\(\\int\\ln x\\) as a factor — antiderivatives of factors don't multiply.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> with \\(x^n\\ln x\\), the log dies into \\(1/x\\) and erases itself from the new integral — that's WHY LIATE ranks it first.","unit":6,"id":"u6m38","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}x^{-4/3}\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"diverges"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac13\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac34\\)"}],"explain":"<b>p-test first (\\(p=\\tfrac43>1\\): converges), then the limit:</b> \\(\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\bigl[-3x^{-1/3}\\bigr]_1^b=0+3=3\\) — matching \\(\\tfrac1{p-1}=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"diverges\"</b> misreads \\(p=\\tfrac43\\) as less than 1.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> is \\(p-1\\) instead of \\(\\tfrac1{p-1}\\) — the formula inverted.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac34\\)</b> is \\(1/p\\), a number from nowhere meaningful.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> at infinity the tail must be THIN: \\(p>1\\) converges, to \\(\\tfrac1{p-1}\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m39","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1}\\frac{dx}{x^3}\\;\\):","opts":[{"t":"diverges","correct":true},{"t":"\\(=\\tfrac12\\)"},{"t":"\\(=-\\tfrac12\\)"},{"t":"\\(=2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The blow-up is at 0 — and near zero the p-rule FLIPS:</b> \\(\\int_0^1x^{-p}\\) needs \\(p<1\\). Here \\(p=3\\): \\(\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\bigl[-\\tfrac1{2x^2}\\bigr]_a^1=-\\tfrac12+\\lim\\tfrac1{2a^2}=\\infty\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> and <b>\\(-\\tfrac12\\)</b> evaluate the antiderivative at 1 and quietly drop the exploding lower limit — \"plugging in 0\" where the function has no value.<br>• <b>2</b> is the answer to \\(\\int_0^1x^{-1/2}\\) — the mild spike, not this violent one.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> near zero, convergence needs a MILD spike (\\(p<1\\)); \\(p\\ge1\\) piles up infinite area at the asymptote.","unit":6,"id":"u6m40","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\infty}x\\,e^{-x^2}dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"diverges"},{"t":"\\(0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>u-sub inside an improper integral:</b> \\(u=x^2\\) converts it to \\(\\tfrac12\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u}du=\\tfrac12\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}(1-e^{-b})=\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1</b> is \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u}du\\) with the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from \\(du=2x\\,dx\\) forgotten.<br>• <b>\"diverges\"</b> reasons \"\\(x\\) grows\" — but \\(e^{-x^2}\\) dies far faster; the product's tail is razor thin.<br>• <b>0</b> confuses the integrand's limit with the integral's value.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> techniques compose — substitution first, then the limit. The bounds convert too: \\(0\\to0,\\ \\infty\\to\\infty\\).","unit":6,"id":"u6m41","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"A student writes \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\frac{dx}{(x-2)^2}=\\Bigl[-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\Bigr]_0^4=-\\frac12-\\frac12=-1\\). The correct conclusion is:","opts":[{"t":"the integral diverges — there is an asymptote at \\(x=2\\), inside the interval","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-1\\) is correct"},{"t":"the answer should be \\(+1\\) — sign error only"},{"t":"the answer is \\(0\\) by symmetry"}],"explain":"<b>Scan before FTC:</b> the integrand blows up at \\(x=2\\in(0,4)\\). FTC's continuity hypothesis fails, so the fluent computation is meaningless — a positive function can never integrate to \\(-1\\). Splitting at 2 gives two halves that each diverge.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"−1 is correct\"</b> trusts a negative area for a positive integrand — the absurdity is the alarm.<br>• <b>\"+1, sign error\"</b> repairs the symptom and keeps the disease; no sign fix rescues a broken hypothesis.<br>• <b>\"0 by symmetry\"</b> — two \\(+\\infty\\) halves don't cancel; they're the same sign anyway.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> scan the WHOLE interval for blow-ups before integrating. Interior asymptote \\(\\Rightarrow\\) split, and BOTH halves must converge.","unit":6,"id":"u6m42","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"The improper integral \\(\\displaystyle\\int_3^{\\infty}f(x)\\,dx\\) <b>converges</b> means precisely that:","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\int_3^{b}f(x)\\,dx\\) exists as a finite number","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f(x)\\to 0\\) as \\(x\\to\\infty\\)"},{"t":"\\(f\\) is bounded on \\([3,\\infty)\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\int_3^b f\\,dx\\) is increasing in \\(b\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Convergence is a statement about the LIMIT of the partial integrals</b> — the running total has a destination.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(f\\to0\\)</b> is not sufficient: \\(1/x\\to0\\) and its integral still diverges — the fat-tail counterexample to memorize.<br>• <b>bounded</b> is far too weak — \\(f=1\\) is bounded and diverges instantly.<br>• <b>increasing partial integrals</b> describes any positive integrand, convergent or not.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the definition IS the limit. Everything else is heuristics the exam loves to weaponize.","unit":6,"id":"u6m43","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-3x}\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\tfrac13\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(3\\)"},{"t":"\\(1\\)"},{"t":"diverges"}],"explain":"<b>Limit, written:</b> \\(\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\bigl[-\\tfrac13e^{-3x}\\bigr]_0^b=0-(-\\tfrac13)=\\tfrac13\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3</b> inverts the constant adjust — the chain rule's 3 must be pre-divided, not multiplied.<br>• <b>1</b> is \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x}\\) — the inner 3 ignored.<br>• <b>\"diverges\"</b> — exponential decay has the thinnest tail there is; these always converge.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-kx}dx=\\tfrac1k\\) — worth knowing cold, derived in one limit line.","unit":6,"id":"u6m44","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"Five-second scan: the right approach to \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{dx}{x^2-1}\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"partial fractions — the denominator factors as \\((x-1)(x+1)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"the arctan card directly"},{"t":"complete the square"},{"t":"integration by parts"}],"explain":"<b>Check factorability FIRST:</b> \\(x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1)\\) — real factors, so it un-adds: cover-up gives \\(\\tfrac{1/2}{x-1}-\\tfrac{1/2}{x+1}\\), two logs.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>arctan</b> requires \\(u^2+a^2\\) — a SUM. \\(x^2-1\\) is a difference; arctan is structurally impossible here.<br>• <b>completing the square</b> changes nothing (no linear term) and solves nothing — it's the tool for irreducible quadratics, and this one reduces.<br>• <b>parts</b> needs a product of unrelated types; there is no product.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(x^2-1\\Rightarrow\\) PF and logs; \\(x^2+1\\Rightarrow\\) arctan. The sign in the middle picks the entire branch.","unit":6,"id":"u6m45","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{\\ln x}{x^2}\\,dx\\;=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"diverges"},{"t":"\\(\\tfrac12\\)"},{"t":"\\(e\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Two machines composed:</b> parts (\\(u=\\ln x,\\ dv=x^{-2}dx\\)) gives \\(\\bigl[-\\tfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\bigr]_1^b+\\int_1^b x^{-2}dx\\). As \\(b\\to\\infty\\): \\(\\tfrac{\\ln b}b\\to0\\) (the log loses to the power) and the second piece \\(\\to1\\). Total: \\(0+1=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"diverges\"</b> sees \\(\\ln x\\) growing and panics — but it's divided by \\(x^2\\), and \\(\\ln\\) loses every race against powers.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> grabs only part of the boundary bookkeeping.<br>• <b>\\(e\\)</b> is log-flavored decoration.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> improper integrals freely compose with parts — evaluate the boundary term WITH a limit, then finish.","unit":6,"id":"u6m46","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"For which values of \\(p\\) does \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1}\\frac{dx}{x^p}\\) <b>diverge</b>?","opts":[{"t":"\\(p\\ge 1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(p\\le 1\\)"},{"t":"\\(p>1\\) only"},{"t":"all \\(p>0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Near zero the knife edge flips:</b> the spike must be MILD — converges iff \\(p<1\\), so divergence is \\(p\\ge1\\) (and \\(p=1\\) itself diverges: \\(\\ln a\\to-\\infty\\)).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(p\\le1\\)</b> applies the at-infinity rule to the at-zero regime — direction confusion, the unit's signature trap.<br>• <b>\\(p>1\\) only</b> forgets that the knife edge \\(p=1\\) loses too.<br>• <b>all \\(p>0\\)</b> — \\(1/\\sqrt x\\) converges to 2, the lesson's own counterexample.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> anchor on \\(1/x\\): it diverges in BOTH regimes, so the convergent side always points away from it — \\(p>1\\) at \\(\\infty\\), \\(p<1\\) at 0.","unit":6,"id":"u6m47","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Water enters a tank at a rate \\(R(t)\\), in gallons per minute, recorded at selected times. Using a right Riemann sum with the four subintervals shown, what is the approximation of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{10}R(t)\\,dt\\)?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(t\\) (min)</th><th>0</th><th>2</th><th>5</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><th>\\(R(t)\\) (gal/min)</th><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(62\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(59\\)"},{"t":"\\(60.5\\)"},{"t":"\\(26\\)"}],"explain":"<b>A right sum uses the height at the RIGHT end of each strip</b>, and the strips here are not equal — the widths are 2, 3, 4 and 1, so you cannot pull a single \\(\\Delta t\\) out front.<br><br>\\(2(5)+3(8)+4(6)+1(4)=10+24+24+4=62\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>59</b> is the LEFT sum \\(2(3)+3(5)+4(8)+1(6)\\) — it uses the opening height of each strip instead of the closing one. <b>60.5</b> is the trapezoidal sum, which averages the two ends; notice it lands between the left and right sums, exactly as it must. <b>26</b> is the sum of the four right-hand heights \\(5+8+6+4\\) with no widths at all — the error of forgetting that a Riemann sum is heights <i>times</i> widths.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> with a table, the subintervals are whatever the table gives you. Compute each rectangle on its own width and add.","unit":6,"id":"u6m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Using the same table, a trapezoidal sum with the four subintervals gives \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{10}R(t)\\,dt\\approx 60.5\\). What is the approximate <b>average</b> rate at which water entered the tank over the 10 minutes?<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(t\\) (min)</th><th>0</th><th>2</th><th>5</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><th>\\(R(t)\\) (gal/min)</th><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr></table></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(6.05\\) gal/min","correct":true},{"t":"\\(60.5\\) gal/min"},{"t":"\\(5.2\\) gal/min"},{"t":"\\(0.605\\) gal/min"}],"explain":"<b>Average value of a function</b> is \\(\\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\displaystyle\\int_a^b R\\,dt\\) — the accumulated amount spread evenly over the time. So divide the integral by the width \\(10-0\\): \\(\\dfrac{60.5}{10}=6.05\\) gal/min.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>60.5</b> is the integral itself, which is a number of <i>gallons</i>, not a rate — the units alone rule it out. <b>5.2</b> is the plain average of the five table values \\(\\frac{3+5+8+6+4}{5}\\); that treats every reading as equally important, but the readings are unevenly spaced and the one at \\(t=5\\) governs four minutes while the one at \\(t=10\\) governs one. <b>0.605</b> divides by 100.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> averaging a <i>function</i> is not averaging its samples. Integrate, then divide by the width — and let the units check your work.","unit":6,"id":"u6m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","calc":false,"prompt":"The tank in the previous question holds \\(20\\) gallons at \\(t=0\\), and \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{10}R(t)\\,dt\\approx 60.5\\). Approximately how many gallons are in the tank at \\(t=10\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(80.5\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(60.5\\)"},{"t":"\\(40.5\\)"},{"t":"\\(1210\\)"}],"explain":"<b>This is the accumulation (net change) idea:</b> the integral of a rate gives the <i>change</i> in the amount, not the amount itself. Final \\(=\\) initial \\(+\\) change \\(=20+60.5=80.5\\) gallons.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>60.5</b> reports the change and forgets the tank started with something in it — the most common accumulation error and the one AP punishes most often. <b>40.5</b> subtracts the initial amount, which would be right only if water were leaving. <b>1210</b> multiplies \\(20\\times 60.5\\), treating a rate integral as a scaling factor.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(A(b)=A(a)+\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A'(t)\\,dt\\). The integral is always the <i>change</i>; you must be told the starting value to get an amount.","unit":6,"id":"u6m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"The graph of \\(f\\) consists of three line segments, with the areas of the three regions shown. Let \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\). What is \\(g(5)\\)?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of f made of three line segments: a triangle of area 6 above the axis on 0 to 3, a triangle of area 4 below the axis on 3 to 5, and a triangle of area 3 above the axis on 5 to 8\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V154\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 100 L66.25 55 L138.75 100 L175 145 L211.25 100 L265.6 77.5 L320 100\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"56\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 6</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"161\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 4</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"68\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 3</text><text x=\"134\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">3</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">5</text><text x=\"316\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">8</text><text x=\"326\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">x</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(2\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(6\\)"},{"t":"\\(-4\\)"}],"explain":"<b>An accumulation function reads areas as SIGNED.</b> From \\(0\\) to \\(3\\) the curve is above the axis, contributing \\(+6\\); from \\(3\\) to \\(5\\) it is below, contributing \\(-4\\).<br><br>\\(g(5)=6-4=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>10</b> adds the two areas as if both were positive — the single most common figure error, and it ignores that \\(f<0\\) on \\((3,5)\\). <b>6</b> is \\(g(3)\\), stopping one region early. <b>-4</b> uses only the second region and forgets everything accumulated before it.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> area below the axis counts negatively. When a figure hands you areas, your only job is to attach the right signs and add.","unit":6,"id":"u6m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"For the same graph and \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\) on \\([0,8]\\), the absolute maximum of \\(g\\) occurs at \\(x=\\)<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same three-segment graph of f\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V154\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 100 L66.25 55 L138.75 100 L175 145 L211.25 100 L265.6 77.5 L320 100\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"56\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 6</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"161\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 4</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"68\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">area 3</text><text x=\"134\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">3</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">5</text><text x=\"316\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">8</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(8\\)"},{"t":"\\(5\\)"},{"t":"\\(0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(g'=f\\)</b>, so \\(g\\) rises where \\(f\\) is above the axis and falls where \\(f\\) is below it. \\(f>0\\) on \\((0,3)\\), \\(f<0\\) on \\((3,5)\\), \\(f>0\\) on \\((5,8)\\) — so \\(g\\) rises, falls, then rises again, and the candidates are \\(x=3\\) and the endpoint \\(x=8\\).<br><br>Compare them: \\(g(3)=6\\) and \\(g(8)=6-4+3=5\\). The maximum is at \\(x=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>8</b> assumes the last value is the biggest because \\(g\\) is rising at the end — but it never recovers the 4 it lost. <b>5</b> is where \\(g\\) is at its <i>minimum</i>, the bottom of the dip. <b>0</b> is where \\(g=0\\), which is its starting value, not its largest.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a local maximum of \\(g\\) sits where \\(f\\) crosses zero going down. To find the <i>absolute</i> maximum you must still compare that candidate against the endpoints.","unit":6,"id":"u6m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"For the same graph and \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\), on which interval is \\(g\\) decreasing and concave up?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 172\" width=\"340\" height=\"172\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The same three-segment graph of f\"><path d=\"M30 100 H332\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 42 V154\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M30 100 L66.25 55 L138.75 100 L175 145 L211.25 100 L265.6 77.5 L320 100\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">3</text><text x=\"171\" y=\"159\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">4</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">5</text><text x=\"316\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".7\">8</text></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\((4,5)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((3,4)\\)"},{"t":"\\((3,5)\\)"},{"t":"\\((5,8)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Two separate readings of the same picture.</b> \\(g'=f\\), so \\(g\\) decreases exactly where \\(f<0\\) — that is all of \\((3,5)\\). And \\(g''=f'\\), so \\(g\\) is concave up exactly where \\(f\\) is <i>increasing</i>. The dip bottoms out at \\(x=4\\): \\(f\\) falls on \\((3,4)\\) and rises on \\((4,5)\\).<br><br>Both conditions hold together only on \\((4,5)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\((3,4)\\)</b> is decreasing but concave <i>down</i> — \\(f\\) is still falling there. <b>\\((3,5)\\)</b> answers only the \"decreasing\" half and ignores concavity. <b>\\((5,8)\\)</b> is where \\(g\\) is increasing.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when you are given a graph of \\(f\\) and asked about \\(g=\\int f\\), the sign of \\(f\\) controls whether \\(g\\) rises, and the slope of \\(f\\) controls how \\(g\\) bends. Two different features of the same curve.","unit":6,"id":"u6m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous and concave up on \\([a,b]\\). Which comparison must hold?","opts":[{"t":"midpoint sum \\(\\le\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\le\\) trapezoidal sum","correct":true},{"t":"trapezoidal sum \\(\\le\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\le\\) midpoint sum"},{"t":"left sum \\(\\le\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\le\\) right sum"},{"t":"the two sums are equal, because the errors cancel"}],"explain":"<b>Concavity alone settles the midpoint and trapezoid</b> — increasing or decreasing does not enter into it.<br><br>For a concave-up curve each chord lies <i>above</i> the arc, so trapezoids overshoot. The midpoint rectangle undershoots, because the tangent at the midpoint lies below the curve. So midpoint \\(\\le\\) integral \\(\\le\\) trapezoid.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the <b>reversed</b> inequality is the concave-down case. <b>left \\(\\le\\) right</b> is a statement about <i>increasing</i> functions and says nothing here — a concave-up function may be decreasing, in which case the left sum is the larger. <b>equal</b> would need the function to be linear.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> increasing/decreasing tells you about left versus right sums; concavity tells you about midpoint versus trapezoid. Ask which property the question actually gave you.","unit":6,"id":"u6m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f'\\) is continuous on \\([1,4]\\), then \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 f'(x)\\,dx\\) equals","opts":[{"t":"\\(f(4)-f(1)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f'(4)-f'(1)\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(4)+f(1)\\)"},{"t":"the average value of \\(f'\\) on \\([1,4]\\)"}],"explain":"<b>This is the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, part two</b>: integrating a rate of change over an interval returns the net change in the original quantity, \\(\\int_a^b f'=f(b)-f(a)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(f'(4)-f'(1)\\)</b> differentiates when it should antidifferentiate — it evaluates the integrand at the limits instead of its antiderivative. <b>\\(f(4)+f(1)\\)</b> adds where the theorem subtracts. <b>average value</b> is this integral <i>divided by</i> the width \\(3\\), a different quantity.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the integral of a rate is a total change. Every \"how much did it change\" question on the exam is this identity wearing a costume.","unit":6,"id":"u6m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"Which integral <b>cannot</b> be evaluated by a single \\(u\\)-substitution?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\cos x\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\cos(x^2)\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{\\ln x}{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{2x}{x^2+1}\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Substitution works when the integrand contains an inner function AND (a constant multiple of) its derivative.</b> Scan each one for that pairing.<br><br>\\(x\\cos(x^2)\\): inner \\(x^2\\), derivative \\(2x\\) — present. \\(\\frac{\\ln x}{x}\\): inner \\(\\ln x\\), derivative \\(\\frac1x\\) — present. \\(\\frac{2x}{x^2+1}\\): inner \\(x^2+1\\), derivative \\(2x\\) — present.<br><br>\\(x\\cos x\\) has no inner function at all; the \\(x\\) is a separate factor multiplying \\(\\cos x\\). That is a product of two unrelated pieces, which is the signature of integration <i>by parts</i> \\((\\to x\\sin x+\\cos x+C)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> each of the other three looks superficially like \\(x\\cos x\\) — a product or a quotient — but in each the extra factor <i>is</i> the inner derivative. Being fooled by surface shape rather than checking for that pairing is the whole trap.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> before choosing a technique, ask \"is one factor the derivative of something inside another?\" If yes, substitute. If it is just two unrelated factors multiplied, reach for parts.","unit":6,"id":"u6m56","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"improper","calc":false,"prompt":"For which value(s) of \\(p\\) does \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{x^{p}}\\) converge?","opts":[{"t":"\\(p>1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(p\\ge 1\\)"},{"t":"\\(p<1\\)"},{"t":"all \\(p>0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Antidifferentiate and take the limit.</b> For \\(p\\ne1\\), \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^{b}x^{-p}dx=\\frac{b^{1-p}-1}{1-p}\\). As \\(b\\to\\infty\\) the term \\(b^{1-p}\\) dies only when \\(1-p<0\\), i.e. \\(p>1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(p\\ge1\\)</b> wrongly includes \\(p=1\\), the borderline case: there the antiderivative is \\(\\ln x\\), and \\(\\ln b\\to\\infty\\), so it diverges. That single endpoint is the entire content of the question. <b>\\(p<1\\)</b> is the condition for the <i>other</i> improper integral, \\(\\int_0^1 x^{-p}dx\\) — same family, opposite end, opposite answer. <b>all \\(p>0\\)</b> ignores the tail behavior completely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(p=1\\) is the dividing line, and it belongs to the divergent side. Near infinity, \\(1/x\\) decays too slowly; below it, decay is fast enough.","unit":6,"id":"u6m57","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"riemann","calc":false,"prompt":"Which expression is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^{6}f(x)\\,dx\\) written as the limit of a right Riemann sum with \\(n\\) equal subintervals?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}f\\!\\left(2+\\frac{4k}{n}\\right)\\frac{4}{n}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}f\\!\\left(2+\\frac{4k}{n}\\right)\\frac{1}{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\!\\left(2+\\frac{6k}{n}\\right)\\frac{6}{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}f\\!\\left(\\frac{4k}{n}\\right)\\frac{4}{n}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Build it piece by piece.</b> The width is \\(\\Delta x=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}\\). The \\(k\\)th right endpoint is \\(x_k=a+k\\Delta x=2+\\frac{4k}{n}\\). A right sum runs \\(k=1\\) to \\(n\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second uses \\(\\frac1n\\) as the width — right endpoints, wrong \\(\\Delta x\\), which quietly integrates over an interval of length 1. The third uses \\(b=6\\) as if it were the <i>width</i> rather than the endpoint, and indexes from \\(k=0\\) (a left sum). The fourth drops the \\(+2\\), which shifts the whole interval to \\([0,4]\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> three things must agree — the width \\(\\frac{b-a}{n}\\), the starting point \\(a\\) inside \\(f\\), and the index range that decides left versus right. Check them one at a time rather than pattern-matching the whole formula.","unit":6,"id":"u6m58","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ftc","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^{2}}f(t)\\,dt\\), where \\(f\\) is continuous. Then \\(g'(x)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(2x\\,f\\!\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(f\\!\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\)"},{"t":"\\(2x\\,f(x)\\)"},{"t":"\\(f'\\!\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\cdot 2x\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The upper limit is a function of \\(x\\), so the chain rule joins the Fundamental Theorem.</b> If \\(g(x)=\\int_0^{u}f\\,dt\\) with \\(u=x^2\\), then \\(g'=f(u)\\cdot\\frac{du}{dx}=f(x^2)\\cdot 2x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(f(x^2)\\)</b> applies the Fundamental Theorem but forgets the inner derivative — the single most common version of this question. <b>\\(2x f(x)\\)</b> multiplies by the right factor but evaluates \\(f\\) at \\(x\\) instead of at the actual upper limit \\(x^2\\). <b>\\(f'(x^2)\\cdot2x\\)</b> differentiates \\(f\\), but the theorem hands you \\(f\\) itself — differentiating an accumulation undoes the integral, it does not differentiate the integrand.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate \\(f\\) at whatever sits in the upper limit, then multiply by that limit's derivative.","unit":6,"id":"u6m59","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"antider","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(F\\) is an antiderivative of \\(f\\) and \\(F(2)=7\\) while \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^{6}f(x)\\,dx=-3\\), then \\(F(6)=\\)","opts":[{"t":"\\(4\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(10\\)"},{"t":"\\(-3\\)"},{"t":"\\(-21\\)"}],"explain":"<b>By the Fundamental Theorem,</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^{6}f=F(6)-F(2)\\). So \\(-3=F(6)-7\\), giving \\(F(6)=4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>10</b> adds instead of subtracting, \\(7+3\\) — the sign of the integral is negative, so the function must have gone <i>down</i>. <b>-3</b> reports the change and ignores the starting value. <b>-21</b> multiplies \\(7\\times(-3)\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a definite integral is a difference of antiderivative values. Given any two of the three numbers, solve for the third — and let the sign tell you which direction the quantity moved.","unit":6,"id":"u6m60","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"parts","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\,e^{2x}\\,dx\\) by parts, which choice of \\(u\\) and \\(dv\\) makes progress?","opts":[{"t":"\\(u=x,\\;dv=e^{2x}dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(u=e^{2x},\\;dv=x\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(u=xe^{2x},\\;dv=dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(u=1,\\;dv=xe^{2x}dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Integration by parts trades \\(\\int u\\,dv\\) for \\(uv-\\int v\\,du\\)</b>, so the trade is only worth making if the new integral is <i>simpler</i>. Differentiating \\(x\\) gives \\(1\\), which removes the polynomial entirely; integrating \\(e^{2x}\\) costs nothing but a \\(\\tfrac12\\). Result: \\(\\tfrac12xe^{2x}-\\tfrac12\\int e^{2x}dx\\) — done.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(u=e^{2x},dv=x\\,dx\\)</b> is the reverse trade: it turns \\(x\\) into \\(\\frac{x^2}{2}\\) and leaves the exponential alone, so the new integral is <i>worse</i>. <b>\\(u=xe^{2x}\\)</b> makes \\(du\\) messier than the original integrand. <b>\\(u=1\\)</b> gives \\(du=0\\) and asks you to integrate the whole thing as \\(v\\) — which is the problem you started with.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> pick as \\(u\\) the factor that gets <i>simpler</i> when differentiated. For polynomial × exponential or trig, that is always the polynomial.","unit":6,"id":"u6m61","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 240' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='18' y1='120' x2='282' y2='120'/><line x1='150' y1='18' x2='150' y2='222'/></g><g stroke='#aab3bd' stroke-width='1.7' stroke-linecap='round'><line x1='16.84' y1='199.34' x2='28.16' y2='210.66'/><line x1='18.92' y1='155.34' x2='26.08' y2='169.66'/><line x1='19.97' y1='112.41' x2='25.03' y2='127.59'/><line x1='20.56' y1='69.74' x2='24.44' y2='85.26'/><line x1='20.93' y1='27.16' x2='24.07' y2='42.84'/><line x1='57' y1='205' x2='73' y2='205'/><line x1='59.34' y1='156.84' x2='70.66' y2='168.16'/><line x1='61.42' y1='112.84' x2='68.58' y2='127.16'/><line x1='62.47' y1='69.91' x2='67.53' y2='85.09'/><line x1='63.06' y1='27.24' x2='66.94' y2='42.76'/><line x1='101.84' y1='210.66' x2='113.16' y2='199.34'/><line x1='99.5' y1='162.5' x2='115.5' y2='162.5'/><line x1='101.84' y1='114.34' x2='113.16' y2='125.66'/><line x1='103.92' y1='70.34' x2='111.08' y2='84.66'/><line x1='104.97' y1='27.41' x2='110.03' y2='42.59'/><line x1='146.42' y1='212.16' x2='153.58' y2='197.84'/><line x1='144.34' y1='168.16' x2='155.66' y2='156.84'/><line x1='142' y1='120' x2='158' y2='120'/><line x1='144.34' y1='71.84' x2='155.66' y2='83.16'/><line x1='146.42' y1='27.84' x2='153.58' y2='42.16'/><line x1='189.97' y1='212.59' x2='195.03' y2='197.41'/><line x1='188.92' y1='169.66' x2='196.08' y2='155.34'/><line x1='186.84' y1='125.66' x2='198.16' y2='114.34'/><line x1='184.5' y1='77.5' x2='200.5' y2='77.5'/><line x1='186.84' y1='29.34' x2='198.16' y2='40.66'/><line x1='233.06' y1='212.76' x2='236.94' y2='197.24'/><line x1='232.47' y1='170.09' x2='237.53' y2='154.91'/><line x1='231.42' y1='127.16' x2='238.58' y2='112.84'/><line x1='229.34' y1='83.16' x2='240.66' y2='71.84'/><line x1='227' y1='35' x2='243' y2='35'/><line x1='275.93' y1='212.84' x2='279.07' y2='197.16'/><line x1='275.56' y1='170.26' x2='279.44' y2='154.74'/><line x1='274.97' y1='127.59' x2='280.03' y2='112.41'/><line x1='273.92' y1='84.66' x2='281.08' y2='70.34'/><line x1='271.84' y1='40.66' x2='283.16' y2='29.34'/></g></svg></div>The slope field above is for \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y\\). As \\(x\\) increases, the solution curves appear to approach which line?","opts":[{"t":"y=x-1","correct":true},{"t":"y=x"},{"t":"y=x+1"},{"t":"y=0"}],"explain":"<b>A line is an attracting solution when its own slope matches the field along it.</b> Test \\(y=x-1\\): its slope is \\(1\\), and the equation gives \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-(x-1)=1\\). They agree, so \\(y=x-1\\) is itself a solution, and nearby curves funnel toward it.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=x\\)</b> looks natural but along it the field gives slope \\(x-x=0\\), not the line's slope \\(1\\) — curves cross it, not hug it.<br>• <b>\\(y=x+1\\)</b> gives field slope \\(x-(x+1)=-1\\), again not \\(1\\).<br>• <b>\\(y=0\\)</b> gives slope \\(x\\), which changes — not a straight-line solution.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(y'=x-y\\) the line \\(y=x-1\\) satisfies the equation and is a slant asymptote that all solutions approach.","unit":7,"id":"u7m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(y=f(x)\\) satisfies \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y\\), and \\(f(2)=3\\). What is \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\) at the point \\((2,3)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"-1"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the differential equation itself.</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x-y)=1-\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-(x-y)\\). At \\((2,3)\\): \\(1-(2-3)=1-(-1)=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(-1\\)</b> is just \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y\\) at \\((2,3)\\) — the <i>first</i> derivative, forgetting to differentiate again.<br>• <b>\\(1\\)</b> differentiates the \\(x\\) to \\(1\\) but drops the \\(-\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) term.<br>• <b>\\(0\\)</b> treats \\(x-y\\) as constant.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> to get \\(y''\\) from a first-order equation, differentiate both sides with respect to \\(x\\) and substitute \\(y'\\) back in — the answer depends on the point.","unit":7,"id":"u7m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"A solution curve of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y\\) has a horizontal tangent at some point. At that point the curve has a:","opts":[{"t":"relative minimum","correct":true},{"t":"relative maximum"},{"t":"point of inflection"},{"t":"vertical tangent"}],"explain":"<b>Classify with the second derivative.</b> A horizontal tangent means \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y=0\\), so \\(x=y\\) there. From \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=1-(x-y)\\), at such a point \\(x-y=0\\) gives \\(y''=1>0\\) — concave up, so a relative <b>minimum</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Maximum</b> would need \\(y''<0\\), but here \\(y''=1>0\\) at every flat point.<br>• <b>Inflection</b> would need \\(y''=0\\).<br>• <b>Vertical tangent</b> contradicts \"horizontal tangent.\"<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate \\(y''\\) at the critical point; since \\(y''=1>0\\) wherever \\(y'=0\\), every horizontal tangent of this equation is a minimum.","unit":7,"id":"u7m2","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 240' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='18' y1='120' x2='282' y2='120'/><line x1='150' y1='18' x2='150' y2='222'/></g><g stroke='#aab3bd' stroke-width='1.7' stroke-linecap='round'><line x1='19.97' y1='197.41' x2='25.03' y2='212.59'/><line x1='19.97' y1='154.91' x2='25.03' y2='170.09'/><line x1='19.97' y1='112.41' x2='25.03' y2='127.59'/><line x1='19.97' y1='69.91' x2='25.03' y2='85.09'/><line x1='19.97' y1='27.41' x2='25.03' y2='42.59'/><line x1='61.42' y1='197.84' x2='68.58' y2='212.16'/><line x1='61.42' y1='155.34' x2='68.58' y2='169.66'/><line x1='61.42' y1='112.84' x2='68.58' y2='127.16'/><line x1='61.42' y1='70.34' x2='68.58' y2='84.66'/><line x1='61.42' y1='27.84' x2='68.58' y2='42.16'/><line x1='101.84' y1='199.34' x2='113.16' y2='210.66'/><line x1='101.84' y1='156.84' x2='113.16' y2='168.16'/><line x1='101.84' y1='114.34' x2='113.16' y2='125.66'/><line x1='101.84' y1='71.84' x2='113.16' y2='83.16'/><line x1='101.84' y1='29.34' x2='113.16' y2='40.66'/><line x1='142' y1='205' x2='158' y2='205'/><line x1='142' y1='162.5' x2='158' y2='162.5'/><line x1='142' y1='120' x2='158' y2='120'/><line x1='142' y1='77.5' x2='158' y2='77.5'/><line x1='142' y1='35' x2='158' y2='35'/><line x1='186.84' y1='210.66' x2='198.16' y2='199.34'/><line x1='186.84' y1='168.16' x2='198.16' y2='156.84'/><line x1='186.84' y1='125.66' x2='198.16' y2='114.34'/><line x1='186.84' y1='83.16' x2='198.16' y2='71.84'/><line x1='186.84' y1='40.66' x2='198.16' y2='29.34'/><line x1='231.42' y1='212.16' x2='238.58' y2='197.84'/><line x1='231.42' y1='169.66' x2='238.58' y2='155.34'/><line x1='231.42' y1='127.16' x2='238.58' y2='112.84'/><line x1='231.42' y1='84.66' x2='238.58' y2='70.34'/><line x1='231.42' y1='42.16' x2='238.58' y2='27.84'/><line x1='274.97' y1='212.59' x2='280.03' y2='197.41'/><line x1='274.97' y1='170.09' x2='280.03' y2='154.91'/><line x1='274.97' y1='127.59' x2='280.03' y2='112.41'/><line x1='274.97' y1='85.09' x2='280.03' y2='69.91'/><line x1='274.97' y1='42.59' x2='280.03' y2='27.41'/></g></svg></div>The slope field above has segments that are identical within each vertical column. Which differential equation could produce it?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2"}],"explain":"<b>Read what the field depends on.</b> Within a vertical column \\(x\\) is fixed and \\(y\\) varies; the segments don't change, so the slope depends on \\(x\\) alone — and it is negative for \\(x<0\\), zero at \\(x=0\\), positive for \\(x>0\\). That is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y\\)</b> would be identical along <i>horizontal</i> rows, not columns.<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\)</b> changes as you move up a column.<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2\\)</b> would make every segment the same everywhere.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> identical along verticals ⇔ slope depends only on \\(x\\); the sign pattern (down–flat–up) pins it to \\(x\\) rather than, say, \\(x^2\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m3","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"For what value of \\(n\\) is \\(y=x^{n}\\) a solution of \\(x\\,\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=3y\\)?","opts":[{"t":"3","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{3}"},{"t":"-3"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>Substitute and match powers.</b> \\(y=x^n\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{dy}{dx}=nx^{n-1}\\), so \\(x\\,\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x\\cdot nx^{n-1}=nx^{n}\\). Setting this equal to \\(3y=3x^{n}\\) gives \\(n=3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> inverts the relationship (solving \\(3n=1\\)).<br>• <b>\\(-3\\)</b> mishandles the sign when matching coefficients.<br>• <b>\\(1\\)</b> gives \\(x\\cdot 1=x\\), but \\(3y=3x\\) — not equal.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for power-function solutions, substitute \\(x^n\\), simplify, and equate the coefficient of \\(x^n\\) on each side.","unit":7,"id":"u7m4","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"A differential equation has \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=5\\) as its only constant (equilibrium) solutions. Which could it be?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(y-2)(y-5)","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(x-2)(x-5)"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(y-2)(y+5)"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y^2-7y"}],"explain":"<b>Equilibrium (constant) solutions are the \\(y\\)-values that make \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0\\).</b> \\((y-2)(y-5)=0\\) exactly when \\(y=2\\) or \\(y=5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\((x-2)(x-5)\\)</b> depends on \\(x\\), so it has no constant-\\(y\\) solutions at all.<br>• <b>\\((y-2)(y+5)\\)</b> has equilibria at \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=-5\\).<br>• <b>\\(y^2-7y=y(y-7)\\)</b> has equilibria at \\(y=0\\) and \\(y=7\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for an autonomous equation, the equilibria are the roots of the right-hand side as a function of \\(y\\); build the equation by placing those roots as factors \\((y-a)(y-b)\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 240' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='18' y1='120' x2='282' y2='120'/><line x1='150' y1='18' x2='150' y2='222'/></g><g stroke='#aab3bd' stroke-width='1.7' stroke-linecap='round'><line x1='15.84' y1='209.44' x2='29.16' y2='200.56'/><line x1='14.91' y1='165.03' x2='30.09' y2='159.97'/><line x1='14.5' y1='120' x2='30.5' y2='120'/><line x1='14.91' y1='74.97' x2='30.09' y2='80.03'/><line x1='15.84' y1='30.56' x2='29.16' y2='39.44'/><line x1='59.34' y1='210.66' x2='70.66' y2='199.34'/><line x1='57.84' y1='166.08' x2='72.16' y2='158.92'/><line x1='57' y1='120' x2='73' y2='120'/><line x1='57.84' y1='73.92' x2='72.16' y2='81.08'/><line x1='59.34' y1='29.34' x2='70.66' y2='40.66'/><line x1='103.92' y1='212.16' x2='111.08' y2='197.84'/><line x1='101.84' y1='168.16' x2='113.16' y2='156.84'/><line x1='99.5' y1='120' x2='115.5' y2='120'/><line x1='101.84' y1='71.84' x2='113.16' y2='83.16'/><line x1='103.92' y1='27.84' x2='111.08' y2='42.16'/><line x1='150' y1='197' x2='150' y2='213'/><line x1='150' y1='154.5' x2='150' y2='170.5'/><line x1='150' y1='112' x2='150' y2='128'/><line x1='150' y1='69.5' x2='150' y2='85.5'/><line x1='150' y1='27' x2='150' y2='43'/><line x1='188.92' y1='197.84' x2='196.08' y2='212.16'/><line x1='186.84' y1='156.84' x2='198.16' y2='168.16'/><line x1='184.5' y1='120' x2='200.5' y2='120'/><line x1='186.84' y1='83.16' x2='198.16' y2='71.84'/><line x1='188.92' y1='42.16' x2='196.08' y2='27.84'/><line x1='229.34' y1='199.34' x2='240.66' y2='210.66'/><line x1='227.84' y1='158.92' x2='242.16' y2='166.08'/><line x1='227' y1='120' x2='243' y2='120'/><line x1='227.84' y1='81.08' x2='242.16' y2='73.92'/><line x1='229.34' y1='40.66' x2='240.66' y2='29.34'/><line x1='270.84' y1='200.56' x2='284.16' y2='209.44'/><line x1='269.91' y1='159.97' x2='285.09' y2='165.03'/><line x1='269.5' y1='120' x2='285.5' y2='120'/><line x1='269.91' y1='80.03' x2='285.09' y2='74.97'/><line x1='270.84' y1='39.44' x2='284.16' y2='30.56'/></g></svg></div>The slope field above belongs to a differential equation whose solution curves all pass through the origin as straight lines. Which of the following is one of its solution curves?","opts":[{"t":"y=2x","correct":true},{"t":"y=x^2"},{"t":"y=\\dfrac{1}{x}"},{"t":"x^2+y^2=4"}],"explain":"<b>The field is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y}{x}\\): each segment points along the ray from the origin.</b> Its solutions are the lines \\(y=Cx\\). Check \\(y=2x\\): \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2\\) and \\(\\dfrac{y}{x}=\\dfrac{2x}{x}=2\\) ✓.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=x^2\\)</b> gives \\(y'=2x\\) but \\(\\dfrac{y}{x}=x\\) — equal only at isolated points, not a solution.<br>• <b>\\(y=\\tfrac1x\\)</b> gives \\(y'=-\\tfrac1{x^2}\\), while \\(\\dfrac{y}{x}=\\tfrac1{x^2}\\) — opposite sign.<br>• <b>\\(x^2+y^2=4\\)</b> solves \\(y'=-x/y\\), a different field (circles).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a radial slope field \\(y'=y/x\\) has straight-line solutions through the origin; verify by checking \\(y'=y/x\\) on the candidate.","unit":7,"id":"u7m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"Which function is a solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{y}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"y=\\sqrt{x^2+1}","correct":true},{"t":"y=\\sqrt{x^2-1}"},{"t":"y=x^2+1"},{"t":"y=\\sqrt{2x^2+1}"}],"explain":"<b>Substitute and use implicit differentiation.</b> For \\(y=\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\), \\(y^2=x^2+1\\Rightarrow 2y\\,y'=2x\\Rightarrow y'=\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) ✓.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\)</b> gives \\(y^2=x^2-1\\), so \\(y'=x/y\\) <i>is</i> satisfied — but it isn't defined near \\(x=0\\); for a single valid solution the \\(+1\\) form is the intended one. (Both satisfy the ODE; the well-defined-at-\\(x=0\\) curve is \\(\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\).)<br>• <b>\\(x^2+1\\)</b> gives \\(y'=2x\\), but \\(\\dfrac{x}{y}=\\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\) — not equal.<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{2x^2+1}\\)</b> gives \\(2y\\,y'=4x\\Rightarrow y'=\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\), off by a factor of \\(2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> separating \\(y'=x/y\\) gives \\(y^2-x^2=C\\); the choice with \\(C=1\\) that is real and smooth through \\(x=0\\) is \\(\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m7","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"In a slope field, every segment along the line \\(y=2\\) is horizontal; segments below \\(y=2\\) point downward and segments above point upward. Which differential equation is consistent?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y-2","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(y-2)^2"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2-y"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-2"}],"explain":"<b>Match the sign behavior.</b> \\(y-2\\) is \\(0\\) on \\(y=2\\), negative below (downward) and positive above (upward) — exactly as described.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\((y-2)^2\\)</b> is zero on \\(y=2\\) but \\(\\ge 0\\) everywhere, so segments never point downward.<br>• <b>\\(2-y\\)</b> reverses it — positive below, negative above.<br>• <b>\\(x-2\\)</b> depends on \\(x\\), so segments are not flat all along \\(y=2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a horizontal line of segments marks where the right-hand side is zero; the sign on either side tells you whether it's \\(y-2\\) (changes sign) or \\((y-2)^2\\) (doesn't).","unit":7,"id":"u7m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"The function \\(y=f(x)\\) satisfies \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y^2-x\\) with \\(f(1)=2\\). What is \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\) at \\((1,2)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"11","correct":true},{"t":"3"},{"t":"12"},{"t":"5"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate the equation (product/chain rule on \\(y^2\\)).</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(y^2-x)=2y\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1\\). At \\((1,2)\\), first \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2^2-1=3\\); then \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=2(2)(3)-1=12-1=11\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3</b> is just \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) at the point.<br>• <b>12</b> forgets the \\(-1\\) from differentiating \\(-x\\).<br>• <b>5</b> uses \\(2y-1\\) without multiplying by \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiating \\(y^2\\) needs the chain rule \\(2y\\,y'\\); compute \\(y'\\) at the point first, then assemble \\(y''\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"Solution curves of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x^2-1}{y}\\) have horizontal tangents along which set?","opts":[{"t":"the lines x=1 and x=-1","correct":true},{"t":"the line y=0"},{"t":"the line x=0"},{"t":"the origin only"}],"explain":"<b>Horizontal tangents occur where \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0\\).</b> The fraction is zero when its numerator is zero (and \\(y\\neq 0\\)): \\(x^2-1=0\\Rightarrow x=\\pm 1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=0\\)</b> makes the slope <i>undefined</i> (division by zero) — vertical, not horizontal.<br>• <b>\\(x=0\\)</b> gives slope \\(\\dfrac{-1}{y}\\neq 0\\).<br>• <b>the origin only</b> is far too restrictive.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{N(x,y)}{D(x,y)}\\), horizontal tangents are where \\(N=0\\) (with \\(D\\neq 0\\)); vertical tangents are where \\(D=0\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"For the autonomous equation \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y(4-y)\\), a solution with \\(0<y(0)<4\\) is increasing for which \\(y\\)-values?","opts":[{"t":"0 < y < 4","correct":true},{"t":"y > 4"},{"t":"y < 0"},{"t":"y > 2"}],"explain":"<b>The solution increases where \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}>0\\) — analyze the sign of the product.</b> \\(y(4-y)>0\\) needs both factors positive: \\(y>0\\) and \\(4-y>0\\), i.e. \\(0<y<4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y>4\\)</b> makes \\(4-y<0\\), so the product is negative — decreasing.<br>• <b>\\(y<0\\)</b> makes \\(y<0\\) with \\(4-y>0\\) — again negative.<br>• <b>\\(y>2\\)</b> is only partly right: between \\(2\\) and \\(4\\) it increases, but for \\(y>4\\) it decreases, so \"all \\(y>2\\)\" is false.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for an autonomous \\(y'=g(y)\\), sign-analyze \\(g(y)\\): the solution rises where \\(g>0\\), falls where \\(g<0\\), and is constant at the roots \\(y=0,4\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":true,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\), use Euler's method with <b>four</b> steps of size \\(h=0.25\\) to approximate \\(y(1)\\).","opts":[{"t":"2.883","correct":true},{"t":"2.500"},{"t":"3.437"},{"t":"2.156"}],"explain":"<b>Run \\(y_{n+1}=y_n+0.25\\,(x_n+y_n)\\) four times.</b> \\((0,1)\\to 1.25\\); \\((0.25,1.25)\\to 1.625\\); \\((0.5,1.625)\\to 2.15625\\); \\((0.75,2.15625)\\to y(1)\\approx 2.883\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.500</b> is the coarse two-step \\((h=0.5)\\) result, farther below the truth.<br>• <b>3.437</b> is the <i>exact</i> value \\(2e-2\\), which Euler only approaches as \\(h\\to 0\\).<br>• <b>2.156</b> stops one step early (at \\(x=0.75\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> recompute the slope at each new point; smaller \\(h\\) gives more steps and a closer (still low) estimate, sitting between the two-step value and the exact value.","unit":7,"id":"u7m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(y=f(x)\\) satisfy \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^2+y^2\\) with \\(f(1)=0\\). The tangent line to \\(f\\) at \\(x=1\\) gives the approximation \\(f(1.1)\\approx 0.1\\). This approximation is:","opts":[{"t":"an underestimate, since the solution is concave up","correct":true},{"t":"an overestimate, since the solution is concave down"},{"t":"an underestimate, since f is increasing"},{"t":"impossible to determine"}],"explain":"<b>Get concavity from the equation.</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=2x+2y\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\). At \\((1,0)\\): \\(2(1)+2(0)(\\,\\cdot\\,)=2>0\\), so the solution is concave up, its tangent lies below the curve, and the estimate is too low — an underestimate.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"overestimate, concave down\"</b> has the concavity backward (here \\(y''>0\\)).<br>• <b>\"underestimate, since f is increasing\"</b> reaches the right verdict for the wrong reason — increasing vs. decreasing doesn't set the error's sign; concavity does.<br>• <b>\"impossible\"</b> ignores that \\(y''\\) is computable from the equation.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the tangent-line (Euler) error direction comes from concavity, which you obtain by differentiating the differential equation — not from whether the function is rising.","unit":7,"id":"u7m13","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":true,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\), a two-step Euler approximation with \\(h=0.5\\) gives \\(y(1)\\approx 2.25\\). By approximately how much does this differ from the exact value?","opts":[{"t":"0.47","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"0.25"},{"t":"0.97"}],"explain":"<b>Compare to the true solution \\(y=e^x\\).</b> The exact \\(y(1)=e\\approx 2.718\\), and \\(2.718-2.25\\approx 0.47\\). (Euler underestimates because \\(e^x\\) is concave up.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> assumes Euler is exact — it isn't, except for straight-line solutions.<br>• <b>0.25</b> compares against \\(2.5\\) (treating the exact value as \\(\\approx 2.5\\)).<br>• <b>0.97</b> doubles the error or mis-reads \\(e\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the Euler error is the gap to the true value \\(e\\); for a concave-up solution that gap is positive and grows with \\(h\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":true,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\sqrt{x+y}\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\), use Euler's method with two steps of size \\(h=0.5\\) to approximate \\(y(1)\\).","opts":[{"t":"2.207","correct":true},{"t":"2.000"},{"t":"2.414"},{"t":"1.707"}],"explain":"<b>Step with the slope \\(\\sqrt{x+y}\\).</b> \\((0,1)\\): slope \\(\\sqrt{1}=1\\), so \\(y(0.5)=1+0.5(1)=1.5\\). \\((0.5,1.5)\\): slope \\(\\sqrt{2}\\approx 1.414\\), so \\(y(1)=1.5+0.5(1.414)\\approx 2.207\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.000</b> reuses the first slope \\(1\\) for both steps.<br>• <b>2.414</b> uses \\(\\sqrt2\\) for <i>both</i> steps.<br>• <b>1.707</b> takes only one step then adds a half-step of the first slope.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate the square-root slope freshly at each point; the second slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) differs from the first, so the increments differ.","unit":7,"id":"u7m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(y=f(x)\\) satisfy \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\cos x+y\\) with \\(f(0)=1\\). Using the tangent line at \\(x=0\\), the approximation of \\(f(0.2)\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"1.4","correct":true},{"t":"1.2"},{"t":"1.0"},{"t":"1.6"}],"explain":"<b>Tangent-line (local linearization) approximation:</b> \\(f(0.2)\\approx f(0)+f'(0)(0.2)\\). Here \\(f'(0)=\\cos 0+f(0)=1+1=2\\), so \\(f(0.2)\\approx 1+2(0.2)=1.4\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1.2</b> uses slope \\(1\\) (only \\(\\cos 0\\), forgetting the \\(+y\\)).<br>• <b>1.0</b> uses slope \\(0\\) or omits the increment.<br>• <b>1.6</b> uses slope \\(3\\) (adding an extra term).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> local linearization is one Euler step — evaluate the full right-hand side at the start to get the slope, then add \\(h\\) times that slope.","unit":7,"id":"u7m16","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"If the solution of a differential equation is concave down on the interval used, then an Euler's-method approximation of a later value is:","opts":[{"t":"an overestimate","correct":true},{"t":"an underestimate"},{"t":"exact"},{"t":"sometimes high, sometimes low, with no pattern"}],"explain":"<b>Concave down means each tangent line lies above the curve,</b> and Euler steps along tangents — so every step overshoots and the result is an overestimate.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Underestimate</b> is the concave-<i>up</i> case.<br>• <b>Exact</b> needs a straight-line solution.<br>• <b>\"no pattern\"</b> ignores that consistent concavity gives a consistent error direction.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> concavity fixes the sign of the Euler error: down ⇒ over, up ⇒ under.","unit":7,"id":"u7m17","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y^2\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\), Euler's method with two steps of size \\(h=0.5\\) gives \\(y(1)\\approx\\):","opts":[{"t":"0.625","correct":true},{"t":"0.5"},{"t":"0.75"},{"t":"1.0"}],"explain":"<b>Re-evaluate \\(x-y^2\\) each step.</b> \\((0,1)\\): slope \\(0-1=-1\\), so \\(y(0.5)=1+0.5(-1)=0.5\\). \\((0.5,0.5)\\): slope \\(0.5-0.25=0.25\\), so \\(y(1)=0.5+0.5(0.25)=0.625\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0.5</b> stops after one step.<br>• <b>0.75</b> mis-signs the second slope (using \\(+0.5\\)).<br>• <b>1.0</b> keeps \\(y\\) fixed or uses slope \\(0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the slope flips from negative to positive between the two points, so the second step <i>adds</i> a little after the first subtracts — you must recompute it.","unit":7,"id":"u7m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"For Euler's method, the size of the approximation error is, to leading order, proportional to:","opts":[{"t":"h","correct":true},{"t":"h^2"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{h}"},{"t":"h^3"}],"explain":"<b>Euler's method is first order: its total (global) error is proportional to the step size \\(h\\).</b> Halve \\(h\\) and you roughly halve the error.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(h^2\\)</b> would be a second-order method (e.g. improved Euler / RK2).<br>• <b>\\(1/h\\)</b> wrongly says smaller steps are <i>worse</i>.<br>• <b>\\(h^3\\)</b> is higher-order still (e.g. RK methods).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> Euler trades accuracy for simplicity — error \\(\\propto h\\), so accuracy improves only linearly as you shrink the step.","unit":7,"id":"u7m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\) with \\(y(0)=2\\), Euler's method with two steps of size \\(h=0.1\\) approximates \\(y(0.2)\\) as:","opts":[{"t":"2.43","correct":true},{"t":"2.40"},{"t":"2.20"},{"t":"2.46"}],"explain":"<b>Two small steps.</b> \\((0,2)\\): slope \\(0+2=2\\), so \\(y(0.1)=2+0.1(2)=2.2\\). \\((0.1,2.2)\\): slope \\(0.1+2.2=2.3\\), so \\(y(0.2)=2.2+0.1(2.3)=2.43\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.40</b> reuses the first slope \\(2\\) for the second step.<br>• <b>2.20</b> stops after one step.<br>• <b>2.46</b> uses slope \\(2.3\\) for both steps.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> each step updates both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), so the slope grows slightly from \\(2\\) to \\(2.3\\); carry the new \\(y\\) forward.","unit":7,"id":"u7m20","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"Euler's method approximates the solution of a differential equation by:","opts":[{"t":"following a sequence of short tangent-line segments","correct":true},{"t":"computing the exact antiderivative"},{"t":"averaging the slopes at the two ends of each step"},{"t":"fitting a parabola through successive points"}],"explain":"<b>Euler walks along tangent lines.</b> At each point it uses the slope \\(f(x,y)\\) to take a straight step of length \\(h\\), producing a polygonal path that approximates the curve.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Exact antiderivative</b> is what Euler avoids — it's for equations you can't integrate.<br>• <b>Averaging endpoint slopes</b> describes improved Euler (Heun), a different method.<br>• <b>Fitting a parabola</b> is a higher-order scheme.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> Euler = repeated local linearization, one tangent step at a time.","unit":7,"id":"u7m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"The solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=e^{x}\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\) is concave up everywhere. A two-step Euler approximation of \\(y(2)\\) will be:","opts":[{"t":"less than the true value","correct":true},{"t":"greater than the true value"},{"t":"equal to the true value"},{"t":"undefined"}],"explain":"<b>Concave up forces Euler low.</b> Since \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=e^x>0\\) everywhere, the solution \\(y=e^x\\) is concave up, its tangents lie below it, and the Euler steps underestimate — the result is <b>less than</b> the true value.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Greater</b> would require concave down.<br>• <b>Equal</b> requires a linear solution.<br>• <b>Undefined</b> — Euler always produces a number.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> you don't need to solve the equation to judge the error — \\(e^x>0\\) tells you it's concave up, hence an underestimate.","unit":7,"id":"u7m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"For \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2x-y\\) with \\(y(0)=4\\), Euler's method with two steps of size \\(h=0.5\\) approximates \\(y(1)\\) as:","opts":[{"t":"1.5","correct":true},{"t":"2.0"},{"t":"1.0"},{"t":"2.5"}],"explain":"<b>Step with slope \\(2x-y\\).</b> \\((0,4)\\): slope \\(0-4=-4\\), so \\(y(0.5)=4+0.5(-4)=2\\). \\((0.5,2)\\): slope \\(1-2=-1\\), so \\(y(1)=2+0.5(-1)=1.5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.0</b> stops after the first step.<br>• <b>1.0</b> reuses slope \\(-2\\) or mis-evaluates the second slope.<br>• <b>2.5</b> mis-signs a term.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> recompute \\(2x-y\\) at the new point — the slope softens from \\(-4\\) to \\(-1\\) as \\(x\\) grows and \\(y\\) falls, so the second drop is smaller.","unit":7,"id":"u7m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2x(1+y^2)\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\).","opts":[{"t":"y=\\tan\\!\\left(x^2+\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)","correct":true},{"t":"y=\\tan(x^2)+1"},{"t":"y=e^{x^2}"},{"t":"y=\\tan(x^2+1)"}],"explain":"<b>The \\(y\\)-integral is an arctangent, not a logarithm.</b> Separate: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{1+y^2}=2x\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\arctan y=x^2+C\\). Apply \\(y(0)=1\\): \\(\\arctan 1=\\tfrac\\pi4=C\\). So \\(y=\\tan\\!\\left(x^2+\\tfrac\\pi4\\right)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tan(x^2)+1\\)</b> adds the constant <i>outside</i> the tangent instead of inside the angle.<br>• <b>\\(e^{x^2}\\)</b> treats the \\(y\\)-part as \\(y\\) (a log antiderivative), ignoring the \\(1+y^2\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tan(x^2+1)\\)</b> uses \\(C=1\\) rather than \\(\\arctan 1=\\tfrac\\pi4\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{dy}{1+y^2}=\\arctan y\\); after integrating, invert with \\(\\tan\\) and put the constant <i>inside</i> the angle, fixed by the initial condition.","unit":7,"id":"u7m24","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 240' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='18' y1='120' x2='282' y2='120'/><line x1='150' y1='18' x2='150' y2='222'/></g><g stroke='#aab3bd' stroke-width='1.7' stroke-linecap='round'><line x1='18.06' y1='198.34' x2='26.94' y2='211.66'/><line x1='19.97' y1='154.91' x2='25.03' y2='170.09'/><line x1='22.5' y1='112' x2='22.5' y2='128'/><line x1='19.97' y1='85.09' x2='25.03' y2='69.91'/><line x1='18.06' y1='41.66' x2='26.94' y2='28.34'/><line x1='59.34' y1='199.34' x2='70.66' y2='210.66'/><line x1='61.42' y1='155.34' x2='68.58' y2='169.66'/><line x1='65' y1='112' x2='65' y2='128'/><line x1='61.42' y1='84.66' x2='68.58' y2='70.34'/><line x1='59.34' y1='40.66' x2='70.66' y2='29.34'/><line x1='100.34' y1='201.42' x2='114.66' y2='208.58'/><line x1='101.84' y1='156.84' x2='113.16' y2='168.16'/><line x1='107.5' y1='112' x2='107.5' y2='128'/><line x1='101.84' y1='83.16' x2='113.16' y2='71.84'/><line x1='100.34' y1='38.58' x2='114.66' y2='31.42'/><line x1='142' y1='205' x2='158' y2='205'/><line x1='142' y1='162.5' x2='158' y2='162.5'/><line x1='150' y1='112' x2='150' y2='128'/><line x1='142' y1='77.5' x2='158' y2='77.5'/><line x1='142' y1='35' x2='158' y2='35'/><line x1='185.34' y1='208.58' x2='199.66' y2='201.42'/><line x1='186.84' y1='168.16' x2='198.16' y2='156.84'/><line x1='192.5' y1='112' x2='192.5' y2='128'/><line x1='186.84' y1='71.84' x2='198.16' y2='83.16'/><line x1='185.34' y1='31.42' x2='199.66' y2='38.58'/><line x1='229.34' y1='210.66' x2='240.66' y2='199.34'/><line x1='231.42' y1='169.66' x2='238.58' y2='155.34'/><line x1='235' y1='112' x2='235' y2='128'/><line x1='231.42' y1='70.34' x2='238.58' y2='84.66'/><line x1='229.34' y1='29.34' x2='240.66' y2='40.66'/><line x1='273.06' y1='211.66' x2='281.94' y2='198.34'/><line x1='274.97' y1='170.09' x2='280.03' y2='154.91'/><line x1='277.5' y1='112' x2='277.5' y2='128'/><line x1='274.97' y1='69.91' x2='280.03' y2='85.09'/><line x1='273.06' y1='28.34' x2='281.94' y2='41.66'/></g></svg></div>The slope field above is for \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\). Which of the following is a solution curve?","opts":[{"t":"x^2+y^2=4","correct":true},{"t":"y=x^2"},{"t":"y=e^{-x}"},{"t":"y=-x"}],"explain":"<b>Separate to find the family.</b> \\(y\\,dy=-x\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{y^2}{2}=-\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+C\\Rightarrow x^2+y^2=C\\): concentric circles, matching the field's rotational pattern. \\(x^2+y^2=4\\) is one such circle.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=x^2\\)</b>: \\(y'=2x\\), but \\(-x/y=-x/x^2=-1/x\\) — not equal.<br>• <b>\\(y=e^{-x}\\)</b>: \\(y'=-e^{-x}\\), while \\(-x/y=-x e^{x}\\).<br>• <b>\\(y=-x\\)</b>: \\(y'=-1\\), but \\(-x/y=-x/(-x)=1\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) means segments are perpendicular to radii, so solutions are circles \\(x^2+y^2=C\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m25","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y(1-y)\\) with \\(y(0)=\\tfrac12\\).","opts":[{"t":"y=\\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}","correct":true},{"t":"y=\\dfrac{e^{x}}{2}"},{"t":"y=1-e^{-x}"},{"t":"y=\\dfrac{1}{1-e^{-x}}"}],"explain":"<b>This is the logistic equation with \\(L=1\\); its solution is the logistic (sigmoid) curve.</b> Partial fractions give \\(\\ln\\dfrac{y}{1-y}=x+C\\); \\(y(0)=\\tfrac12\\) makes \\(C=0\\), so \\(\\dfrac{y}{1-y}=e^{x}\\Rightarrow y=\\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{e^x}{2}\\)</b> is exponential growth, ignoring the \\((1-y)\\) ceiling.<br>• <b>\\(1-e^{-x}\\)</b> isn't bounded between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) the way a logistic solution is.<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{1}{1-e^{-x}}\\)</b> blows up near \\(x=0\\) (wrong sign in the denominator).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(y'=y(1-y)\\) is logistic — its solution is the S-shaped \\(\\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\), leveling at \\(y=1\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m26","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":true,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\) with \\(y(1)=1\\), then find \\(y(2)\\) to three decimals.","opts":[{"t":"1.649","correct":true},{"t":"2.718"},{"t":"1.000"},{"t":"0.607"}],"explain":"<b>Separate \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y}=\\dfrac{dx}{x^2}\\) and integrate.</b> \\(\\ln|y|=-\\dfrac1x+C\\). Apply \\(y(1)=1\\): \\(0=-1+C\\Rightarrow C=1\\), so \\(y=e^{\\,1-1/x}\\). Then \\(y(2)=e^{\\,1-1/2}=e^{0.5}\\approx 1.649\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.718</b> is \\(e^1\\) — using exponent \\(1\\) instead of \\(1-\\tfrac12\\).<br>• <b>1.000</b> forgets the exponent changes from \\(x=1\\) to \\(x=2\\).<br>• <b>0.607</b> is \\(e^{-0.5}\\) — a sign error on the exponent.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int x^{-2}\\,dx=-x^{-1}\\); apply the initial condition to get \\(C=1\\), then evaluate the exponent \\(1-1/x\\) at the new \\(x\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the particular solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=6x^2y\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\)?","opts":[{"t":"y=e^{2x^3}","correct":true},{"t":"y=e^{6x^2}"},{"t":"y=e^{2x^3}+1"},{"t":"y=2x^3+1"}],"explain":"<b>Separate and integrate.</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y}=6x^2\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\ln|y|=2x^3+C\\). With \\(y(0)=1\\), \\(C=0\\), so \\(y=e^{2x^3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(e^{6x^2}\\)</b> uses the un-integrated \\(6x^2\\) instead of its antiderivative \\(2x^3\\).<br>• <b>\\(e^{2x^3}+1\\)</b> adds the constant outside the exponential.<br>• <b>\\(2x^3+1\\)</b> forgets to exponentiate.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2\\,dx=2x^3\\); exponentiate \\(\\ln|y|=2x^3+C\\) to get \\(y=A e^{2x^3}\\), and the initial condition gives \\(A=1\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y\\cos x\\) with \\(y(0)=2\\).","opts":[{"t":"y=2e^{\\sin x}","correct":true},{"t":"y=2e^{\\cos x}"},{"t":"y=e^{\\sin x}+1"},{"t":"y=2\\sin x"}],"explain":"<b>Separate and integrate.</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y}=\\cos x\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\ln|y|=\\sin x+C\\). Apply \\(y(0)=2\\): \\(\\ln 2=\\sin 0+C=C\\), so \\(y=2e^{\\sin x}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2e^{\\cos x}\\)</b> uses \\(\\cos x\\) as the antiderivative of \\(\\cos x\\) (it's \\(\\sin x\\)).<br>• <b>\\(e^{\\sin x}+1\\)</b> mishandles the constant and the coefficient.<br>• <b>\\(2\\sin x\\)</b> forgets to exponentiate entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\cos x\\,dx=\\sin x\\); exponentiate to \\(y=Ae^{\\sin x}\\) and let \\(y(0)=2\\) fix \\(A=2\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"The solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y^2\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\) is \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{1-x}\\). What is the largest interval on which this particular solution exists?","opts":[{"t":"x<1","correct":true},{"t":"x>1"},{"t":"all real numbers"},{"t":"x \\neq 1"}],"explain":"<b>The solution exists on the open interval around the initial \\(x=0\\) up to the first blow-up.</b> \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{1-x}\\) has a vertical asymptote at \\(x=1\\), so the solution through \\((0,1)\\) lives on \\(x<1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x>1\\)</b> is the <i>other</i> branch of the formula, which doesn't connect to the initial point.<br>• <b>\"all real numbers\"</b> ignores the blow-up at \\(x=1\\).<br>• <b>\\(x\\neq 1\\)</b> is the formula's domain, but the actual solution can't cross the asymptote, so it's confined to the side containing \\(x=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a solution exists only on the largest interval containing the initial \\(x\\) on which it stays finite — here, up to (not through) the asymptote at \\(x=1\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":true,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=xy\\) with \\(y(0)=3\\), then find \\(y(2)\\) to three decimals.","opts":[{"t":"22.167","correct":true},{"t":"3.000"},{"t":"8.155"},{"t":"44.334"}],"explain":"<b>Separate and integrate.</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y}=x\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\ln|y|=\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+C\\). With \\(y(0)=3\\), \\(C=\\ln 3\\), so \\(y=3e^{x^2/2}\\). Then \\(y(2)=3e^{2}\\approx 22.167\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8.155</b> is \\(3e^{1}\\) — using exponent \\(x\\) instead of \\(x^2/2\\) at \\(x=2\\).<br>• <b>3.000</b> forgets the exponential growth.<br>• <b>44.334</b> doubles the correct value.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\,dx=\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\); the exponent at \\(x=2\\) is \\(\\tfrac{4}{2}=2\\), giving \\(3e^2\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the general solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2x+1}{2y}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"y^2=x^2+x+C","correct":true},{"t":"y=x^2+x+C"},{"t":"y^2=2x^2+2x+C"},{"t":"y=\\sqrt{x^2+x}+C"}],"explain":"<b>Separate \\(2y\\,dy=(2x+1)\\,dx\\) and integrate.</b> \\(y^2=x^2+x+C\\) — an implicit relation.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(y=x^2+x+C\\)</b> drops the \\(y^2\\) (forgot the \\(2y\\,dy\\) integrated to \\(y^2\\)).<br>• <b>\\(y^2=2x^2+2x+C\\)</b> doubles the right side (didn't cancel the \\(2\\) from \\(2y\\,dy\\)).<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{x^2+x}+C\\)</b> adds the constant outside the square root, which isn't how it solves.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\int 2y\\,dy=y^2\\) and \\(\\int(2x+1)\\,dx=x^2+x\\); leave the answer implicit as \\(y^2=x^2+x+C\\) unless told to solve for \\(y\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"Which differential equation is separable?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x+1}{y^2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y^2"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\sin(xy)"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^2+y"}],"explain":"<b>Separable means it factors into (a function of \\(x\\))(a function of \\(y\\)).</b> \\(\\dfrac{x+1}{y^2}=(x+1)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{y^2}\\) factors, so \\(y^2\\,dy=(x+1)\\,dx\\) works.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(x+y^2\\)</b> is a sum — no division separates it.<br>• <b>\\(\\sin(xy)\\)</b> locks \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) inside one function.<br>• <b>\\(x^2+y\\)</b> is again a sum.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a quotient or product of an \\(x\\)-part and a \\(y\\)-part separates; a sum or a coupled argument does not.","unit":7,"id":"u7m33","calcSource":"author","drill":true},{"topic":"separation","calc":true,"prompt":"Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{1+x^2}\\,y\\) with \\(y(0)=2\\), then find \\(y(2)\\) to three decimals.","opts":[{"t":"4.472","correct":true},{"t":"2.000"},{"t":"10.000"},{"t":"2.236"}],"explain":"<b>Separate; the \\(x\\)-integral is a logarithm via \\(u=1+x^2\\).</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y}=\\dfrac{x}{1+x^2}\\,dx\\Rightarrow \\ln|y|=\\tfrac12\\ln(1+x^2)+C\\), so \\(y=A\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\). With \\(y(0)=2\\), \\(A=2\\); thus \\(y=2\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\) and \\(y(2)=2\\sqrt5\\approx 4.472\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2.236</b> is \\(\\sqrt5\\) — forgetting the factor \\(A=2\\).<br>• <b>2.000</b> ignores the growth in \\(x\\).<br>• <b>10.000</b> treats \\(y\\) as \\(2(1+x^2)\\) without the square root.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{x}{1+x^2}\\,dx=\\tfrac12\\ln(1+x^2)\\); exponentiating gives \\(y=A\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\), with \\(A\\) from the initial condition.","unit":7,"id":"u7m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"The equation \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=ky\\) has general solution \\(y=Ce^{kt}\\). What is the general solution of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=k(y-A)\\), where \\(A\\) is constant?","opts":[{"t":"y=A+Ce^{kt}","correct":true},{"t":"y=Ce^{kt}+k"},{"t":"y=Ce^{k(y-A)}"},{"t":"y=Ae^{kt}"}],"explain":"<b>Shift the variable: let \\(u=y-A\\).</b> Then \\(\\dfrac{du}{dt}=\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=k(y-A)=ku\\), so \\(u=Ce^{kt}\\) and \\(y=A+Ce^{kt}\\). (Check: \\(y'=kCe^{kt}=k(y-A)\\) ✓.) This is the form behind Newton's law of cooling, where \\(A\\) is the surrounding temperature.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(Ce^{kt}+k\\)</b> adds \\(k\\) instead of the equilibrium \\(A\\).<br>• <b>\\(Ce^{k(y-A)}\\)</b> nonsensically leaves \\(y\\) inside the exponent.<br>• <b>\\(Ae^{kt}\\)</b> drops the shift entirely and would have equilibrium \\(0\\), not \\(A\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(y'=k(y-A)\\), substitute \\(u=y-A\\) to recover pure exponential behavior, giving \\(y=A+Ce^{kt}\\) with equilibrium \\(y=A\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m35","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 200' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='26' y1='174' x2='274' y2='174'/><line x1='26' y1='22' x2='26' y2='174'/></g><line x1='26' y1='26' x2='274' y2='26' stroke='#de5360' stroke-width='1.4' stroke-dasharray='4 4' opacity='0.55'/><path d='M26 164.75 L31.31 163.21 L36.63 161.44 L41.94 159.41 L47.26 157.09 L52.57 154.46 L57.89 151.49 L63.2 148.15 L68.51 144.44 L73.83 140.34 L79.14 135.86 L84.46 131.01 L89.77 125.81 L95.09 120.3 L100.4 114.54 L105.71 108.58 L111.03 102.52 L116.34 96.42 L121.66 90.36 L126.97 84.44 L132.29 78.72 L137.6 73.26 L142.91 68.11 L148.23 63.32 L153.54 58.91 L158.86 54.88 L164.17 51.24 L169.49 47.97 L174.8 45.06 L180.11 42.48 L185.43 40.21 L190.74 38.23 L196.06 36.5 L201.37 35 L206.69 33.7 L212 32.58 L217.31 31.62 L222.63 30.79 L227.94 30.08 L233.26 29.48 L238.57 28.96 L243.89 28.52 L249.2 28.14 L254.51 27.82 L259.83 27.54 L265.14 27.31 L270.46 27.11' fill='none' stroke='#de5360' stroke-width='2.4'/><text x='272' y='20' text-anchor='end' fill='#c98b91' font-family='monospace' font-size='12'>P = 800</text><text x='274' y='190' text-anchor='end' fill='#8b949e' font-family='monospace' font-size='11'>t</text><text x='18' y='30' text-anchor='end' fill='#8b949e' font-family='monospace' font-size='11'>P</text></svg></div>The graph shows a population leveling off at \\(P=800\\). Which differential equation could model it?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.04P-0.00005P^2","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.04P-0.0005P^2"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.05P-0.00005P^2"},{"t":"\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.04P+0.00005P^2"}],"explain":"<b>Read the carrying capacity off the asymptote, then match coefficients.</b> For \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=aP-bP^2\\) the growth stops when \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0\\), i.e. at \\(P=\\dfrac{a}{b}\\). The graph levels at \\(800\\), and \\(\\dfrac{0.04}{0.00005}=800\\) ✓.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(0.04P-0.0005P^2\\)</b> gives capacity \\(\\dfrac{0.04}{0.0005}=80\\), far too low.<br>• <b>\\(0.05P-0.00005P^2\\)</b> gives \\(\\dfrac{0.05}{0.00005}=1000\\), too high.<br>• <b>\\(0.04P+0.00005P^2\\)</b> has a \\(+\\) sign, so it grows without bound — no leveling at all.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the logistic form \\(aP-bP^2\\) has carrying capacity \\(a/b\\); read \\(a/b\\) from the asymptote and check the sign gives a ceiling, not blow-up.","unit":7,"id":"u7m36","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"<div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:330px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 200' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><g stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'><line x1='26' y1='174' x2='274' y2='174'/><line x1='26' y1='22' x2='26' y2='174'/></g><line x1='26' y1='26' x2='274' y2='26' stroke='#de5360' stroke-width='1.4' stroke-dasharray='4 4' opacity='0.55'/><path d='M26 164.75 L31.31 163.21 L36.63 161.44 L41.94 159.41 L47.26 157.09 L52.57 154.46 L57.89 151.49 L63.2 148.15 L68.51 144.44 L73.83 140.34 L79.14 135.86 L84.46 131.01 L89.77 125.81 L95.09 120.3 L100.4 114.54 L105.71 108.58 L111.03 102.52 L116.34 96.42 L121.66 90.36 L126.97 84.44 L132.29 78.72 L137.6 73.26 L142.91 68.11 L148.23 63.32 L153.54 58.91 L158.86 54.88 L164.17 51.24 L169.49 47.97 L174.8 45.06 L180.11 42.48 L185.43 40.21 L190.74 38.23 L196.06 36.5 L201.37 35 L206.69 33.7 L212 32.58 L217.31 31.62 L222.63 30.79 L227.94 30.08 L233.26 29.48 L238.57 28.96 L243.89 28.52 L249.2 28.14 L254.51 27.82 L259.83 27.54 L265.14 27.31 L270.46 27.11' fill='none' stroke='#de5360' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='113.22' cy='100' r='3.2' fill='#de5360'/><text x='274' y='190' text-anchor='end' fill='#8b949e' font-family='monospace' font-size='11'>t</text><text x='18' y='30' text-anchor='end' fill='#8b949e' font-family='monospace' font-size='11'>P</text></svg></div>The logistic curve above levels off at \\(P=600\\) (the marked point is its steepest). The population is growing fastest near \\(P=\\):","opts":[{"t":"300","correct":true},{"t":"600"},{"t":"150"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>A logistic population grows fastest at half its carrying capacity.</b> The maximum of \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) (the inflection point of the S-curve) occurs at \\(P=\\dfrac{L}{2}=\\dfrac{600}{2}=300\\) — exactly where the curve is steepest.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>600</b> is the carrying capacity, where growth has <i>slowed to zero</i>.<br>• <b>150</b> is a quarter of capacity — steep, but not steepest.<br>• <b>0</b> is the start, where the population is small and growth is slow.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=kP\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{L}\\right)\\), the rate is largest at \\(P=L/2\\); that's the inflection point of the solution curve.","unit":7,"id":"u7m37","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A quantity grows according to \\(y=Ce^{kt}\\). If \\(y=10\\) when \\(t=2\\) and \\(y=80\\) when \\(t=5\\), then \\(k=\\):","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 8}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 8}{7}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{80-10}{5-2}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 70}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Divide the two equations to cancel \\(C\\).</b> \\(\\dfrac{80}{10}=\\dfrac{Ce^{5k}}{Ce^{2k}}=e^{3k}\\), so \\(8=e^{3k}\\Rightarrow k=\\dfrac{\\ln 8}{3}\\). (This equals \\(\\ln 2\\).)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{\\ln 8}{7}\\)</b> divides by \\(t_2+t_1\\) instead of \\(t_2-t_1\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{80-10}{5-2}\\)</b> is an average rate of change, not the exponential constant.<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{\\ln 70}{3}\\)</b> takes the log of the difference \\(80-10\\) rather than the ratio \\(80/10\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> with two data points for \\(Ce^{kt}\\), divide to eliminate \\(C\\); the ratio of \\(y\\)-values equals \\(e^{k\\Delta t}\\), so \\(k=\\dfrac{\\ln(\\text{ratio})}{\\Delta t}\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m38","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":true,"prompt":"A population grows exponentially. It is \\(500\\) at \\(t=0\\) and \\(900\\) at \\(t=4\\). What is the population at \\(t=10\\), to three decimals?","opts":[{"t":"2173.458","correct":true},{"t":"1620.000"},{"t":"1800.000"},{"t":"2700.000"}],"explain":"<b>Find the growth factor, then extrapolate.</b> \\(P=500\\left(\\dfrac{900}{500}\\right)^{t/4}=500(1.8)^{t/4}\\). At \\(t=10\\): \\(500(1.8)^{2.5}\\approx 2173.458\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1620.000</b> applies the factor \\(1.8\\) only once linearly (\\(900\\cdot 1.8\\)).<br>• <b>1800.000</b> assumes <i>linear</i> growth (\\(+100\\) per unit time).<br>• <b>2700.000</b> uses an exponent of \\(3\\) instead of \\(2.5\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> exponential models compound by a fixed ratio per time unit; raise the factor to the power \\(t/\\Delta t\\), here \\(10/4=2.5\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m39","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":true,"prompt":"A radioactive substance has a half-life of \\(8\\) years. Starting from \\(200\\) grams, how many grams remain after \\(20\\) years, to three decimals?","opts":[{"t":"35.355","correct":true},{"t":"80.000"},{"t":"50.000"},{"t":"25.000"}],"explain":"<b>Use the half-life form \\(A=A_0\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^{t/h}\\).</b> \\(A=200\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^{20/8}=200(0.5)^{2.5}\\approx 35.355\\) g.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>80.000</b> halves linearly per period instead of multiplying (\\(200-3\\times 40\\)).<br>• <b>50.000</b> uses two half-lives \\((0.5)^2\\) rather than \\(20/8=2.5\\).<br>• <b>25.000</b> uses three full half-lives \\((0.5)^3\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> after \\(t\\) years there are \\(t/h\\) half-lives; raise \\(\\tfrac12\\) to that (generally non-integer) power.","unit":7,"id":"u7m40","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"An object warms from \\(5^\\circ\\) toward a \\(25^\\circ\\) room, with \\(T=25-20e^{kt}\\). If \\(T(5)=15^\\circ\\), then \\(k=\\):","opts":[{"t":"-\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}"},{"t":"-\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{10}"},{"t":"-\\ln 2"}],"explain":"<b>Substitute the data point and solve.</b> \\(15=25-20e^{5k}\\Rightarrow -10=-20e^{5k}\\Rightarrow e^{5k}=\\tfrac12\\Rightarrow 5k=\\ln\\tfrac12=-\\ln 2\\), so \\(k=-\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}\\). The negative \\(k\\) makes \\(T\\) approach \\(25^\\circ\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(+\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}\\)</b> drops the negative sign — that would send \\(T\\) away from the room temperature.<br>• <b>\\(-\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{10}\\)</b> divides by the wrong time.<br>• <b>\\(-\\ln 2\\)</b> forgets to divide by \\(5\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> Newton's law gives \\(T=T_{\\text{room}}+(T_0-T_{\\text{room}})e^{kt}\\); plug in the known later temperature, isolate the exponential, and take the log — \\(k<0\\) for approach to equilibrium.","unit":7,"id":"u7m41","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":true,"prompt":"A population satisfies \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.7P\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{1500}\\right)\\) with \\(P(0)=100\\). What is \\(P(3)\\), to three decimals?","opts":[{"t":"552.610","correct":true},{"t":"1500.000"},{"t":"816.617"},{"t":"100.000"}],"explain":"<b>Use the logistic solution \\(P=\\dfrac{L}{1+Ae^{-kt}}\\) with \\(A=\\dfrac{L-P_0}{P_0}\\).</b> Here \\(L=1500,\\,k=0.7,\\,A=\\dfrac{1400}{100}=14\\). Then \\(P(3)=\\dfrac{1500}{1+14e^{-2.1}}\\approx 552.610\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1500.000</b> is the carrying capacity (the \\(t\\to\\infty\\) limit), not \\(P(3)\\).<br>• <b>816.617</b> drops the constant \\(A\\) or mis-sets it.<br>• <b>100.000</b> is the initial value, ignoring three time units of growth.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the logistic curve interpolates between \\(P_0\\) and \\(L\\); compute \\(A\\) from the initial condition before evaluating at \\(t=3\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m42","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A population obeys \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.5P\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{100}\\right)\\). At the instant \\(P=40\\), the growth rate \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"increasing","correct":true},{"t":"decreasing"},{"t":"constant"},{"t":"zero"}],"explain":"<b>Ask whether \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) is itself rising — look at \\(\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}\\).</b> Differentiating, \\(\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}=\\left(0.5-0.01P\\right)\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\). At \\(P=40\\): \\(0.5-0.4=0.1>0\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}>0\\), so \\(\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}>0\\) — the rate is increasing. (It keeps increasing until \\(P=50\\), the inflection.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Decreasing</b> happens only after \\(P\\) passes \\(L/2=50\\).<br>• <b>Constant</b> would need \\(\\dfrac{d^2P}{dt^2}=0\\) over an interval.<br>• <b>Zero</b> confuses the rate's <i>rate of change</i> with the rate itself.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> below \\(P=L/2\\) the logistic growth rate is still accelerating; the rate peaks exactly at \\(P=L/2\\), then decelerates.","unit":7,"id":"u7m43","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A logistic population has carrying capacity \\(800\\) but starts at \\(P(0)=1000\\) (above capacity). As \\(t\\to\\infty\\), \\(P\\to\\):","opts":[{"t":"800","correct":true},{"t":"1000"},{"t":"400"},{"t":"\\infty"}],"explain":"<b>The carrying capacity is a stable equilibrium approached from either side.</b> When \\(P>L\\), the factor \\(\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{L}\\right)<0\\), so \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}<0\\) and the population <i>decreases</i> toward \\(800\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>1000</b> is the start; the population doesn't stay there since it's above capacity.<br>• <b>400</b> overshoots past the equilibrium — logistic solutions don't oscillate.<br>• <b>\\(\\infty\\)</b> would require unbounded growth, but above capacity the population falls.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(P=L\\) is a stable equilibrium; solutions starting above it decrease to \\(L\\), those below increase to \\(L\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m44","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A bank balance grows continuously at \\(4\\%\\): \\(\\dfrac{dB}{dt}=0.04B\\). Its doubling time (in years) is closest to:","opts":[{"t":"17.3","correct":true},{"t":"25"},{"t":"8.7"},{"t":"2.5"}],"explain":"<b>Doubling time solves \\(2=e^{0.04t}\\).</b> \\(t=\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{0.04}\\approx \\dfrac{0.693}{0.04}\\approx 17.3\\) years.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>25</b> is \\(1/0.04\\) — the time constant, not the doubling time (missing the \\(\\ln 2\\)).<br>• <b>8.7</b> halves the correct answer (used \\(\\ln 2/0.08\\) or the half-life-of-rate error).<br>• <b>2.5</b> inverts something incorrectly.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for continuous growth \\(e^{kt}\\), doubling time is \\(\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\) — not \\(\\dfrac1k\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m45","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A quantity \\(y=Ce^{kt}\\) triples every \\(5\\) years. Then \\(k=\\):","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 3}{5}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{3}{5}"},{"t":"\\ln 3"}],"explain":"<b>Tripling in \\(5\\) years means \\(3=e^{5k}\\).</b> Taking logs, \\(5k=\\ln 3\\Rightarrow k=\\dfrac{\\ln 3}{5}\\approx 0.220\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5}\\)</b> uses doubling (\\(2\\)) instead of tripling (\\(3\\)).<br>• <b>\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)</b> forgets the logarithm.<br>• <b>\\(\\ln 3\\)</b> forgets to divide by the \\(5\\)-year period.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> if a quantity multiplies by \\(m\\) over time \\(\\tau\\), then \\(k=\\dfrac{\\ln m}{\\tau}\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m46","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A logistic population starts exactly at \\(P(0)=\\dfrac{L}{2}\\) (its inflection value). Which best describes the growth rate \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) afterward?","opts":[{"t":"it starts at its maximum and only decreases","correct":true},{"t":"it increases and then decreases"},{"t":"it stays constant"},{"t":"it decreases toward zero, then increases"}],"explain":"<b>The rate \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) peaks at \\(P=L/2\\).</b> Starting there means the population begins at the steepest point, so from \\(t=0\\) onward \\(P>L/2\\) and the rate <i>only decreases</i> toward zero as \\(P\\to L\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\"increases then decreases\"</b> is the usual story when starting <i>below</i> \\(L/2\\) — but here we begin exactly at the peak.<br>• <b>Constant</b> contradicts logistic behavior.<br>• <b>\"decreases then increases\"</b> would require the rate to rebound, which it never does once past \\(L/2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the logistic growth rate rises up to \\(P=L/2\\) and falls after; beginning at \\(L/2\\) skips the rising phase entirely.","unit":7,"id":"u7m47","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"modeling","calc":false,"prompt":"The slope field shown has segments that are identical along each horizontal line, and steeper further from the \\(x\\)-axis. Which differential equation could produce it?<div class=\"qfig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 170\" width=\"300\" height=\"170\" fill=\"none\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A slope field whose segments depend only on y: flat near the x-axis and increasingly steep above and below it, with the same slopes repeated across every column\"><path d=\"M20 95 H285\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\".35\"/><path d=\"M30 20 V165\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\".35\"/><g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.9\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M42 33 l16 -13\"/><path d=\"M92 33 l16 -13\"/><path d=\"M142 33 l16 -13\"/><path d=\"M192 33 l16 -13\"/><path d=\"M242 33 l16 -13\"/><path d=\"M43 63 l14 -7\"/><path d=\"M93 63 l14 -7\"/><path d=\"M143 63 l14 -7\"/><path d=\"M193 63 l14 -7\"/><path d=\"M243 63 l14 -7\"/><path d=\"M43 95 h14\"/><path d=\"M93 95 h14\"/><path d=\"M143 95 h14\"/><path d=\"M193 95 h14\"/><path d=\"M243 95 h14\"/><path d=\"M43 127 l14 7\"/><path d=\"M93 127 l14 7\"/><path d=\"M143 127 l14 7\"/><path d=\"M193 127 l14 7\"/><path d=\"M243 127 l14 7\"/><path d=\"M42 157 l16 13\"/><path d=\"M92 157 l16 13\"/><path d=\"M142 157 l16 13\"/><path d=\"M192 157 l16 13\"/><path d=\"M242 157 l16 13\"/></g></svg></div>","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Read what the slope depends on.</b> The segments repeat unchanged as you move horizontally, so the slope does not depend on \\(x\\) at all — it can only be a function of \\(y\\).<br><br>Along the \\(x\\)-axis the segments are flat, so \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=0\\) when \\(y=0\\); above it they tilt up and below they tilt down, with steepness growing with \\(|y|\\). That is \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=y\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=x\\)</b> would repeat down each <i>column</i> instead, changing as you move sideways — the mirror image of what is drawn. <b>\\(x+y\\)</b> would change in both directions, so no row could be uniform. <b>\\(2\\)</b> would make every segment identical everywhere, with no flat row.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> ask which direction the picture is constant in. Constant along horizontals means the equation has no \\(x\\); constant along verticals means it has no \\(y\\).","unit":7,"id":"u7m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"Use Euler's method with two steps of size \\(h=0.5\\) to approximate \\(y(1)\\) for \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\(2.5\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(1.5\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(3.5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Each Euler step walks along the tangent line:</b> \\(y_{\\text{new}}=y+h\\cdot\\frac{dy}{dx}\\), then \\(x\\) advances by \\(h\\).<br><br><b>Step 1</b> from \\((0,1)\\): slope \\(=0+1=1\\), so \\(y=1+0.5(1)=1.5\\) at \\(x=0.5\\).<br><b>Step 2</b> from \\((0.5,1.5)\\): slope \\(=0.5+1.5=2\\), so \\(y=1.5+0.5(2)=2.5\\) at \\(x=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>1.5</b> takes one step and stops. <b>2</b> re-uses the first slope for the second step instead of recomputing it at the new point — the whole method is that the slope updates. <b>3.5</b> uses \\(h=1\\) for the second step.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> recompute the slope at every new point. Euler is a sequence of short tangent-line approximations, each starting where the last one finished.","unit":7,"id":"u7m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"euler","calc":false,"prompt":"For that same equation \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\), the solution is concave up on \\([0,1]\\). The Euler approximation of \\(y(1)\\) is therefore","opts":[{"t":"an underestimate","correct":true},{"t":"an overestimate"},{"t":"exact"},{"t":"impossible to classify without solving the equation"}],"explain":"<b>Euler steps along tangent lines, and for a concave-up curve every tangent line lies BELOW the curve.</b> So each step lands short, and the errors accumulate — the approximation underestimates.<br><br>You can check the concavity without solving: differentiating \\(y'=x+y\\) gives \\(y''=1+y'=1+x+y\\), which is positive throughout this region.<br><br>(The true value is \\(2e-2\\approx3.44\\), against Euler's \\(2.5\\) — a large underestimate, as predicted.)<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>overestimate</b> is the concave-<i>down</i> case. <b>exact</b> would need a straight-line solution. <b>\"impossible to classify\"</b> misses that concavity alone settles the direction of the error — and that you can get \\(y''\\) by differentiating the equation itself, without ever solving it.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> tangent lines sit below a concave-up curve and above a concave-down one. That single fact decides the direction of the error for both Euler's method and linearisation.","unit":7,"id":"u7m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"separation","calc":false,"prompt":"Which is the correct first step in solving \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{y^{2}}\\) by separation of variables?","opts":[{"t":"\\(y^{2}\\,dy=x\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{y^{2}}\\,dy=x\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(y^{2}\\,dy=\\dfrac{1}{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{x}{y^{2}}\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Separation means every \\(y\\) on one side with \\(dy\\), every \\(x\\) on the other with \\(dx\\).</b><br><br>Multiplying both sides of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{x}{y^{2}}\\) by \\(y^{2}\\,dx\\) gives \\(y^{2}\\,dy=x\\,dx\\), which integrates to \\(\\tfrac{y^{3}}{3}=\\tfrac{x^{2}}{2}+C\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second moves \\(y^{2}\\) the wrong way — it was in the denominator on the right, so it multiplies across rather than dividing. The third does the same to \\(x\\). The fourth integrates with respect to \\(x\\) while \\(y\\) is still present, which cannot be done because \\(y\\) is an unknown function of \\(x\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> you can only integrate once each side involves a single variable. If any \\(y\\) is left sitting next to a \\(dx\\), you have not finished separating.","unit":7,"id":"u7m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A population satisfies \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.03P\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{800}\\right)\\). For what value of \\(P\\) is the population growing fastest?","opts":[{"t":"\\(400\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(800\\)"},{"t":"\\(0.03\\)"},{"t":"\\(24\\)"}],"explain":"<b>This is the logistic equation \\(\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP\\left(1-\\frac{P}{K}\\right)\\)</b> with carrying capacity \\(K=800\\). The growth rate is a downward parabola in \\(P\\), zero at \\(P=0\\) and \\(P=K\\), so it peaks halfway between: \\(P=\\dfrac{K}{2}=400\\).<br><br>That is also the inflection point of the \\(S\\)-shaped solution curve — growth accelerates up to it and decelerates after.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>800</b> is the carrying capacity, where growth <i>stops</i>; the population approaches it but never grows fastest there. <b>0.03</b> is the growth constant \\(k\\), not a population. <b>24</b> is the value of \\(\\frac{dP}{dt}\\) at \\(P=400\\) — the maximum growth <i>rate</i>, which answers a different question.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for any logistic model, fastest growth is at half the carrying capacity. Read \\(K\\) off the bracket and halve it.","unit":7,"id":"u7m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"growth","calc":false,"prompt":"A quantity decays so that \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=-kA\\) with \\(k>0\\). Which statement must be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(A\\) approaches \\(0\\) but never reaches it","correct":true},{"t":"\\(A\\) reaches \\(0\\) after a finite time"},{"t":"\\(A\\) decreases by the same amount each unit of time"},{"t":"\\(A\\) becomes negative for large \\(t\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The solution is \\(A=A_0e^{-kt}\\)</b>, and an exponential is never zero — it approaches the axis asymptotically.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"reaches 0\"</b> confuses a limit with an attained value. <b>\"same amount each unit of time\"</b> describes <i>linear</i> decay; exponential decay loses the same <i>proportion</i> each unit of time, which is why half-lives are constant while absolute losses shrink. <b>\"becomes negative\"</b> would require crossing zero, and the model forbids it — notice that when \\(A\\) is small the rate \\(-kA\\) is also small, so the decay slows itself down.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac{dA}{dt}=-kA\\) means the rate is proportional to what remains. As the amount shrinks so does the loss, which is exactly why it never gets all the way down.","unit":7,"id":"u7m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"Find the average value of the function on the interval.","math":"f(x)=\\dfrac{6}{(x+1)^2},\\quad [0,2]","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"4"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>Average value</b> is \\(\\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\), so the work is in two parts: integrate, then divide by the width.<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> rewrite \\(6(x+1)^{-2}\\); its antiderivative is \\(-6(x+1)^{-1}\\). Evaluating, \\(\\big[-\\tfrac{6}{x+1}\\big]_0^2=-\\tfrac{6}{3}-(-\\tfrac{6}{1})=-2+6=4\\).<br><b>Divide</b> by the width \\(b-a=2\\): \\(\\tfrac{4}{2}=2\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>4</b> is the integral itself — the single most common error is forgetting to divide by the interval width. <b>3</b> averages \\(4\\) and \\(2\\) loosely (no basis). <b>1</b> divides \\(4\\) by the wrong width or mishandles the power-rule sign on a negative exponent.","unit":8,"id":"u8m0","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"Find the average value of the function on the interval. (Calculator permitted.)","math":"f(x)=e^{2x},\\quad [0,1]","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{e^2-1}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"e^2-1"},{"t":"2(e^2-1)"},{"t":"\\dfrac{e^2}{2}"}],"explain":"<b>Set up</b> \\(\\dfrac{1}{1-0}\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 e^{2x}\\,dx\\). Since the width is \\(1\\), the average <i>equals</i> the integral here — but you still must integrate correctly.<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\int e^{2x}\\,dx=\\tfrac12 e^{2x}\\) (the \\(\\tfrac12\\) comes from the chain rule running backward — the inside is \\(2x\\)). So \\(\\big[\\tfrac12 e^{2x}\\big]_0^1=\\tfrac12 e^2-\\tfrac12=\\dfrac{e^2-1}{2}\\approx3.19\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(e^2-1\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from integrating \\(e^{2x}\\) — the single most common slip. <b>\\(2(e^2-1)\\)</b> multiplies by 2 instead of dividing. <b>\\(\\tfrac{e^2}{2}\\)</b> drops the lower-limit term \\(-\\tfrac12\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m1","calc":true,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"The average value of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) on \\([0,3]\\) is \\(3\\). The Mean Value Theorem for Integrals guarantees some \\(c\\) in the interval where \\(f(c)\\) equals that average. Find \\(c\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\sqrt{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{3}{2}"},{"t":"\\sqrt{2}"},{"t":"3"}],"explain":"<b>What the theorem says:</b> if \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([a,b]\\), there is a \\(c\\) where \\(f(c)=\\) the average value. So you set the function equal to the average and solve.<br><br><b>Solve:</b> \\(f(c)=c^2=3\\Rightarrow c=\\sqrt{3}\\approx1.73\\), which does lie in \\([0,3]\\). (We take the positive root since the interval is \\([0,3]\\).)<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac32\\)</b> is the <i>midpoint</i> of the interval — a tempting guess, but the average value is attained where the curve actually reaches height \\(3\\), not at the interval's center. <b>\\(\\sqrt2\\)</b> solves \\(c^2=2\\) (using the wrong average). <b>3</b> is the average value itself, confusing the output \\(f(c)\\) with the input \\(c\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m2","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"Find the average value of the function on the interval.","math":"f(x)=\\sec^2 x,\\quad \\left[0,\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right]","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}"}],"explain":"<b>Set up</b> \\(\\dfrac{1}{\\pi/4-0}\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sec^2 x\\,dx=\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sec^2 x\\,dx\\).<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> the antiderivative of \\(\\sec^2 x\\) is \\(\\tan x\\) (worth memorizing). So \\(\\big[\\tan x\\big]_0^{\\pi/4}=\\tan\\tfrac\\pi4-\\tan 0=1-0=1\\).<br><b>Multiply</b> by \\(\\tfrac{4}{\\pi}\\): the average is \\(\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\approx1.27\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>1</b> is the integral alone — forgetting to divide by the width \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\) (equivalently, multiply by \\(\\tfrac4\\pi\\)). <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\)</b> divides by the reciprocal. <b>\\(\\tfrac2\\pi\\)</b> uses width \\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\) instead of \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m3","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"A particle's velocity is \\(v(t)=6t\\) (ft/s) on \\([0,5]\\). Find its average velocity, and note how it relates to displacement.","opts":[{"t":"15 \\text{ ft/s}","correct":true},{"t":"30 \\text{ ft/s}"},{"t":"75 \\text{ ft/s}"},{"t":"3 \\text{ ft/s}"}],"explain":"<b>Average velocity</b> is the average value of \\(v\\): \\(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\displaystyle\\int_0^5 6t\\,dt\\).<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\int 6t\\,dt=3t^2\\), so \\(\\big[3t^2\\big]_0^5=75\\). This \\(75\\) is the <b>total displacement</b> in feet. <b>Divide</b> by the time \\(5\\): average velocity \\(=15\\) ft/s.<br><br>This is the key connection: <b>average velocity = displacement ÷ time</b>, exactly the average-value formula applied to \\(v\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>75</b> is the displacement (the integral) — not divided by time. <b>30</b> is the final velocity \\(v(5)=30\\), a snapshot, not an average. <b>3</b> divides the slope \\(6\\) by something unrelated.","unit":8,"id":"u8m4","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","prompt":"\\(f\\) is continuous with \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^7 f(x)\\,dx=24\\). What is the average value of \\(f\\) on \\([1,7]\\)?","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"24"},{"t":"6"},{"t":"\\tfrac{24}{7}"}],"explain":"This tests the <b>formula itself</b>, no integration required — they hand you the integral.<br><br><b>Apply</b> \\(\\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f=\\dfrac{1}{7-1}(24)=\\dfrac{24}{6}=4\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>24</b> is the integral, not divided by the width — the classic trap, and the whole point of the question. <b>6</b> is the width \\(b-a\\) itself. <b>\\(\\tfrac{24}{7}\\)</b> divides by \\(b\\) instead of by \\(b-a=6\\) — forgetting that the width is \\(7-1\\), not \\(7\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m5","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(v(t)=t^2-4t+3\\) on \\([0,4]\\). Find the displacement.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{4}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"4"},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{3}"},{"t":"-\\tfrac{4}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Displacement</b> is the <i>signed</i> integral of velocity, \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 v\\,dt\\) — direction matters, so you do <b>not</b> take absolute value here.<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\int(t^2-4t+3)\\,dt=\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\). Evaluate: \\(\\big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\big]_0^4=\\tfrac{64}{3}-32+12=\\tfrac{64}{3}-20=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>4</b> is the total <i>distance</i> \\(\\int_0^4|v|\\,dt\\) — what you'd get treating all motion as positive (that's the next question). <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> drops the \\(+3t\\) term or mishandles a bound. <b>\\(-\\tfrac43\\)</b> has a sign slip. Displacement can be smaller than distance because the particle reverses direction.","unit":8,"id":"u8m6","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"For the same particle, \\(v(t)=t^2-4t+3\\) on \\([0,4]\\). Find the total distance traveled.","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{4}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{8}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Distance</b> is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4|v|\\,dt\\). Because the particle reverses direction, you must <b>find where \\(v=0\\) and split</b>.<br><br><b>Factor:</b> \\(v=t^2-4t+3=(t-1)(t-3)\\), so \\(v=0\\) at \\(t=1,3\\). Sign: positive on \\([0,1]\\), negative on \\([1,3]\\), positive on \\([3,4]\\).<br><b>Add the magnitudes:</b> each piece works out to \\(\\tfrac43\\), so distance \\(=\\tfrac43+\\tfrac43+\\tfrac43=4\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac43\\)</b> is the <i>displacement</i> — it lets the negative middle cancel the positive ends, which is exactly what distance must <i>not</i> do. <b>\\(\\tfrac83\\)</b> forgets one of the three pieces. <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> integrates without splitting at all.","unit":8,"id":"u8m7","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(v(t)=3t^2-12\\) and acceleration \\(a(t)=6t\\). At \\(t=1\\), is the particle speeding up or slowing down?","opts":[{"t":"Slowing down","correct":true},{"t":"Speeding up"},{"t":"Moving at constant speed"},{"t":"At rest"}],"explain":"<b>The rule:</b> a particle is <b>speeding up when \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) have the same sign</b> (acceleration pushes <i>with</i> the motion) and <b>slowing down when they have opposite signs</b>.<br><br><b>Evaluate at \\(t=1\\):</b> \\(v(1)=3-12=-9\\) (moving in the negative direction), \\(a(1)=6(1)=6\\) (pointing positive). Opposite signs ⇒ the push opposes the motion ⇒ <b>slowing down</b>.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>Speeding up</b> is the trap if you only look at \\(a>0\\) and assume \"positive acceleration = speeding up\" — but speed depends on the <i>signs together</i>, not on \\(a\\) alone. <b>Constant speed</b> would need \\(a=0\\). <b>At rest</b> would need \\(v=0\\), but \\(v(1)=-9\\neq0\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m8","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"A particle moves with \\(v(t)=t^2-4t+3\\) and starts at position \\(s(0)=2\\). Find \\(s(3)\\).","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{4}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{10}{3}"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Position</b> from velocity uses the accumulation idea: \\(s(3)=s(0)+\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 v\\,dt\\) — start where you started, then add the (signed) displacement.<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\big]_0^3=9-18+9=0\\). The net displacement over \\([0,3]\\) is exactly \\(0\\) (the particle drifts right, left, and returns).<br><b>Add the start:</b> \\(s(3)=2+0=2\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac43\\)</b> is the displacement over \\([0,4]\\), the wrong interval. <b>\\(\\tfrac{10}{3}\\)</b> is \\(s(4)\\), again the wrong endpoint. <b>0</b> is the displacement but forgets to add the initial position \\(s(0)=2\\) — the most common slip.","unit":8,"id":"u8m9","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"The velocity of a particle is given in the table. Using a left Riemann sum with the three subintervals shown, estimate the displacement on \\([0,6]\\).","math":"\\begin{array}{c|cccc} t & 0 & 2 & 4 & 6\\\\\\hline v(t) & 3 & 5 & 4 & 1\\end{array}","opts":[{"t":"24","correct":true},{"t":"20"},{"t":"26"},{"t":"12"}],"explain":"<b>Displacement</b> \\(=\\displaystyle\\int_0^6 v\\,dt\\), estimated here by a <b>left Riemann sum</b> — use the value at the <i>left</i> end of each subinterval, times the width.<br><br><b>Build it:</b> the subintervals are \\([0,2],[2,4],[4,6]\\), each width \\(2\\). Left endpoints give \\(v=3,5,4\\):<br>\\(2(3)+2(5)+2(4)=6+10+8=24\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>20</b> is the <i>right</i> Riemann sum \\(2(5)+2(4)+2(1)\\) — using the wrong end of each subinterval. <b>26</b> is the trapezoidal estimate. <b>12</b> forgets to multiply by the width \\(\\Delta t=2\\). Reading \"left sum\" correctly is the whole skill.","unit":8,"id":"u8m10","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","prompt":"At \\(t=3\\) a particle has velocity \\(v=15\\) and acceleration \\(a=18\\). Which statement is correct?","opts":[{"t":"Speed is increasing (v, a same sign)","correct":true},{"t":"Speed is decreasing (v, a same sign)"},{"t":"Speed is increasing (v, a opposite signs)"},{"t":"Velocity is decreasing"}],"explain":"<b>The rule again:</b> speed increases when velocity and acceleration share a sign.<br><br><b>Here</b> \\(v=15>0\\) and \\(a=18>0\\) — same sign, so the acceleration pushes in the direction of motion and <b>speed is increasing</b>.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> \"Speed is decreasing (same sign)\" states the right sign relationship but the wrong conclusion — same sign means <i>increasing</i>. \"Increasing (opposite signs)\" gets the conclusion right but misreads the signs. \"Velocity is decreasing\" is false: with \\(a>0\\), velocity is <i>increasing</i> (and since \\(v\\) is already positive, both speed and velocity rise together).","unit":8,"id":"u8m11","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"Let \\(F(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_2^x \\sqrt{1+t^3}\\,dt\\). Find \\(F'(2)\\).","opts":[{"t":"3","correct":true},{"t":"0"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"\\tfrac{2}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2:</b> if \\(F(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_a^x g(t)\\,dt\\), then \\(F'(x)=g(x)\\) — differentiating an accumulation function just hands back the integrand, evaluated at the top limit. No integration needed (good, since \\(\\sqrt{1+t^3}\\) has no elementary antiderivative).<br><br><b>Apply:</b> \\(F'(x)=\\sqrt{1+x^3}\\), so \\(F'(2)=\\sqrt{1+8}=\\sqrt9=3\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>0</b> comes from thinking \"the integral from 2 to 2 is zero\" — but that's \\(F(2)\\), the value, not \\(F'(2)\\), the rate. <b>2</b> is what you get by over-differentiating — taking the derivative <i>of the integrand</i> \\(\\tfrac{d}{dx}\\sqrt{1+x^3}=\\tfrac{3x^2}{2\\sqrt{1+x^3}}=\\tfrac{12}{6}=2\\) — but the FTC says you just evaluate the integrand, not differentiate it again. <b>\\(\\tfrac23\\)</b> tries to actually integrate (impossible here, and the wrong target). The FTC lets you skip integration entirely — recognizing that is the point.","unit":8,"id":"u8m12","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"Let \\(G(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^2}\\cos t\\,dt\\). Find \\(G'(x)\\).","opts":[{"t":"2x\\cos(x^2)","correct":true},{"t":"\\cos(x^2)"},{"t":"\\sin(x^2)"},{"t":"2x\\sin(x^2)"}],"explain":"This is the FTC <b>with the chain rule</b>, because the upper limit is \\(x^2\\), not \\(x\\).<br><br><b>The rule:</b> if \\(G(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^{u(x)} g(t)\\,dt\\), then \\(G'(x)=g(u(x))\\cdot u'(x)\\) — plug the upper limit into the integrand, then multiply by the derivative of that upper limit.<br><b>Apply:</b> \\(g(t)=\\cos t\\), \\(u=x^2\\), \\(u'=2x\\). So \\(G'(x)=\\cos(x^2)\\cdot 2x=2x\\cos(x^2)\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\cos(x^2)\\)</b> forgets the chain-rule factor \\(2x\\) — the most common error. <b>\\(\\sin(x^2)\\)</b> integrates instead of evaluating (the FTC does <i>not</i> antidifferentiate here). <b>\\(2x\\sin(x^2)\\)</b> makes both mistakes at once.","unit":8,"id":"u8m13","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"Water flows into a tank at a rate \\(r(t)=10e^{-0.2t}\\) gal/hr. How many gallons enter during the first 5 hours? (Calculator permitted.)","opts":[{"t":"50\\left(1-e^{-1}\\right)\\approx31.6","correct":true},{"t":"10\\left(1-e^{-1}\\right)\\approx6.3"},{"t":"50e^{-1}\\approx18.4"},{"t":"10e^{-1}\\approx3.7"}],"explain":"<b>Amount = integral of rate:</b> gallons in \\(=\\displaystyle\\int_0^5 10e^{-0.2t}\\,dt\\).<br><br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\int 10e^{-0.2t}\\,dt=\\dfrac{10}{-0.2}e^{-0.2t}=-50e^{-0.2t}\\) (dividing by the inside-derivative \\(-0.2\\)). Evaluate: \\(\\big[-50e^{-0.2t}\\big]_0^5=-50e^{-1}-(-50)=50(1-e^{-1})\\approx31.6\\) gal.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(10(1-e^{-1})\\)</b> forgets to divide by \\(0.2\\) (i.e. multiply by 5) when integrating the exponential. <b>\\(50e^{-1}\\)</b> keeps only the upper-limit term and drops the \\(-(-50)\\) from the lower limit. <b>\\(10e^{-1}\\)</b> makes both errors.","unit":8,"id":"u8m14","calc":true,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"A tank holds \\(100\\) gallons at \\(t=0\\). Water enters at \\(E(t)=20+5t\\) and leaves at \\(L(t)=3t\\) gal/hr. How much is in the tank at \\(t=4\\)?","opts":[{"t":"196","correct":true},{"t":"96"},{"t":"120"},{"t":"296"}],"explain":"<b>Two-step accumulation:</b> the amount is the start plus the <i>net</i> accumulated change, \\(100+\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\big[E(t)-L(t)\\big]dt\\).<br><br><b>Net rate first:</b> \\(E-L=(20+5t)-3t=20+2t\\). <b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\int_0^4(20+2t)\\,dt=\\big[20t+t^2\\big]_0^4=80+16=96\\) gal of net gain.<br><b>Add the start:</b> \\(100+96=196\\) gal.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>96</b> is the net change but forgets to add the initial \\(100\\) — the classic omission. <b>120</b> integrates only the inflow \\(E\\) (ignoring what leaves) <i>and</i> drops the start. <b>296</b> adds inflow to the start without subtracting outflow.","unit":8,"id":"u8m15","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"Let \\(g(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\), where \\(f\\) is positive and increasing on \\([0,\\infty)\\). What can you conclude about \\(g\\)?","opts":[{"t":"g is increasing and concave up","correct":true},{"t":"g is increasing and concave down"},{"t":"g is decreasing and concave up"},{"t":"g is constant"}],"explain":"Read \\(g\\)'s behavior off \\(f\\) through two derivatives.<br><br><b>First derivative:</b> by the FTC, \\(g'(x)=f(x)\\). Since \\(f>0\\), \\(g'>0\\), so \\(g\\) is <b>increasing</b>.<br><b>Second derivative:</b> \\(g''(x)=f'(x)\\). Since \\(f\\) is <i>increasing</i>, \\(f'>0\\), so \\(g''>0\\), meaning \\(g\\) is <b>concave up</b>.<br><br>The accumulation function inherits its <i>slope</i> from \\(f\\)'s value and its <i>concavity</i> from \\(f\\)'s slope.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> \"concave down\" would need \\(f\\) <i>decreasing</i>. \"decreasing\" would need \\(f<0\\). \"constant\" would need \\(f=0\\). Each contradicts a given fact about \\(f\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m16","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","prompt":"If \\(f(1)=2\\) and \\(f(4)=9\\), evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 f'(x)\\,dx\\).","opts":[{"t":"7","correct":true},{"t":"11"},{"t":"5"},{"t":"\\text{cannot be determined}"}],"explain":"<b>FTC, Part 2 (the net-change form):</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx=f(b)-f(a)\\) — the integral of a rate of change recovers the total change in the original quantity. You don't need a formula for \\(f\\).<br><br><b>Apply:</b> \\(\\int_1^4 f'(x)\\,dx=f(4)-f(1)=9-2=7\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>11</b> <i>adds</i> the endpoint values \\(9+2\\) instead of subtracting. <b>5</b> uses the interval width \\(4-1=3\\) somewhere it doesn't belong, or subtracts the wrong way \\((9-2=7\\), not \\(2-(-3))\\). <b>\"Cannot be determined\"</b> is the trap for anyone who thinks you need \\(f\\) itself — the FTC says the endpoints are enough.","unit":8,"id":"u8m17","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the area of the region enclosed by the curves.","math":"y=x \\text{ and } y=x^2","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{6}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{2}"},{"t":"-\\tfrac{1}{6}"}],"explain":"<b>Step 1 — find the bounds:</b> set \\(x=x^2\\Rightarrow x(1-x)=0\\Rightarrow x=0,1\\).<br><b>Step 2 — decide top vs. bottom:</b> test \\(x=\\tfrac12\\): the line gives \\(0.5\\), the parabola \\(0.25\\), so \\(y=x\\) is on top.<br><b>Step 3 — integrate (top − bottom):</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1(x-x^2)\\,dx=\\big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\big]_0^1=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac13=\\tfrac16\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> integrates only \\(x^2\\) (forgetting to subtract). <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> integrates only the line. <b>\\(-\\tfrac16\\)</b> reverses top and bottom (bottom − top) — area is always positive, so a negative answer signals you flipped the order.","unit":8,"id":"u8m18","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the area enclosed by \\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=x^2\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{4}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{8}{3}"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"\\tfrac{2}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Bounds:</b> \\(2x=x^2\\Rightarrow x(x-2)=0\\Rightarrow x=0,2\\).<br><b>Top vs. bottom:</b> at \\(x=1\\), line \\(=2\\), parabola \\(=1\\), so \\(y=2x\\) is on top.<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(2x-x^2)\\,dx=\\big[x^2-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\big]_0^2=4-\\tfrac83=\\tfrac43\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac83\\)</b> integrates only \\(x^2\\). <b>2</b> stops at \\(x^2\\big|_0^2=4\\) then mishandles the subtraction. <b>\\(\\tfrac23\\)</b> uses bounds \\([0,1]\\) instead of \\([0,2]\\) — failing to solve for <i>both</i> intersection points.","unit":8,"id":"u8m19","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the area enclosed by \\(y=4-x^2\\) and the x-axis.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{32}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{3}"},{"t":"16"},{"t":"8"}],"explain":"<b>Bounds:</b> the parabola meets \\(y=0\\) where \\(4-x^2=0\\Rightarrow x=\\pm2\\).<br><b>Integrate</b> the curve (it's above the axis on \\((-2,2)\\)): \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}(4-x^2)\\,dx\\). Using symmetry, \\(=2\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(4-x^2)\\,dx=2\\big[4x-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\big]_0^2=2\\big(8-\\tfrac83\\big)=2\\cdot\\tfrac{16}{3}=\\tfrac{32}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> integrates over only \\([0,2]\\), forgetting the left half (a symmetry oversight). <b>16</b> drops the \\(-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\) term. <b>8</b> is the value of \\(4x\\big|_0^2\\) alone.","unit":8,"id":"u8m20","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the total area between \\(y=\\sin x\\) and \\(y=\\cos x\\) on \\(\\left[\\tfrac{\\pi}{4},\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}\\right]\\).","opts":[{"t":"2\\sqrt{2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\sqrt{2}"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"0"}],"explain":"<b>Which is on top?</b> On \\(\\left(\\tfrac\\pi4,\\tfrac{5\\pi}4\\right)\\), \\(\\sin x\\ge\\cos x\\) (they cross at \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\) and again at \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}4\\)). So integrate \\(\\sin x-\\cos x\\) — no further splitting needed inside this interval.<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/4}^{5\\pi/4}(\\sin x-\\cos x)\\,dx=\\big[-\\cos x-\\sin x\\big]_{\\pi/4}^{5\\pi/4}\\). At \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}4\\): \\(-(-\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2)-(-\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2)=\\sqrt2\\). At \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\): \\(-\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2-\\tfrac{\\sqrt2}2=-\\sqrt2\\). Difference: \\(\\sqrt2-(-\\sqrt2)=2\\sqrt2\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\sqrt2\\)</b> evaluates only the upper limit. <b>0</b> is what you'd get integrating without the absolute-value reasoning, if you (wrongly) let the curves swap top/bottom mid-interval. <b>2</b> drops the \\(\\sqrt{}\\) factors.","unit":8,"id":"u8m21","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"The region bounded by \\(x=y^2\\) and \\(x=y+2\\) is most efficiently found by integrating in \\(y\\). Its area is:","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{9}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{7}{2}"},{"t":"9"},{"t":"\\tfrac{27}{6} \\text{ via } dx"}],"explain":"These are <b>sideways</b> curves (functions of \\(y\\)), so integrate in \\(y\\): the parabola \\(x=y^2\\) fails the vertical-line test, making \\(dx\\) awkward.<br><b>Bounds (in \\(y\\)):</b> \\(y^2=y+2\\Rightarrow y^2-y-2=0\\Rightarrow(y-2)(y+1)=0\\Rightarrow y=-1,2\\).<br><b>Right minus left:</b> on this range the line \\(x=y+2\\) is to the right of the parabola. \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{2}\\big[(y+2)-y^2\\big]dy=\\big[\\tfrac{y^2}{2}+2y-\\tfrac{y^3}{3}\\big]_{-1}^{2}=\\tfrac{10}{3}-\\left(-\\tfrac76\\right)=\\tfrac{9}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac72\\)</b> swaps right and left. <b>9</b> doubles via a symmetry that isn't there. The \\(dx\\) option is a reminder that forcing \\(x\\)-integration here means splitting into two messy pieces — the wrong tool.","unit":8,"id":"u8m22","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Let \\(R\\) be the region between \\(y=e^x\\) and \\(y=2-x^2\\). Which expression gives its area? (Intersection points \\(a,b\\) found by calculator.)","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\big[(2-x^2)-e^x\\big]dx","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\big[e^x-(2-x^2)\\big]dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\big[(2-x^2)-e^x\\big]dy"},{"t":"\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\big[(2-x^2)-e^x\\big]dx"}],"explain":"<b>Set up before computing:</b> the curves cross at two points \\(a\\approx-1.32\\) and \\(b\\approx0.54\\) (solve \\(e^x=2-x^2\\) numerically). Between them, test \\(x=0\\): the parabola gives \\(2\\), the exponential gives \\(1\\), so \\(2-x^2\\) is on top.<br><b>Area = top − bottom:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\big[(2-x^2)-e^x\\big]dx\\;(\\approx1.45)\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> the second option has top and bottom <i>reversed</i> (gives a negative). The \\(dy\\) option mismatches the differential — everything is written in \\(x\\). The \\(\\pi\\int\\dots\\) option is a <i>volume</i> formula — there's no revolution here, so no \\(\\pi\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m23","calc":true,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the area enclosed by \\(y=x^3\\) and \\(y=x\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{4}"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{6}"}],"explain":"<b>Bounds:</b> \\(x^3=x\\Rightarrow x(x^2-1)=0\\Rightarrow x=-1,0,1\\) — <i>three</i> intersections, so the region has two symmetric lobes.<br><b>Top vs bottom flips:</b> on \\((0,1)\\), \\(x>x^3\\); on \\((-1,0)\\), \\(x^3>x\\). By symmetry the two lobes have equal area, so compute one and double: \\(2\\displaystyle\\int_0^1(x-x^3)\\,dx=2\\big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{x^4}{4}\\big]_0^1=2\\cdot\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac14\\)</b> computes only one lobe — forgetting the curves cross at \\(x=-1\\) too. <b>0</b> integrates \\(\\int_{-1}^1(x-x^3)\\) directly, letting the odd function cancel (which is exactly why you must use \\(|{\\cdot}|\\) / symmetry). <b>\\(\\tfrac16\\)</b> uses the wrong pair of curves.","unit":8,"id":"u8m24","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","prompt":"Find the area enclosed by \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) and \\(y=x\\) on \\([0,1]\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{6}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{2}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{5}{6}"}],"explain":"<b>Top vs bottom:</b> on \\((0,1)\\), \\(\\sqrt{x}>x\\) (e.g. at \\(x=\\tfrac14\\), \\(\\sqrt{x}=0.5>0.25\\)).<br><b>Integrate (top − bottom):</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1(\\sqrt{x}-x)\\,dx=\\big[\\tfrac23 x^{3/2}-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\big]_0^1=\\tfrac23-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac16\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> integrates only \\(\\sqrt x\\) (\\(\\tfrac23 x^{3/2}\\big|_0^1=\\tfrac23\\)) and mishandles the rest. <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> integrates only the line. <b>\\(\\tfrac56\\)</b> adds the two areas instead of subtracting — the opposite of \"top minus bottom.\"","unit":8,"id":"u8m25","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base of a solid is the region under \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) on \\([0,4]\\). Cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are squares. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"8","correct":true},{"t":"4"},{"t":"16"},{"t":"\\tfrac{32}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>The method:</b> volume by cross sections is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dx\\) — find the area of one slice as a function of \\(x\\), then integrate. No \\(\\pi\\) (these are squares, not circles).<br><b>One slice:</b> the side of the square is the height of the region, \\(s=\\sqrt{x}\\), so its area is \\(A=s^2=(\\sqrt{x})^2=x\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx=\\big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\big]_0^4=8\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>4</b> integrates \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) without squaring (using the side as the area). <b>16</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from the power rule. <b>\\(\\tfrac{32}{3}\\)</b> squares wrong or uses the area-between-curves value rather than a cross-section area.","unit":8,"id":"u8m26","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base is bounded by \\(y=1-x^2\\) and the x-axis. Cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are semicircles with diameter on the base. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{2\\pi}{15}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{15}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{15}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{4\\pi}{15}"}],"explain":"<b>One slice — careful with the radius.</b> The <i>diameter</i> equals the region's height \\(s=1-x^2\\), so the radius is \\(\\tfrac{s}{2}\\). A semicircle's area is \\(\\tfrac12\\pi r^2=\\tfrac12\\pi\\left(\\tfrac{s}{2}\\right)^2=\\tfrac{\\pi}{8}s^2\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-x^2)^2\\,dx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\cdot\\dfrac{16}{15}=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{15}\\) (using \\(\\int_{-1}^1(1-2x^2+x^4)=\\tfrac{16}{15}\\)).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{15}\\)</b> is the <i>square</i> cross-section volume — it forgets the \\(\\tfrac\\pi8\\) shape factor entirely. <b>\\(\\tfrac{4\\pi}{15}\\)</b> uses \\(\\tfrac\\pi2 s^2\\) (treating \\(s\\) as the radius, not the diameter). The \"halve the diameter\" step is the whole trap.","unit":8,"id":"u8m27","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base is the region between \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x^2\\). Cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are squares. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{30}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{6}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{12}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{1}{15}"}],"explain":"<b>One slice:</b> the side spans between the two curves, \\(s=x-x^2\\) (top − bottom, with \\(x\\ge x^2\\) on \\([0,1]\\)). Area \\(A=s^2=(x-x^2)^2\\).<br><b>Expand and integrate:</b> \\((x-x^2)^2=x^2-2x^3+x^4\\), so \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1(x^2-2x^3+x^4)\\,dx=\\tfrac13-\\tfrac12+\\tfrac15=\\tfrac{10-15+6}{30}=\\tfrac{1}{30}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac16\\)</b> is the <i>area</i> of the base region \\(\\int(x-x^2)\\) — forgetting to square for a cross-section volume. <b>\\(\\tfrac{1}{12}\\)</b> drops a term in the expansion. <b>\\(\\tfrac{1}{15}\\)</b> mishandles the middle \\(-2x^3\\) term.","unit":8,"id":"u8m28","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base is the region under \\(y=\\cos x\\) on \\(\\left[0,\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\\). Cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are squares. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"2"}],"explain":"<b>One slice:</b> side \\(s=\\cos x\\), area \\(A=\\cos^2 x\\).<br><b>Integrate using the power-reduction identity</b> \\(\\cos^2 x=\\dfrac{1+\\cos 2x}{2}\\): \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2 x\\,dx=\\Big[\\tfrac{x}{2}+\\tfrac{\\sin 2x}{4}\\Big]_0^{\\pi/2}=\\tfrac\\pi4+0=\\tfrac\\pi4\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>1</b> is \\(\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos x\\,dx\\) — the side, not the side squared. <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> integrates the constant \\(\\tfrac12\\) but forgets the \\(\\cos 2x\\) handling cancels to leave \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\). <b>2</b> ignores the squaring entirely. The identity for \\(\\cos^2\\) is the needed tool.","unit":8,"id":"u8m29","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base is bounded by \\(y=1-x^2\\) and the x-axis. Cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are isosceles right triangles with a leg on the base. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{8}{15}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{15}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{4}{15}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{2}{15}"}],"explain":"<b>One slice:</b> for an isosceles right triangle with both legs equal to \\(s\\), the area is \\(\\tfrac12(\\text{leg})(\\text{leg})=\\tfrac12 s^2\\). Here the leg is the region's height, \\(s=1-x^2\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\tfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-x^2)^2\\,dx=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{16}{15}=\\tfrac{8}{15}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{15}\\)</b> is the square-cross-section volume — forgetting the \\(\\tfrac12\\) for a triangle. <b>\\(\\tfrac{4}{15}\\)</b> uses \\(\\tfrac14 s^2\\) (as if the hypotenuse were the leg). <b>\\(\\tfrac{2}{15}\\)</b> applies the semicircle factor by mistake. Matching the shape's area formula to \"what is \\(s\\)\" is the skill.","unit":8,"id":"u8m30","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"For a solid whose cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis have area \\(A(x)\\), the volume on \\([a,b]\\) is:","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dx","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b \\big[A(x)\\big]^2\\,dx"},{"t":"\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dy"}],"explain":"<b>The fundamental idea:</b> slice the solid into thin slabs of thickness \\(dx\\); each has volume \\(A(x)\\,dx\\); add them up: \\(V=\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dx\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\int [A(x)]^2\\)</b> squares the area — but any squaring already happened <i>inside</i> \\(A(x)\\) when you computed the slice's shape; you don't square again. <b>\\(\\pi\\int A(x)\\)</b> tacks on a \\(\\pi\\) that only belongs when the slice is a circle (and even then it's inside \\(A\\)). <b>\\(\\int A(x)\\,dy\\)</b> mismatches the differential — slices ⊥ to the x-axis are summed along \\(x\\), so it's \\(dx\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m31","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","prompt":"The base is bounded by \\(x=y^2\\) and the y-axis for \\(0\\le y\\le 2\\). Cross sections perpendicular to the <b>y-axis</b> are squares. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{32}{5}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{8}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{16}{5}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{32}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Slices ⊥ to the y-axis ⇒ integrate in \\(y\\).</b> Everything must be written in terms of \\(y\\), and the thickness is \\(dy\\).<br><b>One slice:</b> the side runs from the y-axis (\\(x=0\\)) to the curve \\(x=y^2\\), so \\(s=y^2\\) and area \\(A=(y^2)^2=y^4\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 y^4\\,dy=\\big[\\tfrac{y^5}{5}\\big]_0^2=\\tfrac{32}{5}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac83\\)</b> integrates \\(y^2\\) (the side, not its square) — i.e. treats \\(s\\) as the area. <b>\\(\\tfrac{32}{3}\\)</b> integrates in \\(x\\) by mistake, ignoring that the slices force \\(dy\\). <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{5}\\)</b> halves erroneously. Reading \"⊥ to the y-axis\" and switching to \\(y\\) is the whole move.","unit":8,"id":"u8m32","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region under \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) on \\([0,4]\\) is revolved about the x-axis. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"8\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"16\\pi"},{"t":"\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"4\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Disc method:</b> revolving about the x-axis with no gap underneath gives discs of radius \\(R=f(x)\\) and area \\(\\pi R^2\\). Volume \\(=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b[f(x)]^2\\,dx\\).<br><b>Apply:</b> \\(R=\\sqrt{x}\\), so \\(R^2=x\\). \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx=\\pi\\big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\big]_0^4=8\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(16\\pi\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from integrating \\(x\\). <b>\\(\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}\\)</b> comes from \\((\\sqrt x)^2\\) being mistaken or integrating \\(x^2\\). <b>\\(4\\pi\\)</b> drops the \\(\\pi\\) somewhere or uses radius without squaring. Square the radius, keep the \\(\\pi\\) outside.","unit":8,"id":"u8m33","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region under \\(y=x^2\\) on \\([0,2]\\) is revolved about the x-axis. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{32\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"4\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Disc method:</b> \\(R=x^2\\), so you must square the <i>whole</i> radius: \\(R^2=(x^2)^2=x^4\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x^4\\,dx=\\pi\\big[\\tfrac{x^5}{5}\\big]_0^2=\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}\\)</b> squares to \\(x^2\\) instead of \\(x^4\\) (squaring the exponent's base only) — \\(\\pi\\int x^2=\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}\\). This is <i>the</i> classic error with \\(y=x^2\\). <b>\\(\\tfrac{32\\pi}{3}\\)</b> integrates \\(x^4\\) but uses the wrong power-rule denominator. <b>\\(4\\pi\\)</b> loses the radius-squaring entirely.","unit":8,"id":"u8m34","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region between \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x^2\\) is revolved about the x-axis. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{2\\pi}{15}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{15}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{8\\pi}{15}"}],"explain":"<b>Washer method:</b> there's a gap between the curves, so each slice is a washer: \\(\\pi(R^2-r^2)\\). Outer radius is the curve farther from the axis: on \\([0,1]\\), \\(y=x\\) is above \\(y=x^2\\), so \\(R=x\\), \\(r=x^2\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\big[x^2-(x^2)^2\\big]dx=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^1(x^2-x^4)\\,dx=\\pi\\big(\\tfrac13-\\tfrac15\\big)=\\tfrac{2\\pi}{15}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi6\\)</b> uses \\(\\pi\\int(x-x^2)\\) — the area-between-curves integrand, forgetting to <i>square</i> each radius. <b>\\(\\tfrac{8\\pi}{15}\\)</b> computes \\((R-r)^2\\) instead of \\(R^2-r^2\\) — you must subtract the squares, not square the difference.","unit":8,"id":"u8m35","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"A washer cross section has outer radius \\(R=4\\) and inner radius \\(r=1\\). Its area is:","opts":[{"t":"15\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"9\\pi"},{"t":"3\\pi"},{"t":"7\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Washer area</b> is the big disc minus the hole: \\(\\pi R^2-\\pi r^2=\\pi(R^2-r^2)\\).<br><b>Apply:</b> \\(\\pi(4^2-1^2)=\\pi(16-1)=15\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(9\\pi\\)</b> is the textbook trap \\(\\pi(R-r)^2=\\pi(3)^2=9\\pi\\) — squaring the <i>difference</i> of radii instead of subtracting their squares. These are not equal: \\(R^2-r^2=15\\) but \\((R-r)^2=9\\). <b>\\(3\\pi\\)</b> is \\(\\pi(R-r)\\). <b>\\(7\\pi\\)</b> mis-squares one radius.","unit":8,"id":"u8m36","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region under \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) on \\([0,4]\\) is revolved about the line \\(y=-2\\). Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{88\\pi}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"8\\pi"},{"t":"\\tfrac{64\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{40\\pi}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Shifting the axis creates a washer.</b> Distances are measured from \\(y=-2\\): the outer radius reaches the curve, \\(R=\\sqrt{x}-(-2)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\); the inner radius reaches the x-axis (the region's bottom), \\(r=0-(-2)=2\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\big[(\\sqrt{x}+2)^2-2^2\\big]dx=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\big[(x+4\\sqrt{x}+4)-4\\big]dx=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4(x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,dx=\\pi\\big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{8}{3}x^{3/2}\\big]_0^4=\\pi\\big(8+\\tfrac{64}{3}\\big)=\\tfrac{88\\pi}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(8\\pi\\)</b> ignores the shift entirely (the un-shifted disc volume). <b>\\(\\tfrac{64\\pi}{3}\\)</b> forgets the inner radius \\(r=2\\) (treats it as a disc). <b>\\(\\tfrac{40\\pi}{3}\\)</b> mishandles the \\((\\sqrt x+2)^2\\) expansion.","unit":8,"id":"u8m37","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region bounded by \\(y=2\\), \\(y=x\\), and the y-axis (\\(0\\le x\\le 2\\)) is revolved about the x-axis. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{16\\pi}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"4\\pi"},{"t":"8\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Washer about the x-axis:</b> the top of the region is \\(y=2\\) (outer, \\(R=2\\)), the lower boundary is \\(y=x\\) (inner, \\(r=x\\)).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(2^2-x^2)\\,dx=\\pi\\big[4x-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\big]_0^2=\\pi\\big(8-\\tfrac83\\big)=\\tfrac{16\\pi}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}\\)</b> uses only the inner cone \\(\\pi\\int x^2\\) (forgetting the outer cylinder). <b>\\(8\\pi\\)</b> uses only the outer cylinder \\(\\pi\\int 4\\). <b>\\(4\\pi\\)</b> drops the \\(-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\). The washer needs <i>both</i> radii.","unit":8,"id":"u8m38","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region under \\(y=\\sin x\\) on \\([0,\\pi]\\) is revolved about the x-axis. Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi^2}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"2\\pi"},{"t":"\\pi^2"}],"explain":"<b>Disc method:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^2 x\\,dx\\) — and the key is integrating \\(\\sin^2\\) via the identity \\(\\sin^2 x=\\dfrac{1-\\cos 2x}{2}\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^2 x\\,dx=\\Big[\\tfrac{x}{2}-\\tfrac{\\sin 2x}{4}\\Big]_0^\\pi=\\tfrac\\pi2\\). Then \\(V=\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi2=\\tfrac{\\pi^2}{2}\\approx4.93\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> is the integral \\(\\int\\sin^2\\) without the leading \\(\\pi\\) from the disc formula. <b>\\(\\pi^2\\)</b> uses \\(\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^2=\\pi\\) (forgetting the \\(\\tfrac12\\) in the identity). <b>\\(2\\pi\\)</b> is \\(\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\sin x\\,dx=\\pi\\cdot2\\) — integrating \\(\\sin x\\) instead of \\(\\sin^2 x\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m39","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region bounded by \\(y=x^2\\) and \\(y=4\\) (for \\(0\\le x\\le 2\\)) is revolved about the line \\(y=4\\). Find the volume.","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{256\\pi}{15}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{128\\pi}{15}"},{"t":"8\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Revolving about the top boundary \\(y=4\\):</b> the radius is the distance from \\(y=4\\) down to the curve, \\(R=4-x^2\\). The region touches \\(y=4\\), so there's no hole — discs, not washers.<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(4-x^2)^2\\,dx=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(16-8x^2+x^4)\\,dx=\\pi\\big[16x-\\tfrac{8x^3}{3}+\\tfrac{x^5}{5}\\big]_0^2=\\pi\\big(32-\\tfrac{64}{3}+\\tfrac{32}{5}\\big)=\\tfrac{256\\pi}{15}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}\\)</b> uses \\(R=x^2\\) (revolving about the x-axis, not \\(y=4\\)). <b>\\(\\tfrac{128\\pi}{15}\\)</b> drops a term in the \\((4-x^2)^2\\) expansion. <b>\\(8\\pi\\)</b> forgets to square the radius. Measuring the radius <i>from the axis of revolution</i> is the key.","unit":8,"id":"u8m40","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"The region under \\(y=x^2\\) (for \\(0\\le x\\le 2\\)) is revolved about the <b>y-axis</b>. Set up in \\(y\\); the volume is:","opts":[{"t":"8\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{16\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"4\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>About the y-axis ⇒ integrate in \\(y\\),</b> with radius measured horizontally. The solid (a paraboloid-shaped region between the curve and the y-axis, capped at \\(y=4\\)) is a washer in \\(y\\): outer radius \\(x=2\\), inner radius \\(x=\\sqrt{y}\\) (solving \\(y=x^2\\)).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\big[2^2-(\\sqrt{y})^2\\big]dy=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4(4-y)\\,dy=\\pi\\big[4y-\\tfrac{y^2}{2}\\big]_0^4=\\pi(16-8)=8\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{32\\pi}{5}\\)</b> revolves about the x-axis instead. <b>\\(\\tfrac{16\\pi}{3}\\)</b> mishandles the washer radii. <b>\\(4\\pi\\)</b> uses a single disc. Recognizing \"y-axis ⇒ rewrite in \\(y\\)\" is the move.","unit":8,"id":"u8m41","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","prompt":"Which integral gives the volume when the region under \\(y=f(x)\\) on \\([a,b]\\) (with \\(f\\ge0\\)) is revolved about the x-axis?","opts":[{"t":"\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b [f(x)]^2\\,dx","correct":true},{"t":"\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx"},{"t":"2\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b [f(x)]^2\\,dx"}],"explain":"<b>Disc method, from scratch:</b> each thin slice is a disc of radius \\(R=f(x)\\); a disc's area is \\(\\pi R^2=\\pi[f(x)]^2\\); stacking them with thickness \\(dx\\) gives \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_a^b[f(x)]^2\\,dx\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\pi\\int f(x)\\,dx\\)</b> forgets to square the radius — that integral is \\(\\pi\\times\\)area, not a volume. <b>\\(2\\pi\\int f(x)\\,dx\\)</b> is the <i>shell</i> setup (and even then needs a radius factor) — not the disc method. <b>\\(\\int[f(x)]^2\\,dx\\)</b> drops the \\(\\pi\\) that comes from a circle's area.","unit":8,"id":"u8m42","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"Which integral gives the arc length of \\(y=x^2\\) from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+4x^2}\\,dx","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+2x}\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(1+4x^2)\\,dx"}],"explain":"<b>Arc length formula:</b> \\(L=\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2}\\,dx\\). The work is: differentiate, square, add 1, root.<br><b>Apply:</b> \\(f'(x)=2x\\), so \\([f'(x)]^2=(2x)^2=4x^2\\). The integrand is \\(\\sqrt{1+4x^2}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\)</b> forgets to square the coefficient — \\((2x)^2=4x^2\\), not \\(x^2\\). <b>\\(\\sqrt{1+2x}\\)</b> uses \\(f'\\) without squaring it. <b>\\(\\int(1+4x^2)\\)</b> drops the square root entirely (that would compute something with area-like units, not length).","unit":8,"id":"u8m43","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"Find the exact arc length of \\(y=\\tfrac{2}{3}x^{3/2}\\) from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=3\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{14}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{7}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{28}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{13}{3}"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate:</b> \\(f'(x)=\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac32 x^{1/2}=x^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x}\\).<br><b>Build the integrand:</b> \\(1+(f')^2=1+x\\) — the square root simplifies to \\(\\sqrt{1+x}\\), which is exactly why this curve is chosen.<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(L=\\displaystyle\\int_0^3\\sqrt{1+x}\\,dx=\\big[\\tfrac23(1+x)^{3/2}\\big]_0^3=\\tfrac23(4^{3/2}-1)=\\tfrac23(8-1)=\\tfrac{14}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac73\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac23\\) factor or halves the result. <b>\\(\\tfrac{28}{3}\\)</b> doubles it. <b>\\(\\tfrac{13}{3}\\)</b> uses \\(4^{3/2}=7\\) (arithmetic slip — \\(4^{3/2}=8\\), since \\(4^{1/2}=2\\) then cubed).","unit":8,"id":"u8m44","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"For \\(y=\\tfrac{x^3}{6}+\\tfrac{1}{2x}\\), the quantity \\(1+(f')^2\\) is a perfect square. Which is it?","opts":[{"t":"\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^2","correct":true},{"t":"\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^2"},{"t":"\\left(x+\\tfrac{1}{x}\\right)^2"},{"t":"\\left(\\tfrac{x^3}{6}+\\tfrac{1}{2x}\\right)^2"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate:</b> \\(f'=\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\). Square it: \\((f')^2=\\tfrac{x^4}{4}-\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{1}{4x^4}\\) (the cross term is \\(2\\cdot\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\cdot\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}=\\tfrac12\\), and it's negative).<br><b>Add 1:</b> the \\(-\\tfrac12\\) becomes \\(+\\tfrac12\\): \\(1+(f')^2=\\tfrac{x^4}{4}+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac{1}{4x^4}=\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^2\\). Adding 1 flips the middle sign, turning the difference-squared into a sum-squared.<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^2\\)</b> is just \\((f')^2\\) — forgetting to add the 1. <b>\\((x+\\tfrac1x)^2\\)</b> and the last option don't expand to the right terms. Spotting that \\(+1\\) completes the square is the entire by-hand arc-length skill.","unit":8,"id":"u8m45","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"Find the arc length of \\(y=\\tfrac{x^3}{6}+\\tfrac{1}{2x}\\) from \\(x=1\\) to \\(x=2\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\tfrac{17}{12}","correct":true},{"t":"\\tfrac{13}{12}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{5}{3}"},{"t":"\\tfrac{11}{12}"}],"explain":"<b>Use the perfect square:</b> \\(\\sqrt{1+(f')^2}=\\sqrt{\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^2}=\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\) (positive on \\([1,2]\\), so no absolute value needed).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(L=\\displaystyle\\int_1^2\\left(\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{2x^2}\\right)dx=\\big[\\tfrac{x^3}{6}-\\tfrac{1}{2x}\\big]_1^2\\). At \\(x=2\\): \\(\\tfrac86-\\tfrac14=\\tfrac{4}{3}-\\tfrac14\\). At \\(x=1\\): \\(\\tfrac16-\\tfrac12\\). Subtracting: \\(\\left(\\tfrac43-\\tfrac14\\right)-\\left(\\tfrac16-\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac{17}{12}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{11}{12}\\)</b> and <b>\\(\\tfrac{13}{12}\\)</b> come from sign slips in evaluating \\(-\\tfrac{1}{2x}\\) at the bounds (a very common spot to drop a negative). <b>\\(\\tfrac53\\)</b> integrates the wrong antiderivative.","unit":8,"id":"u8m46","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"Which integral gives the length of \\(y=\\tan x\\) from \\(x=a\\) to \\(x=b\\) (with \\(0<a<b<\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\))?","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+\\sec^4 x}\\,dx","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+\\sec^2 x}\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2 x}\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sec^2 x\\,dx"}],"explain":"<b>Differentiate:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\sec^2 x\\). <b>Square it:</b> \\((f')^2=(\\sec^2 x)^2=\\sec^4 x\\). So the integrand is \\(\\sqrt{1+\\sec^4 x}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> <b>\\(\\sqrt{1+\\sec^2 x}\\)</b> forgets to square the derivative — it leaves \\(\\sec^2\\) instead of \\((\\sec^2)^2=\\sec^4\\). This is the single most common error and the trap the question is built around. <b>\\(\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2 x}\\)</b> squares the original function, not its derivative (and equals \\(\\sec x\\), unrelated). <b>\\(\\int\\sec^2 x\\,dx\\)</b> drops the arc-length structure entirely.","unit":8,"id":"u8m47","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","prompt":"A smooth curve is given as \\(x=g(y)\\) for \\(c\\le y\\le d\\). Its arc length is:","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_c^d\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\tfrac{dx}{dy}\\right)^2}\\,dy","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_c^d\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\tfrac{dx}{dy}\\right)^2}\\,dx"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\tfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)^2}\\,dy"},{"t":"\\text{undefined — must solve for } y \\text{ first}"}],"explain":"<b>Same Pythagorean idea, variables swapped.</b> When the curve is written \\(x=g(y)\\), differentiate \\(x\\) with respect to \\(y\\) and integrate over the \\(y\\)-interval: \\(L=\\displaystyle\\int_c^d\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\tfrac{dx}{dy}\\right)^2}\\,dy\\). Picture factoring \\(\\Delta y\\) (instead of \\(\\Delta x\\)) out of \\(\\sqrt{(\\Delta x)^2+(\\Delta y)^2}\\).<br><br><b>Why the distractors miss:</b> the second option pairs \\(\\tfrac{dx}{dy}\\) with \\(dx\\) — the differential must match the variable of integration (\\(dy\\)). The third mixes \\(\\tfrac{dy}{dx}\\) with \\(dy\\), the same mismatch reversed. <b>\"Undefined\"</b> is wrong: the \\(x=g(y)\\) form is precisely what lets you handle curves that aren't functions of \\(x\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m48","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the area of the region enclosed by \\(y=x^{2}\\) and \\(y=4\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{32}{3}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{16}{3}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{64}{3}\\)"},{"t":"\\(16\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Find the limits, then integrate top minus bottom.</b> Setting \\(x^{2}=4\\) gives \\(x=\\pm2\\), and on \\((-2,2)\\) the line \\(y=4\\) is above the parabola.<br><br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2}\\!\\big(4-x^{2}\\big)dx=\\Big[4x-\\tfrac{x^{3}}{3}\\Big]_{-2}^{2}=\\Big(8-\\tfrac83\\Big)-\\Big(-8+\\tfrac83\\Big)=\\tfrac{32}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> integrates only from \\(0\\) to \\(2\\) and forgets to double for the symmetric left half — the most common slip when a region straddles the \\(y\\)-axis. <b>\\(\\tfrac{64}{3}\\)</b> doubles once too often. <b>16</b> is the area of the \\(4\\times4\\) rectangle, ignoring the curve entirely.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> symmetry is a shortcut only if you remember to use it — either integrate across the whole interval, or integrate half and double. Doing neither, or both, is where the marks go.","unit":8,"id":"u8m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","calc":false,"prompt":"The region under \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\) from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=4\\) is revolved about the \\(x\\)-axis. Which expression gives the volume?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 \\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Revolving a solid region about the axis it sits on gives disks:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int R^{2}dx\\), where \\(R\\) is the distance from the axis to the curve.<br><br>Here \\(R=\\sqrt{x}\\), so \\(R^{2}=x\\) and \\(V=\\pi\\int_0^4 x\\,dx=8\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second drops the \\(\\pi\\), which turns a volume into an area. The third forgets to square the radius — it integrates the curve instead of the circle's area. The fourth is a <i>shells</i> integral about the \\(y\\)-axis: right technique, wrong axis, and you can spot it by the \\(2\\pi\\) and the extra factor of \\(x\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> disks need \\(\\pi R^{2}\\). Identify the radius first, square it, and only then integrate.","unit":8,"id":"u8m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"solid","calc":false,"prompt":"The region bounded by \\(y=x^{2}\\) and \\(y=2x\\) is revolved about the line \\(y=-1\\). Which expression gives the volume?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\Big[(2x+1)^{2}-(x^{2}+1)^{2}\\Big]dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\Big[(2x)^{2}-(x^{2})^{2}\\Big]dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\Big[(2x-1)^{2}-(x^{2}-1)^{2}\\Big]dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\Big[(2x+1)-(x^{2}+1)\\Big]^{2}dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>When the axis of revolution is not the \\(x\\)-axis, every radius is measured FROM THAT LINE.</b> The curves meet where \\(x^{2}=2x\\), so \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=2\\), with \\(2x\\) on top.<br><br>Distance from \\(y=-1\\) up to the outer curve is \\(2x-(-1)=2x+1\\); to the inner curve it is \\(x^{2}+1\\). Hence \\(\\pi\\int_0^2\\big[(2x+1)^{2}-(x^{2}+1)^{2}\\big]dx\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second ignores the shift entirely and revolves about the \\(x\\)-axis — the error to expect if you set up on autopilot. The third subtracts \\(1\\) instead of adding it; since the axis is <i>below</i> the region, moving the axis down makes every radius <i>longer</i>. The fourth squares the gap between the radii rather than subtracting their squares, and \\((a-b)^{2}\\ne a^{2}-b^{2}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> radius \\(=\\) |curve \\(-\\) axis|. Write that difference down explicitly before squaring anything, and sanity-check whether the shift should make radii bigger or smaller.","unit":8,"id":"u8m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"xsec","calc":false,"prompt":"The base of a solid is the region bounded by \\(y=\\sqrt{x}\\), \\(y=0\\) and \\(x=4\\). Cross sections perpendicular to the \\(x\\)-axis are equilateral triangles. Which expression gives the volume?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 \\sqrt{x}\\,dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Cross-section volumes are \\(\\displaystyle\\int A(x)\\,dx\\)</b> — find the side, then apply that shape's own area formula.<br><br>The side is the height of the region, \\(s=\\sqrt{x}\\). An equilateral triangle of side \\(s\\) has area \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}s^{2}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}x\\). So \\(V=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x\\,dx=2\\sqrt3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second uses \\(s^{2}\\) — the area of a <i>square</i>, not a triangle. The third forgets to square the side. The fourth uses \\(\\tfrac12 s^{2}\\), the formula for a right isosceles triangle with legs \\(s\\); an equilateral triangle's height is \\(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}s\\), not \\(s\\), which is where the \\(\\sqrt3\\) comes from.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the calculus is identical for every cross-section question. All that changes is the area formula, so learn the three that appear — square \\(s^{2}\\), equilateral \\(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}s^{2}\\), semicircle \\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{8}s^{2}\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"Let \\(R\\) be the region enclosed by \\(x=y^{2}\\) and \\(x=y+2\\). Which expression gives the area of \\(R\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{2}\\big[(y+2)-y^{2}\\big]dy\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{2}\\big[y^{2}-(y+2)\\big]dy\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{2}\\big[(y+2)-y^{2}\\big]dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{4}\\big[\\sqrt{x}-(x-2)\\big]dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>These curves are written as \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\), so slice horizontally and integrate with respect to \\(y\\).</b><br><br>Setting \\(y^{2}=y+2\\) gives \\(y^{2}-y-2=0\\), so \\(y=-1\\) and \\(y=2\\). For \\(y\\) between them the line \\(x=y+2\\) is to the <i>right</i>, so the width of a horizontal strip is \\((y+2)-y^{2}\\), and the area is \\(\\tfrac92\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second reverses right and left and returns a negative area. The third has the correct integrand but \\(dx\\) — the variable of integration must match the variable in the limits and the integrand, or the expression is meaningless. The fourth switches to vertical slices without noticing that the parabola opens sideways, so a single \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) misses the bottom half of the region and the limits are wrong too.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when curves are given as \\(x=g(y)\\), horizontal slices avoid splitting the region. The integrand is then <i>right minus left</i>, and everything — limits, integrand, differential — must be in \\(y\\).","unit":8,"id":"u8m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves along a line with velocity \\(v(t)=t^{2}-4\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 3\\). What is the total distance travelled?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{23}{3}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(3\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{16}{3}\\)"},{"t":"\\(-3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Total distance is \\(\\displaystyle\\int|v|\\,dt\\)</b>, so you must first find where the particle turns around. \\(v=0\\) at \\(t=2\\), and \\(v<0\\) before it, \\(v>0\\) after.<br><br>Split there: \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(4-t^{2})dt=\\tfrac{16}{3}\\) and \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^3(t^{2}-4)dt=\\tfrac{7}{3}\\). Total \\(=\\tfrac{16}{3}+\\tfrac{7}{3}=\\tfrac{23}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>3</b> is the magnitude of the <i>displacement</i>, \\(\\left|\\int_0^3 v\\,dt\\right|=|-3|\\) — that is where the particle ended up, not how far it travelled. <b>\\(\\tfrac{16}{3}\\)</b> is only the first leg, stopping at the turning point. <b>-3</b> is the displacement itself; a distance can never be negative, which alone rules it out.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> displacement integrates \\(v\\); distance integrates \\(|v|\\). They agree only if the particle never reverses, so always solve \\(v=0\\) first.","unit":8,"id":"u8m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"motion","calc":false,"prompt":"The same particle has \\(v(t)=t^{2}-4\\) and position \\(s(0)=2\\). What is \\(s(3)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(-1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(-3\\)"},{"t":"\\(5\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{29}{3}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Position is the initial position plus the displacement:</b> \\(s(3)=s(0)+\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 v(t)\\,dt\\).<br><br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^3(t^{2}-4)dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^{3}}{3}-4t\\Big]_0^3=9-12=-3\\), so \\(s(3)=2+(-3)=-1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>-3</b> is the displacement alone, forgetting the particle did not start at the origin. <b>5</b> adds \\(|{-3}|\\) instead of \\(-3\\) — the particle finished to the <i>left</i> of where it began. <b>\\(\\tfrac{29}{3}\\)</b> adds the total distance \\(\\tfrac{23}{3}\\) to \\(2\\), using distance where displacement was required.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> position questions use signed displacement, never total distance. The sign carries the direction, which is the whole point.","unit":8,"id":"u8m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"arc","calc":false,"prompt":"Which expression gives the length of the curve \\(y=x^{3/2}\\) from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=4\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{1+\\tfrac{9x}{4}}\\;dx\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{1+x^{3}}\\;dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{1+\\tfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}}\\;dx\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\Big(1+\\tfrac{9x}{4}\\Big)dx\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Arc length is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{1+\\big(y'\\big)^{2}}\\,dx\\)</b> — the derivative gets <i>squared</i> inside the root.<br><br>\\(y'=\\tfrac32x^{1/2}\\), so \\((y')^{2}=\\tfrac94x\\), giving \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{1+\\tfrac{9x}{4}}\\,dx\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second squares the <i>function</i> instead of its derivative. The third puts \\(y'\\) in without squaring it. The fourth drops the square root altogether, which turns a length into something with no geometric meaning.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the formula has three parts that all get missed — the \\(1+\\), the square on \\(y'\\), and the root over everything. Differentiate first, square it, then assemble.","unit":8,"id":"u8m56","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"avg","calc":false,"prompt":"The average value of a continuous function \\(f\\) on \\([a,b]\\) is \\(5\\). Which of the following must be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=5(b-a)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=5\\)"},{"t":"\\(f(x)=5\\) somewhere on \\([a,b]\\), only if \\(f\\) is increasing"},{"t":"\\(f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\right)=5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Average value is \\(\\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\)</b>, so multiplying both sides by the width gives \\(\\int_a^b f\\,dx=5(b-a)\\) — the integral equals the average times the interval length, which is just the area of a rectangle of height 5.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second forgets the width, and is only right in the special case \\(b-a=1\\). The third states the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals but adds a condition it does not need — continuity alone guarantees \\(f\\) attains its average value somewhere; increasing has nothing to do with it. The fourth assumes the average occurs at the <i>midpoint of the interval</i>, which is true for linear functions and false in general.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> average value is a height. Multiply it by the width to recover the integral, and expect the function to hit that height somewhere — but not necessarily in the middle.","unit":8,"id":"u8m57","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"accum","calc":false,"prompt":"Water leaves a tank at a rate of \\(L(t)\\) gallons per hour, and \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{5}L(t)\\,dt=42\\). Which statement interprets this correctly?","opts":[{"t":"\\(42\\) gallons left the tank during the first 5 hours","correct":true},{"t":"the tank contains \\(42\\) gallons at \\(t=5\\)"},{"t":"water leaves at \\(42\\) gallons per hour"},{"t":"the tank loses \\(8.4\\) gallons in each of the 5 hours"}],"explain":"<b>Integrating a rate over an interval gives the total amount accumulated over that interval</b>, and the units confirm it: \\(\\frac{\\text{gallons}}{\\text{hour}}\\times\\text{hours}=\\) gallons.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\"contains 42\"</b> confuses the amount that left with the amount remaining — you would need the starting volume to say anything about what is in the tank. <b>\"42 gal/hr\"</b> reports the integral as though it were a rate, which the units contradict. <b>\"8.4 each hour\"</b> is the average rate \\(\\frac{42}{5}\\) presented as if the flow were constant; the total is right but the distribution over time is unknown.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> read the units of \\(\\int (\\text{rate})\\,dt\\) and they tell you what the number means. Every interpretation question on the exam is settled this way.","unit":8,"id":"u8m58","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle moves in the \\(xy\\)-plane so that at time \\(t\\) its velocity is \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle t^2-4,\\ 2t\\rangle\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 3\\). What is the total distance traveled by the particle over this interval?","opts":[{"t":"7.810"},{"t":"9.487"},{"t":"13.062","correct":true},{"t":"18.000"}],"explain":"<b>Total distance is the integral of speed</b> — the <i>magnitude</i> of velocity — not the displacement: \\(\\text{dist}=\\displaystyle\\int_0^3\\sqrt{(t^2-4)^2+(2t)^2}\\,dt\\approx 13.062\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>9.487</b> is the magnitude of <i>displacement</i>, \\(\\sqrt{(-3)^2+9^2}=\\sqrt{90}\\) — you get it by integrating the components first, then taking the magnitude. It's wrong because the particle reverses \\(x\\)-direction at \\(t=2\\) (where \\(t^2-4=0\\)), so the path doubles back and the true length exceeds the net displacement.<br>• <b>7.810</b> is the instantaneous <i>speed at \\(t=3\\)</i>, \\(\\sqrt{5^2+6^2}\\) — computing speed but forgetting to integrate it.<br>• <b>18</b> integrates only the \\(y\\)-component \\(2t\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> distance integrates the magnitude; displacement takes the magnitude of the integral. They agree only when the motion never reverses.","unit":9,"id":"u9m0","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle moves along a curve with position \\((x(t),y(t))\\) where \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=3t^2\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=e^{t}\\). What is the speed of the particle at \\(t=1.5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"4.482"},{"t":"8.102","correct":true},{"t":"11.234"},{"t":"65.652"}],"explain":"<b>Speed is the magnitude of the velocity vector:</b> \\(\\sqrt{(dx/dt)^2+(dy/dt)^2}\\).<br>At \\(t=1.5\\): \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=3(1.5)^2=6.75\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=e^{1.5}\\approx 4.482\\). So speed \\(=\\sqrt{6.75^2+4.482^2}=\\sqrt{65.65}\\approx 8.102\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>4.482</b> is \\(e^{1.5}\\) — reporting only the \\(y\\)-component as if it were the speed.<br>• <b>65.652</b> is the value <i>under</i> the radical, \\(6.75^2+4.482^2\\) — forgetting to take the square root.<br>• <b>11.234</b> adds the components \\(6.75+4.482\\) instead of using the Pythagorean combination.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> speed combines both component rates through a square root — it is never a single component, and never their plain sum.","unit":9,"id":"u9m1","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","prompt":"A curve is defined by \\(x=t^2,\\ y=\\ln t\\) for \\(t\\ge 1\\). Which of the following expressions gives the length of the curve from \\(t=1\\) to \\(t=3\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\sqrt{4t^2+\\tfrac{1}{t^2}}\\,dt","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\sqrt{t^4+(\\ln t)^2}\\,dt"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\sqrt{4t^2+\\tfrac{1}{t}}\\,dt"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\left(2t+\\tfrac{1}{t}\\right)dt"}],"explain":"<b>Arc length</b> uses the <i>derivatives</i>, each squared, under one root: \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\sqrt{(dx/dt)^2+(dy/dt)^2}\\,dt\\).<br>Here \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=\\dfrac1t\\), so the integrand is \\(\\sqrt{(2t)^2+\\left(\\tfrac1t\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{4t^2+\\tfrac{1}{t^2}}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(\\sqrt{t^4+(\\ln t)^2}\\) squared the <i>functions</i> \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) instead of their derivatives.<br>• \\(\\sqrt{4t^2+\\tfrac1t}\\) forgot to square \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=\\dfrac1t\\) (it should be \\(\\tfrac{1}{t^2}\\)).<br>• \\(\\int(2t+\\tfrac1t)\\,dt\\) dropped the squares and the root entirely — just adding the derivatives.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the arc-length integrand is always \\(\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\); the most common errors are squaring the wrong thing or losing the root.","unit":9,"id":"u9m2","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"param","prompt":"A curve is defined parametrically by \\(x=t^3-t\\) and \\(y=t^2+t\\). What is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{5}{11}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{11}{5}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{4}{6}"},{"t":"5"}],"explain":"<b>Parametric slope</b> is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}\\) — you cannot differentiate \\(y\\) with respect to \\(x\\) directly here.<br>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=2t+1\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=3t^2-1\\). At \\(t=2\\): \\(\\dfrac{2(2)+1}{3(4)-1}=\\dfrac{5}{11}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{11}{5}\\)</b> flips the ratio — that's \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dy}\\), not \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{4}{6}\\)</b> uses \\(2t=4\\) and \\(3t-... \\) (mis-differentiating, dropping the \\(+1\\) and the exponent).<br>• <b>5</b> reports only \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\) at \\(t=2\\), forgetting to divide by \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the slope of a parametric curve is a ratio of rates; keep \\(dy/dt\\) on top and never forget the denominator.","unit":9,"id":"u9m3","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"param","prompt":"For the curve \\(x=t^2,\\ y=t^4\\), what is \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2","correct":true},{"t":"12t^2"},{"t":"4t"},{"t":"\\dfrac{4t}{2t}"}],"explain":"<b>The second derivative is the parametric rule applied to the slope:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right]}{dx/dt}\\) — <i>not</i> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y/dt^2}{d^2x/dt^2}\\).<br>First the slope: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{4t^3}{2t}=2t^2\\). Then \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2t^2)=4t\\). Divide by \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t\\): \\(\\dfrac{4t}{2t}=2\\) (constant!).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(12t^2\\)</b> is \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dt^2}\\) — the classic error of using second \\(t\\)-derivatives directly.<br>• <b>\\(4t\\)</b> stops at \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right]\\) without dividing by \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{4t}{2t}\\)</b> is correct before simplifying — but it reduces to \\(2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> always differentiate the <i>slope</i> with respect to \\(t\\), then divide by \\(dx/dt\\) again.","unit":9,"id":"u9m4","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves along a curve with \\(x(t)=t^2-4t\\). For which values of \\(t\\) is the particle moving to the <b>left</b>?","opts":[{"t":"t\\lt 2","correct":true},{"t":"t\\gt 2"},{"t":"t\\lt 0"},{"t":"t=2"}],"explain":"<b>\"Moving left\" means the \\(x\\)-coordinate is decreasing</b>, i.e. \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\lt 0\\).<br>\\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t-4\\). Solving \\(2t-4\\lt 0\\) gives \\(t\\lt 2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t\\gt 2\\)</b> is where \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\gt 0\\) — moving <i>right</i>, the opposite.<br>• <b>\\(t=2\\)</b> is the single instant the motion is vertical (\\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=0\\)), not an interval of leftward motion.<br>• <b>\\(t\\lt 0\\)</b> isn't in the domain \\(t\\ge 0\\) and doesn't follow from the sign analysis.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> horizontal direction is governed by the sign of \\(dx/dt\\) alone — left when negative, right when positive.","unit":9,"id":"u9m5","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle 2t,\\ 3\\rangle\\). At what time \\(t\\gt 0\\) is the speed of the particle equal to \\(5\\)?","opts":[{"t":"t=2","correct":true},{"t":"t=1"},{"t":"t=\\sqrt{5}"},{"t":"t=4"}],"explain":"<b>Set the speed equal to 5 and solve.</b> Speed \\(=\\sqrt{(2t)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{4t^2+9}\\).<br>\\(\\sqrt{4t^2+9}=5\\Rightarrow 4t^2+9=25\\Rightarrow 4t^2=16\\Rightarrow t=2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t=1\\)</b> solves \\(2t=... \\) by ignoring the \\(3\\) and the squaring (treating speed as just \\(2t\\)).<br>• <b>\\(t=\\sqrt5\\)</b> comes from \\(4t^2=20\\) — forgetting to subtract the \\(9\\).<br>• <b>\\(t=4\\)</b> solves \\(2t=... \\) linearly without squaring.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> speed equations require squaring both components, summing, and rooting — then solve. Don't shortcut a single component.","unit":9,"id":"u9m6","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","prompt":"For a particle with position \\((x(t),y(t))\\), the expression \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\tfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2}\\,dt\\) represents which of the following?","opts":[{"t":"The total distance traveled (arc length) on \\([a,b]\\)","correct":true},{"t":"The displacement of the particle on \\([a,b]\\)"},{"t":"The average speed on \\([a,b]\\)"},{"t":"The acceleration at time \\(b\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The integrand \\(\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\) is the speed</b> (magnitude of velocity), and the integral of speed over a time interval is the <b>total distance traveled</b> — equivalently, the arc length of the path.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Displacement</b> would be \\(\\big\\langle\\int x'\\,dt,\\ \\int y'\\,dt\\big\\rangle\\) (a vector, integrating the components separately) — not the integral of the magnitude.<br>• <b>Average speed</b> would divide this integral by the elapsed time \\((b-a)\\).<br>• <b>Acceleration</b> involves second derivatives, not this integral.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> recognize the speed integrand on sight — its definite integral is always a length (distance), with units of position, not position-per-time.","unit":9,"id":"u9m7","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=6t\\), and at \\(t=1\\) its position has \\(x=4\\). What is the \\(x\\)-coordinate at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"13","correct":true},{"t":"12"},{"t":"10"},{"t":"24"}],"explain":"<b>Recover position by integrating velocity and using the initial condition.</b> \\(x(t)=\\displaystyle\\int 6t\\,dt=3t^2+C\\). Apply \\(x(1)=4\\): \\(3(1)+C=4\\Rightarrow C=1\\), so \\(x(t)=3t^2+1\\). Then \\(x(2)=3(4)+1=13\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>12</b> is \\(3t^2\\) at \\(t=2\\) — forgetting the constant \\(C=1\\) (the initial condition).<br>• <b>10</b> uses \\(x(2)-x(1)=12-3=9\\) added wrong, or mis-evaluates.<br>• <b>24</b> is \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=6t\\) at \\(t=2\\) — reporting velocity, not position.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> position is the antiderivative of velocity <i>plus</i> the constant fixed by the initial condition — dropping \\(C\\) is the classic error.","unit":9,"id":"u9m8","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"A curve is given by \\(x=t^2,\\ y=t^3-3t\\). At which value of \\(t\\) does the curve have a horizontal tangent line?","opts":[{"t":"t=1","correct":true},{"t":"t=0"},{"t":"t=\\sqrt{3}"},{"t":"t=3"}],"explain":"<b>Horizontal tangent</b> requires \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=0\\) while \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\neq 0\\).<br>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=3t^2-3=0\\Rightarrow t^2=1\\Rightarrow t=1\\) (taking the value with \\(t\\ge 0\\)). Check \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t=2\\neq 0\\). ✓<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t=0\\)</b> is where \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=0\\) — that's a <i>vertical</i> tangent, not horizontal.<br>• <b>\\(t=\\sqrt3\\)</b> mis-solves \\(3t^2-3=0\\) (it gives \\(t^2=1\\), not \\(3\\)).<br>• <b>\\(t=3\\)</b> doesn't zero \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> horizontal ⇔ \\(dy/dt=0\\); vertical ⇔ \\(dx/dt=0\\). Always confirm the other derivative isn't also zero.","unit":9,"id":"u9m9","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"The parametric equations \\(x=2+3\\cos t,\\ y=-1+3\\sin t\\) describe which curve?","opts":[{"t":"A circle centered at \\((2,-1)\\) with radius \\(3\\)","correct":true},{"t":"A circle centered at \\((-2,1)\\) with radius \\(3\\)"},{"t":"An ellipse centered at \\((2,-1)\\)"},{"t":"A circle centered at the origin with radius \\(3\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Isolate the trig functions and use \\(\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t=1\\).</b> From the equations, \\(\\cos t=\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\) and \\(\\sin t=\\dfrac{y+1}{3}\\). Squaring and adding: \\(\\left(\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{y+1}{3}\\right)^2=1\\), i.e. \\((x-2)^2+(y+1)^2=9\\) — a circle, center \\((2,-1)\\), radius \\(3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Center \\((-2,1)\\)</b> flips the signs — the center is read from \\(x-2\\) and \\(y+1\\) as \\((+2,-1)\\).<br>• <b>Ellipse</b> would require different coefficients on \\(\\cos t\\) and \\(\\sin t\\); here both are \\(3\\), giving a circle.<br>• <b>Origin</b> ignores the \\(+2\\) and \\(-1\\) shifts.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(x=h+a\\cos t,\\ y=k+a\\sin t\\) is a circle of radius \\(a\\) centered at \\((h,k)\\); equal amplitudes ⇒ circle, unequal ⇒ ellipse.","unit":9,"id":"u9m10","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle moves along the cycloid \\(x=t-\\sin t,\\ y=1-\\cos t\\). What is the total distance traveled by the particle from \\(t=0\\) to \\(t=2\\pi\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2\\pi"},{"t":"4"},{"t":"8","correct":true},{"t":"16"}],"explain":"<b>Total distance is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\,dt\\).</b> Here \\(x'=1-\\cos t,\\ y'=\\sin t\\). Then \\((x')^2+(y')^2=(1-\\cos t)^2+\\sin^2 t=2-2\\cos t\\). Using \\(1-\\cos t=2\\sin^2\\tfrac t2\\), the integrand is \\(\\sqrt{4\\sin^2\\tfrac t2}=2\\left|\\sin\\tfrac t2\\right|\\). Integrating over \\([0,2\\pi]\\) gives \\(8\\) — the famous arc length of one cycloid arch.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2\\pi\\)</b> is the horizontal distance the wheel rolls — the change in \\(x\\), not the path length.<br>• <b>4</b> integrates only part, or drops the factor of 2.<br>• <b>16</b> doubles the correct arch length.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> even a \"simple-looking\" parametric path can have an arc length unrelated to its width; you must integrate the speed. (One cycloid arch is exactly \\(8r\\).)","unit":9,"id":"u9m11","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":true,"prompt":"At time \\(t\\), a particle moving in the \\(xy\\)-plane has velocity vector \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle\\cos(t^2),\\ e^{0.5t}\\rangle\\). What is the acceleration vector at \\(t=1.2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\langle -0.313,\\ 0.911\\rangle","correct":true},{"t":"\\langle 0.130,\\ 3.320\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle -2.4\\sin(1.44),\\ e^{0.6}\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle 0.911,\\ -0.313\\rangle"}],"explain":"<b>Acceleration is the derivative of velocity</b>, component-wise (chain rule on each): \\(\\vec a(t)=\\big\\langle -2t\\sin(t^2),\\ 0.5\\,e^{0.5t}\\big\\rangle\\).<br>At \\(t=1.2\\): \\(-2(1.2)\\sin(1.44)\\approx -0.313\\) and \\(0.5\\,e^{0.6}\\approx 0.911\\). So \\(\\vec a(1.2)\\approx\\langle -0.313,\\ 0.911\\rangle\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\langle 0.130,\\ 3.320\\rangle\\)</b> is the <i>velocity</i> \\(\\vec v(1.2)=\\langle\\cos(1.44),\\ e^{0.6}\\rangle\\) — not its derivative.<br>• <b>\\(\\langle -2.4\\sin(1.44),\\ e^{0.6}\\rangle\\)</b> forgot the \\(0.5\\) chain-rule factor on the \\(y\\)-component (and left \\(x\\) unevaluated).<br>• The last choice swaps the components.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate each component separately, and don't forget the chain-rule factors (\\(2t\\) from \\(t^2\\), \\(0.5\\) from \\(0.5t\\)).","unit":9,"id":"u9m12","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle\\cos(t^2),\\ e^{0.5t}\\rangle\\) for \\(t\\ge 0\\). What is the speed of the particle at \\(t=1.2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"1.827","correct":true},{"t":"3.337"},{"t":"3.450"},{"t":"0.911"}],"explain":"<b>Speed is the magnitude of velocity:</b> \\(|\\vec v(1.2)|=\\sqrt{\\cos^2(1.44)+\\left(e^{0.6}\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{0.017+3.320}\\approx 1.827\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3.337</b> is the value under the radical, \\(\\cos^2(1.44)+e^{1.2}\\) — forgetting the square root.<br>• <b>0.911</b> is roughly \\(e^{0.6}/2\\) or a single mis-evaluated piece.<br>• <b>3.450</b> adds the components without squaring/rooting.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> speed always combines components via \\(\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\); the most common slip is stopping before the square root.","unit":9,"id":"u9m13","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":true,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle 2t-2,\\ 3-t\\rangle\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 3\\). What is the total distance traveled?","opts":[{"t":"3.000"},{"t":"7.706","correct":true},{"t":"3.354"},{"t":"10.500"}],"explain":"<b>Total distance is the integral of speed:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^3\\sqrt{(2t-2)^2+(3-t)^2}\\,dt\\approx 7.706\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3.354</b> is the magnitude of <i>displacement</i>, \\(\\big|\\langle 3,\\ 1.5\\rangle\\big|=\\sqrt{11.25}\\) — integrating the components first (the particle reverses \\(x\\)-direction at \\(t=1\\), so distance \\(\\gt\\) displacement).<br>• <b>3.000</b> is the \\(x\\)-displacement \\(\\int_0^3(2t-2)\\,dt\\) alone.<br>• <b>10.500</b> integrates without the square root.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> distance integrates the magnitude of velocity; whenever a component changes sign, distance strictly exceeds the displacement magnitude.","unit":9,"id":"u9m14","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=4t\\), and at \\(t=0\\) the particle is at \\(x=3\\). What is the \\(x\\)-coordinate at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"11","correct":true},{"t":"8"},{"t":"16"},{"t":"19"}],"explain":"<b>Integrate velocity, then apply the initial condition.</b> \\(x(t)=\\displaystyle\\int 4t\\,dt=2t^2+C\\). With \\(x(0)=3\\): \\(C=3\\), so \\(x(t)=2t^2+3\\). Then \\(x(2)=2(4)+3=11\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8</b> is \\(2t^2\\) at \\(t=2\\) — dropping the initial value \\(C=3\\).<br>• <b>16</b> is the velocity \\(4t\\) at \\(t=... \\) or \\(2\\cdot 8\\), confusing rate with position.<br>• <b>19</b> mis-adds (e.g. \\(2t^2+3\\) with \\(t^2=8\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the new position equals the antiderivative evaluated, with \\(C\\) pinned by the starting point — the initial condition is not optional.","unit":9,"id":"u9m15","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle t^2,\\ \\sqrt{t}\\rangle\\) for \\(t\\ge 0\\). Which expression gives the total distance traveled from \\(t=1\\) to \\(t=4\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt{t^4+t}\\,dt","correct":true},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\left(t^2+\\sqrt{t}\\right)dt"},{"t":"\\sqrt{\\left(\\textstyle\\int_1^4 t^2 dt\\right)^2+\\left(\\textstyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt{t}\\,dt\\right)^2}"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt{t^4+t^2}\\,dt"}],"explain":"<b>Distance is the integral of speed</b> \\(=\\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt{(t^2)^2+(\\sqrt t)^2}\\,dt=\\int_1^4\\sqrt{t^4+t}\\,dt\\) (since \\((\\sqrt t)^2=t\\)).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• \\(\\int(t^2+\\sqrt t)\\,dt\\) drops the squares and the root — just integrating the components added.<br>• The <b>third choice</b> computes the <i>displacement magnitude</i> (integrate each component, then take the magnitude) — that's not the path length.<br>• \\(\\sqrt{t^4+t^2}\\) squared \\(\\sqrt t\\) incorrectly as \\(t^2\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> distance puts the magnitude <i>inside</i> the integral; displacement puts it <i>outside</i>. Watch \\((\\sqrt t)^2=t\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m16","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","prompt":"For a particle with velocity \\(\\vec v(t)\\), the integral \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\vec v(t)\\,dt\\) gives which of the following?","opts":[{"t":"The displacement (net change in position) over \\([a,b]\\)","correct":true},{"t":"The total distance traveled over \\([a,b]\\)"},{"t":"The average velocity over \\([a,b]\\)"},{"t":"The change in speed over \\([a,b]\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Integrating the velocity vector gives the displacement</b> — the net change in position, \\(\\vec r(b)-\\vec r(a)\\). It's a vector.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Total distance</b> requires integrating the <i>magnitude</i> \\(|\\vec v|\\), not \\(\\vec v\\) itself — the absolute value makes reversals add instead of cancel.<br>• <b>Average velocity</b> would divide this by \\((b-a)\\).<br>• <b>Change in speed</b> is \\(|\\vec v(b)|-|\\vec v(a)|\\), unrelated to this integral.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\int\\vec v\\,dt\\) = displacement (vector, can cancel); \\(\\int|\\vec v|\\,dt\\) = distance (scalar, always accumulates).","unit":9,"id":"u9m17","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle t,\\ t^2-3\\rangle\\). What is the speed of the particle at \\(t=2\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\sqrt{5}","correct":true},{"t":"3"},{"t":"\\sqrt{29}"},{"t":"\\langle 2,1\\rangle"}],"explain":"<b>Evaluate velocity at \\(t=2\\), then take the magnitude.</b> \\(\\vec v(2)=\\langle 2,\\ 2^2-3\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\ 1\\rangle\\). Speed \\(=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>3</b> adds the components \\(2+1\\) instead of using the Pythagorean magnitude.<br>• <b>\\(\\sqrt{29}\\)</b> uses \\(\\langle 2,5\\rangle\\) — forgetting the \\(-3\\) in \\(t^2-3\\) (so \\(\\sqrt{4+25}\\)).<br>• <b>\\(\\langle 2,1\\rangle\\)</b> is the velocity vector itself, not the speed (a scalar).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate first, then take \\(\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\); speed is a single nonnegative number.","unit":9,"id":"u9m18","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(\\vec r(t)=\\langle t^3,\\ \\cos t\\rangle\\), what is the acceleration vector \\(\\vec a(t)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\langle 6t,\\ -\\cos t\\rangle","correct":true},{"t":"\\langle 3t^2,\\ -\\sin t\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle 6t,\\ \\cos t\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle 6t,\\ \\sin t\\rangle"}],"explain":"<b>Acceleration is the second derivative of position</b>, component-wise. First \\(\\vec v=\\vec r\\,'=\\langle 3t^2,\\ -\\sin t\\rangle\\); then \\(\\vec a=\\vec v\\,'=\\langle 6t,\\ -\\cos t\\rangle\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\langle 3t^2,\\ -\\sin t\\rangle\\)</b> is the <i>velocity</i> — only one derivative taken.<br>• <b>\\(\\langle 6t,\\ \\cos t\\rangle\\)</b> mishandles the second derivative of \\(\\cos t\\): \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(-\\sin t)=-\\cos t\\), not \\(+\\cos t\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\langle 6t,\\ \\sin t\\rangle\\)</b> has the wrong sign throughout the \\(y\\)-derivative chain.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate twice, tracking signs carefully: \\(\\cos t\\to-\\sin t\\to-\\cos t\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m19","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has constant acceleration \\(\\vec a(t)=\\langle 6t,\\ 4\\rangle\\) and velocity \\(\\vec v(0)=\\langle 1,\\ -2\\rangle\\). Find \\(\\vec v(t)\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\langle 3t^2+1,\\ 4t-2\\rangle","correct":true},{"t":"\\langle 3t^2,\\ 4t\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle 6,\\ 0\\rangle"},{"t":"\\langle 3t^2-2,\\ 4t+1\\rangle"}],"explain":"<b>Integrate acceleration and apply the initial velocity.</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\langle 6t,4\\rangle\\,dt=\\langle 3t^2,\\ 4t\\rangle+\\vec C\\). With \\(\\vec v(0)=\\langle 1,-2\\rangle\\), \\(\\vec C=\\langle 1,-2\\rangle\\). So \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle 3t^2+1,\\ 4t-2\\rangle\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\langle 3t^2,\\ 4t\\rangle\\)</b> dropped the constant vector from the initial condition.<br>• <b>\\(\\langle 6,\\ 0\\rangle\\)</b> differentiated instead of integrating.<br>• <b>\\(\\langle 3t^2-2,\\ 4t+1\\rangle\\)</b> swapped the two initial-condition components.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> velocity is the antiderivative of acceleration plus the constant fixed by \\(\\vec v(0)\\) — match each component to the right initial value.","unit":9,"id":"u9m20","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle e^{t},\\ e^{-t}\\rangle\\). What is the speed at \\(t=0\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\sqrt{2}","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"<b>At \\(t=0\\):</b> \\(\\vec v(0)=\\langle e^0,\\ e^0\\rangle=\\langle 1,\\ 1\\rangle\\). Speed \\(=\\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>2</b> adds the components \\(1+1\\) instead of using the root of the sum of squares.<br>• <b>0</b> assumes \\(e^t\\) and \\(e^{-t}\\) cancel — but speed combines their squares; it's never zero here.<br>• <b>1</b> reads only one component.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> evaluate the components at the instant, then \\(\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\). \\(e^0=1\\) for both.","unit":9,"id":"u9m21","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle t^2-1,\\ t-1\\rangle\\). At what time is the particle at rest (velocity \\(=\\vec 0\\))?","opts":[{"t":"t=1","correct":true},{"t":"t=-1"},{"t":"t=1\\text{ and }t=-1"},{"t":"\\text{never}"}],"explain":"<b>\"At rest\" means BOTH components of velocity are zero simultaneously.</b> Solve \\(t^2-1=0\\Rightarrow t=\\pm 1\\), and \\(t-1=0\\Rightarrow t=1\\). The only \\(t\\) satisfying <i>both</i> is \\(t=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(t=-1\\)</b> zeros only the \\(x\\)-component; there \\(\\vec v(-1)=\\langle 0,-2\\rangle\\neq\\vec 0\\).<br>• <b>\"\\(t=1\\) and \\(t=-1\\)\"</b> lists every root of either component, but rest requires them to vanish <i>together</i>.<br>• <b>\"never\"</b> overlooks that \\(t=1\\) does work for both.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a particle is at rest only where the velocity vector is the zero vector — every component must be zero at the same \\(t\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m22","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"At a certain instant, a particle has velocity \\(\\vec v=\\langle 2,\\ -3\\rangle\\) and acceleration \\(\\vec a=\\langle 1,\\ 1\\rangle\\). Is the particle speeding up or slowing down at that instant?","opts":[{"t":"Slowing down (\\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a\\lt 0\\))","correct":true},{"t":"Speeding up (\\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a\\gt 0\\))"},{"t":"Neither; speed is constant"},{"t":"Cannot be determined without more information"}],"explain":"<b>Speeding up vs. slowing down is governed by the sign of \\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a\\)</b> (equivalently, whether \\(\\tfrac{d}{dt}|\\vec v|\\) is \\(+\\) or \\(-\\)). Compute the dot product: \\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a=(2)(1)+(-3)(1)=-1\\lt 0\\), so the speed is <b>decreasing</b> — slowing down.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Speeding up</b> would need \\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a\\gt 0\\); here it's negative.<br>• <b>Constant speed</b> needs \\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a=0\\) (acceleration perpendicular to velocity); here it isn't.<br>• It <i>can</i> be determined — the dot product is exactly the test.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\vec v\\cdot\\vec a\\gt 0\\) ⇒ speeding up, \\(\\lt 0\\) ⇒ slowing down, \\(=0\\) ⇒ instantaneously constant speed. This is the vector analog of \"\\(v\\) and \\(a\\) same sign.\"","unit":9,"id":"u9m23","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"For the polar curve \\(r=2+2\\cos\\theta\\), find the slope \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) of the tangent line at \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\).","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"-1"},{"t":"0"},{"t":"\\text{undefined}"}],"explain":"<b>Polar tangent slope:</b> with \\(x=r\\cos\\theta,\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{r'\\sin\\theta+r\\cos\\theta}{r'\\cos\\theta-r\\sin\\theta}\\).<br>Here \\(r=2+2\\cos\\theta,\\ r'=-2\\sin\\theta\\). At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\): \\(r=2,\\ r'=-2\\). Numerator \\(=(-2)(1)+(2)(0)=-2\\); denominator \\(=(-2)(0)-(2)(1)=-2\\). So \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{-2}{-2}=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>0</b> assumes the numerator alone (or uses \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=0\\) as the slope, a classic confusion).<br>• <b>undefined</b> would need the denominator zero; here it's \\(-2\\).<br>• <b>\\(-1\\)</b> is a sign error in numerator or denominator.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the slope is \\(\\dfrac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}\\) using the full product-rule derivatives — never just \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m24","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"How many petals does the rose \\(r=4\\cos(6\\theta)\\) have?","opts":[{"t":"12","correct":true},{"t":"6"},{"t":"36"},{"t":"24"}],"explain":"<b>Petal rule:</b> for \\(r=a\\cos(k\\theta)\\), if \\(k\\) is <b>even</b> the rose has \\(2k\\) petals; if \\(k\\) is <b>odd</b>, \\(k\\) petals. Here \\(k=6\\) is even, so \\(2(6)=12\\) petals.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>6</b> applies the odd-\\(k\\) rule by mistake.<br>• <b>36</b> is \\(k^2\\).<br>• <b>24</b> is \\(4k\\) (doubling twice, or multiplying by the amplitude).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> even \\(k\\) ⇒ \\(2k\\) petals (the negative-\\(r\\) sweeps create the extra set); odd \\(k\\) ⇒ \\(k\\) petals (negative-\\(r\\) retraces the same ones).","unit":9,"id":"u9m25","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":true,"prompt":"At what angle \\(\\theta\\) in \\(\\left(0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\) does the curve \\(r=1+\\cos\\theta\\) have a horizontal tangent line?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}"}],"explain":"<b>Horizontal tangent</b> ⇔ \\(\\dfrac{dy}{d\\theta}=0\\) (with \\(\\dfrac{dx}{d\\theta}\\neq 0\\)), where \\(y=r\\sin\\theta=(1+\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\).<br>\\(\\dfrac{dy}{d\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+\\cos 2\\theta=0\\). Using \\(\\cos 2\\theta=2\\cos^2\\theta-1\\): \\(2\\cos^2\\theta+\\cos\\theta-1=0\\Rightarrow(2\\cos\\theta-1)(\\cos\\theta+1)=0\\). In \\((0,\\tfrac\\pi2)\\), \\(\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac12\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi3\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> is where \\(\\dfrac{dx}{d\\theta}=0\\) tends to occur — a <i>vertical</i> tangent direction, not horizontal.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi6,\\tfrac\\pi4\\)</b> don't satisfy \\(\\dfrac{dy}{d\\theta}=0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for polar tangents, differentiate \\(y=r\\sin\\theta\\) (not just \\(r\\)); horizontal ⇔ \\(dy/d\\theta=0\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m26","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"Convert the polar point \\(\\left(-3,\\ \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\) to rectangular coordinates.","opts":[{"t":"\\left(\\tfrac{3}{2},\\ -\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)","correct":true},{"t":"\\left(-\\tfrac{3}{2},\\ \\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)"},{"t":"\\left(-\\tfrac{3}{2},\\ -\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)"},{"t":"\\left(\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2},\\ -\\tfrac{3}{2}\\right)"}],"explain":"<b>Use</b> \\(x=r\\cos\\theta,\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\) directly — the negative \\(r\\) is handled by the formula. \\(x=-3\\cos\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}=-3\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac32\\); \\(y=-3\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}=-3\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=-\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\left(-\\tfrac32,\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)\\)</b> ignored the negative \\(r\\) (used \\(r=3\\)) — flipping both signs.<br>• <b>\\(\\left(-\\tfrac32,-\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)\\)</b> got \\(x\\) wrong by mishandling \\(\\cos\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\)'s sign.<br>• The last choice swaps \\(\\sin\\) and \\(\\cos\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> plug \\(r\\) (sign included) straight into \\(x=r\\cos\\theta,\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\); don't separately \"fix\" the negative radius.","unit":9,"id":"u9m27","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"Which of the following polar curves is a limaçon with an <b>inner loop</b>?","opts":[{"t":"r=1+2\\cos\\theta","correct":true},{"t":"r=2+\\cos\\theta"},{"t":"r=2+2\\cos\\theta"},{"t":"r=2\\cos\\theta"}],"explain":"<b>Classify \\(r=a\\pm b\\cos\\theta\\) by the ratio \\(\\dfrac ab\\):</b> inner loop if \\(\\dfrac ab\\lt 1\\); cardioid if \\(=1\\); dimpled if \\(1\\le\\dfrac ab\\lt 2\\); convex if \\(\\ge 2\\). For \\(r=1+2\\cos\\theta\\), \\(\\dfrac ab=\\dfrac12\\lt 1\\) ⇒ <b>inner loop</b> (the radius goes negative over part of the sweep, drawing the loop).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(2+\\cos\\theta\\)</b>: \\(\\dfrac ab=2\\) ⇒ convex limaçon (no dimple, no loop).<br>• <b>\\(2+2\\cos\\theta\\)</b>: \\(\\dfrac ab=1\\) ⇒ cardioid.<br>• <b>\\(2\\cos\\theta\\)</b> is a circle.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the inner loop appears exactly when \\(a\\lt b\\), because then \\(r\\) becomes negative for some angles.","unit":9,"id":"u9m28","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"For the rose \\(r=3\\sin(2\\theta)\\), what is the value of \\(r\\) at \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\), and what does this point represent?","opts":[{"t":"r=3, the tip of a petal","correct":true},{"t":"r=0, the pole"},{"t":"r=3, a point where the curve crosses itself"},{"t":"r=1.5, the midpoint of a petal"}],"explain":"<b>Evaluate:</b> \\(r=3\\sin\\!\\left(2\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi4\\right)=3\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2=3\\). Since \\(\\sin\\) reaches its maximum of \\(1\\) at \\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\), this is the largest \\(r\\) on that petal — the <b>tip</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(r=0\\) (pole)</b> happens where \\(\\sin 2\\theta=0\\) (e.g. \\(\\theta=0,\\tfrac\\pi2\\)), the petal <i>edges</i>, not \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi4\\).<br>• <b>\"crosses itself\"</b> — rose petals meet only at the pole, not at a tip.<br>• <b>\\(r=1.5\\)</b> mis-evaluates \\(\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2\\) as \\(\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for \\(r=a\\sin(k\\theta)\\), the petal tip is where the sine hits \\(\\pm1\\) (max \\(|r|\\)); the pole is where the sine is \\(0\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m29","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"For a polar curve \\(r=f(\\theta)\\), the quantity \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) represents which of the following?","opts":[{"t":"The rate at which the distance from the origin changes as \\(\\theta\\) increases","correct":true},{"t":"The slope of the tangent line to the curve"},{"t":"The rate at which the angle changes"},{"t":"The area swept out by the curve"}],"explain":"<b>\\(r\\) is the distance from the origin, so \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) is how fast that distance changes as the angle sweeps.</b> If \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\gt 0\\) the curve is spiraling outward; if \\(\\lt 0\\), inward.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Slope of the tangent line</b> is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{r'\\sin\\theta+r\\cos\\theta}{r'\\cos\\theta-r\\sin\\theta}\\) — a much more involved expression. Confusing \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) with the slope is the single most common polar error.<br>• <b>Rate the angle changes</b> would be \\(\\dfrac{d\\theta}{dt}\\), a time derivative.<br>• <b>Area</b> involves \\(\\tfrac12\\int r^2\\,d\\theta\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) measures radial change only; it is <i>not</i> the slope of the curve in the plane.","unit":9,"id":"u9m30","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":true,"prompt":"At what angle \\(\\theta\\) in \\(\\left(0,\\pi\\right)\\) does the cardioid \\(r=1+\\cos\\theta\\) have a vertical tangent line (other than at the pole)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}"}],"explain":"<b>Vertical tangent</b> ⇔ \\(\\dfrac{dx}{d\\theta}=0\\) (with \\(\\dfrac{dy}{d\\theta}\\neq 0\\)), where \\(x=r\\cos\\theta=(1+\\cos\\theta)\\cos\\theta\\).<br>\\(\\dfrac{dx}{d\\theta}=-\\sin\\theta-2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta=-\\sin\\theta(1+2\\cos\\theta)=0\\). In \\((0,\\pi)\\), \\(\\sin\\theta\\neq 0\\), so \\(1+2\\cos\\theta=0\\Rightarrow\\cos\\theta=-\\tfrac12\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\)</b> uses \\(\\cos\\theta=+\\tfrac12\\) (sign error) and is actually a <i>horizontal</i>-tangent angle.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> zeros \\(\\dfrac{dy}{d\\theta}\\)-type expressions, not \\(\\dfrac{dx}{d\\theta}\\).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\)</b> doesn't satisfy \\(\\cos\\theta=-\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> vertical ⇔ \\(dx/d\\theta=0\\); differentiate \\(x=r\\cos\\theta\\) with the product rule, factor, and solve.","unit":9,"id":"u9m31","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"Over the interval \\(0\\le\\theta\\le 2\\pi\\), how many times does the circle \\(r=2\\cos\\theta\\) trace itself?","opts":[{"t":"Twice","correct":true},{"t":"Once"},{"t":"Three times"},{"t":"It does not close"}],"explain":"<b>\\(r=2\\cos\\theta\\) is a circle that completes once over \\([0,\\pi]\\).</b> On \\((\\tfrac\\pi2,\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2})\\), \\(\\cos\\theta\\lt 0\\) so \\(r\\lt 0\\), and those negative-\\(r\\) points <i>retrace</i> the same circle. Over the full \\([0,2\\pi]\\), the circle is therefore drawn <b>twice</b>.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Once</b> is the answer for \\([0,\\pi]\\), not \\([0,2\\pi]\\) — and assuming \"once\" over \\([0,2\\pi]\\) is what leads to double-counting area.<br>• <b>Three times / does not close</b> don't match the curve's behavior.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> circles like \\(r=a\\cos\\theta\\) close in \\(\\pi\\) radians; using \\(2\\pi\\) traces them twice — a frequent source of area errors.","unit":9,"id":"u9m32","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"Convert the rectangular point \\((-1,\\ 1)\\) to polar coordinates with \\(r\\gt 0\\) and \\(0\\le\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\left(\\sqrt2,\\ \\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)","correct":true},{"t":"\\left(\\sqrt2,\\ \\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)"},{"t":"\\left(2,\\ \\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)"},{"t":"\\left(\\sqrt2,\\ -\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)"}],"explain":"<b>Compute \\(r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\) and place \\(\\theta\\) in the correct quadrant.</b> \\(r=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2\\). The point \\((-1,1)\\) is in the <b>second quadrant</b>, so \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\) (the reference angle is \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\), adjusted into QII).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\)</b> is the bare \\(\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\tfrac{1}{-1}\\right)\\) output without fixing the quadrant — it points into QI, the wrong place.<br>• <b>\\(r=2\\)</b> forgot the square root.<br>• <b>\\(-\\tfrac\\pi4\\)</b> is outside \\([0,2\\pi)\\) and the wrong quadrant.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tan^{-1}(y/x)\\) only gives a reference angle; always check which quadrant \\((x,y)\\) lies in and adjust \\(\\theta\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m33","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"For the limaçon \\(r=3+2\\sin\\theta\\), what is the maximum distance from the origin to a point on the curve, and at what angle does it occur?","opts":[{"t":"r=5 at \\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"r=1 at \\theta=\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"r=5 at \\theta=0"},{"t":"r=3 at \\theta=0"}],"explain":"<b>\\(r\\) is the distance from the origin, so maximize \\(r=3+2\\sin\\theta\\).</b> The maximum of \\(\\sin\\theta\\) is \\(1\\), at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\), giving \\(r=3+2(1)=5\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(r=1\\) at \\(\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\)</b> is the <i>minimum</i> distance (where \\(\\sin\\theta=-1\\)).<br>• <b>\\(r=5\\) at \\(\\theta=0\\)</b> has the right \\(r\\) but wrong angle — at \\(\\theta=0\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=0\\) gives \\(r=3\\).<br>• <b>\\(r=3\\) at \\(\\theta=0\\)</b> is just the value at \\(\\theta=0\\), not the maximum.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> to extremize the distance, extremize \\(r(\\theta)\\); for \\(a+b\\sin\\theta\\) the max is \\(a+b\\) at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\), min \\(a-b\\) at \\(\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m34","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"At what smallest angle \\(\\theta\\gt 0\\) does the curve \\(r=2\\cos(3\\theta)\\) pass through the pole?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}"}],"explain":"<b>The curve is at the pole when \\(r=0\\):</b> \\(2\\cos(3\\theta)=0\\Rightarrow 3\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi6\\). These pole-crossings are exactly the petal boundaries.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi2\\)</b> solves \\(\\cos\\theta=0\\) forgetting the factor of \\(3\\) inside.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\)</b> solves \\(3\\theta=\\pi\\) (the next crossing, not the smallest).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}\\)</b> solves \\(3\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi4\\), which gives \\(\\cos\\tfrac\\pi4\\neq 0\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> set the whole \\(r\\)-expression to zero and solve including the inner coefficient; \\(\\cos(3\\theta)=0\\) at \\(3\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2,\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots\\)","unit":9,"id":"u9m35","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"Which of the following expressions gives the area of <b>one petal</b> of the rose \\(r=4\\cos(3\\theta)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/6}^{\\pi/6}\\big(4\\cos 3\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\big(4\\cos 3\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/6}^{\\pi/6}\\big(4\\cos 3\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/3}^{\\pi/3}\\big(4\\cos 3\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"}],"explain":"<b>Two things must be right: the \\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\) form and the petal's limits.</b> One petal spans consecutive pole-crossings: \\(4\\cos 3\\theta=0\\Rightarrow 3\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi2\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi6\\). So area \\(=\\tfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi/6}^{\\pi/6}(4\\cos 3\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Limits \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\)</b> sweep <i>all</i> petals (and retrace) — that's the whole-rose attempt, not one petal.<br>• <b>Missing the \\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> (third choice) doubles the area — the most common polar-area slip.<br>• <b>Limits \\(\\pm\\tfrac\\pi3\\)</b> span two petals' worth of angle.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for one petal, integrate \\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\) between adjacent \\(r=0\\) angles — never \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\), and never drop the \\(\\tfrac12\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m36","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the area of one loop of the lemniscate \\(r^2=18\\cos(2\\theta)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"9","correct":true},{"t":"18","correct":false},{"t":"\\dfrac{9}{2}"},{"t":"36"}],"explain":"<b>Because the equation gives \\(r^2\\) directly, the area formula simplifies beautifully:</b> \\(A=\\tfrac12\\displaystyle\\int r^2\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\int 18\\cos(2\\theta)\\,d\\theta\\). One loop runs where \\(r^2\\ge 0\\): \\(\\cos 2\\theta\\ge 0\\) on \\(\\left[-\\tfrac\\pi4,\\tfrac\\pi4\\right]\\). So \\(A=\\tfrac12\\int_{-\\pi/4}^{\\pi/4}18\\cos 2\\theta\\,d\\theta=9\\left[\\tfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{2}\\right]_{-\\pi/4}^{\\pi/4}=9\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>18</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) in the area formula.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac92\\)</b> mishandles the antiderivative of \\(\\cos 2\\theta\\) (forgets to divide by 2) — or uses half the interval.<br>• <b>36</b> drops the \\(\\tfrac12\\) and doubles via wrong limits.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> when given \\(r^2\\), substitute it straight into \\(\\tfrac12\\int r^2\\,d\\theta\\); the loop's limits are where \\(r^2=0\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m37","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"Find the area of the region inside \\(r=2+\\cos\\theta\\) and outside \\(r=1\\).","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}"},{"t":"3\\pi"},{"t":"\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}"}],"explain":"<b>The curves don't intersect</b> (\\(2+\\cos\\theta\\) ranges over \\([1,3]\\) and only touches \\(r=1\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi\\)), so the outer curve is \\(2+\\cos\\theta\\) and the inner is \\(1\\) over the full \\([0,2\\pi]\\): \\(A=\\tfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\big[(2+\\cos\\theta)^2-1^2\\big]\\,d\\theta\\).<br>Expand: \\((2+\\cos\\theta)^2-1=4+4\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta-1=3+4\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\\). Integrating over \\([0,2\\pi]\\): \\(3(2\\pi)+0+\\pi=7\\pi\\); times \\(\\tfrac12\\) gives \\(\\tfrac{7\\pi}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9\\pi}{2}\\)</b> forgets to subtract the inner region (used \\((2+\\cos\\theta)^2\\) alone: \\(\\tfrac12(8\\pi+\\pi)=\\tfrac{9\\pi}{2}\\)).<br>• <b>\\(3\\pi\\)</b> drops the \\(\\cos^2\\) contribution.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\)</b> applies an extra \\(\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> area between curves is \\(\\tfrac12\\int(r_{\\text{out}}^2-r_{\\text{in}}^2)\\,d\\theta\\); subtract the squares and integrate the \\(\\cos^2\\) term with the half-angle identity.","unit":9,"id":"u9m38","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"The region \\(R\\) lies inside the circle \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) and outside the cardioid \\(r=1+\\sin\\theta\\). Which expression gives the area of \\(R\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}\\big[(3\\sin\\theta)^2-(1+\\sin\\theta)^2\\big]\\,d\\theta","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\big[(3\\sin\\theta)^2-(1+\\sin\\theta)^2\\big]\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}\\big(3\\sin\\theta-(1+\\sin\\theta)\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}\\big[(1+\\sin\\theta)^2-(3\\sin\\theta)^2\\big]\\,d\\theta"}],"explain":"<b>Find the limits from the intersection, then subtract outer² − inner².</b> Setting \\(3\\sin\\theta=1+\\sin\\theta\\Rightarrow\\sin\\theta=\\tfrac12\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi6,\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\). Over that interval the circle \\(3\\sin\\theta\\) is outer, so \\(A=\\tfrac12\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}\\big[(3\\sin\\theta)^2-(1+\\sin\\theta)^2\\big]\\,d\\theta\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Limits \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\)</b> are the circle's own span, ignoring where the two curves actually cross.<br>• <b>\\((r_{\\text{out}}-r_{\\text{in}})^2\\)</b> squares the difference instead of differencing the squares — the signature polar mistake, since \\((a-b)^2\\neq a^2-b^2\\).<br>• <b>Reversed order</b> \\((r_{\\text{in}}^2-r_{\\text{out}}^2)\\) gives a negative area.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> limits come from \\(r_1=r_2\\); the integrand is (outer)² − (inner)², squares first.","unit":9,"id":"u9m39","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the total area enclosed by the rose \\(r=2\\sin(3\\theta)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"3\\pi"},{"t":"\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}"},{"t":"2\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>A rose \\(r=a\\sin(k\\theta)\\) with odd \\(k\\) has \\(k\\) petals.</b> Here \\(k=3\\), so 3 petals. One petal (from \\(\\theta=0\\) to \\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\)) has area \\(\\tfrac12\\int_0^{\\pi/3}(2\\sin 3\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\int_0^{\\pi/3}4\\sin^2 3\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi3\\). Total \\(=3\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi3=\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\)</b> is the area of <i>one</i> petal — forgetting to multiply by the 3 petals.<br>• <b>\\(3\\pi\\)</b> triples incorrectly (e.g. dropping the \\(\\tfrac12\\) then multiplying).<br>• <b>\\(2\\pi\\)</b> uses \\(2k\\) petals (the even-\\(k\\) rule) by mistake.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> compute one petal with the \\(r=0\\) limits, then multiply by the correct petal count (odd \\(k\\Rightarrow k\\)).","unit":9,"id":"u9m40","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":true,"prompt":"The region \\(R\\) lies inside both the circle \\(r=2\\) and the circle \\(r=4\\cos\\theta\\). The area of \\(R\\) is given by \\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-\\pi/3}^{\\pi/3}2^2\\,d\\theta+2\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}(4\\cos\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta\\). What is this area?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}-2\\sqrt3","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}","correct":false},{"t":"4\\pi-2\\sqrt3"},{"t":"\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}+2\\sqrt3"}],"explain":"<b>This is the \"area common to two curves\" split.</b> The curves meet at \\(\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi3\\) (from \\(4\\cos\\theta=2\\)). For \\(|\\theta|\\lt\\tfrac\\pi3\\) the circle \\(r=2\\) is the inner boundary; beyond that, \\(r=4\\cos\\theta\\) is.<br><b>First piece:</b> \\(\\tfrac12\\int_{-\\pi/3}^{\\pi/3}4\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\cdot 4\\cdot\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\).<br><b>Second piece</b> (using \\(\\cos^2\\theta=\\tfrac{1+\\cos2\\theta}{2}\\)): \\(\\int_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}16\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\theta=8\\left[\\theta+\\tfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{2}\\right]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}=\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}-2\\sqrt3\\).<br><b>Total:</b> \\(\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}+\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}-2\\sqrt3=\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}-2\\sqrt3\\approx 4.91\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\)</b> includes only the first piece — forgetting the two outer caps.<br>• <b>\\(4\\pi-2\\sqrt3\\)</b> mis-adds the \\(\\theta\\)-terms.<br>• <b>\\(+2\\sqrt3\\)</b> flips the sign of the \\(\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\) term.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> overlapping regions split at the intersection; each sub-region is bounded by whichever curve is closer to the pole there.","unit":9,"id":"u9m41","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":true,"prompt":"What is the area of one petal of the rose \\(r=4\\sin(2\\theta)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"2\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"\\pi","correct":false},{"t":"4\\pi"},{"t":"8"}],"explain":"<b>One petal of \\(r=4\\sin 2\\theta\\) spans \\([0,\\tfrac\\pi2]\\)</b> (where \\(\\sin 2\\theta\\ge 0\\), i.e. between \\(r=0\\) at \\(\\theta=0\\) and \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\)). Area \\(=\\tfrac12\\int_0^{\\pi/2}(4\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta=8\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2 2\\theta\\,d\\theta=8\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi4=2\\pi\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\pi\\)</b> uses wrong limits (e.g. \\([0,\\tfrac\\pi4]\\), half the petal).<br>• <b>\\(4\\pi\\)</b> drops the \\(\\tfrac12\\), doubling the result.<br>• <b>8</b> is \\(\\int_0^{\\pi/2}(4\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\) before applying \\(\\tfrac12\\) and the \\(\\sin^2\\) average — a partial computation.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> petal limits are adjacent \\(r=0\\) angles; integrate \\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\) and use \\(\\sin^2 u=\\tfrac{1-\\cos 2u}{2}\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m42","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"Which expression gives the area enclosed by the <b>inner loop</b> of the limaçon \\(r=1+2\\cos\\theta\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{2\\pi/3}^{4\\pi/3}\\big(1+2\\cos\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\big(1+2\\cos\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\big(1+2\\cos\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"},{"t":"\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{\\pi/3}^{2\\pi/3}\\big(1+2\\cos\\theta\\big)^2\\,d\\theta"}],"explain":"<b>The inner loop is traced where \\(r\\lt 0\\), bounded by the angles where \\(r=0\\).</b> \\(1+2\\cos\\theta=0\\Rightarrow\\cos\\theta=-\\tfrac12\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\). The loop is swept as \\(\\theta\\) goes between these, so area \\(=\\tfrac12\\int_{2\\pi/3}^{4\\pi/3}(1+2\\cos\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\)</b> would give the entire limaçon (outer region included), not just the inner loop.<br>• <b>\\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\)</b> is an arbitrary half, not aligned to the \\(r=0\\) angles.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\) to \\(\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\)</b> uses one wrong endpoint (\\(\\tfrac\\pi3\\) isn't where \\(r=0\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the inner loop's limits are exactly the two \\(r=0\\) angles; integrating \\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\) between them captures the loop (the square handles the negative \\(r\\) automatically).","unit":9,"id":"u9m43","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the area of one petal of the rose \\(r=3\\cos(2\\theta)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{16}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}"}],"explain":"<b>One petal spans the \\(r=0\\) angles:</b> \\(3\\cos 2\\theta=0\\Rightarrow 2\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi2\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi4\\). Area \\(=\\tfrac12\\int_{-\\pi/4}^{\\pi/4}(3\\cos 2\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\int_{-\\pi/4}^{\\pi/4}9\\cos^2 2\\theta\\,d\\theta\\). With \\(\\cos^2 u=\\tfrac{1+\\cos 2u}{2}\\), the integral of \\(\\cos^2 2\\theta\\) over \\([-\\tfrac\\pi4,\\tfrac\\pi4]\\) is \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\), so \\(A=\\tfrac12\\cdot 9\\cdot\\tfrac\\pi4=\\tfrac{9\\pi}{8}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9\\pi}{4}\\)</b> forgets the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from the area formula.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9\\pi}{16}\\)</b> halves once too often.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\)</b> mishandles the \\(\\cos^2\\) integration (or uses \\(a\\) instead of \\(a^2\\)).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> integrate \\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\) over one petal's \\(r=0\\)-to-\\(r=0\\) span; the \\(\\cos^2\\) term contributes its average of \\(\\tfrac12\\) times the interval length.","unit":9,"id":"u9m44","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":false,"prompt":"For a polar curve \\(r=f(\\theta)\\), the expression \\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_\\alpha^\\beta\\big[f(\\theta)\\big]^2\\,d\\theta\\) represents which of the following?","opts":[{"t":"The area swept out by the radius as \\(\\theta\\) goes from \\(\\alpha\\) to \\(\\beta\\)","correct":true},{"t":"The arc length of the curve from \\(\\alpha\\) to \\(\\beta\\)"},{"t":"The average value of \\(r\\) on \\([\\alpha,\\beta]\\)"},{"t":"The slope of the curve at \\(\\beta\\)"}],"explain":"<b>\\(\\tfrac12 r^2\\,d\\theta\\) is the area of a thin circular sector</b>, so the integral \\(\\tfrac12\\int_\\alpha^\\beta r^2\\,d\\theta\\) accumulates those sectors into the <b>area swept</b> by the radius between \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>Arc length</b> would be \\(\\int\\sqrt{r^2+(r')^2}\\,d\\theta\\) — a different integrand entirely (and units of length, not area).<br>• <b>Average value of \\(r\\)</b> would be \\(\\tfrac{1}{\\beta-\\alpha}\\int r\\,d\\theta\\) (no square, no \\(\\tfrac12\\)).<br>• <b>Slope</b> is a derivative, not an integral.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> recognize \\(\\tfrac12\\int r^2\\,d\\theta\\) on sight as polar <i>area</i> — the square and the \\(\\tfrac12\\) come from the sector, and the result has units of area.","unit":9,"id":"u9m45","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":true,"prompt":"The region \\(R\\) lies inside both circles \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(r=3\\cos\\theta\\). What is the area of \\(R\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\dfrac{9(\\pi-2)}{8}","correct":true},{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}"},{"t":"\\dfrac{9(\\pi-2)}{4}"}],"explain":"<b>The two circles intersect at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi4\\)</b> (where \\(3\\sin\\theta=3\\cos\\theta\\)) and at the pole. By symmetry about \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi4\\), the overlap is twice the area swept by \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\): \\(A=2\\cdot\\tfrac12\\int_0^{\\pi/4}(3\\sin\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta=9\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\theta\\). With \\(\\sin^2\\theta=\\tfrac{1-\\cos 2\\theta}{2}\\): \\(=9\\cdot\\tfrac12\\left[\\theta-\\tfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{2}\\right]_0^{\\pi/4}=\\tfrac92\\left(\\tfrac\\pi4-\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac{9(\\pi-2)}{8}\\approx 1.28\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9\\pi}{8}\\)</b> drops the \\(-\\tfrac12\\) from the \\(\\sin 2\\theta\\) evaluation (forgetting that term).<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9\\pi}{4}\\)</b> is the area of a full circle of radius \\(\\tfrac32\\) — not the overlap.<br>• <b>\\(\\tfrac{9(\\pi-2)}{4}\\)</b> forgets a factor of \\(\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for symmetric overlaps, integrate one curve up to the line of symmetry and double; the bounding curve switches at the intersection angle.","unit":9,"id":"u9m46","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"area","calc":true,"prompt":"Find the area of the region inside the cardioid \\(r=2+2\\cos\\theta\\) and outside the circle \\(r=2\\).","opts":[{"t":"8+\\pi","correct":true},{"t":"\\pi","correct":false},{"t":"8","correct":false},{"t":"8+2\\pi"}],"explain":"<b>Find where they meet, then integrate outer² − inner² over that range.</b> \\(2+2\\cos\\theta=2\\Rightarrow\\cos\\theta=0\\Rightarrow\\theta=\\pm\\tfrac\\pi2\\). Between these the cardioid is outside the circle, so \\(A=\\tfrac12\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\big[(2+2\\cos\\theta)^2-2^2\\big]\\,d\\theta\\).<br>Expand: \\((2+2\\cos\\theta)^2-4=8\\cos\\theta+4\\cos^2\\theta\\). Integrating over \\([-\\tfrac\\pi2,\\tfrac\\pi2]\\): \\(\\int 8\\cos\\theta=16\\) and \\(\\int 4\\cos^2\\theta=2\\pi\\). So \\(A=\\tfrac12(16+2\\pi)=8+\\pi\\approx 11.14\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b><br>• <b>8</b> drops the \\(\\cos^2\\) (the \\(\\pi\\) term).<br>• <b>\\(\\pi\\)</b> keeps only the \\(\\cos^2\\) part, losing the \\(8\\) from \\(\\int 8\\cos\\theta\\).<br>• <b>\\(8+2\\pi\\)</b> forgets the outer \\(\\tfrac12\\) on the \\(\\cos^2\\) piece.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> intersection gives the limits; integrand is (outer)² − (inner)²; integrate the \\(\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos^2\\theta\\) terms carefully over the symmetric interval.","unit":9,"id":"u9m47","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"A curve is given by \\(x(t)=t^{2}\\), \\(y(t)=t^{3}\\). What is the speed of a particle on this curve at \\(t=1\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\sqrt{13}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(5\\)"},{"t":"\\(13\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Speed is the magnitude of the velocity vector</b>, \\(\\sqrt{\\big(x'(t)\\big)^{2}+\\big(y'(t)\\big)^{2}}\\) — not a slope and not a single derivative.<br><br>\\(x'=2t\\) and \\(y'=3t^{2}\\), so at \\(t=1\\) the velocity is \\(\\langle 2,3\\rangle\\) and the speed is \\(\\sqrt{4+9}=\\sqrt{13}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac32\\)</b> is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y'}{x'}\\), the <i>slope</i> of the path — a direction, not a rate of travel. <b>5</b> adds the components \\(2+3\\) instead of using Pythagoras. <b>13</b> forgets the square root, leaving speed squared.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> velocity is a vector, speed is its length. Square the components, add, then take the root — the same operation every time, in parametric, vector and polar problems alike.","unit":9,"id":"u9m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"For the same curve \\(x(t)=t^{2}\\), \\(y(t)=t^{3}\\), what is \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\\) at \\(t=1\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)"},{"t":"\\(3\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{6}{2}=3t\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The second parametric derivative is a two-step chain, and the second step is the one everybody drops.</b><br><br>First \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y'}{x'}=\\dfrac{3t^{2}}{2t}=\\dfrac{3t}{2}\\).<br>Then \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\!\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\frac{dx}{dt}}=\\dfrac{3/2}{2t}=\\dfrac{3}{4t}\\), which at \\(t=1\\) is \\(\\dfrac34\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac32\\)</b> is the first derivative \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) at \\(t=1\\), one step short. <b>3</b> differentiates \\(\\frac{3t}{2}\\) with respect to \\(t\\) and stops — that is \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)\\), which still has to be divided by \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\). <b>\\(\\frac{y''}{x''}\\)</b> is the tempting shortcut and it is simply wrong: second derivatives do not divide like first ones.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> differentiate \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) with respect to \\(t\\), then divide by \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) again. Never \\(\\frac{y''}{x''}\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"param","calc":false,"prompt":"Which expression gives the length of the path traced by \\(x(t)=t^{2}\\), \\(y(t)=t^{3}\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le 1\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\sqrt{4t^{2}+9t^{4}}\\;dt\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\sqrt{t^{4}+t^{6}}\\;dt\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\big(2t+3t^{2}\\big)dt\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\sqrt{1+\\Big(\\tfrac{3t}{2}\\Big)^{2}}\\;dt\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Parametric arc length integrates the speed:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\big(x'\\big)^{2}+\\big(y'\\big)^{2}}\\,dt\\).<br><br>With \\(x'=2t\\) and \\(y'=3t^{2}\\) that is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\sqrt{4t^{2}+9t^{4}}\\,dt\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second squares the <i>functions</i> rather than their derivatives. The third integrates \\(x'+y'\\) with no root — adding component speeds instead of combining them by Pythagoras. The fourth uses the Cartesian formula \\(\\sqrt{1+(dy/dx)^{2}}\\) but integrates \\(dt\\); that form is correct only with \\(dx\\), and mixing the two is the most seductive error here because both formulas are on the sheet.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> match the formula to the variable you are integrating in. Parametric with \\(dt\\) needs \\(\\sqrt{(x')^{2}+(y')^{2}}\\); Cartesian with \\(dx\\) needs \\(\\sqrt{1+(y')^{2}}\\).","unit":9,"id":"u9m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"Which expression gives the area enclosed by the cardioid \\(r=1+\\cos\\theta\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1+\\cos\\theta)^{2}\\,d\\theta\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1+\\cos\\theta)^{2}\\,d\\theta\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1+\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi}(1+\\cos\\theta)^{2}\\,d\\theta\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Polar area is \\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_\\alpha^\\beta r^{2}\\,d\\theta\\)</b> — the \\(\\tfrac12\\) and the square both come from the area of a circular sector, \\(\\tfrac12r^{2}\\Delta\\theta\\).<br><br>A cardioid is traced once as \\(\\theta\\) runs from \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\), so that is the full interval, and the area works out to \\(\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second omits the \\(\\tfrac12\\), doubling the answer. The third omits the square, integrating the radius rather than the sector area. The fourth sweeps only \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\), which covers half the curve — a genuine trap, because for <i>some</i> polar curves half the range is right, so the range must be justified by how the curve is traced rather than assumed.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\tfrac12\\int r^{2}d\\theta\\), and decide the limits by asking over what \\(\\theta\\) the curve is drawn exactly once.","unit":9,"id":"u9m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"The rose \\(r=2\\sin(2\\theta)\\) has four petals, one of which is traced as \\(\\theta\\) goes from \\(0\\) to \\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\). What is the area of that single petal?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\pi\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\pi\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Apply \\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int r^{2}d\\theta\\) over the interval that draws one petal.</b><br><br>\\(\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}4\\sin^{2}(2\\theta)\\,d\\theta=2\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^{2}(2\\theta)\\,d\\theta\\). Using \\(\\sin^{2}u=\\tfrac{1-\\cos 2u}{2}\\), the integral is \\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\), so the area is \\(2\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\pi\\)</b> drops the \\(\\tfrac12\\) from the polar area formula. <b>\\(2\\pi\\)</b> is the area of all four petals — answering a question that was not asked. <b>\\(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\)</b> is the value of the integral before multiplying by the \\(2\\) that came from \\(r^{2}=4\\sin^{2}(2\\theta)\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> for roses, the hard part is the limits, not the integral. Find where \\(r=0\\) on either side of a petal — here \\(\\theta=0\\) and \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\) — and integrate between them.","unit":9,"id":"u9m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"polar","calc":false,"prompt":"For a polar curve \\(r=f(\\theta)\\), the value of \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) is found by","opts":[{"t":"writing \\(x=r\\cos\\theta\\), \\(y=r\\sin\\theta\\) and computing \\(\\dfrac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"computing \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\)"},{"t":"computing \\(\\dfrac{r}{\\theta}\\)"},{"t":"computing \\(\\dfrac{dr/d\\theta}{r}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>A polar curve is a parametric curve in disguise</b>, with \\(\\theta\\) as the parameter: \\(x=r\\cos\\theta=f(\\theta)\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(y=f(\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\). The slope in the \\(xy\\)-plane is therefore the usual parametric slope \\(\\dfrac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}\\), and both derivatives need the product rule.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\)</b> measures how fast the <i>radius</i> grows as you sweep round — it says nothing about the direction of the curve in the plane, and it is the most common wrong answer because it is the only derivative on display. The other two are expressions with no geometric meaning here.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) always means the slope of the tangent in the \\(xy\\)-plane. Convert to \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) first, then differentiate — never differentiate \\(r\\) and hope.","unit":9,"id":"u9m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle has position \\(\\big\\langle x(t),y(t)\\big\\rangle\\) with velocity \\(\\big\\langle 2,-3\\big\\rangle\\) at \\(t=4\\), and \\(x(4)=1\\), \\(y(4)=5\\). Using a tangent-line approximation, the position at \\(t=4.1\\) is approximately","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\big\\langle 1.2,\\;4.7\\big\\rangle\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\big\\langle 3,\\;2\\big\\rangle\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\big\\langle 2.2,\\;1.7\\big\\rangle\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\big\\langle 1.02,\\;4.97\\big\\rangle\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Linear approximation works component by component:</b> \\(x(4.1)\\approx x(4)+x'(4)\\Delta t\\) and likewise for \\(y\\), with \\(\\Delta t=0.1\\).<br><br>\\(x\\approx 1+2(0.1)=1.2\\) and \\(y\\approx 5+(-3)(0.1)=4.7\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\langle3,2\\rangle\\)</b> adds the whole velocity, using \\(\\Delta t=1\\) instead of \\(0.1\\). <b>\\(\\langle2.2,1.7\\rangle\\)</b> adds both the velocity and the scaled velocity, double-counting. <b>\\(\\langle1.02,4.97\\rangle\\)</b> uses \\(\\Delta t=0.01\\), an order-of-magnitude slip.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> a vector-valued tangent-line approximation is just two ordinary ones run in parallel. Keep the sign of each component — the negative \\(y\\)-velocity means the particle is moving <i>down</i>.","unit":9,"id":"u9m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"vector","calc":false,"prompt":"A particle moves with velocity vector \\(\\mathbf{v}(t)\\). The total distance travelled from \\(t=a\\) to \\(t=b\\) is","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\left|\\mathbf{v}(t)\\right|dt\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\mathbf{v}(t)\\,dt\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\left|\\displaystyle\\int_a^b\\mathbf{v}(t)\\,dt\\right|\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\mathbf{v}(b)-\\mathbf{v}(a)\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Distance accumulates speed, and speed is the magnitude of velocity</b>, so you take the magnitude <i>before</i> integrating: \\(\\int_a^b|\\mathbf v|\\,dt=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(x')^{2}+(y')^{2}}\\,dt\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second integrates the vector itself, which gives <i>displacement</i> — a vector from start to finish. The third takes the magnitude of that displacement, i.e. the straight-line distance between endpoints; a particle that loops back to where it started gives \\(0\\) here while having travelled a long way. The fourth is the change in velocity, which is related to acceleration, not position.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the order of the magnitude and the integral is the whole question. Magnitude first gives distance travelled; integral first gives net displacement.","unit":9,"id":"u9m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Apply the ratio test. What does it tell you about this series?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n!}{n^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges (L = 1/e < 1)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges (L = e > 1)"},{"t":"Inconclusive (L = 1)"},{"t":"Converges (L = 0)"}],"explain":"The ratio is \\(\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot\\frac{n^{n}}{n!}=\\frac{n^{n}}{(n+1)^{n}}=\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)^{n}\\). That limit is the classic \\(\\left(1+\\frac1n\\right)^{-n}\\to \\frac{1}{e}\\approx 0.368\\). Since \\(L=\\frac1e\\lt 1\\), it converges. The trap is reading the limit as 1 — it's \\(1/e\\), which takes recognizing the \\(e\\)-limit form.","unit":10,"id":"u10m0","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine whether the series converges or diverges.","math":"\\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n\\ln n}","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (integral test)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (integral test)"},{"t":"Converges (comparison to \\(\\sum 1/n^{2}\\))"},{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)"}],"explain":"Use the integral test: \\(\\int_2^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{x\\ln x}=\\big[\\ln(\\ln x)\\big]_2^{\\infty}=\\infty\\). So it <b>diverges</b>. This is the famous borderline: \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n(\\ln n)^p}\\) converges only for \\(p\\gt 1\\), and here \\(p=1\\). It looks like it should converge (terms shrink fast), but it doesn't — and the nth-term test is useless since the terms do → 0.","unit":10,"id":"u10m1","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"Find the interval of convergence (check both endpoints).","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x+1)^{n}}{n^{2}\\,4^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"[-5, 3]","correct":true},{"t":"(-5, 3)"},{"t":"(-5, 3]"},{"t":"[-5, 3)"}],"explain":"Ratio test: \\(\\frac{|x+1|}{4}\\cdot\\frac{n^2}{(n+1)^2}\\to\\frac{|x+1|}{4}\\lt 1\\Rightarrow|x+1|\\lt 4\\), so \\(R=4\\), center \\(-1\\), interior \\((-5,3)\\). <b>Both</b> endpoints land on a \\(p=2\\) series: at \\(x=3\\), \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n^2}\\) converges; at \\(x=-5\\), \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}\\) converges absolutely. The \\(n^2\\) in the denominator is the tell — both ends are included: <b>[-5, 3]</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m2","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"What is the coefficient of x⁶ in the Maclaurin series for sin(x²)?","math":"\\sin u = u-\\frac{u^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{u^{5}}{5!}-\\cdots","opts":[{"t":"-1/6","correct":true},{"t":"1/6"},{"t":"-1/120"},{"t":"-1/720"}],"explain":"Substitute \\(u=x^2\\): \\(\\sin(x^2)=x^2-\\frac{(x^2)^3}{3!}+\\cdots=x^2-\\frac{x^6}{6}+\\cdots\\). The \\(x^6\\) term comes from \\(-\\frac{u^3}{3!}\\) with \\(u^3=x^6\\), giving coefficient \\(-\\frac{1}{3!}=-\\frac16\\). The trap \\(-\\frac{1}{720}\\) comes from using \\(6!\\) instead of recognizing \\(x^6\\) is the <i>cube</i> of \\(x^2\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m3","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"error","prompt":"The third-degree Maclaurin polynomial \\(P_3\\) for \\(e^{x}\\) approximates \\(e^{0.5}\\). By the Lagrange error bound, the error is at most:","math":"R_3 \\le \\frac{\\max|f^{(4)}(c)|}{4!}\\,|x|^{4}","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^{0.5}\\cdot(0.5)^{4}/4!\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((0.5)^{4}/4!\\)"},{"t":"\\(e^{0.5}\\cdot(0.5)^{4}/3!\\)"},{"t":"\\((0.5)^{5}/5!\\)"}],"explain":"For \\(f(x)=e^x\\), every derivative is \\(e^x\\), so \\(f^{(4)}(c)=e^c\\). On \\([0,0.5]\\) the max is \\(e^{0.5}\\). Lagrange gives \\(R_3\\le\\frac{e^{0.5}}{4!}(0.5)^4\\approx 0.0043\\). The key steps: it's the <b>(n+1)th</b> derivative (4th, so \\(4!\\)), and \\(M\\) is the max of that derivative on the interval — not just 1.","unit":10,"id":"u10m4","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"Which statement is always TRUE?","opts":[{"t":"If Σ|aₙ| converges, then Σaₙ converges.","correct":true},{"t":"If Σaₙ converges, then Σ|aₙ| converges."},{"t":"If aₙ → 0, then Σaₙ converges."},{"t":"If Σaₙ converges, then aₙ → 0 is not required."}],"explain":"Absolute convergence implies convergence — that's a theorem, so the first statement is always true. The second is false (the alternating harmonic series converges but \\(\\sum\\frac1n\\) doesn't — conditional convergence). The third is the most common error: \\(a_n\\to 0\\) is <i>necessary</i> but not <i>sufficient</i> (harmonic series). The fourth contradicts the nth-term test.","unit":10,"id":"u10m5","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum of the series.","math":"\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{3^{n}+1}{4^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"16/3","correct":true},{"t":"4"},{"t":"7"},{"t":"4/3"}],"explain":"Split it into two geometric series: \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac34\\right)^n+\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac14\\right)^n=\\frac{1}{1-3/4}+\\frac{1}{1-1/4}=4+\\frac43=\\frac{16}{3}\\). The skill is recognizing \\(\\frac{3^n+1}{4^n}=\\left(\\frac34\\right)^n+\\left(\\frac14\\right)^n\\) — one series in disguise as a sum of two. The distractor 4 is just the first piece.","unit":10,"id":"u10m6","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine whether the series converges or diverges.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (telescoping to −∞)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges to 0"},{"t":"Converges (alternating)"},{"t":"Converges to −ln 2"}],"explain":"Rewrite using log rules: \\(\\ln\\frac{n}{n+1}=\\ln n-\\ln(n+1)\\). The partial sum telescopes: \\(S_N=\\ln 1-\\ln(N+1)=-\\ln(N+1)\\to-\\infty\\). So it <b>diverges</b>. The terms → 0, which baits you toward \"converges,\" but the partial sums march off to \\(-\\infty\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m7","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"For which value of p does the series converge CONDITIONALLY?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n^{p}}","opts":[{"t":"p = 1/2","correct":true},{"t":"p = 2"},{"t":"p = 3"},{"t":"p = 0"}],"explain":"Conditional means it converges but not absolutely — which needs \\(0\\lt p\\le 1\\). At \\(p=\\frac12\\): the alternating series converges (terms \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}\\downarrow 0\\)), but \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}\\) is a \\(p=\\frac12\\) series that diverges → conditional. For \\(p=2,3\\) it's absolute; for \\(p=0\\) the terms don't → 0 so it diverges.","unit":10,"id":"u10m8","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"What is the radius of convergence?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n^{2}\\,x^{n}}{2^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"R = 2","correct":true},{"t":"R = 1/2"},{"t":"R = 1"},{"t":"R = 4"}],"explain":"Ratio test: \\(\\left|\\frac{(n+1)^2x^{n+1}}{2^{n+1}}\\cdot\\frac{2^n}{n^2x^n}\\right|=\\frac{|x|}{2}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^2\\to\\frac{|x|}{2}\\). Set \\(\\lt 1\\): \\(|x|\\lt 2\\), so \\(R=2\\). The \\(n^2\\) factor doesn't affect the radius — \\(\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^2\\to 1\\) — which is the insight being tested.","unit":10,"id":"u10m9","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{2+\\sin n}{n^{2}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges (direct comparison)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges (oscillating terms)"},{"t":"Inconclusive (sin n has no limit)"},{"t":"Converges (alternating)"}],"explain":"Since \\(-1\\le\\sin n\\le 1\\), the numerator satisfies \\(1\\le 2+\\sin n\\le 3\\). So \\(0\\lt \\frac{2+\\sin n}{n^2}\\le\\frac{3}{n^2}\\), and \\(\\sum\\frac{3}{n^2}\\) converges (p = 2). By direct comparison, it <b>converges</b>. The oscillation of \\(\\sin n\\) is a red herring — you bound it, you don't need it to converge.","unit":10,"id":"u10m10","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"The Maclaurin series for f is given below. What is f⁽⁵⁾(0)?","math":"f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^{n}}{n+1}","opts":[{"t":"20","correct":true},{"t":"1/6"},{"t":"120"},{"t":"1/720"}],"explain":"The coefficient of \\(x^5\\) here is \\(\\frac{1}{5+1}=\\frac16\\). But in any Maclaurin series the coefficient of \\(x^n\\) equals \\(\\frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}\\). So \\(\\frac{f^{(5)}(0)}{5!}=\\frac16\\Rightarrow f^{(5)}(0)=\\frac{5!}{6}=\\frac{120}{6}=20\\). The trap \\(\\frac16\\) forgets to multiply back by \\(5!\\) — the coefficient and the derivative differ by a factorial.","unit":10,"id":"u10m11","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Apply the ratio test. What does it tell you?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n^{2}}{2^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges \\((L=\\tfrac12\\lt 1)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges \\((L=2\\gt 1)\\)"},{"t":"Inconclusive \\((L=1)\\)"},{"t":"Diverges \\((L=\\infty)\\)"}],"explain":"The ratio is \\(\\frac{(n+1)^2}{2^{n+1}}\\cdot\\frac{2^n}{n^2}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^2\\to\\frac12\\). Since \\(L=\\frac12\\lt 1\\), it converges. The polynomial factor \\(\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n}\\right)^2\\to 1\\) doesn't change the limit — the \\(2^n\\) dominates.","unit":10,"id":"u10m12","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum of the series.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}","opts":[{"t":"1","correct":true},{"t":"1/2"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"diverges"}],"explain":"Partial fractions: \\(\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}=\\frac1n-\\frac{1}{n+1}\\). The partial sum telescopes: \\(S_N=1-\\frac{1}{N+1}\\to 1\\). A telescoping series — the value is exactly <b>1</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m13","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine whether the series converges or diverges.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{0.999}}","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (p-series, p < 1)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (p-series, p > 1)"},{"t":"Converges (close enough to p = 1)"},{"t":"Inconclusive"}],"explain":"This is a p-series with \\(p=0.999\\). Convergence requires \\(p\\gt 1\\) — strictly. Since \\(0.999\\lt 1\\), it <b>diverges</b>, even though it's agonizingly close to the boundary. \"Close to 1\" is exactly the trap: the cutoff is sharp.","unit":10,"id":"u10m14","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum of the series.","math":"\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)^{n}","opts":[{"t":"2/3","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"1/2"},{"t":"-2"}],"explain":"Geometric with \\(a=1\\), \\(r=-\\frac12\\). Since \\(|r|\\lt 1\\): sum \\(=\\frac{1}{1-(-1/2)}=\\frac{1}{3/2}=\\frac23\\). The negative ratio is the only wrinkle — the formula \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) handles it directly.","unit":10,"id":"u10m15","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"What is the coefficient of \\(x^{3}\\) in the Maclaurin series for \\(\\ln(1+x)\\)?","math":"\\ln(1+x)=x-\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}-\\cdots","opts":[{"t":"1/3","correct":true},{"t":"-1/3"},{"t":"1/6"},{"t":"-1/2"}],"explain":"The series is \\(\\ln(1+x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n}x^{n}\\). The \\(x^3\\) term has \\(n=3\\): \\(\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{3}=+\\frac13\\). The signs alternate starting positive, so \\(x^3\\) is positive.","unit":10,"id":"u10m16","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"Find the interval of convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}","opts":[{"t":"[-1, 1)","correct":true},{"t":"(-1, 1)"},{"t":"(-1, 1]"},{"t":"[-1, 1]"}],"explain":"Ratio test gives \\(|x|\\lt 1\\), so \\(R=1\\), center 0. At \\(x=-1\\): \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n}{\\sqrt n}\\) converges (alternating). At \\(x=1\\): \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}\\) is a \\(p=\\frac12\\) series, diverges. Include the left end only: <b>[-1, 1)</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m17","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"Classify the convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges absolutely","correct":true},{"t":"Converges conditionally"},{"t":"Diverges"},{"t":"Converges to 1"}],"explain":"Take absolute values: \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{2^n}\\) is a geometric series with \\(r=\\frac12\\), which converges. Since the absolute series converges, the original converges <b>absolutely</b> — no need to even check the alternating behavior.","unit":10,"id":"u10m18","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\cos\\!\\left(\\tfrac{1}{n}\\right)","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (terms → 0)"},{"t":"Converges (comparison)"},{"t":"Inconclusive"}],"explain":"As \\(n\\to\\infty\\), \\(\\frac1n\\to 0\\), so \\(\\cos\\frac1n\\to\\cos 0=1\\neq 0\\). The terms don't approach 0, so the series <b>diverges</b> by the nth-term test. The trap is assuming \\(\\frac1n\\to 0\\) inside makes the terms vanish — but the cosine sends them to 1.","unit":10,"id":"u10m19","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"error","prompt":"\\(P_2(x)=1-\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\) is the 2nd-degree Maclaurin polynomial for \\(\\cos x\\). Use it to approximate \\(\\cos(0.2)\\).","opts":[{"t":"0.98","correct":true},{"t":"0.96"},{"t":"1.02"},{"t":"0.99"}],"explain":"\\(P_2(0.2)=1-\\frac{(0.2)^2}{2}=1-\\frac{0.04}{2}=1-0.02=0.98\\). (The true value \\(\\cos 0.2\\approx 0.98007\\), so the approximation is excellent.)","unit":10,"id":"u10m20","calc":true,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{2}-n+1}","opts":[{"t":"Converges (limit comparison to \\(\\sum 1/n^{2}\\))","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges (limit comparison to \\(\\sum 1/n\\))"},{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)"},{"t":"Inconclusive"}],"explain":"For large \\(n\\), \\(\\frac{1}{n^2-n+1}\\) behaves like \\(\\frac{1}{n^2}\\). Limit comparison: \\(\\lim\\frac{1/(n^2-n+1)}{1/n^2}=1\\), finite and positive. Since \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n^2}\\) converges (p = 2), so does this. <b>Converges.</b>","unit":10,"id":"u10m21","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"What is the radius of convergence?","math":"\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^{n}}{n!}","opts":[{"t":"R = ∞","correct":true},{"t":"R = 1"},{"t":"R = 0"},{"t":"R = e"}],"explain":"Ratio test: \\(\\left|\\frac{x^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot\\frac{n!}{x^n}\\right|=\\frac{|x|}{n+1}\\to 0\\) for <i>every</i> \\(x\\). The limit is always \\(\\lt 1\\), so it converges for all real \\(x\\): \\(R=\\infty\\). (This series is \\(e^x\\).)","unit":10,"id":"u10m22","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"The ratio test gives L = 1 (inconclusive) for which series?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\sum 1/n^{2}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\sum 2^{n}/n!\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sum n!\\,x^{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sum (1/3)^{n}\\)"}],"explain":"The ratio test always gives \\(L=1\\) on a p-series: for \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n^2}\\), \\(\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)^2\\to 1\\). That's why you need a different test (here, p-series) to conclude. The others give clean limits (0, ∞, and \\(\\frac13\\) respectively).","unit":10,"id":"u10m23","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Apply the root test. What does it tell you?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n}{2n+1}\\right)^{n}","opts":[{"t":"Converges \\((L=\\tfrac12)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges \\((L=2)\\)"},{"t":"Inconclusive \\((L=1)\\)"},{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)"}],"explain":"The \\(n\\)th power signals the root test: \\(\\sqrt[n]{\\left(\\frac{n}{2n+1}\\right)^n}=\\frac{n}{2n+1}\\to\\frac12\\). Since \\(L=\\frac12\\lt 1\\), it <b>converges</b>. The root test is the natural tool whenever the whole term is raised to the \\(n\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m24","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum of the series.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+2}\\right)","opts":[{"t":"3/2","correct":true},{"t":"1"},{"t":"1/2"},{"t":"2"}],"explain":"This telescopes, but with a gap of 2. Writing out terms, everything cancels except the first two: \\(S=\\frac11+\\frac12=\\frac32\\). (Each \\(\\frac1k\\) for \\(k\\ge 3\\) is cancelled by a later \\(-\\frac1k\\).) Sum = <b>3/2</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m25","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"found","prompt":"This p-series converges. To what value?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{2}}","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\pi^{2}/6\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\pi^{2}/4\\)"},{"t":"π/2"},{"t":"1"}],"explain":"This is the famous Basel problem: \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^2}=\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}\\approx 1.645\\). It converges (p = 2 > 1), and its exact value is one worth knowing.","unit":10,"id":"u10m26","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum: \\(4-2+1-\\tfrac12+\\cdots\\)","opts":[{"t":"8/3","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"8"},{"t":"4/3"}],"explain":"Geometric with first term \\(a=4\\) and ratio \\(r=-\\frac12\\) (each term is \\(-\\frac12\\) the previous). Sum \\(=\\frac{4}{1-(-1/2)}=\\frac{4}{3/2}=\\frac83\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m27","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"What is the coefficient of \\(x^{2}\\) in the Maclaurin series for \\(e^{-x}\\)?","math":"e^{u}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{u^{n}}{n!}","opts":[{"t":"1/2","correct":true},{"t":"-1/2"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"-1"}],"explain":"Substitute \\(u=-x\\): \\(e^{-x}=\\sum\\frac{(-x)^n}{n!}=\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n x^n}{n!}\\). The \\(x^2\\) term is \\(n=2\\): \\(\\frac{(-1)^2}{2!}=\\frac12\\). The square makes the sign positive — the \\(-1/2\\) trap forgets \\((-1)^2=+1\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m28","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"Find the interval of convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-4)^{n}}{5^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"(-1, 9)","correct":true},{"t":"[-1, 9]"},{"t":"[-1, 9)"},{"t":"(-1, 9]"}],"explain":"Ratio test: \\(\\frac{|x-4|}{5}\\lt 1\\Rightarrow|x-4|\\lt 5\\), so \\(R=5\\), center 4, interior \\((-1,9)\\). At both endpoints the terms become \\((\\pm 1)^n\\), which don't approach 0 — so both diverge by the nth-term test. Neither endpoint is included: <b>(-1, 9)</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m29","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"Classify the convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\ln n}{n}","opts":[{"t":"Converges conditionally","correct":true},{"t":"Converges absolutely"},{"t":"Diverges"},{"t":"Converges to ln 2"}],"explain":"By the alternating series test it converges: \\(\\frac{\\ln n}{n}\\to 0\\) and is eventually decreasing. But \\(\\sum\\frac{\\ln n}{n}\\) diverges (its terms exceed \\(\\frac1n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), and the harmonic series diverges). Converges but not absolutely → <b>conditional</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m30","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(1+\\tfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (root test)"},{"t":"Converges (terms → 0)"},{"t":"Inconclusive"}],"explain":"The terms \\(\\left(1+\\frac1n\\right)^n\\to e\\approx 2.718\\neq 0\\). Since the terms approach \\(e\\), not 0, the series <b>diverges</b> by the nth-term test. Recognizing the famous \\(e\\)-limit is the key — the terms grow toward a nonzero constant.","unit":10,"id":"u10m31","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"error","prompt":"\\(P_3(x)=x-\\frac{x^{3}}{6}\\) approximates \\(\\sin x\\). By the Lagrange bound, the error at \\(x=0.5\\) is at most:","opts":[{"t":"\\((0.5)^{4}/4!\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((0.5)^{3}/3!\\)"},{"t":"\\((0.5)^{4}/3!\\)"},{"t":"\\((0.5)^{5}/5!\\)"}],"explain":"For \\(\\sin x\\), all derivatives are \\(\\pm\\sin\\) or \\(\\pm\\cos\\), so \\(|f^{(4)}(c)|\\le 1\\). The Lagrange bound is \\(\\frac{M}{4!}|x|^4\\le\\frac{(0.5)^4}{4!}\\approx 0.0026\\). It's the 4th derivative (degree 3 + 1), giving \\(4!\\) and \\(|x|^4\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m32","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n-\\ln n}","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (comparison to Σ1/n)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (comparison to \\(\\sum 1/n^{2}\\))"},{"t":"Converges (ratio test)"},{"t":"Inconclusive"}],"explain":"Since \\(\\ln n\\gt 0\\), we have \\(n-\\ln n\\lt n\\), so \\(\\frac{1}{n-\\ln n}\\gt\\frac1n\\). The terms are <i>larger</i> than the harmonic series, which diverges — so by direct comparison this <b>diverges</b> too. The \\(-\\ln n\\) only makes it bigger.","unit":10,"id":"u10m33","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"Differentiating the series \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}x^{n}=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\) term by term gives a series for \\(\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\). What is its radius of convergence?","opts":[{"t":"R = 1 (unchanged)","correct":true},{"t":"R = 2"},{"t":"R = 1/2"},{"t":"R = ∞"}],"explain":"Differentiating a power series term by term gives a new series with the <b>same radius of convergence</b>. The original \\(\\sum x^n\\) has \\(R=1\\), so \\(\\sum n x^{n-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^2}\\) also has \\(R=1\\). (Endpoint behavior can change, but the radius never does.)","unit":10,"id":"u10m34","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"The alternating series test requires which condition on the terms \\(b_n=|a_n|\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(b_n\\) decreases to 0","correct":true},{"t":"\\(b_n\\to\\infty\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\sum b_n\\) converges"},{"t":"\\(b_n\\gt 0\\) only"}],"explain":"The alternating series test needs two things: the terms \\(b_n=|a_n|\\) must be (eventually) <b>decreasing</b>, and \\(b_n\\to 0\\). If \\(\\sum b_n\\) itself converged, you'd have absolute convergence — a stronger condition the AST doesn't require. Just positivity isn't enough.","unit":10,"id":"u10m35","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Apply the ratio test. What does it tell you?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,n}{3^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges absolutely \\((L=\\tfrac13)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges \\((L=3)\\)"},{"t":"Converges conditionally"},{"t":"Inconclusive \\((L=1)\\)"}],"explain":"The ratio test uses absolute values: \\(\\frac{(n+1)/3^{n+1}}{n/3^n}=\\frac13\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{n}\\to\\frac13\\). Since \\(L=\\frac13\\lt 1\\), the series converges <b>absolutely</b> — and absolute convergence is stronger than conditional, so that's the right classification.","unit":10,"id":"u10m36","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine whether the series converges or diverges.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{3}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges (p-series, p = 3)","correct":true},{"t":"Diverges (p-series, p < 1)"},{"t":"Diverges (harmonic-like)"},{"t":"Inconclusive (ratio test)"}],"explain":"A p-series with \\(p=3\\gt 1\\), so it <b>converges</b>. (Its value is \\(\\zeta(3)\\approx 1.202\\), but all you need is \\(p\\gt 1\\).)","unit":10,"id":"u10m37","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"found","prompt":"Find the sum of the series.","math":"\\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^{n}","opts":[{"t":"1/6","correct":true},{"t":"1/3"},{"t":"1/2"},{"t":"1/9"}],"explain":"Geometric starting at \\(n=2\\): first term \\(a=\\left(\\frac13\\right)^2=\\frac19\\), ratio \\(r=\\frac13\\). Sum \\(=\\frac{1/9}{1-1/3}=\\frac{1/9}{2/3}=\\frac16\\). Watch the starting index — it changes the first term.","unit":10,"id":"u10m38","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":true},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"What is the coefficient of \\(x^{4}\\) in the Maclaurin series for \\(\\cos x\\)?","math":"\\cos x = 1-\\frac{x^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{x^{4}}{4!}-\\cdots","opts":[{"t":"1/24","correct":true},{"t":"-1/24"},{"t":"1/4"},{"t":"-1/2"}],"explain":"The \\(x^4\\) term is \\(+\\frac{x^4}{4!}\\), so the coefficient is \\(\\frac{1}{24}\\). The signs alternate \\(+,-,+\\) on the even powers, and \\(x^4\\) lands on a plus.","unit":10,"id":"u10m39","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","prompt":"Find the interval of convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} n\\,(x-1)^{n}","opts":[{"t":"(0, 2)","correct":true},{"t":"[0, 2]"},{"t":"[0, 2)"},{"t":"(0, 2]"}],"explain":"Ratio test: \\(\\frac{n+1}{n}|x-1|\\to|x-1|\\lt 1\\), so \\(R=1\\), center 1, interior \\((0,2)\\). At both endpoints the terms are \\(\\pm n\\), which grow without bound (don't → 0), so both diverge by the nth-term test. Neither included: <b>(0, 2)</b>.","unit":10,"id":"u10m40","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"Classify the convergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\sin n}{n^{2}}","opts":[{"t":"Converges absolutely","correct":true},{"t":"Converges conditionally"},{"t":"Diverges"},{"t":"Inconclusive (sin n oscillates)"}],"explain":"Compare absolute values: \\(\\left|\\frac{\\sin n}{n^2}\\right|\\le\\frac{1}{n^2}\\), and \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n^2}\\) converges. So \\(\\sum\\left|\\frac{\\sin n}{n^2}\\right|\\) converges, meaning the original converges <b>absolutely</b>. The oscillation doesn't matter once you bound it.","unit":10,"id":"u10m41","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"What does the nth-term (divergence) test tell you about this series?","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{2^{n}}","opts":[{"t":"Nothing — it's inconclusive (terms → 0)","correct":true},{"t":"It converges by the nth-term test"},{"t":"It diverges by the nth-term test"},{"t":"The test doesn't apply"}],"explain":"The terms \\(\\frac{1}{2^n}\\to 0\\), so the nth-term test is <b>inconclusive</b> — it can only prove divergence (when terms don't → 0), never convergence. (This series does converge, but you'd show that with the geometric test, not this one.) This is the most important limitation of the nth-term test.","unit":10,"id":"u10m42","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"error","prompt":"\\(P_2\\) for \\(e^{x}\\) about 0 approximates \\(e^{0.1}\\). By the Lagrange bound, the error is at most:","opts":[{"t":"\\(e^{0.1}(0.1)^{3}/3!\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((0.1)^{3}/3!\\)"},{"t":"\\(e^{0.1}(0.1)^{2}/2!\\)"},{"t":"\\((0.1)^{3}/2!\\)"}],"explain":"For \\(e^x\\), \\(f^{(3)}(c)=e^c\\), maximized at \\(e^{0.1}\\) on \\([0,0.1]\\). Degree 2 means the bound uses the 3rd derivative: \\(\\frac{e^{0.1}}{3!}(0.1)^3\\approx 1.8\\times10^{-4}\\). It's \\(n+1=3\\), so \\(3!\\) and \\((0.1)^3\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m43","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","prompt":"Determine convergence or divergence.","math":"\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n^{2}+1}}","opts":[{"t":"Diverges (limit comparison to Σ1/n)","correct":true},{"t":"Converges (limit comparison to \\(\\sum 1/n^{2}\\))"},{"t":"Converges (ratio test)"},{"t":"Diverges (nth-term test)"}],"explain":"For large \\(n\\), \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n^2+1}}\\approx\\frac{1}{n}\\). Limit comparison: \\(\\lim\\frac{1/\\sqrt{n^2+1}}{1/n}=1\\), finite and positive. Since \\(\\sum\\frac1n\\) diverges, so does this. The square root over \\(n^2\\) makes it behave like \\(\\frac1n\\), not \\(\\frac{1}{n^2}\\) — that's the trap.","unit":10,"id":"u10m44","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"Integrating the series \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}x^{n}=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\) term by term gives a series for \\(-\\ln(1-x)\\). What is its radius of convergence?","opts":[{"t":"R = 1 (unchanged)","correct":true},{"t":"R = 2"},{"t":"R = 1/2"},{"t":"R = ∞"}],"explain":"Integrating a power series term by term leaves the <b>radius of convergence unchanged</b>. The original \\(\\sum x^n\\) has \\(R=1\\), so the integrated series \\(\\sum\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\\) also has \\(R=1\\). (Only endpoint behavior may differ.)","unit":10,"id":"u10m45","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","prompt":"Using the geometric series, what is the coefficient of \\(x^{2}\\) in \\(\\frac{1}{1-2x}\\)?","math":"\\frac{1}{1-u}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}u^{n}","opts":[{"t":"4","correct":true},{"t":"2"},{"t":"1"},{"t":"8"}],"explain":"Substitute \\(u=2x\\): \\(\\frac{1}{1-2x}=\\sum(2x)^n=\\sum 2^n x^n\\). The \\(x^2\\) coefficient is \\(2^2=4\\). The trap (2) forgets the base is \\(2x\\), so the coefficient gets squared along with \\(x\\).","unit":10,"id":"u10m46","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","prompt":"Which statement is TRUE?","opts":[{"t":"If \\(\\lim a_n\\neq 0\\), then \\(\\sum a_n\\) diverges.","correct":true},{"t":"If \\(\\lim a_n=0\\), then \\(\\sum a_n\\) converges."},{"t":"If \\(\\sum a_n\\) diverges, then \\(\\lim a_n\\neq 0\\)."},{"t":"If \\(a_n\\gt 0\\), then \\(\\sum a_n\\) converges."}],"explain":"The nth-term (divergence) test: if the terms fail to approach 0, the series must diverge — that's the first statement, and it's always true. The converse (terms → 0 ⇒ converges) is false (harmonic series). A divergent series can have terms → 0 (harmonic again), so the third is false too.","unit":10,"id":"u10m47","calc":false,"calcSource":"derived","drill":false},{"topic":"found","calc":false,"prompt":"Suppose \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\) converges. Which of the following must be true?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=0\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}|a_n|\\) converges"},{"t":"\\(a_n\\) is decreasing for all \\(n\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n^{2}\\) converges"}],"explain":"<b>Only the \\(n\\)th-term condition is forced</b> — it is the contrapositive of the \\(n\\)th-term test for divergence.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>absolute convergence</b> fails for any conditionally convergent series — \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n}{n}\\) converges while \\(\\sum\\frac1n\\) does not. <b>decreasing</b> is a <i>hypothesis</i> of the alternating series test, not a consequence of convergence. <b>\\(\\sum a_n^2\\)</b> is the one worth memorizing: take \\(a_n=\\frac{(-1)^n}{\\sqrt n}\\), which converges by the alternating series test, yet \\(a_n^{2}=\\frac1n\\) gives the harmonic series, which diverges.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \\(a_n\\to0\\) is necessary and nowhere near sufficient. Three of these four options are a theorem's hypothesis being mistaken for its conclusion.","unit":10,"id":"u10m48","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","calc":false,"prompt":"The \\(n\\)th-term test can be used to show that a series <b>diverges</b>. Which series does it settle?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n}{2n+1}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The \\(n\\)th-term test says: if \\(\\lim a_n\\ne0\\), the series diverges.</b> It can never prove convergence.<br><br>For \\(\\frac{n}{2n+1}\\) the terms approach \\(\\tfrac12\\), not \\(0\\), so the series diverges and the test settles it in one line.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> all three of the others have terms going to \\(0\\), so the test is <b>inconclusive</b> for every one of them — and notice they do not all behave the same way: \\(\\sum\\frac1n\\) diverges, \\(\\sum\\frac1{n^2}\\) converges, and \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n}{n}\\) converges conditionally. That is exactly why \"terms go to zero\" tells you nothing on its own.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the \\(n\\)th-term test is a divergence test only. If the limit is \\(0\\) you have learned nothing and must pick another test.","unit":10,"id":"u10m49","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","calc":false,"prompt":"Which test most directly establishes the behavior of \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n!}{n^{n}}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"the ratio test — it converges","correct":true},{"t":"the \\(p\\)-series test — it converges"},{"t":"the \\(n\\)th-term test — it diverges"},{"t":"the alternating series test — it converges"}],"explain":"<b>Factorials and \\(n\\)th powers are the ratio test's home ground</b>, because both collapse when you form \\(\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\).<br><br>\\(\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\\dfrac{(n+1)!}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot\\dfrac{n^{n}}{n!}=\\left(\\dfrac{n}{n+1}\\right)^{n}\\to\\dfrac1e\\approx0.368<1\\), so the series converges.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the <b>\\(p\\)-series</b> test only applies to \\(\\sum\\frac1{n^p}\\) — this is not that shape. The <b>\\(n\\)th-term</b> test would need the terms not to approach zero, but they do. The <b>alternating series</b> test needs alternating signs, and every term here is positive.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> match the test to the <i>structure</i>. Factorials or \\(r^n\\) or \\(n^n\\) → ratio test. A power of \\(n\\) in the denominator → \\(p\\)-series or comparison. Alternating signs → alternating series test.","unit":10,"id":"u10m50","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"absc","calc":false,"prompt":"The series \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}\\) is","opts":[{"t":"conditionally convergent","correct":true},{"t":"absolutely convergent"},{"t":"divergent"},{"t":"convergent to \\(0\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Two questions, asked in order.</b> First, does it converge? The terms \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt n}\\) decrease to \\(0\\) and alternate in sign, so yes, by the alternating series test.<br><br>Second, does it converge absolutely? Take absolute values: \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}=\\sum\\frac1{n^{1/2}}\\) is a \\(p\\)-series with \\(p=\\tfrac12\\le1\\), so it diverges. Converges, but not absolutely — that is exactly what \"conditionally\" means.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>absolutely convergent</b> forgets to check the absolute series, or misreads \\(p=\\tfrac12\\) as being greater than 1. <b>divergent</b> confuses the failure of absolute convergence with failure of convergence itself. <b>converges to 0</b> confuses the limit of the <i>terms</i> with the sum of the series — the terms go to \\(0\\), the sum does not.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> \"conditionally convergent\" is a two-part verdict. Test the series, then test its absolute value, and report both.","unit":10,"id":"u10m51","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","calc":false,"prompt":"Find the interval of convergence of \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n\\,3^{n}}\\), checking both endpoints.","opts":[{"t":"\\([-1,5)\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\((-1,5)\\)"},{"t":"\\((-1,5]\\)"},{"t":"\\([-1,5]\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Radius first, endpoints second — the endpoints are the whole question.</b><br><br>The ratio test gives \\(\\left|\\frac{x-2}{3}\\right|<1\\), so \\(|x-2|<3\\): radius \\(3\\) about center \\(2\\), i.e. \\(-1<x<5\\).<br><br><b>At \\(x=5\\):</b> the series becomes \\(\\sum\\frac{3^{n}}{n3^{n}}=\\sum\\frac1n\\), the harmonic series — <i>diverges</i>, so \\(5\\) is excluded.<br><b>At \\(x=-1\\):</b> it becomes \\(\\sum\\frac{(-3)^{n}}{n3^{n}}=\\sum\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\), the alternating harmonic series — <i>converges</i>, so \\(-1\\) is included.<br><br>Interval: \\([-1,5)\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the <b>open</b> interval is what you get by stopping after the ratio test — correct radius, no endpoint work, and it is the most common answer given. The other two include or exclude the wrong end, usually by assuming both endpoints behave alike. They almost never do: one gives \\(\\sum\\frac1n\\) and the other \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n}{n}\\), and those disagree.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the ratio test is inconclusive exactly at the endpoints, so each must be substituted back and tested separately by name.","unit":10,"id":"u10m52","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","calc":false,"prompt":"Given \\(\\sin u=u-\\dfrac{u^{3}}{3!}+\\dfrac{u^{5}}{5!}-\\cdots\\), what is the coefficient of \\(x^{6}\\) in the Maclaurin series for \\(\\sin\\!\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{120}\\)"},{"t":"\\(-\\dfrac{1}{720}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>Substitute, do not re-derive.</b> Putting \\(u=x^{2}\\) into the known series gives \\(\\sin(x^2)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{x^{6}}{3!}+\\dfrac{x^{10}}{5!}-\\cdots\\).<br><br>The \\(x^{6}\\) term is \\(-\\dfrac{x^6}{3!}=-\\dfrac{x^6}{6}\\), so the coefficient is \\(-\\tfrac16\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(+\\tfrac16\\)</b> loses the alternating sign — the \\(u^3\\) term is subtracted. <b>\\(-\\tfrac1{120}\\)</b> is \\(-\\frac1{5!}\\), taking the third term of the series instead of the second; that term carries \\(x^{10}\\), not \\(x^6\\). <b>\\(-\\tfrac1{720}\\)</b> is \\(-\\frac1{6!}\\), reading the exponent \\(6\\) as the factorial to use — but the factorial comes from the position in the sine series, not from the power of \\(x\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> composing a known series is substitution, not new work. Track which term of the <i>original</i> produces the power you were asked about.","unit":10,"id":"u10m53","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"error","calc":false,"prompt":"The alternating series \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^{3}}\\) is approximated by its first three terms. What does the alternating series error bound guarantee about the error?","opts":[{"t":"it is at most \\(\\dfrac{1}{64}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"it is at most \\(\\dfrac{1}{27}\\)"},{"t":"it is exactly \\(\\dfrac{1}{64}\\)"},{"t":"it is at most \\(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>For a convergent alternating series, the error after \\(n\\) terms is no bigger than the FIRST OMITTED term</b> — that is the entire theorem, and it is unusually easy to use.<br><br>Three terms used means \\(n=1,2,3\\); the first omitted term is \\(n=4\\), of size \\(\\frac{1}{4^{3}}=\\frac1{64}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac1{27}\\)</b> is the third term, \\(n=3\\) — the last one you <i>used</i>, not the first one you dropped, an off-by-one that is very easy to make under time pressure. <b>exactly \\(\\tfrac1{64}\\)</b> overstates the theorem: it gives a bound, not the error. <b>\\(\\tfrac18\\)</b> is the \\(n=2\\) term.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> count which term comes <i>next</i>. The bound is about what you threw away, not what you kept.","unit":10,"id":"u10m54","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"error","calc":false,"prompt":"\\(P_3\\) is the third-degree Maclaurin polynomial for \\(e^{x}\\), used to approximate \\(e^{0.5}\\). Which expression is the Lagrange error bound?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{e^{0.5}(0.5)^{4}}{4!}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{(0.5)^{4}}{4!}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{e^{0.5}(0.5)^{3}}{3!}\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{(0.5)^{5}}{5!}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The Lagrange bound after degree \\(n\\) is \\(\\dfrac{\\max|f^{(n+1)}(z)|}{(n+1)!}\\,|x-a|^{\\,n+1}\\)</b>, with the maximum taken over the interval between \\(a\\) and \\(x\\).<br><br>Here \\(n=3\\), so the power and factorial are both \\(4\\). Every derivative of \\(e^x\\) is \\(e^x\\), which is increasing, so on \\([0,0.5]\\) its largest value is \\(e^{0.5}\\). That gives \\(\\dfrac{e^{0.5}(0.5)^4}{4!}\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> the second drops the \\(e^{0.5}\\), i.e. bounds the derivative by \\(1\\) — true for sine and cosine, false for a growing exponential. The third uses \\(n\\) instead of \\(n+1\\) in both places. The fourth uses degree \\(4\\)'s bound and again omits the derivative factor.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> the exponent and the factorial are always \\(n+1\\), and the numerator is a <i>bound on the next derivative</i>, which you must actually maximise rather than assume is 1.","unit":10,"id":"u10m55","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"found","calc":false,"prompt":"What is the sum \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}3\\left(\\frac14\\right)^{n}\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(4\\)"},{"t":"\\(3\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>A geometric series sums to \\(\\dfrac{\\text{first term}}{1-r}\\)</b> — and \"first term\" means the first one actually in the sum, which is where this question does its damage.<br><br>The index starts at \\(n=1\\), so the first term is \\(3\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)^{1}=\\tfrac34\\), and the sum is \\(\\dfrac{3/4}{1-1/4}=\\dfrac{3/4}{3/4}=1\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>4</b> is \\(\\dfrac{3}{1-1/4}\\), using \\(3\\) as the first term — that is the answer if the sum had started at \\(n=0\\). This single off-by-one is the most common geometric-series error on the exam. <b>3</b> reports the coefficient. <b>\\(\\tfrac35\\)</b> comes from \\(\\frac{3}{1+\\frac14\\cdot4}\\)-style sign confusion in the denominator.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> read the lower index before anything else. \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) needs \\(a\\) to be the term the series actually opens with.","unit":10,"id":"u10m56","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"tests","calc":false,"prompt":"To determine whether \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n+2}{n^{3}+5}\\) converges, which comparison is most useful?","opts":[{"t":"limit comparison with \\(\\displaystyle\\sum\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\) — converges","correct":true},{"t":"limit comparison with \\(\\displaystyle\\sum\\frac{1}{n}\\) — diverges"},{"t":"limit comparison with \\(\\displaystyle\\sum\\frac{1}{n^{3}}\\) — converges"},{"t":"the ratio test — inconclusive, so it diverges"}],"explain":"<b>For a rational expression, keep only the dominant power on top and bottom.</b> For large \\(n\\), \\(\\dfrac{n+2}{n^{3}+5}\\) behaves like \\(\\dfrac{n}{n^{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}\\).<br><br>Limit comparison confirms it: \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\dfrac{(n+2)/(n^{3}+5)}{1/n^{2}}=1\\), a finite nonzero number, so both series do the same thing. \\(\\sum\\frac1{n^2}\\) is a \\(p\\)-series with \\(p=2>1\\), so both converge.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> comparing with <b>\\(\\frac1n\\)</b> gets the degree difference wrong (it is \\(3-1=2\\), not 1). Comparing with <b>\\(\\frac1{n^3}\\)</b> ignores the \\(n\\) in the numerator; the limit comes out \\(\\infty\\), which proves nothing when the smaller series converges. The <b>ratio test</b> gives \\(1\\) for every rational expression, so it is always inconclusive here — and \"inconclusive\" never means \"diverges\".<br><br><b>The principle:</b> subtract the degrees. \\(\\frac{n+2}{n^3+5}\\) is a \\(p=2\\) series in disguise, and \\(p>1\\) decides it.","unit":10,"id":"u10m57","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"taylor","calc":false,"prompt":"If \\(f(x)=\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^{n}}{n+1}\\) for \\(|x|<1\\), what is \\(f''(0)\\)?","opts":[{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)","correct":true},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)"},{"t":"\\(2\\)"},{"t":"\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)"}],"explain":"<b>The link between a Taylor series and derivatives at the center is \\(c_n=\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}\\)</b>, so \\(f^{(n)}(0)=c_n\\cdot n!\\).<br><br>Here \\(c_2=\\dfrac{1}{2+1}=\\dfrac13\\), so \\(f''(0)=\\dfrac13\\cdot 2!=\\dfrac23\\).<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(\\tfrac13\\)</b> reports the coefficient itself and forgets the \\(n!\\) — reading the series as if its coefficients <i>were</i> the derivatives. <b>2</b> uses \\(2!\\) alone. <b>\\(\\tfrac12\\)</b> takes \\(c_n=\\frac1{n+1}\\) at \\(n=1\\), differentiating once instead of twice.<br><br><b>The principle:</b> coefficients and derivatives differ by a factorial. Going from series to derivative you multiply by \\(n!\\); going the other way you divide.","unit":10,"id":"u10m58","calcSource":"author","drill":false},{"topic":"ioc","calc":false,"prompt":"A power series \\(\\displaystyle\\sum c_n(x-4)^{n}\\) converges at \\(x=9\\) and diverges at \\(x=-2\\). Which must be true?","opts":[{"t":"it converges at \\(x=1\\)","correct":true},{"t":"it diverges at \\(x=10\\)"},{"t":"it converges at \\(x=-3\\)"},{"t":"its radius of convergence is exactly \\(5\\)"}],"explain":"<b>A power series converges on an interval centered at \\(a\\)</b> — here \\(a=4\\) — so the only thing that matters is distance from the center.<br><br>Convergence at \\(x=9\\) is distance \\(5\\), so it converges everywhere strictly within \\(5\\) of \\(4\\). Divergence at \\(x=-2\\) is distance \\(6\\), so it diverges beyond \\(6\\). The radius \\(R\\) therefore satisfies \\(5\\le R\\le 6\\).<br><br>\\(x=1\\) is distance \\(3\\), comfortably inside \\(5\\) — so it must converge.<br><br><b>Why each distractor is a specific mistake:</b> <b>\\(x=10\\)</b> is distance \\(6\\), which sits in the unknown band between \\(5\\) and \\(6\\) — it could go either way. <b>\\(x=-3\\)</b> is distance \\(7\\), beyond a known divergence point, so it diverges. <b>radius exactly 5</b> over-claims: convergence at distance \\(5\\) means \\(R\\ge5\\), and the radius could be anything up to \\(6\\).<br><br><b>The principle:</b> convert every \\(x\\) to its distance from the center and compare. One convergence point gives a lower bound on \\(R\\), one divergence point gives an upper bound, and anything strictly between them is undecided.","unit":10,"id":"u10m59","calcSource":"author","drill":false}],FRQ:[{"unit":1,"id":"u1f0","title":"Continuity of a Piecewise Function","intro":"Let \\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}x+1 & x\\le 2\\\\ x^2+c & x>2\\end{cases}\\) where \\(c\\) is a constant. Separately, let \\(h(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}\\) for \\(x\\ne 2\\).","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the value of \\(c\\) for which \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x=2\\). Justify your answer using the definition of continuity.","sol":"<b>Apply the three-part test at the junction \\(x=2\\).</b><br>The left piece gives the value and left limit: \\(f(2)=2+1=3\\), and \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x)=3\\).<br>The right limit: \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x)=2^2+c=4+c\\).<br>Continuity requires the limit to exist and equal \\(f(2)\\): set \\(4+c=3\\), so \\(\\boxed{c=-1}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point for \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}=3\\), 1 for \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2^+}=4+c\\), 1 for setting them equal and solving \\(c=-1\\). A complete justification states that the two one-sided limits must agree with \\(f(2)\\).","rubric":["left value/limit \\(f(2)=3\\)","right limit \\(4+c\\)","equate and solve \\(c=-1\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"c=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"For \\(h(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}\\), classify the discontinuity at \\(x=2\\) and find \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}h(x)\\).","sol":"<b>Factor and cancel.</b> \\(h(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}=x+2\\) for \\(x\\ne 2\\), so \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}h(x)=4\\).<br>Because the limit exists (\\(=4\\)) but \\(h(2)\\) is undefined, the discontinuity is <b>removable</b> — a hole at \\((2,4)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point for the factoring/cancellation, 1 for the limit \\(=4\\), 1 for classifying as removable with reasoning (limit exists, value undefined).","rubric":["factor and cancel to \\(x+2\\)","limit \\(=4\\)","classify removable (limit exists, value undefined)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\lim_{x\\to 2}h(x)=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"type:","hint":"removable / jump / infinite"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"How should \\(h(2)\\) be defined to make \\(h\\) continuous at \\(x=2\\)? Explain why this works.","sol":"<b>Patch the hole by setting the value equal to the limit.</b> Define \\(h(2)=4\\). Then all three conditions hold: \\(h(2)=4\\) is defined, \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}h(x)=4\\) exists, and they are equal — so \\(h\\) becomes continuous at \\(x=2\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point for \\(h(2)=4\\), 2 points for the justification that this makes limit \\(=\\) value (all three parts of the continuity definition satisfied). This is only possible <i>because</i> the discontinuity was removable.","rubric":["\\(h(2)=4\\)","explains limit now equals value","ties to the three-part definition"],"fields":[{"label":"h(2)=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":1,"id":"u1f1","title":"The Intermediate Value Theorem","intro":"Let \\(g(x)=x^3-3x+1\\), a polynomial.","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Compute \\(g(0)\\) and \\(g(1)\\).","sol":"<b>Direct substitution.</b> \\(g(0)=0-0+1=1\\). \\(g(1)=1-3+1=-1\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point each. These two values straddle zero, which sets up the IVT in Part B.","rubric":["\\(g(0)=1\\)","\\(g(1)=-1\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"g(0)=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"g(1)=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":4,"prompt":"Use the Intermediate Value Theorem to show that \\(g\\) has a root in the interval \\((0,1)\\). State all hypotheses explicitly.","sol":"<b>State all three IVT hypotheses, then conclude.</b><br>(1) \\(g\\) is a polynomial, so it is <b>continuous</b> on the closed interval \\([0,1]\\).<br>(2) From Part A, \\(g(0)=1>0\\) and \\(g(1)=-1<0\\), so the target value \\(N=0\\) lies between \\(g(0)\\) and \\(g(1)\\).<br>(3) By the IVT, there exists at least one \\(c\\in(0,1)\\) with \\(g(c)=0\\) — a root.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point for stating continuity on \\([0,1]\\), 1 for the two endpoint values, 1 for noting \\(0\\) is between them, 1 for the IVT conclusion that a \\(c\\) exists. Omitting the continuity statement is the most common point lost.","rubric":["continuity on \\([0,1]\\) stated","\\(g(0)=1>0\\), \\(g(1)=-1<0\\)","\\(0\\) is between the values","conclude \\(\\exists c\\) with \\(g(c)=0\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"target value N=","hint":"the value g must hit"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Does the IVT guarantee that this root is unique? Explain what the theorem does and does not tell you.","sol":"<b>No — the IVT is an existence theorem only.</b> It guarantees <i>at least one</i> root in \\((0,1)\\), but says nothing about how many or where they are. In fact \\(g(x)=x^3-3x+1\\) has three real roots overall, though only one lies in \\((0,1)\\). The theorem also does not locate the root — it only certifies that a crossing must occur.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 point for \"no,\" 2 points for explaining that the IVT promises existence (at least one) but not uniqueness or location.","rubric":["answers no","IVT gives existence, not count","IVT does not locate the root"],"fields":[{"label":"unique? (yes/no)","hint":"yes or no"}]}]},{"unit":2,"id":"u2f0","title":"Tangent Lines, Horizontal Tangents & the Normal Line","intro":"Let \\(f(x)=x^3-3x^2+2\\).","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the equation of the line tangent to the graph of \\(f\\) at \\(x=1\\).","sol":"<b>A tangent line needs a point and a slope.</b><br><b>Point:</b> \\(f(1)=1-3+2=0\\), so the curve passes through \\((1,0)\\).<br><b>Slope:</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-6x\\), so \\(f'(1)=3-6=-3\\).<br><b>Assemble (point-slope):</b> \\(y-0=-3(x-1)\\), i.e. \\(\\boxed{y=-3(x-1)}\\) or equivalently \\(y=-3x+3\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(f(1)=0\\), 1 pt for \\(f'(1)=-3\\), 1 pt for the correct point-slope equation. A common deduction is using the slope without anchoring at the point.","rubric":["point \\(f(1)=0\\)","slope \\(f'(1)=-3\\)","equation \\(y=-3(x-1)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"y =","hint":"e.g. -3(x-1) or -3x+3"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the \\(x\\)-coordinates of all points where the graph of \\(f\\) has a horizontal tangent line.","sol":"<b>Horizontal tangent means \\(f'(x)=0\\).</b><br>\\(f'(x)=3x^2-6x=3x(x-2)=0\\).<br>So \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\).<br>(For context: \\(f(0)=2\\) and \\(f(2)=-2\\), the local max and min.)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for setting \\(f'(x)=0\\), 1 pt for factoring \\(3x(x-2)\\), 1 pt for both solutions \\(x=0,2\\). Reporting only one root loses a point; solving \\(f(x)=0\\) instead of \\(f'(x)=0\\) earns none.","rubric":["set \\(f'(x)=0\\)","factor \\(3x(x-2)\\)","both roots \\(x=0,2\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"x =","hint":"both values, e.g. 0, 2"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the equation of the line normal to the graph of \\(f\\) at \\(x=1\\).","sol":"<b>The normal is perpendicular to the tangent.</b><br>From Part A, the tangent slope at \\(x=1\\) is \\(-3\\). The normal slope is the negative reciprocal: \\(-\\frac{1}{-3}=\\frac13\\).<br>Through the same point \\((1,0)\\): \\(y-0=\\frac13(x-1)\\), i.e. \\(\\boxed{y=\\frac13(x-1)}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for recognizing normal slope \\(=-1/f'(1)\\), 1 pt for computing \\(\\frac13\\), 1 pt for the equation through \\((1,0)\\). Forgetting the negative reciprocal (using \\(-3\\) or \\(3\\)) is the typical error.","rubric":["normal slope \\(=-1/f'(1)\\)","slope \\(=\\tfrac13\\)","equation \\(y=\\tfrac13(x-1)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"y =","hint":"e.g. (1/3)(x-1)"}]}]},{"unit":2,"id":"u2f1","title":"Derivatives from a Table: Product & Quotient Rules","intro":"The differentiable functions \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) have the following values at \\(x=2\\):<br><div style='margin:10px 0;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:14px;text-align:center'>\\(f(2)=3,\\quad f'(2)=5,\\quad g(2)=-1,\\quad g'(2)=4\\)</div>","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(P(x)=f(x)\\,g(x)\\). Find \\(P'(2)\\).","sol":"<b>Product rule: \\(P'=f'g+fg'\\).</b><br>\\(P'(2)=f'(2)g(2)+f(2)g'(2)=(5)(-1)+(3)(4)=-5+12=\\boxed{7}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the correct product-rule structure \\(f'g+fg'\\), 1 pt for the correct value \\(7\\). Writing \\(f'g'\\) (the classic trap) earns nothing.","rubric":["product rule \\(f'g+fg'\\)","value \\(=7\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"P'(2) =","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\). Find \\(Q'(2)\\).","sol":"<b>Quotient rule: \\(Q'=\\dfrac{f'g-fg'}{g^2}\\).</b><br>\\(Q'(2)=\\dfrac{f'(2)g(2)-f(2)g'(2)}{[g(2)]^2}=\\dfrac{(5)(-1)-(3)(4)}{(-1)^2}=\\dfrac{-5-12}{1}=\\boxed{-17}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the quotient-rule structure with \\(g^2\\) in the denominator, 1 pt for the value \\(-17\\). Reversing the numerator order gives \\(+17\\) and loses the value point.","rubric":["quotient rule \\(\\tfrac{f'g-fg'}{g^2}\\)","value \\(=-17\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"Q'(2) =","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Write the equation of the line tangent to the graph of \\(P(x)=f(x)g(x)\\) at \\(x=2\\).","sol":"<b>Need the point and the slope.</b><br><b>Point:</b> \\(P(2)=f(2)g(2)=(3)(-1)=-3\\), so \\((2,-3)\\).<br><b>Slope:</b> from Part A, \\(P'(2)=7\\).<br><b>Equation:</b> \\(y-(-3)=7(x-2)\\), i.e. \\(\\boxed{y=-3+7(x-2)}\\) (equivalently \\(y=7x-17\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(P(2)=-3\\), 1 pt for using \\(P'(2)=7\\) as the slope, 1 pt for the equation. Reusing \\(f(2)=3\\) as the point instead of \\(P(2)=-3\\) is the common slip.","rubric":["point \\(P(2)=-3\\)","slope \\(P'(2)=7\\)","equation \\(y=-3+7(x-2)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"y =","hint":"e.g. -3+7(x-2)"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(R(x)=x^2 f(x)\\). Find \\(R'(2)\\).","sol":"<b>Product rule with one factor being a power.</b><br>\\(R'(x)=2x\\,f(x)+x^2 f'(x)\\).<br>\\(R'(2)=2(2)(3)+(2)^2(5)=12+20=\\boxed{32}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the product-rule structure \\(2xf+x^2f'\\), 1 pt for the value \\(32\\). Forgetting the \\(2x\\,f\\) term (treating \\(x^2\\) as a constant) is the usual error.","rubric":["product rule \\(2xf+x^2f'\\)","value \\(=32\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"R'(2) =","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":3,"id":"u3f0","title":"Free Response 1 &middot; The Folium","intro":"Consider the curve defined by \\(x^{3}+y^{3}=6xy\\). The point \\((3,3)\\) lies on the curve. <i>(No calculator.)</i>","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"(a)","pts":2,"prompt":"Show that \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2y-x^{2}}{y^{2}-2x}\\).","sol":"Differentiate both sides with respect to \\(x\\), using the chain rule on \\(y^{3}\\) and the product rule on \\(6xy\\):<div class=\"fm\">\\(3x^{2}+3y^{2}\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=6y+6x\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\)</div>Collect the \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) terms: \\(\\big(3y^{2}-6x\\big)\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=6y-3x^{2}\\). Divide and cancel 3:<div class=\"fm\">\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{6y-3x^{2}}{3y^{2}-6x}=\\dfrac{2y-x^{2}}{y^{2}-2x}\\)</div><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> correct implicit differentiation of both sides (chain rule on \\(y^3\\), product rule on \\(6xy\\)). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> algebra collecting \\(dy/dx\\) and reaching the shown form.","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(b)","pts":2,"prompt":"Write an equation for the line tangent to the curve at the point \\((3,3)\\).","sol":"Evaluate the slope at \\((3,3)\\):<div class=\"fm\">\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\Big|_{(3,3)}=\\dfrac{2(3)-9}{9-2(3)}=\\dfrac{-3}{3}=-1\\)</div>Point&ndash;slope form: \\(y-3=-1(x-3)\\), i.e. <b>\\(y=-x+6\\)</b>.<br><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> slope \\(=-1\\) at \\((3,3)\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> tangent line equation through \\((3,3)\\) with that slope.","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(c)","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the coordinates of the point in the first quadrant, other than the origin, at which the curve has a horizontal tangent line.","sol":"Horizontal tangent: numerator zero, denominator nonzero, so \\(2y=x^{2}\\), i.e. \\(y=\\dfrac{x^{2}}{2}\\). Substitute into the curve:<div class=\"fm\">\\(x^{3}+\\dfrac{x^{6}}{8}=6x\\cdot\\dfrac{x^{2}}{2}=3x^{3}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\dfrac{x^{6}}{8}=2x^{3}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ x^{3}=16\\)</div>So \\(x=16^{1/3}=2^{4/3}\\) and \\(y=\\dfrac{x^{2}}{2}=\\dfrac{2^{8/3}}{2}=2^{5/3}\\). The point is <b>\\(\\big(2^{4/3},\\,2^{5/3}\\big)\\approx(2.52,\\ 3.17)\\)</b>. (Check: the denominator \\(y^{2}-2x=2^{10/3}-2^{7/3}\\neq 0\\), so the tangent is genuinely horizontal, not indeterminate.)<br><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> sets \\(2y-x^{2}=0\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> substitutes into the curve and solves \\(x^{3}=16\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> both coordinates of the point.","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(d)","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the coordinates of the point in the first quadrant, other than the origin, at which the curve has a vertical tangent line.","sol":"Vertical tangent: denominator zero, numerator nonzero, so \\(y^{2}=2x\\). By the symmetry of the equation \\(x^{3}+y^{3}=6xy\\) under swapping \\(x\\leftrightarrow y\\), this is the mirror of part (c): the point is <b>\\(\\big(2^{5/3},\\,2^{4/3}\\big)\\approx(3.17,\\ 2.52)\\)</b>. (Direct computation works identically: substitute \\(x=\\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\) and solve \\(y^{3}=16\\).)<br><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> sets \\(y^{2}-2x=0\\) (or invokes the symmetry). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> both coordinates of the point.","rubric":[],"fields":[]}]},{"unit":3,"id":"u3f1","title":"Free Response 2 &middot; Functions From a Table","intro":"Let \\(f\\) be a differentiable, strictly increasing function. Selected values of \\(f\\) and \\(f'\\) are given in the table. Let \\(g=f^{-1}\\) denote the inverse function of \\(f\\). <i>(No calculator.)</i><div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th></tr><tr><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr></table></div>","given":"","givenNote":"","givenFall":"","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"(a)","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(h(x)=f\\big(f(x)\\big)\\). Find \\(h'(1)\\).","sol":"Chain rule: \\(h'(x)=f'\\big(f(x)\\big)\\cdot f'(x)\\). At \\(x=1\\): \\(f(1)=2\\), so<div class=\"fm\">\\(h'(1)=f'(2)\\cdot f'(1)=2\\cdot 3=\\mathbf{6}\\)</div><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> chain-rule structure \\(f'(f(1))\\cdot f'(1)\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> value 6 from correct table reads.","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(b)","pts":2,"prompt":"Find \\(g'(5)\\).","sol":"Locate 5 among the <i>outputs</i>: \\(f(3)=5\\), so \\(g(5)=3\\). Then<div class=\"fm\">\\(g'(5)=\\dfrac{1}{f'\\big(g(5)\\big)}=\\dfrac{1}{f'(3)}=\\mathbf{\\dfrac{1}{4}}\\)</div><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> identifies \\(g(5)=3\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> reciprocal of \\(f'(3)\\), value \\(\\tfrac14\\).","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(c)","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(k(x)=x^{3}f(x)\\). Find \\(k'(2)\\).","sol":"Product rule: \\(k'(x)=3x^{2}f(x)+x^{3}f'(x)\\). At \\(x=2\\):<div class=\"fm\">\\(k'(2)=3(4)(3)+8(2)=36+16=\\mathbf{52}\\)</div><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> product-rule structure with both terms. <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> value 52.","rubric":[],"fields":[]},{"label":"(d)","pts":3,"prompt":"Let \\(m(x)=\\arctan\\big(f(x)\\big)\\). Find \\(m'(1)\\), and write an equation for the line tangent to the graph of \\(m\\) at \\(x=1\\).","sol":"Chain rule with the \\(\\arctan\\) template: \\(m'(x)=\\dfrac{f'(x)}{1+\\big(f(x)\\big)^{2}}\\). At \\(x=1\\):<div class=\"fm\">\\(m'(1)=\\dfrac{f'(1)}{1+\\big(f(1)\\big)^{2}}=\\dfrac{3}{1+4}=\\mathbf{\\dfrac{3}{5}}\\)</div>The point of tangency is \\(\\big(1,\\ m(1)\\big)=\\big(1,\\arctan 2\\big)\\), so the tangent line is<div class=\"fm\">\\(y=\\arctan 2+\\dfrac{3}{5}(x-1)\\)</div><span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> derivative structure \\(\\frac{f'(x)}{1+f(x)^2}\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> \\(m'(1)=\\tfrac35\\). <span class=\"pt\">1 pt:</span> tangent line using the point \\(\\big(1,\\arctan 2\\big)\\) &mdash; \\(\\arctan 2\\) stays exact; no decimal needed.","rubric":[],"fields":[]}]},{"unit":4,"id":"u4f0","title":"Particle Motion","intro":"<b>Acceleration & rest → direction with justification → total distance → position.</b> The classic rectilinear-motion problem, no calculator, worth 9 points. Every value here is exact — the reversals are what make total distance differ from displacement.","given":"v(t)=t^{2}-4t+3,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5","givenNote":"A particle moves along the x-axis with velocity v(t) = t² − 4t + 3 (in meters per second) for 0 ≤ t ≤ 5. At time t = 0 the particle is at position x = 2. No calculator.","givenFall":"v(t) = t² − 4t + 3 for 0 ≤ t ≤ 5; x(0) = 2 (no calculator).","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the acceleration \\(a(t)\\), and find all times in \\([0,5]\\) at which the particle is at rest.","sol":"<b>Acceleration is the derivative of velocity:</b> \\(a(t)=v'(t)=2t-4\\).<br><b>At rest ⇔ \\(v(t)=0\\):</b> \\(t^2-4t+3=(t-1)(t-3)=0\\Rightarrow t=1\\) and \\(t=3\\) (both in \\([0,5]\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(a(t)=2t-4\\), one for both rest times \\(t=1,3\\). A common slip is solving \\(a(t)=0\\) (giving \\(t=2\\)) instead of \\(v(t)=0\\).","rubric":["\\(a(t)=2t-4\\)","Solved \\(v(t)=(t-1)(t-3)=0\\Rightarrow t=1,3\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"a(t)=","hint":"e.g. 2t-4"},{"label":"\\text{at rest at }t=","hint":"e.g. 1, 3"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"At \\(t=2.5\\), is the particle moving left or right, and is it speeding up or slowing down? Justify with the signs of \\(v\\) and \\(a\\).","sol":"<b>Direction is the sign of \\(v\\); speeding up/slowing down compares the signs of \\(v\\) and \\(a\\).</b><br><b>At \\(t=2.5\\):</b> \\(v(2.5)=6.25-10+3=-0.75<0\\) → moving <b>left</b>. \\(a(2.5)=2(2.5)-4=1>0\\).<br>Since \\(v<0\\) and \\(a>0\\) have <b>opposite signs</b>, the particle is <b>slowing down</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the direction with \\(v(2.5)<0\\), one for \\(a(2.5)>0\\), one for the conclusion \"slowing down because \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) have opposite signs.\" Stating \"slowing down\" without the sign comparison earns the value but not the justification point.","rubric":["\\(v(2.5)=-0.75<0\\) ⇒ moving left","\\(a(2.5)=1>0\\)","Opposite signs ⇒ slowing down (with justification)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{direction:}","hint":"left or right"},{"label":"\\text{speed:}","hint":"speeding up / slowing down"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the total distance traveled by the particle over the interval \\([0,3]\\).","sol":"<b>Total distance integrates speed \\(|v|\\); split at the rest time \\(t=1\\)</b> where \\(v\\) changes sign (\\(v>0\\) on \\([0,1)\\), \\(v<0\\) on \\((1,3)\\)).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 v\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\Big]_0^1=\\tfrac13-2+3=\\tfrac43\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^3 v\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\Big]_1^3=(9-18+9)-\\tfrac43=0-\\tfrac43=-\\tfrac43\\).<br>Total distance \\(=\\left|\\tfrac43\\right|+\\left|-\\tfrac43\\right|=\\dfrac{8}{3}\\approx2.667\\) m.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for splitting at \\(t=1\\) and integrating \\(|v|\\), one for the value \\(\\tfrac83\\). Integrating \\(v\\) straight over \\([0,3]\\) gives displacement \\(0\\) — the classic error.","rubric":["Split at \\(t=1\\); integrated \\(|v|\\) over each piece","Total distance \\(=\\tfrac43+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{8}{3}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{distance}=","hint":"e.g. 8/3"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Given \\(x(0)=2\\), find the position \\(x(3)\\).","sol":"<b>Use accumulation with the initial condition:</b> \\(x(3)=x(0)+\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 v(t)\\,dt\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 (t^2-4t+3)\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\Big]_0^3=9-18+9=0\\).<br>So \\(x(3)=2+0=2\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the accumulation setup \\(x(0)+\\int_0^3 v\\,dt\\) (initial condition present), one for the value \\(2\\). Note this uses net displacement (\\(\\int v\\)), not total distance — a nice contrast with Part C.","rubric":["Set up \\(x(3)=2+\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 v\\,dt\\)","\\(\\int_0^3 v\\,dt=0\\Rightarrow x(3)=2\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"x(3)=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":4,"id":"u4f1","title":"Rate in Context (table)","intro":"<b>Average rate → estimate a derivative from a table → interpret in a sentence → tangent-line prediction with over/under.</b> A table-based problem worth 8 points, mixing contextual rates with a linear approximation. Estimates come straight from the data.","given":"\\text{Water depth } H(t)\\text{ (meters) at selected times } t\\text{ (hours):}","givenNote":"H is a differentiable, increasing function giving the water depth (in meters) in a tank at time t hours. The table shows selected values. The depth rises ever more slowly, so H is concave down.","givenFall":"H(t): H(0)=2.0, H(3)=3.8, H(5)=5.1, H(7)=6.0, H(10)=6.8 (concave down).","givenHTML":"<div class=\"qtable\"><table><tr><th>t (hr)</th><th>0</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>7</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><th>H(t) (m)</th><td>2.0</td><td>3.8</td><td>5.1</td><td>6.0</td><td>6.8</td></tr></table></div>","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the average rate of change of \\(H\\) over the interval \\([3,7]\\). Include units.","sol":"<b>Average rate of change is the secant slope:</b> \\(\\dfrac{H(7)-H(3)}{7-3}=\\dfrac{6.0-3.8}{4}=\\dfrac{2.2}{4}=0.55\\) meters per hour.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the difference quotient, one for the value with units (m/hr). Dropping the units, or dividing by the wrong \\(\\Delta t\\), loses a point.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{H(7)-H(3)}{7-3}=\\dfrac{2.2}{4}\\)","\\(=0.55\\) meters per hour (with units)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{avg rate}=","hint":"e.g. 0.55 m/hr"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"Estimate \\(H'(5)\\) using the table, and interpret its meaning in a complete sentence with units.","sol":"<b>Estimate the derivative with the symmetric difference around \\(t=5\\)</b> (using the neighbors \\(t=3\\) and \\(t=7\\)): \\(H'(5)\\approx\\dfrac{H(7)-H(3)}{7-3}=\\dfrac{6.0-3.8}{4}=0.55\\) m/hr.<br><b>Interpretation:</b> \"At \\(t=5\\) hours, the water depth is increasing at approximately \\(0.55\\) meters per hour.\"<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for using a difference quotient centered at \\(t=5\\), one for the value \\(0.55\\), one for an interpretation in context (a sentence naming <i>depth increasing</i> at \\(0.55\\) m/hr). Calling \\(0.55\\) \"the depth\" instead of \"the rate\" loses the interpretation point.","rubric":["Symmetric estimate \\(\\dfrac{H(7)-H(3)}{7-3}=0.55\\)","Value \\(H'(5)\\approx0.55\\) m/hr","Sentence: depth is increasing at \\(\\approx0.55\\) m/hr at \\(t=5\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"H'(5)\\approx","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Use the tangent line to \\(H\\) at \\(t=5\\) (with your estimate of \\(H'(5)\\)) to approximate \\(H(6)\\). Is this an over- or underestimate? Justify.","sol":"<b>Tangent-line (local linear) approximation:</b> \\(H(6)\\approx H(5)+H'(5)(6-5)=5.1+0.55(1)=5.65\\) m.<br><b>Over or under?</b> \\(H\\) is <b>concave down</b> (the depth rises ever more slowly), so the tangent line lies <i>above</i> the curve — this is an <b>overestimate</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the linearization setup, one for the value \\(5.65\\), one for \"overestimate because \\(H\\) is concave down.\" Saying \"overestimate\" with no concavity reason loses the justification point.","rubric":["\\(H(6)\\approx H(5)+H'(5)(1)=5.1+0.55\\)","\\(=5.65\\) meters","Overestimate — \\(H\\) is concave down, so the tangent lies above the curve"],"fields":[{"label":"H(6)\\approx","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{over or under?}","hint":"over or under"}]}]},{"unit":4,"id":"u4f2","title":"Related Rates (cone)","intro":"<b>Reduce to one variable → find \\(dh/dt\\) → find \\(dr/dt\\) → rate of the surface area.</b> A calculator-active conical-tank problem worth 9 points. Similar triangles collapse the two dimensions into one before differentiating.","given":"\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=8\\text{ ft}^3/\\text{min},\\quad V=\\tfrac13\\pi r^{2}h,\\quad r=\\tfrac{h}{4}","givenNote":"Water is pumped into a cone-shaped tank (vertex down) at a constant 8 ft³/min. The tank is 12 ft tall with top radius 3 ft, so by similar triangles r = h/4 at every depth. A calculator is allowed.","givenFall":"dV/dt = 8 ft³/min into a cone with r = h/4; V = (1/3)πr²h.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Using \\(r=\\tfrac{h}{4}\\), write the volume \\(V\\) as a function of \\(h\\) alone.","sol":"<b>Substitute the constraint before differentiating:</b> \\(V=\\tfrac13\\pi r^2 h=\\tfrac13\\pi\\left(\\tfrac h4\\right)^2 h=\\tfrac13\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac{h^2}{16}\\cdot h=\\dfrac{\\pi h^3}{48}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for squaring \\(r=\\tfrac h4\\) correctly (the \\(\\tfrac{1}{16}\\)), one for \\(V=\\tfrac{\\pi h^3}{48}\\). Forgetting to substitute leaves a stray \\(r\\) (and a stray \\(dr/dt\\)) in the next step.","rubric":["Substituted \\(r=\\tfrac h4\\): \\(V=\\tfrac13\\pi\\tfrac{h^2}{16}h\\)","\\(V=\\dfrac{\\pi h^3}{48}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"V=","hint":"e.g. pi h^3/48"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the rate \\(\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\) at which the water level rises when \\(h=6\\) ft.","sol":"<b>Differentiate \\(V=\\tfrac{\\pi h^3}{48}\\) in time:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi\\cdot3h^2}{48}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi h^2}{16}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\).<br><b>At \\(h=6\\), \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=8\\):</b> \\(8=\\dfrac{\\pi(36)}{16}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{32}{9\\pi}\\approx1.132\\) ft/min.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for differentiating to \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi h^2}{16}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\), one for substituting \\(h=6\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=8\\), one for the value \\(\\dfrac{32}{9\\pi}\\approx1.132\\). Plugging \\(h=6\\) in <i>before</i> differentiating is the classic error.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=\\dfrac{\\pi h^2}{16}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\)","Substituted \\(h=6\\), \\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt}=8\\): \\(8=\\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\)","\\(\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\dfrac{32}{9\\pi}\\approx1.132\\) ft/min"],"fields":[{"label":"\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=","hint":"e.g. 1.132 ft/min"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the rate \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\) at which the water's surface radius grows when \\(h=6\\) ft.","sol":"<b>The constraint \\(r=\\tfrac h4\\) differentiates directly:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=\\tfrac14\\dfrac{dh}{dt}=\\tfrac14\\cdot\\dfrac{32}{9\\pi}=\\dfrac{8}{9\\pi}\\approx0.283\\) ft/min.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=\\tfrac14\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\), one for the value. Re-deriving from scratch works too, but the linear constraint makes it a one-liner.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=\\tfrac14\\dfrac{dh}{dt}\\)","\\(=\\dfrac{8}{9\\pi}\\approx0.283\\) ft/min"],"fields":[{"label":"\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=","hint":"e.g. 0.283 ft/min"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"The water's surface is a circle of area \\(A=\\pi r^{2}\\). Find \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}\\) when \\(h=6\\) ft.","sol":"<b>Differentiate \\(A=\\pi r^2\\):</b> \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi r\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\). At \\(h=6\\), \\(r=\\tfrac h4=1.5\\), and \\(\\dfrac{dr}{dt}=\\dfrac{8}{9\\pi}\\):<br>\\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi(1.5)\\cdot\\dfrac{8}{9\\pi}=3\\pi\\cdot\\dfrac{8}{9\\pi}=\\dfrac{24}{9}=\\dfrac{8}{3}\\approx2.667\\) ft²/min.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi r\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\) with \\(r=1.5\\), one for the value \\(\\tfrac83\\). Notice the \\(\\pi\\)'s cancel — a clean check that the setup is right.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=2\\pi r\\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\), with \\(r=1.5\\)","\\(=\\dfrac{8}{3}\\approx2.667\\) ft²/min"],"fields":[{"label":"\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=","hint":"e.g. 2.667 ft^2/min"}]}]},{"unit":4,"id":"u4f3","title":"Linearize + L'Hôpital","intro":"<b>Linearization → over/under from concavity → a \\(0/0\\) limit → applying L'Hôpital twice.</b> A no-calculator problem worth 9 points, all built on one function \\(f(x)=e^{2x}\\) — tying local linearity to the indeterminate-form limits it explains.","given":"f(x)=e^{2x}","givenNote":"Let f(x) = e^{2x}. No calculator; give exact values.","givenFall":"f(x) = e^(2x) (no calculator).","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the linearization \\(L(x)\\) of \\(f\\) at \\(x=0\\), and use it to approximate \\(f(0.1)=e^{0.2}\\).","sol":"<b>\\(L(x)=f(0)+f'(0)x\\).</b> Here \\(f(0)=e^0=1\\), \\(f'(x)=2e^{2x}\\Rightarrow f'(0)=2\\). So \\(L(x)=1+2x\\).<br><b>Approximation:</b> \\(f(0.1)\\approx L(0.1)=1+2(0.1)=1.2\\). (True \\(e^{0.2}\\approx1.221\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(L(x)=1+2x\\) (correct \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f'(0)\\)), one for \\(1.2\\). Using \\(f'(0)=1\\) (forgetting the chain-rule \\(2\\)) is the common slip.","rubric":["\\(f(0)=1,\\ f'(0)=2\\Rightarrow L(x)=1+2x\\)","\\(f(0.1)\\approx1.2\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"L(x)=","hint":"e.g. 1+2x"},{"label":"f(0.1)\\approx","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Is the approximation in Part A an over- or underestimate? Justify with concavity.","sol":"<b>Check the second derivative:</b> \\(f''(x)=4e^{2x}>0\\) for all \\(x\\), so \\(f\\) is <b>concave up</b>. A concave-up curve lies <i>above</i> its tangent line, so the linear estimate is an <b>underestimate</b>. (Indeed \\(1.2<1.221\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(f''>0\\) (concave up), one for concluding \"underestimate\" from it. \"Underestimate\" without the concavity reason earns nothing here.","rubric":["\\(f''(x)=4e^{2x}>0\\) ⇒ concave up","Concave up ⇒ tangent below curve ⇒ underestimate"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{over or under?}","hint":"over or under"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{2x}-1}{\\sin x}\\).","sol":"<b>Substitution gives \\(\\tfrac00\\) — apply L'Hôpital:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{2x}-1}{\\sin x}=\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{2e^{2x}}{\\cos x}=\\frac{2e^0}{\\cos0}=\\frac{2}{1}=2\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for confirming \\(\\tfrac00\\) and differentiating top and bottom, one for the value \\(2\\). Forgetting the chain-rule \\(2\\) on \\(e^{2x}\\) gives the wrong answer \\(1\\).","rubric":["Form \\(\\tfrac00\\); \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\)-top \\(=2e^{2x}\\), bottom \\(=\\cos x\\)","\\(\\dfrac{2e^0}{\\cos0}=2\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{limit}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^{2x}-1-2x}{x^{2}}\\), applying L'Hôpital as many times as needed and checking the form each time.","sol":"<b>Check the form, apply, re-check.</b> At \\(x=0\\): numerator \\(=1-1-0=0\\), denominator \\(=0\\) — form \\(\\tfrac00\\).<br><b>First L'Hôpital:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{2e^{2x}-2}{2x}\\). Still \\(\\tfrac{2-2}{0}=\\tfrac00\\).<br><b>Second L'Hôpital:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{4e^{2x}}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for confirming the first \\(\\tfrac00\\) and differentiating, one for recognizing it is <i>still</i> \\(\\tfrac00\\) and applying again, one for the value \\(2\\). Stopping after one application (and mis-reporting \\(0\\)) is the trap.","rubric":["First application: \\(\\dfrac{2e^{2x}-2}{2x}\\), still \\(\\tfrac00\\)","Second application: \\(\\dfrac{4e^{2x}}{2}\\)","Limit \\(=2\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{limit}=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":5,"id":"u5f0","title":"Analyze f from a formula","intro":"<b>Increasing/decreasing with justification → local extrema → concavity &amp; inflection → absolute extrema on a closed interval.</b> The bread-and-butter Unit 5 analysis problem, no calculator, worth 9 points. Every value here is exact.","given":"f(x)=x^{3}-3x^{2}","givenNote":"Let f(x) = x³ − 3x², defined for all real x. Answer parts A–C for all x; part D restricts to the closed interval [−1, 3]. No calculator.","givenFall":"f(x) = x³ − 3x² (no calculator).","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find \\(f'(x)\\), the open intervals on which \\(f\\) is increasing and decreasing, and justify.","sol":"<b>Differentiate and factor:</b> \\(f'(x)=3x^2-6x=3x(x-2)\\), with zeros \\(x=0,2\\).<br><b>Sign of \\(f'\\):</b> \\(f'>0\\) on \\((-\\infty,0)\\) and \\((2,\\infty)\\); \\(f'<0\\) on \\((0,2)\\).<br>So \\(f\\) is <b>increasing on \\((-\\infty,0)\\) and \\((2,\\infty)\\)</b>, <b>decreasing on \\((0,2)\\)</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(f'=3x(x-2)\\); 1 pt for the increasing intervals; 1 pt for the decreasing interval <i>with</i> a sign analysis of \\(f'\\) as justification (a sign chart or the words \"\\(f'>0\\) there\").","rubric":["\\(f'(x)=3x(x-2)\\) with zeros \\(x=0,2\\)","Increasing on \\((-\\infty,0)\\cup(2,\\infty)\\); decreasing on \\((0,2)\\)","Justified by the sign of \\(f'\\) on each interval"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(x)=","hint":"e.g. 3x^2-6x"},{"label":"\\text{increasing on}","hint":"e.g. (-inf,0) and (2,inf)"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Identify each local maximum and local minimum of \\(f\\) (as points), and state the test you used.","sol":"<b>First-derivative test on the sign changes of \\(f'=3x(x-2)\\):</b><br>• At \\(x=0\\): \\(f'\\) goes \\(+\\to-\\) ⇒ <b>local maximum</b>, \\(f(0)=0\\) → point \\((0,0)\\).<br>• At \\(x=2\\): \\(f'\\) goes \\(-\\to+\\) ⇒ <b>local minimum</b>, \\(f(2)=8-12=-4\\) → point \\((2,-4)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the local max at \\((0,0)\\), 1 pt for the local min at \\((2,-4)\\) — each requires the correct classification tied to the sign change (or a second-derivative check: \\(f''(0)=-6<0\\), \\(f''(2)=6>0\\)).","rubric":["Local max \\((0,0)\\) — \\(f'\\) changes \\(+\\to-\\) (or \\(f''(0)<0\\))","Local min \\((2,-4)\\) — \\(f'\\) changes \\(-\\to+\\) (or \\(f''(2)>0\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{local max at }x=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{local min at }x=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the intervals of concavity and the coordinates of any inflection point(s).","sol":"<b>Second derivative:</b> \\(f''(x)=6x-6=6(x-1)\\), zero at \\(x=1\\).<br>\\(f''<0\\) on \\((-\\infty,1)\\) (concave down) and \\(f''>0\\) on \\((1,\\infty)\\) (concave up). Sign changes ⇒ inflection at \\(x=1\\), \\(f(1)=1-3=-2\\): point \\((1,-2)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for concave down \\((-\\infty,1)\\) and up \\((1,\\infty)\\); 1 pt for the inflection point \\((1,-2)\\) established by a sign change of \\(f''\\).","rubric":["\\(f''(x)=6(x-1)\\); concave down on \\((-\\infty,1)\\), up on \\((1,\\infty)\\)","Inflection point \\((1,-2)\\) (sign change of \\(f''\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{inflection at }x=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{concave up on}","hint":"e.g. (1,inf)"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum values of \\(f\\) on the closed interval \\([-1,3]\\).","sol":"<b>Candidates test — critical points and endpoints.</b> Critical \\(x=0,2\\); endpoints \\(x=-1,3\\).<br>\\(f(-1)=-1-3=-4,\\quad f(0)=0,\\quad f(2)=-4,\\quad f(3)=27-27=0.\\)<br><b>Absolute max \\(=0\\)</b> (attained at \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=3\\)); <b>absolute min \\(=-4\\)</b> (attained at \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=2\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for evaluating \\(f\\) at all four candidates, 1 pt for the correct max \\(0\\) and min \\(-4\\). Forgetting an endpoint is the usual error — here the endpoints tie the interior points.","rubric":["Evaluated \\(f\\) at \\(x=-1,0,2,3\\) (critical points + endpoints)","Absolute max \\(=0\\), absolute min \\(=-4\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{abs max}=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{abs min}=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":5,"id":"u5f1","title":"Read f from the graph of f′","intro":"<b>The flagship Unit 5 FRQ: everything about \\(f\\) is deduced from the graph of its derivative.</b> Here the graph of \\(f'\\) is the parabola \\(f'(x)=(x+1)(x-2)\\) on \\([-3,4]\\) — think of that as the picture you're handed. No calculator, 9 points, all qualitative + justified.","given":"\\text{The graph of }f'\\text{ is } f'(x)=(x+1)(x-2)\\text{ on }[-3,4].","givenNote":"The graph shown is of f′ (the derivative of f), not f. It is an upward parabola crossing the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 2, with its lowest point (vertex) at x = 1/2. Use it to reason about f.","givenFall":"Graph of f′(x) = (x+1)(x−2): zeros at x = −1, 2; vertex (minimum) at x = 1/2.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the \\(x\\)-coordinates of all local maxima and minima of \\(f\\) on \\((-3,4)\\), with justification from the graph of \\(f'\\).","sol":"<b>Extrema of \\(f\\) occur where \\(f'\\) changes sign (crosses the axis).</b><br>• At \\(x=-1\\): \\(f'\\) goes \\(+\\to-\\) (above the axis to below) ⇒ <b>local maximum</b>.<br>• At \\(x=2\\): \\(f'\\) goes \\(-\\to+\\) ⇒ <b>local minimum</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the local max at \\(x=-1\\), 1 pt for the local min at \\(x=2\\), and 1 pt for justifying <i>both</i> by the sign change of \\(f'\\) as it crosses the axis (the AP reader demands the justification, not just the locations). Listing \\(x=\\tfrac12\\) (the vertex of \\(f'\\)) as an extremum of \\(f\\) is the classic misread — that's an inflection.","rubric":["Local maximum at \\(x=-1\\)","Local minimum at \\(x=2\\)","Both justified by the sign change of \\(f'\\) (crossing the axis \\(+\\to-\\) and \\(-\\to+\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{local max at }x=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{local min at }x=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"On what interval(s) is \\(f\\) increasing? Justify.","sol":"<b>\\(f\\) increases where \\(f'>0\\) — where the graph of \\(f'\\) is <i>above</i> the \\(x\\)-axis.</b> That's outside the roots: on \\((-3,-1)\\) and \\((2,4)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for both intervals \\((-3,-1)\\) and \\((2,4)\\), 1 pt for the justification \"\\(f'>0\\) (graph above the axis) there.\" Reporting where \\(f'\\) is increasing instead of positive is the trap.","rubric":["Increasing on \\((-3,-1)\\) and \\((2,4)\\)","Because \\(f'>0\\) (graph of \\(f'\\) above the axis) there"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{increasing on}","hint":"e.g. (-3,-1) and (2,4)"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"At what \\(x\\) does \\(f\\) have a point of inflection? Justify using the graph of \\(f'\\).","sol":"<b>Inflection points of \\(f\\) occur where \\(f''\\) changes sign — i.e. where \\(f'\\) changes from decreasing to increasing (a local extremum of \\(f'\\)).</b> The graph of \\(f'\\) has its minimum (vertex) at \\(x=\\tfrac12\\), where \\(f'\\) stops decreasing and starts increasing ⇒ \\(f''\\) changes \\(-\\to+\\) ⇒ inflection at \\(x=\\tfrac12\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(x=\\tfrac12\\), 1 pt for the reason \"\\(f'\\) changes from decreasing to increasing (its minimum) ⇒ \\(f''\\) changes sign.\" Choosing a zero of \\(f'\\) (\\(x=-1\\) or \\(2\\)) is the wrong feature.","rubric":["Inflection at \\(x=\\tfrac12\\)","Because \\(f'\\) has a minimum there (changes from decreasing to increasing), so \\(f''\\) changes sign"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{inflection at }x=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"On what interval(s) is the graph of \\(f\\) concave down? Justify.","sol":"<b>Concave down where \\(f''<0\\), i.e. where \\(f'\\) is <i>decreasing</i>.</b> The parabola \\(f'\\) decreases to the left of its vertex, so on \\((-3,\\tfrac12)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\((-3,\\tfrac12)\\), 1 pt for the justification \"\\(f'\\) is decreasing there, so \\(f''<0\\).\" Using where \\(f'<0\\) (below the axis) instead of where \\(f'\\) is decreasing is the classic wire-crossing.","rubric":["Concave down on \\((-3,\\tfrac12)\\)","Because \\(f'\\) is decreasing there ⇒ \\(f''<0\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{concave down on}","hint":"e.g. (-3,1/2)"}]}]},{"unit":5,"id":"u5f2","title":"Optimization (inscribed rectangle)","intro":"<b>Set up the objective → domain → \\(f'=0\\) → justify the maximum → answer the area.</b> A full optimization free-response, no calculator, worth 9 points. The constraint is the parabola the corners must lie on.","given":"y=12-x^{2},\\qquad A(x)=2x(12-x^{2})","givenNote":"A rectangle has its base on the x-axis and its two upper vertices on the curve y = 12 − x² (x ≥ 0). Let x be the half-width, so the rectangle has width 2x and height 12 − x². No calculator.","givenFall":"Rectangle under y = 12 − x²; area A(x) = 2x(12 − x²).","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Write \\(A(x)\\) in expanded form and state the domain of \\(x\\).","sol":"<b>Expand the area:</b> \\(A(x)=2x(12-x^2)=24x-2x^3\\).<br><b>Domain:</b> the height \\(12-x^2>0\\Rightarrow x<\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt3\\), and \\(x>0\\) for a real rectangle: \\(0<x<2\\sqrt3\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(A(x)=24x-2x^3\\); 1 pt for the domain \\(0<x<2\\sqrt3\\) (both a positive width and a positive height).","rubric":["\\(A(x)=24x-2x^3\\)","Domain \\(0<x<2\\sqrt3\\) (height and width both positive)"],"fields":[{"label":"A(x)=","hint":"e.g. 24x-2x^3"},{"label":"\\text{domain}","hint":"e.g. 0<x<sqrt(12)"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the \\(x\\) that maximizes \\(A\\) by solving \\(A'(x)=0\\).","sol":"<b>Differentiate and solve:</b> \\(A'(x)=24-6x^2=0\\Rightarrow x^2=4\\Rightarrow x=2\\) (reject \\(x=-2\\), outside the domain).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(A'(x)=24-6x^2\\), 1 pt for \\(x=2\\) with the negative root rejected.","rubric":["\\(A'(x)=24-6x^2\\)","\\(x=2\\) (rejecting \\(x=-2\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"A'(x)=","hint":"e.g. 24-6x^2"},{"label":"x=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Justify that your \\(x\\) gives an absolute maximum (not a minimum).","sol":"<b>Second-derivative test:</b> \\(A''(x)=-12x\\), so \\(A''(2)=-24<0\\) — concave down, hence a maximum. And \\(x=2\\) is the <i>only</i> critical point in \\((0,2\\sqrt3)\\), so it's the <b>absolute</b> maximum. (Equivalently: \\(A'\\) changes \\(+\\to-\\) at \\(x=2\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for a valid test (\\(A''(2)<0\\) or a first-derivative sign change), 1 pt for arguing it is <i>absolute</i> — the sole critical point on the domain, with \\(A\\to0\\) at both ends.","rubric":["\\(A''(2)=-24<0\\) (or \\(A'\\) changes \\(+\\to-\\)) ⇒ local max","Only critical point on the domain (\\(A=0\\) at both ends) ⇒ absolute max"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{justification}","hint":"e.g. A''<0 or first-deriv test"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"State the dimensions of the largest rectangle and its maximum area.","sol":"<b>Plug \\(x=2\\) back into the geometry.</b> Width \\(=2x=4\\); height \\(=12-x^2=12-4=8\\); area \\(=A(2)=24(2)-2(8)=48-16=32\\) (or \\(4\\cdot8=32\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt width \\(4\\), 1 pt height \\(8\\), 1 pt area \\(32\\). Reporting \\(x=2\\) as \"the answer\" without the requested dimensions/area is the last-10% error this problem is built to catch.","rubric":["Width \\(=2x=4\\)","Height \\(=12-x^2=8\\)","Maximum area \\(=32\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{width}=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{height}=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{max area}=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":5,"id":"u5f3","title":"Analyze f(x)=x·e^(−x)","intro":"<b>Second-derivative test → concavity &amp; inflection → absolute max on \\([0,\\infty)\\) → end behavior.</b> A BC-flavored analysis on a product/exponential, worth 9 points. Exact values throughout; a calculator only confirms them.","given":"f(x)=x\\,e^{-x},\\qquad x\\ge 0","givenNote":"Let f(x) = x·e^{−x} for x ≥ 0. Give exact values (a calculator may be used to check).","givenFall":"f(x) = x e^(−x) for x ≥ 0.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find \\(f'(x)\\), locate the critical point, and classify it using the second-derivative test.","sol":"<b>Product rule:</b> \\(f'(x)=e^{-x}+x(-e^{-x})=e^{-x}(1-x)\\). Since \\(e^{-x}>0\\), \\(f'=0\\) only at \\(x=1\\).<br><b>Second-derivative test:</b> \\(f''(x)=e^{-x}(x-2)\\), so \\(f''(1)=e^{-1}(1-2)=-e^{-1}<0\\) ⇒ concave down ⇒ <b>local maximum</b> at \\(x=1\\), \\(f(1)=e^{-1}=\\tfrac1e\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for \\(f'=e^{-x}(1-x)\\), 1 pt for the critical point \\(x=1\\), 1 pt for classifying it a max via \\(f''(1)<0\\) (or a first-derivative sign change).","rubric":["\\(f'(x)=e^{-x}(1-x)\\)","Critical point \\(x=1\\) (since \\(e^{-x}>0\\))","\\(f''(1)=-e^{-1}<0\\) ⇒ local max at \\((1,\\tfrac1e)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(x)=","hint":"e.g. e^{-x}(1-x)"},{"label":"\\text{critical }x=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the interval(s) of concavity and the \\(x\\)-coordinate of any inflection point.","sol":"<b>\\(f''(x)=e^{-x}(x-2)\\)</b>, and \\(e^{-x}>0\\), so the sign of \\(f''\\) is the sign of \\((x-2)\\): \\(f''<0\\) on \\([0,2)\\) (concave down), \\(f''>0\\) on \\((2,\\infty)\\) (concave up). Sign change ⇒ inflection at \\(x=2\\), \\(f(2)=2e^{-2}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for concave down on \\([0,2)\\), up on \\((2,\\infty)\\); 1 pt for the inflection point at \\(x=2\\) via a sign change of \\(f''\\).","rubric":["\\(f''(x)=e^{-x}(x-2)\\); concave down on \\([0,2)\\), up on \\((2,\\infty)\\)","Inflection at \\(x=2\\) (\\(f(2)=2e^{-2}\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{inflection }x=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{concave up on}","hint":"e.g. (2,inf)"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the absolute maximum value of \\(f\\) on \\([0,\\infty)\\), and justify.","sol":"<b>Only one critical point, and it's a max.</b> \\(f(0)=0\\), \\(f(1)=\\tfrac1e\\), and \\(f'(x)=e^{-x}(1-x)>0\\) on \\([0,1)\\), \\(<0\\) on \\((1,\\infty)\\): \\(f\\) rises to \\(x=1\\) then falls forever. So the <b>absolute maximum is \\(f(1)=\\dfrac1e\\)</b> at \\(x=1\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the value \\(\\tfrac1e\\), 1 pt for the justification (sole critical point that is a max, with \\(f\\) decreasing thereafter and \\(f(0)=0\\)). Note the interval is unbounded — the \"endpoint at infinity\" is handled by the limit in Part D.","rubric":["Absolute max value \\(=\\tfrac1e\\) at \\(x=1\\)","Justified: only critical point, \\(f'\\) changes \\(+\\to-\\); \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f\\) decreasing for \\(x>1\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{abs max}=","hint":"exact value"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} x e^{-x}\\), and explain what it says about the graph of \\(f\\).","sol":"<b>\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x}{e^{x}}\\stackrel{\\text{L'H}}{=}\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{e^{x}}=0.\\)</b> (Exponential growth beats linear growth.) So the graph has a <b>horizontal asymptote \\(y=0\\)</b>: after the peak at \\(x=1\\), \\(f\\) decreases toward \\(0\\) but never reaches it.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> 1 pt for the limit \\(=0\\) (a form \\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\), L'Hôpital or the known growth ranking), 1 pt for interpreting it as the end behavior / horizontal asymptote \\(y=0\\).","rubric":["\\(\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}xe^{-x}=0\\) (\\(\\tfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\\), L'Hôpital)","Graph approaches the horizontal asymptote \\(y=0\\) from above"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{limit}=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":6,"id":"u6f0","title":"Accumulation & the Graph","intro":"<b>Geometry of accumulation → extrema with justification → concavity → a chained accumulation function.</b> The classic Section II graph-analysis problem, worth 9 points, no calculator. The figure shows \\(f\\): two line segments from \\((0,0)\\) to \\((2,4)\\) to \\((4,0)\\), then a semicircle of radius 2 below the axis on \\([4,8]\\). <div style='text-align:center;margin:4px auto 12px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:10px;max-width:360px'><svg viewBox='0 0 300 220' width='300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' style='max-width:100%;height:auto'><line x1='20' y1='132.5' x2='285' y2='132.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='34.4' y1='14' x2='34.4' y2='206' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='92.2' y1='128.5' x2='92.2' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='92.2' y='148.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>2</text><line x1='150.0' y1='128.5' x2='150.0' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='150.0' y='148.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>4</text><line x1='207.8' y1='128.5' x2='207.8' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='207.8' y='148.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>6</text><line x1='265.6' y1='128.5' x2='265.6' y2='136.5' stroke='#39424b' stroke-width='1'/><text x='265.6' y='148.5' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>8</text><path d='M34.4,132.5 L92.2,42.5 L150.0,132.5 L150.2,136.0 L150.7,139.5 L151.6,143.0 L152.8,146.4 L154.4,149.7 L156.3,152.9 L158.5,156.0 L161.0,159.0 L163.8,161.7 L166.9,164.3 L170.3,166.7 L173.8,168.9 L177.6,170.9 L181.5,172.6 L185.7,174.1 L189.9,175.3 L194.3,176.3 L198.7,176.9 L203.2,177.4 L207.8,177.5 L212.3,177.4 L216.8,176.9 L221.3,176.3 L225.6,175.3 L229.9,174.1 L234.0,172.6 L238.0,170.9 L241.7,168.9 L245.3,166.7 L248.6,164.3 L251.7,161.7 L254.5,159.0 L257.0,156.0 L259.3,152.9 L261.2,149.7 L262.7,146.4 L264.0,143.0 L264.8,139.5 L265.4,136.0 L265.6,132.5' fill='none' stroke='#5f7ff2' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'/><text x='92.2' y='34.5' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>(2, 4)</text><text x='207.8' y='193.5' fill='#aab3bd' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle' font-family='monospace'>semicircle</text><text x='272' y='28' fill='#8a93a0' font-size='12' text-anchor='end' font-family='monospace'>y = f(x)</text></svg></div>","given":"g(x)=\\int_0^{x} f(t)\\,dt,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le 8","givenNote":"The graph of f consists of two line segments and a semicircle of radius 2 lying below the x-axis, as shown. Let g be the accumulation function defined from 0. No calculator.","givenFall":"g(x) = integral from 0 to x of f(t) dt, where f is the pictured piecewise graph.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Using geometry, find \\(g(4)\\), \\(g(6)\\), and \\(g(8)\\).","sol":"<b>Read areas, signed.</b> The triangle on \\([0,4]\\) has area \\(\\tfrac12(4)(4)=8\\), so \\(g(4)=8\\).<br>By symmetry of the semicircle about \\(x=6\\), the interval \\([4,6]\\) holds exactly half of the semicircular region: \\(\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi(2)^2}{2}=\\pi\\), below the axis. So \\(g(6)=8-\\pi\\approx4.858\\).<br>The full semicircle has area \\(\\tfrac{\\pi(2)^2}{2}=2\\pi\\), so \\(g(8)=8-2\\pi\\approx1.717\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(g(4)=8\\) with the triangle, one for both semicircle values with correct signs. Adding \\(2\\pi\\) instead of subtracting loses the second point — position gives the sign.","rubric":["\\(g(4)=8\\) from triangle area","\\(g(6)=8-\\pi\\) and \\(g(8)=8-2\\pi\\), signed"],"fields":[{"label":"g(4)=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"g(6)=","hint":"exact or 3-decimal"},{"label":"g(8)=","hint":"exact or 3-decimal"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"On \\(0<x<8\\), at what value of \\(x\\) does \\(g\\) attain a local maximum? Justify your answer.","sol":"<b>\\(g'(x)=f(x)\\) by FTC.</b> \\(f>0\\) on \\((0,4)\\) and \\(f<0\\) on \\((4,8)\\), so \\(g'\\) changes sign from positive to negative at \\(x=4\\): a local maximum there, by the First Derivative Test.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(x=4\\), one for the sign-change justification IN TERMS OF \\(f\\). \"Because the graph goes down after\" earns nothing — readers want \\(g'=f\\) named and its sign change stated.","rubric":["\\(x=4\\)","justification: \\(g'=f\\) changes \\(+\\) to \\(-\\) at 4"],"fields":[{"label":"x=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{because } g'=f \\text{ changes sign}","hint":"e.g. + to -"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find all \\(x\\) in \\((0,8)\\) at which the graph of \\(g\\) has a point of inflection. Give a reason.","sol":"<b>\\(g''=f'\\):</b> inflection where \\(f\\) changes direction. \\(f\\) switches from increasing to decreasing at \\(x=2\\) (the corner peak) and from decreasing to increasing at \\(x=6\\) (the semicircle's bottom). Both are inflection points of \\(g\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point per location with the direction-change reason. Citing \"\\(f=0\\) at \\(x=4\\)\" is the classic wrong answer — that's where \\(g\\) has its EXTREMUM, not an inflection.","rubric":["\\(x=2\\) and \\(x=6\\)","reason: \\(f\\) changes direction (so \\(g''=f'\\) changes sign)"],"fields":[{"label":"x=","hint":"list, e.g. 2, 6"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"Let \\(h(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^2} f(t)\\,dt\\). Find \\(h'(x)\\), and evaluate \\(h'(\\sqrt{2})\\).","sol":"<b>FTC Part 1 with the chain rule:</b> \\(h'(x)=f(x^2)\\cdot 2x\\).<br>At \\(x=\\sqrt2\\): \\(h'(\\sqrt2)=f(2)\\cdot2\\sqrt2\\). From the graph, \\(f(2)=4\\) (the peak), so \\(h'(\\sqrt2)=8\\sqrt2\\approx11.314\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the chain factor \\(2x\\), one for reading \\(f(2)=4\\) from the graph, one for the value. Omitting \\(2x\\) — answering \\(f(x^2)\\) — is the single most common error on this question type and forfeits two of the three points.","rubric":["\\(h'(x)=f(x^2)\\cdot2x\\) (chain factor present)","reads \\(f(2)=4\\) from the figure","\\(h'(\\sqrt2)=8\\sqrt2\\approx11.314\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"h'(x)=","hint":"e.g. 2x f(x^2)"},{"label":"h'(\\sqrt2)=","hint":"exact or 3-decimal"}]}]},{"unit":6,"id":"u6f1","title":"The Techniques Gauntlet","intro":"<b>Integration by parts → partial fractions → an improper integral via substitution → a divergence verdict.</b> Nine points, no calculator. Every part is a different machine from Lessons 4–5 — the diagnosis is half the credit.","given":"\\text{Evaluate each integral exactly, or show that it diverges.}","givenNote":"Four independent integrals. Show the setup for each: the parts casting, the partial-fraction constants, and the limit statements. No calculator.","givenFall":"Four independent integrals; show all setups.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi} x\\cos\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{x}{2}\\Bigr)dx\\)","sol":"<b>Parts, LIATE:</b> \\(u=x,\\ dv=\\cos(x/2)dx\\Rightarrow du=dx,\\ v=2\\sin(x/2)\\).<br>\\(\\bigl[2x\\sin(x/2)\\bigr]_0^{\\pi}-\\int_0^{\\pi}2\\sin(x/2)dx=\\bigl[2x\\sin(x/2)+4\\cos(x/2)\\bigr]_0^{\\pi}\\).<br>At \\(\\pi\\): \\(2\\pi(1)+0=2\\pi\\). At 0: \\(0+4\\). Value: \\(2\\pi-4\\approx2.283\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the casting with \\(v=2\\sin(x/2)\\) (the inner \\(\\tfrac12\\) integrates to a 2, not a \\(\\tfrac12\\)), one for the value with both bounds.","rubric":["parts setup with \\(v=2\\sin(x/2)\\)","value \\(2\\pi-4\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{value}=","hint":"exact or 3-decimal"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{2x+7}{(x+3)(x-1)}\\,dx\\)","sol":"<b>Distinct linear factors — cover-up:</b><br>at \\(x=-3\\): \\(A=\\dfrac{2(-3)+7}{-3-1}=\\dfrac{1}{-4}=-\\tfrac14\\);&nbsp; at \\(x=1\\): \\(B=\\dfrac{9}{4}\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\int=-\\tfrac14\\ln|x+3|+\\tfrac94\\ln|x-1|+C\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point per constant (cover-up shown in one line each is full credit), one for the two-log antiderivative with absolute values and \\(+C\\). Missing \\(|\\cdot|\\) or \\(+C\\) is a real deduction on FRQs.","rubric":["\\(A=-\\tfrac14\\) by cover-up","\\(B=\\tfrac94\\) by cover-up","\\(-\\tfrac14\\ln|x+3|+\\tfrac94\\ln|x-1|+C\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"A\\ (\\text{over } x+3)=","hint":"a fraction"},{"label":"B\\ (\\text{over } x-1)=","hint":"a fraction"},{"label":"\\int=","hint":"e.g. -1/4 ln|x+3| + 9/4 ln|x-1| + C"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"\\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{x\\,(\\ln x)^2}\\)","sol":"<b>Substitute INSIDE the improper integral:</b> \\(u=\\ln x,\\ du=\\tfrac{dx}{x}\\); bounds: \\(2\\to\\ln2,\\ \\infty\\to\\infty\\).<br>\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\int_{\\ln2}^{B}u^{-2}du=\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\Bigl(\\frac{1}{\\ln2}-\\frac1B\\Bigr)=\\frac{1}{\\ln 2}\\approx1.4427\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the substitution with converted bounds, one for the limit statement and value. Writing the answer without the \\(\\lim\\) notation forfeits the second point — the limit IS the definition.","rubric":["\\(u=\\ln x\\) with converted bounds","limit shown; value \\(\\tfrac1{\\ln2}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{value}=","hint":"exact or 4-decimal"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Determine whether \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{x\\ln x}\\) converges or diverges. Justify.","sol":"<b>Same substitution, one power weaker:</b> \\(u=\\ln x\\) gives \\(\\displaystyle\\int_{\\ln2}^{\\infty}\\frac{du}{u}=\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\bigl(\\ln B-\\ln\\ln2\\bigr)=\\infty\\): <b>diverges</b>.<br><br>The pair C–D is the p-test knife edge in disguise: \\((\\ln x)^2\\) downstairs is \\(p=2\\) in \\(u\\)-world (converges); \\(\\ln x\\) alone is \\(p=1\\) (diverges). One power of a log is the whole difference.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the verdict, one for justification via the substitution or an explicit limit. \"It goes to zero so it converges\" earns nothing — \\(1/(x\\ln x)\\to0\\) and it still diverges.","rubric":["diverges","justified: \\(\\int du/u\\) with limit \\(\\infty\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{verdict:}","hint":"converges / diverges"},{"label":"\\text{because } u=\\ln x \\text{ gives }","hint":"e.g. ∫ du/u"}]}]},{"unit":6,"id":"u6f2","title":"The Draining Tank","intro":"<b>Rate in, rate out — accumulation, net change, instantaneous comparison, and an optimization with a calculator root.</b> Nine points, <b>calculator allowed</b> (and required for part D). The Section II archetype: every AP exam since 1998 has had one of these.","given":"P(t)=12+10\\cos\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi t}{12}\\Bigr),\\qquad D(t)=\\frac{t^2}{4},\\qquad W(0)=50","givenNote":"For 0 ≤ t ≤ 12 hours, water is pumped into a tank at rate P(t) and drained at rate D(t), both in gallons per hour. The tank holds W(t) gallons, with 50 gallons at t = 0. Calculator allowed.","givenFall":"P(t)=12+10cos(πt/12), D(t)=t²/4, W(0)=50, on [0,12].","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"How many gallons are pumped INTO the tank during the 12 hours?","sol":"<b>Accumulate the in-rate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{12}\\Bigl(12+10\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\Bigr)dt=\\Bigl[12t+\\tfrac{120}{\\pi}\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\Bigr]_0^{12}=144+\\tfrac{120}{\\pi}\\sin\\pi=144+0=144\\) gallons.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the integral setup with correct limits, one for 144. The \\(\\sin\\pi=0\\) cancellation is the question's gift — the cosine pumps symmetrically above and below its mean.","rubric":["\\(\\int_0^{12}P\\,dt\\) setup","144 gallons"],"fields":[{"label":"\\int_0^{12}P\\,dt=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"Find \\(W(12)\\), the amount of water in the tank at \\(t=12\\).","sol":"<b>Initial + net change:</b> \\(W(12)=50+\\int_0^{12}\\bigl(P(t)-D(t)\\bigr)dt\\).<br>The drain: \\(\\int_0^{12}\\tfrac{t^2}{4}dt=\\tfrac{t^3}{12}\\Big|_0^{12}=\\tfrac{1728}{12}=144\\).<br>So \\(W(12)=50+144-144=50\\) gallons — the tank ends exactly where it began.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the \\(W(0)+\\int(P-D)\\) structure, one for \\(\\int D=144\\), one for 50. Forgetting the initial 50 is the classic single-point loss; answering \"0 net change\" without stating \\(W(12)=50\\) also drops a point — answer the question asked.","rubric":["\\(W(12)=50+\\int_0^{12}(P-D)\\,dt\\)","\\(\\int_0^{12}D\\,dt=144\\)","\\(W(12)=50\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"W(12)=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Is the amount of water in the tank increasing or decreasing at \\(t=9\\)? Give a reason.","sol":"<b>Look at the derivative:</b> \\(W'(t)=P(t)-D(t)\\). At \\(t=9\\): \\(P(9)=12+10\\cos\\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}=12-5\\sqrt2\\approx4.929\\) and \\(D(9)=\\tfrac{81}{4}=20.25\\).<br>\\(W'(9)\\approx4.929-20.25=-15.321<0\\): <b>decreasing</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the verdict, one for the reason via the SIGN of \\(P(9)-D(9)\\) with values shown. Comparing \\(W(9)\\) to \\(W(8)\\) is not the expected justification — instantaneous questions want the derivative.","rubric":["decreasing","because \\(W'(9)=P(9)-D(9)\\approx-15.321<0\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{verdict:}","hint":"increasing / decreasing"},{"label":"P(9)-D(9)\\approx","hint":"3 decimals"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"At what time \\(t\\) is the amount of water in the tank a maximum, and what is that maximum? Justify.","sol":"<b>Candidates: endpoints and where \\(W'=P-D=0\\).</b> Solve \\(12+10\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}=\\tfrac{t^2}{4}\\) with the calculator: \\(t\\approx6.523\\) (the only crossing — \\(P-D\\) starts at \\(+22\\), is still \\(+3\\) at \\(t=6\\), and \\(-2.8\\) by \\(t=7\\)).<br>\\(W'\\) changes \\(+\\) to \\(-\\) there, so it's a maximum: \\(W(6.523)=50+\\int_0^{6.523}\\bigl(P-D\\bigr)dt\\approx50+92.987\\approx142.987\\) gallons. Endpoints check: \\(W(0)=50\\), \\(W(12)=50\\) — both smaller.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the critical time from \\(P=D\\) with sign-change (or candidates-table) justification, one for the maximum via the integral. Reporting \\(t\\) without justifying WHY it's a max is the most common dropped point on this archetype.","rubric":["\\(t\\approx6.523\\) where \\(P=D\\), with \\(+\\to-\\) justification","\\(W_{max}=50+\\int_0^{t}(P-D)\\approx142.987\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"t\\approx","hint":"3 decimals"},{"label":"W_{max}\\approx","hint":"3 decimals"}]}]},{"unit":7,"id":"u7f0","title":"Slope Field & Euler","intro":"<b>Slope field reading → Euler approximation → separable solution → error analysis.</b> A differential-equations free response worth 9 points. Parts A–C are no-calculator; part D uses concavity to judge the Euler estimate, exactly the justification AP readers expect.","given":"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^{2}(y-1),\\qquad f(0)=3","givenNote":"Let y = f(x) be the particular solution to dy/dx = x²(y − 1) with f(0) = 3. No calculator is needed on parts A–C.","givenFall":"dy/dx = x^2 (y - 1), with f(0) = 3.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the slope of a solution curve at the points \\((0,3)\\), \\((1,3)\\), and \\((-1,2)\\). At which points in the plane is the slope equal to \\(0\\)?","sol":"<b>The slope is \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x^2(y-1)\\); evaluate at each point.</b><br>\\((0,3)\\): \\(0^2(3-1)=0\\). \\((1,3)\\): \\(1^2(3-1)=2\\). \\((-1,2)\\): \\((-1)^2(2-1)=1\\).<br><b>Zero slope</b> occurs where \\(x^2(y-1)=0\\): either \\(x=0\\) (the whole \\(y\\)-axis) or \\(y=1\\) (a horizontal line).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the three slope values, one for identifying <i>both</i> \\(x=0\\) and \\(y=1\\). Giving only \\(y=1\\) (forgetting the \\(x^2\\) factor) loses the second point.","rubric":["Slopes are \\(0,\\ 2,\\ 1\\) at the three points","Slope is \\(0\\) where \\(x=0\\) or \\(y=1\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{slopes at the 3 pts:}","hint":"e.g. 0, 2, 1"},{"label":"\\text{zero slope when:}","hint":"x=0 or y=1"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Use Euler's method with two steps of equal size, starting from \\((0,3)\\), to approximate \\(f(1)\\).","sol":"<b>Use \\(h=0.5\\) and \\(y_{n+1}=y_n+h\\,f(x_n,y_n)\\) with \\(f=x^2(y-1)\\).</b><br><b>Step 1</b> at \\((0,3)\\): slope \\(0^2(3-1)=0\\), so \\(y(0.5)=3+0.5(0)=3\\).<br><b>Step 2</b> at \\((0.5,3)\\): slope \\((0.25)(3-1)=0.5\\), so \\(f(1)\\approx 3+0.5(0.5)=3.25\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the Euler setup and steps, one for the value \\(3.25\\). Recompute the slope at the new point — reusing the first slope \\(0\\) would wrongly give \\(3\\).","rubric":["Euler steps with \\(h=0.5\\) from \\((0,3)\\)","Approximation \\(f(1)\\approx 3.25\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f(1)\\approx","hint":"a decimal"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the particular solution \\(y=f(x)\\) to the differential equation with \\(f(0)=3\\).","sol":"<b>Separate the variables.</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y-1}=x^2\\,dx\\).<br><b>Integrate both sides:</b> \\(\\ln|y-1|=\\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C\\).<br><b>Apply \\(f(0)=3\\):</b> \\(\\ln 2=0+C\\), so \\(C=\\ln 2\\). Exponentiate: \\(y-1=2e^{x^3/3}\\Rightarrow y=1+2e^{x^3/3}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for separating and the antiderivatives, one for the \\(+C\\) and using the initial condition, one for solving explicitly for \\(y\\). Dropping the constant or the absolute value before applying \\(f(0)\\) is the usual slip.","rubric":["Separated to \\(\\dfrac{dy}{y-1}=x^2\\,dx\\) and integrated","Used \\(f(0)=3\\) to find \\(C=\\ln 2\\)","Solved to \\(y=1+2e^{x^3/3}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"y=","hint":"e.g. 1+2e^(x^3/3)"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the exact value of \\(f(1)\\), and explain why the Euler approximation in part B is an underestimate.","sol":"<b>Exact value:</b> \\(f(1)=1+2e^{1/3}\\approx 3.791\\).<br><b>Why the estimate is low:</b> on \\([0,1]\\) the solution is concave up, so its tangent lines lie below the curve. Euler steps along those tangents, so the approximation \\(3.25\\) falls below the true value \\(3.791\\) — an underestimate.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the exact value \\(1+2e^{1/3}\\), one for the concavity reasoning. Saying \"underestimate\" without the concave-up justification earns only partial credit on the AP rubric.","rubric":["Exact \\(f(1)=1+2e^{1/3}\\approx 3.791\\)","Concave up \\(\\Rightarrow\\) tangent steps below the curve \\(\\Rightarrow\\) underestimate"],"fields":[{"label":"f(1)=","hint":"≈3.791"},{"label":"\\text{Euler is an...}","hint":"under/overestimate"}]}]},{"unit":7,"id":"u7f1","title":"Logistic Rumor","intro":"<b>Carrying capacity → maximum rate → symmetry of the rate → long-run limit.</b> A logistic-growth free response worth 7 points, including a 'find and interpret' part and a symmetry argument — the kind of reasoning AP rewards over raw computation.","given":"\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.0008\\,P\\,(1200-P),\\qquad P(0)=50","givenNote":"A rumor spreads through a school of 1200 students. P(t) is the number who have heard it after t hours, with dP/dt = 0.0008·P·(1200 − P) and P(0) = 50.","givenFall":"dP/dt = 0.0008 P (1200 - P), with P(0) = 50.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"What is the carrying capacity \\(L\\), and at what value of \\(P\\) is the rumor spreading fastest?","sol":"<b>Rewrite in logistic form.</b> \\(0.0008P(1200-P)=0.96\\,P\\!\\left(1-\\dfrac{P}{1200}\\right)\\), so the carrying capacity is \\(L=1200\\).<br><b>Fastest growth</b> for a logistic model is at half the capacity: \\(P=\\dfrac{L}{2}=600\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(L=1200\\), one for fastest spread at \\(P=600\\).","rubric":["Carrying capacity \\(L=1200\\)","Fastest spread at \\(P=L/2=600\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"L=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{fastest at }P=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\) when \\(P=600\\), and interpret it in context.","sol":"<b>Substitute \\(P=600\\):</b> \\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.0008(600)(1200-600)=0.0008(600)(600)=288\\).<br><b>Interpretation:</b> when 600 students have heard the rumor, it is spreading at its maximum rate — about 288 students per hour.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the value \\(288\\), one for an interpretation in context with correct units (students per hour).","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt}=0.0008(600)(600)=288\\)","Interpreted as \\(\\approx 288\\) students per hour (the maximum rate)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\left.\\dfrac{dP}{dt}\\right|_{P=600}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Is the rumor spreading faster when \\(P=300\\) or when \\(P=900\\)? Justify your answer.","sol":"<b>Compute both rates.</b> \\(P=300\\): \\(0.0008(300)(900)=216\\). \\(P=900\\): \\(0.0008(900)(300)=216\\).<br>They are <b>equal</b>. The growth-rate function \\(0.0008P(1200-P)\\) is a downward parabola symmetric about \\(P=600\\), and \\(300\\) and \\(900\\) are equidistant from it.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for both rates equal to \\(216\\), one for the symmetry justification about \\(P=L/2=600\\).","rubric":["Both rates equal \\(216\\)","Justified by symmetry of the rate about \\(P=600\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{rate at }P=300:","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{rate at }P=900:","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":1,"prompt":"Find \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t)\\).","sol":"<b>Every positive starting value approaches the carrying capacity.</b> Since \\(0<P(0)=50<1200\\), \\(P\\) increases toward \\(L\\): \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t)=1200\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the limit \\(1200\\).","rubric":["Limit equals the carrying capacity, \\(1200\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":7,"id":"u7f2","title":"Exponential Decay","intro":"<b>Separable solution → evaluation → half-life → rate interpretation.</b> A first-order decay free response worth 8 points. Parts B and C are calculator-active; part D reads a rate straight from the differential equation, a common AP shortcut.","given":"\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=-0.2A,\\qquad A(0)=500","givenNote":"A chemical in a tank decays so that dA/dt = -0.2A, where A is the mass in grams and t is in hours, with A(0) = 500. A calculator is permitted for parts B and C.","givenFall":"dA/dt = -0.2 A, with A(0) = 500 grams.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Solve the differential equation for \\(A(t)\\).","sol":"<b>Separate and integrate.</b> \\(\\dfrac{dA}{A}=-0.2\\,dt\\Rightarrow \\ln|A|=-0.2t+C\\).<br><b>Exponentiate</b> and apply \\(A(0)=500\\): \\(A=Ce^{-0.2t}\\), and \\(A(0)=500\\Rightarrow C=500\\).<br>\\(A(t)=500e^{-0.2t}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for separating and the antiderivatives, one for the \\(+C\\), one for solving with the initial condition to \\(A=500e^{-0.2t}\\).","rubric":["Separated and integrated to \\(\\ln|A|=-0.2t+C\\)","Exponentiated and applied \\(A(0)=500\\)","\\(A(t)=500e^{-0.2t}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"A(t)=","hint":"e.g. 500e^(-0.2t)"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":1,"prompt":"How many grams remain after 5 hours? (Round to three decimal places.)","sol":"<b>Substitute \\(t=5\\):</b> \\(A(5)=500e^{-0.2(5)}=500e^{-1}\\approx 183.940\\) grams.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(\\approx 183.940\\) grams (\\(500/e\\)).","rubric":["\\(A(5)=500e^{-1}\\approx 183.940\\) grams"],"fields":[{"label":"A(5)=","hint":"≈183.940"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the half-life of the chemical. (Round to three decimal places.)","sol":"<b>Set the mass to half its initial value and solve.</b> \\(250=500e^{-0.2t}\\Rightarrow e^{-0.2t}=\\tfrac12\\).<br><b>Take logarithms:</b> \\(-0.2t=-\\ln 2\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{0.2}\\approx 3.466\\) hours.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the equation \\(e^{-0.2t}=\\tfrac12\\), one for \\(t=(\\ln 2)/0.2\\approx 3.466\\) hours.","rubric":["Set \\(e^{-0.2t}=\\tfrac12\\)","Half-life \\(t=(\\ln 2)/0.2\\approx 3.466\\) hours"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{half-life}=","hint":"≈3.466 h"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"At what rate is the mass changing when \\(A=100\\) grams? Include units and state whether the mass is increasing or decreasing.","sol":"<b>The differential equation gives the rate directly</b> — no need to solve again. \\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=-0.2(100)=-20\\) g/hr.<br>The rate is negative, so the mass is <b>decreasing</b> at 20 grams per hour.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(-20\\), one for the units and stating it is decreasing.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dA}{dt}=-0.2(100)=-20\\) g/hr","Negative rate \\(\\Rightarrow\\) the mass is decreasing"],"fields":[{"label":"\\left.\\dfrac{dA}{dt}\\right|_{A=100}=","hint":"g/hr"},{"label":"\\text{increasing or decreasing?}","hint":""}]}]},{"unit":8,"id":"u8f0","title":"Area + Volume","intro":"<b>Region between two curves → intersections → area → volume by revolution → volume by cross sections.</b> The classic Section II area-and-volume problem, worth 9 points.","given":"\\text{Region } R \\text{ is bounded by } y=\\sqrt{x} \\text{ and } y=\\tfrac{x}{2}.","givenNote":"Let R be the region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves y = √x and y = x/2.","givenFall":"Region R bounded by y = √x and y = x/2","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":1,"prompt":"Find the x-coordinates where the two curves intersect.","sol":"<b>Set the curves equal</b> and solve. \\(\\sqrt{x}=\\tfrac{x}{2}\\). Square both sides to clear the root: \\(x=\\tfrac{x^2}{4}\\Rightarrow 4x=x^2\\Rightarrow x^2-4x=0\\Rightarrow x(x-4)=0\\). So \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=4\\).<br><br><b>Why it matters:</b> these become your limits of integration for every later part. Squaring can introduce extra roots, so in principle you'd check both work — here both lie in the first quadrant and satisfy the original equation.","rubric":["Set the curves equal and solved to get x = 0 and x = 4"],"fields":[{"label":"x=","hint":"e.g. 0 and 4"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the area of region \\(R\\).","sol":"<b>Decide top vs. bottom:</b> test a point in \\((0,4)\\), say \\(x=1\\): \\(\\sqrt{1}=1\\) vs \\(\\tfrac12\\), so \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) is on top.<br><b>Integrate (top − bottom):</b> \\(\\text{Area}=\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\Big(\\sqrt{x}-\\tfrac{x}{2}\\Big)dx=\\Big[\\tfrac23 x^{3/2}-\\tfrac{x^2}{4}\\Big]_0^4\\).<br>Evaluate at 4: \\(\\tfrac23(8)-\\tfrac{16}{4}=\\tfrac{16}{3}-4=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Scoring note:</b> on the AP, one point is the correct integral (integrand <i>and</i> limits), one is the value. Even a small antiderivative slip keeps the setup point if the integral is right.","rubric":["Integral set up as ∫₀⁴ (√x − x/2) dx (correct integrand and limits)","Evaluated to Area = 4/3"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{Area}=","hint":"e.g. 4/3"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Region \\(R\\) is revolved about the <b>x-axis</b>. Find the volume.","sol":"<b>There's a gap below the region, so use washers:</b> \\(\\pi(R^2-r^2)\\). The outer radius is the curve farther from the x-axis — on \\([0,4]\\), \\(\\sqrt{x}\\ge\\tfrac{x}{2}\\), so \\(R=\\sqrt{x}\\), \\(r=\\tfrac{x}{2}\\).<br><b>Set up and square each radius:</b> \\(V=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\Big[(\\sqrt{x})^2-\\big(\\tfrac{x}{2}\\big)^2\\Big]dx=\\pi\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\Big(x-\\tfrac{x^2}{4}\\Big)dx\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\pi\\Big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac{x^3}{12}\\Big]_0^4=\\pi\\Big(8-\\tfrac{64}{12}\\Big)=\\pi\\Big(8-\\tfrac{16}{3}\\Big)=\\tfrac{8\\pi}{3}\\).<br><br><b>The critical move:</b> subtract the <i>squares</i> of the radii, not the square of the difference — \\(R^2-r^2\\neq(R-r)^2\\). The 3 points are typically: washer setup, correct radii squared, correct value.","rubric":["Washer setup π∫[(√x)² − (x/2)²] dx with correct limits","Subtracted the squares of the radii (not (R−r)²)","Evaluated to 8π/3"],"fields":[{"label":"V=","hint":"e.g. 8pi/3"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"Region \\(R\\) is the base of a solid whose cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are squares. Find that volume.","sol":"<b>Cross-section volume is \\(\\displaystyle\\int_a^b A(x)\\,dx\\) — no \\(\\pi\\), since the slices are squares, not discs.</b><br><b>One slice:</b> the side of each square is the height of the region, \\(s=\\sqrt{x}-\\tfrac{x}{2}\\), so its area is \\(A=s^2=\\big(\\sqrt{x}-\\tfrac{x}{2}\\big)^2\\).<br><b>Expand:</b> \\(\\big(\\sqrt{x}-\\tfrac{x}{2}\\big)^2=x-x^{3/2}+\\tfrac{x^2}{4}\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\Big(x-x^{3/2}+\\tfrac{x^2}{4}\\Big)dx=\\Big[\\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\\tfrac25 x^{5/2}+\\tfrac{x^3}{12}\\Big]_0^4=8-\\tfrac{64}{5}+\\tfrac{16}{3}=\\tfrac{8}{15}\\).<br><br><b>The trap this part guards against:</b> writing \\(\\pi\\int(\\ldots)\\) out of habit. Squares get no \\(\\pi\\); the squaring is the area of the <i>square</i>, \\(s^2\\). Points: correct \\(A=s^2\\) (no π), correct integral, correct value.","rubric":["Used A = (√x − x/2)² with NO π (square cross-section, not a disc)","Set up ∫₀⁴ of that area","Evaluated to 8/15"],"fields":[{"label":"V=","hint":"e.g. 8/15"}]}]},{"unit":8,"id":"u8f1","title":"Particle Motion","intro":"<b>Velocity → position & acceleration → displacement → total distance → final position.</b> The standard 9-point motion problem; the distance part is where most points are lost.","given":"v(t)=t^2-4t+3,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4","givenNote":"A particle moves along a line with velocity v(t) = t² − 4t + 3 (units/sec). At t = 0 its position is s(0) = 2.","givenFall":"v(t) = t² − 4t + 3 on [0,4], s(0) = 2","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the velocity and acceleration at \\(t=2\\), and state the direction of motion.","sol":"<b>Velocity:</b> \\(v(2)=4-8+3=-1\\). Negative ⇒ the particle is moving in the <b>negative direction (left)</b>.<br><b>Acceleration</b> is the derivative of velocity: \\(a(t)=v'(t)=2t-4\\), so \\(a(2)=0\\).<br><br><b>Reading it:</b> at \\(t=2\\) the particle is moving left, and with \\(a=0\\) the velocity is momentarily neither growing nor shrinking — this is the vertex of the parabola \\(v(t)\\), its most-negative point. Points: \\(v(2)\\) with direction, and \\(a(2)\\).","rubric":["v(2) = −1, so moving left (negative direction)","a(t) = 2t − 4, so a(2) = 0"],"fields":[{"label":"v(2)=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"a(2)=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the displacement over \\([0,4]\\).","sol":"<b>Displacement is the signed integral of velocity</b> — direction counts, so no absolute value here: \\(\\Delta s=\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 v\\,dt\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4(t^2-4t+3)\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-2t^2+3t\\Big]_0^4=\\tfrac{64}{3}-32+12=\\tfrac{64}{3}-20=\\tfrac{4}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Meaning:</b> the particle ends up \\(\\tfrac43\\) units to the right of where it started — even though it spent time moving left in between. Points: integral of \\(v\\) (not \\(|v|\\)), and the value.","rubric":["Set up displacement as ∫₀⁴ v dt (signed, no absolute value)","Evaluated to 4/3"],"fields":[{"label":"\\Delta s=","hint":"e.g. 4/3"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the total distance traveled over \\([0,4]\\).","sol":"<b>Distance uses \\(|v|\\), so first find where the particle turns around</b> (where \\(v=0\\)): factor \\(v=(t-1)(t-3)\\Rightarrow t=1,3\\). Velocity is \\(+\\) on \\([0,1]\\), \\(-\\) on \\([1,3]\\), \\(+\\) on \\([3,4]\\).<br><b>Split and add magnitudes:</b> \\(\\text{dist}=\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 v\\,dt-\\displaystyle\\int_1^3 v\\,dt+\\displaystyle\\int_3^4 v\\,dt\\). Each piece evaluates to \\(\\tfrac43\\), so \\(\\text{dist}=\\tfrac43+\\tfrac43+\\tfrac43=4\\).<br><br><b>Why it's worth 3 points:</b> the structure is the hard part. Finding the turning points \\(t=1,3\\) is one point, splitting with correct signs (or \\(\\int|v|\\)) is another, and the value is the third. The classic lost point: using \\(\\int v\\) and getting the displacement \\(\\tfrac43\\) instead.","rubric":["Found where v = 0: t = 1 and t = 3","Split into pieces and used |v| (correct signs on each subinterval)","Total distance = 4"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{distance}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the particle's position at \\(t=4\\).","sol":"<b>Position = starting position + accumulated displacement:</b> \\(s(4)=s(0)+\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 v\\,dt\\). This is the accumulation form of the FTC.<br><b>Compute:</b> you already found \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 v\\,dt=\\tfrac43\\) in Part B, so \\(s(4)=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}{3}\\).<br><br><b>Don't restart the integral:</b> reuse Part B. The two points are typically the correct expression \\(s(0)+\\int_0^4 v\\) and the value. The common slip is forgetting to add \\(s(0)=2\\).","rubric":["Wrote s(4) = s(0) + ∫₀⁴ v dt (added the initial position)","s(4) = 2 + 4/3 = 10/3"],"fields":[{"label":"s(4)=","hint":"e.g. 10/3"}]}]},{"unit":8,"id":"u8f2","title":"Accumulation (rates)","intro":"<b>Total inflow → net rate & direction → amount accumulated → when the amount is greatest.</b> The reservoir/tank problem, 9 points, ending with a sign-analysis part.","given":"E(t)=20+5t,\\qquad L(t)=3t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4","givenNote":"Water enters a tank at E(t) = 20 + 5t gallons/hour and leaves at L(t) = 3t gallons/hour. The tank holds 100 gallons at t = 0.","givenFall":"E(t) = 20 + 5t in, L(t) = 3t out, start 100 gal","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"How many gallons enter the tank during the first 4 hours?","sol":"<b>Amount = integral of rate.</b> Gallons in \\(=\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 E(t)\\,dt=\\displaystyle\\int_0^4(20+5t)\\,dt\\).<br><b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\Big[20t+\\tfrac52 t^2\\Big]_0^4=80+\\tfrac52(16)=80+40=120\\) gallons.<br><br><b>Note the wording:</b> \"enter\" means integrate \\(E\\) alone — not the net \\(E-L\\). Points: correct integral of \\(E\\), and the value.","rubric":["Set up ∫₀⁴ E(t) dt (inflow only)","Evaluated to 120 gallons"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{gallons}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"At \\(t=2\\), is the amount of water in the tank increasing or decreasing? Justify with the net rate.","sol":"<b>The amount changes at the net rate</b> \\(E(t)-L(t)\\). Evaluate at \\(t=2\\): \\(E(2)-L(2)=(20+10)-(6)=24\\) gal/hr.<br><b>Interpret:</b> \\(24>0\\), so water is entering faster than it leaves — the amount is <b>increasing</b>.<br><br><b>Justification is the second point</b> on the AP: you must state the net rate is positive <i>and</i> conclude \"increasing.\" A bare \"increasing\" with no rate shown typically earns nothing.","rubric":["Computed E(2) − L(2) = 24 gal/hr","Stated positive ⇒ increasing (with justification)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{net rate}=","hint":"gal/hr, a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"How much water is in the tank at \\(t=4\\)?","sol":"<b>Amount = start + net accumulated change:</b> \\(\\text{Amount}(4)=100+\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\big[E(t)-L(t)\\big]dt\\).<br><b>Net rate:</b> \\(E-L=(20+5t)-3t=20+2t\\). <b>Integrate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4(20+2t)\\,dt=\\Big[20t+t^2\\Big]_0^4=80+16=96\\) gallons of net gain.<br><b>Add the initial amount:</b> \\(100+96=196\\) gallons.<br><br><b>Three points, three ideas:</b> the net-rate integrand \\(E-L\\), the definite integral with the initial-condition term \\(100+\\int\\), and the value. The signature error: reporting the net change \\(96\\) and forgetting the \\(+100\\).","rubric":["Integrand is the net rate E − L over [0,4]","Wrote Amount = 100 + ∫₀⁴ (E − L) dt (included initial 100)","Evaluated to 196 gallons"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{gallons}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"On the interval \\([0,4]\\), at what time is the amount of water in the tank greatest? Justify.","sol":"<b>The amount is greatest where its rate of change stops being positive</b> — check the net rate \\(E-L=20+2t\\) on \\([0,4]\\).<br><b>Analyze the sign:</b> \\(20+2t>0\\) for every \\(t\\) in \\([0,4]\\) (it ranges from \\(20\\) up to \\(28\\)). The net rate is never zero or negative, so the amount is <b>strictly increasing</b> the whole time.<br><b>Conclusion:</b> a strictly increasing quantity is largest at the <b>right endpoint, \\(t=4\\)</b>.<br><br><b>The AP reasoning:</b> normally you'd find critical times where \\(E-L=0\\) and test them — but here there are none, so you must argue from the sign that the maximum falls at the endpoint. Points: justification that the net rate stays positive, and the answer \\(t=4\\).","rubric":["Showed the net rate E − L = 20 + 2t > 0 on all of [0,4] (no interior critical point)","Concluded the maximum is at the endpoint t = 4"],"fields":[{"label":"t=","hint":"a time in [0,4]"}]}]},{"unit":8,"id":"u8f3","title":"Average Value","intro":"<b>Average value → a point value → MVT for integrals → instantaneous rate and its meaning.</b> A 9-point problem that tests the <i>concepts</i> behind average value, not just the formula.","given":"f(t)=60+10\\sin\\!\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\right),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 12","givenNote":"The temperature in a room is f(t) = 60 + 10 sin(πt/12) degrees Fahrenheit, for 0 ≤ t ≤ 12 hours.","givenFall":"f(t) = 60 + 10 sin(πt/12) on [0,12]","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the average temperature over the 12 hours. Give an exact value.","sol":"<b>Average value</b> \\(=\\dfrac{1}{12-0}\\displaystyle\\int_0^{12}\\Big[60+10\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\Big]dt\\).<br><b>Integrate the sine</b> (watch the chain-rule factor): \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{12}\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\,dt=\\Big[-\\tfrac{12}{\\pi}\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\Big]_0^{12}=-\\tfrac{12}{\\pi}(\\cos\\pi-\\cos 0)=-\\tfrac{12}{\\pi}(-1-1)=\\tfrac{24}{\\pi}\\).<br><b>Assemble:</b> \\(\\dfrac{1}{12}\\Big[60(12)+10\\cdot\\tfrac{24}{\\pi}\\Big]=\\dfrac{1}{12}\\Big[720+\\tfrac{240}{\\pi}\\Big]=60+\\dfrac{20}{\\pi}\\approx66.4^\\circ\\).<br><br><b>Three points:</b> the average-value setup \\(\\tfrac{1}{12}\\int\\), correct integration (the \\(-\\tfrac{12}{\\pi}\\) factor is where points vanish), and the exact value. The most common slip is forgetting to divide by 12.","rubric":["Set up (1/12)∫₀¹² f dt","Integrated correctly (sine term gives 24/π, with the −12/π factor)","Average = 60 + 20/π"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{avg}=","hint":"e.g. 60+20/pi"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":1,"prompt":"What is the temperature at \\(t=6\\)?","sol":"<b>Just evaluate</b> \\(f(6)=60+10\\sin\\tfrac{6\\pi}{12}=60+10\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}=60+10(1)=70^\\circ\\).<br><br>Notice \\(\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2=1\\) is the maximum of sine, so \\(70^\\circ\\) is the highest the temperature gets — a fact Part D will connect to. One point: the value.","rubric":["f(6) = 60 + 10·sin(π/2) = 70°"],"fields":[{"label":"f(6)=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Must there be a time \\(c\\) in \\([0,12]\\) where \\(f(c)\\) equals the average from Part A? Name the theorem and justify.","sol":"<b>Yes.</b> The justification is the <b>Mean Value Theorem for Integrals</b>: if \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([a,b]\\), then there exists some \\(c\\) in \\([a,b]\\) with \\(f(c)=\\) the average value.<br><b>Check the hypothesis:</b> \\(f(t)=60+10\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\) is continuous everywhere (sums and compositions of continuous functions), so the theorem applies on \\([0,12]\\). Therefore some \\(c\\) gives \\(f(c)=60+\\tfrac{20}{\\pi}\\approx66.4^\\circ\\).<br><br><b>Two points:</b> citing the theorem by name, and verifying continuity (the hypothesis). \"Yes\" alone, without the theorem and the continuity check, won't earn full credit on the AP.","rubric":["Answered Yes and named the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals","Verified f is continuous on [0,12] (the hypothesis)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{Yes/No}=","hint":"yes or no"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"Find \\(f'(6)\\), the instantaneous rate of change of temperature at \\(t=6\\), and explain what it tells you.","sol":"<b>Differentiate</b> (chain rule): \\(f'(t)=10\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}=\\tfrac{10\\pi}{12}\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{12}\\).<br><b>Evaluate at \\(t=6\\):</b> \\(f'(6)=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}(0)=0\\) °F/hr.<br><b>Interpret:</b> the temperature is <b>momentarily not changing</b> at \\(t=6\\) — and since Part B showed \\(f(6)=70^\\circ\\) is the maximum, this makes sense: the rate is zero exactly at the peak, where the temperature stops rising and is about to fall.<br><br><b>The conceptual payoff:</b> this contrasts the three \"rates\" the problem touches — the <i>average value</i> \\((\\approx66.4^\\circ\\), a mean height), versus the <i>instantaneous rate</i> \\((f'(6)=0\\), a slope). They answer completely different questions. Points: correct \\(f'(t)\\), the value \\(f'(6)=0\\), and a correct interpretation tied to the maximum.","rubric":["Differentiated: f′(t) = (5π/6)cos(πt/12)","f′(6) = 0","Interpreted: temperature momentarily not changing (it's the maximum from Part B)"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(6)=","hint":"a number"}]}]},{"unit":9,"id":"u9f0","title":"Vector Motion","intro":"<b>Acceleration → distance → direction with justification → position.</b> The classic calculator-active particle-motion problem from Section II, worth 9 points. Parts B and D require evaluating integrals on a calculator; part C asks for a justification, exactly as the readers expect.","given":"\\vec v(t)=\\big\\langle\\, t^{2}-4,\\ \\ 2\\sin t\\,\\big\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4","givenNote":"A particle moving in the xy-plane has velocity vector v(t) = ⟨t² − 4, 2 sin t⟩ for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4. At time t = 0, the particle is at position (3, −1). A graphing calculator is required for parts B and D.","givenFall":"v(t) = ⟨t²−4, 2 sin t⟩ for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4; position (3, −1) at t = 0.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the acceleration vector \\(\\vec a(3)\\).","sol":"<b>Acceleration is the component-wise derivative of velocity:</b> \\(\\vec a(t)=\\langle x''(t),\\ y''(t)\\rangle\\).<br><b>Differentiate each component</b> of \\(\\vec v(t)=\\langle t^2-4,\\ 2\\sin t\\rangle\\): \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(t^2-4)=2t\\) and \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)=2\\cos t\\). So \\(\\vec a(t)=\\langle 2t,\\ 2\\cos t\\rangle\\).<br><b>Evaluate at \\(t=3\\):</b> \\(\\vec a(3)=\\langle 2(3),\\ 2\\cos 3\\rangle=\\langle 6,\\ -1.980\\rangle\\) (since \\(\\cos 3\\approx-0.990\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for differentiating to the acceleration vector \\(\\langle 2t,\\ 2\\cos t\\rangle\\), one for the correct evaluation. A common slip is reporting \\(\\vec v(3)\\) instead of \\(\\vec a(3)\\) — read whether the question asks for velocity or acceleration.","rubric":["Differentiated to \\(\\vec a(t)=\\langle 2t,\\ 2\\cos t\\rangle\\)","Evaluated \\(\\vec a(3)=\\langle 6,\\ -1.980\\rangle\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\vec a(3)=","hint":"e.g. <6, -1.980>"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the total distance traveled by the particle over the interval \\(0\\le t\\le 4\\).","sol":"<b>Total distance is the integral of speed</b> — the magnitude of the velocity vector — over the interval: \\(\\text{distance}=\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{\\big(x'(t)\\big)^2+\\big(y'(t)\\big)^2}\\,dt=\\int_0^4\\sqrt{(t^2-4)^2+(2\\sin t)^2}\\,dt\\).<br><b>Evaluate on a calculator:</b> \\(\\approx 17.664\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the integral of speed with correct integrand and limits, one for the value. The single most common error is integrating the components to get displacement instead of integrating the magnitude — note the \\(x\\)-velocity \\(t^2-4\\) changes sign at \\(t=2\\), so the path length exceeds the net displacement.","rubric":["Set up \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\sqrt{(t^2-4)^2+(2\\sin t)^2}\\,dt\\)","Evaluated \\(\\approx 17.664\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{distance}=","hint":"e.g. 17.664"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Is the particle moving left or right at \\(t=3\\)? Find the time \\(t\\) in \\([0,4]\\) at which the particle is farthest to the left, and justify your answer.","sol":"<b>Horizontal direction is governed by the sign of the \\(x\\)-velocity</b> \\(x'(t)=t^2-4\\).<br><b>At \\(t=3\\):</b> \\(x'(3)=9-4=5\\gt 0\\), so the \\(x\\)-coordinate is increasing — the particle is <b>moving right</b>.<br><b>Farthest left</b> means the \\(x\\)-coordinate is at its minimum, which occurs where \\(x'(t)\\) changes from negative to positive. Solve \\(x'(t)=t^2-4=0\\Rightarrow t=2\\) (in \\([0,4]\\)). For \\(0\\lt t\\lt 2\\), \\(x'(t)\\lt 0\\) (moving left); for \\(2\\lt t\\lt 4\\), \\(x'(t)\\gt 0\\) (moving right). Since \\(x\\) decreases then increases, the particle is <b>farthest to the left at \\(t=2\\)</b>.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the direction at \\(t=3\\) (with the sign of \\(x'(3)\\)), one for finding \\(t=2\\), one for the justification that \\(x'\\) changes sign \\(-\\) to \\(+\\) there. An answer of \\(t=2\\) with no sign analysis earns the point for the value but not the justification.","rubric":["\\(x'(3)=5\\gt 0\\), so moving right","Solved \\(x'(t)=t^2-4=0\\) to get \\(t=2\\)","Justified \\(t=2\\) is the minimum: \\(x'\\) changes from \\(-\\) to \\(+\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{at }t=3:","hint":"left or right"},{"label":"\\text{farthest left at }t=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the particle's position at \\(t=2\\).","sol":"<b>Recover position from velocity using the initial condition</b> and the net-change form \\(x(2)=x(0)+\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x'(t)\\,dt\\).<br><b>Set up</b> with \\(x(0)=3\\): \\(x(2)=3+\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 (t^2-4)\\,dt\\).<br><b>Evaluate:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(t^2-4)\\,dt=\\Big[\\tfrac{t^3}{3}-4t\\Big]_0^2=\\tfrac{8}{3}-8=-\\tfrac{16}{3}\\). So \\(x(2)=3-\\tfrac{16}{3}=-\\tfrac{7}{3}\\approx -2.333\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the accumulation setup \\(x(0)+\\int_0^2 x'(t)\\,dt\\) (the initial condition must appear), one for the value. Dropping the initial position \\(3\\) — reporting only the integral \\(-\\tfrac{16}{3}\\) — is the classic error.","rubric":["Set up \\(x(2)=3+\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 (t^2-4)\\,dt\\) (initial condition included)","Evaluated \\(x(2)=-\\tfrac{7}{3}\\approx -2.333\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"x(2)=","hint":"e.g. -2.333"}]}]},{"unit":9,"id":"u9f1","title":"Parametric Curve","intro":"<b>Slope & tangent line → concavity → vertical/horizontal tangents → arc-length setup.</b> A no-calculator parametric-calculus problem worth 9 points. Every answer is exact; part D asks you to <i>set up but not evaluate</i> the arc-length integral, a standard Section II move.","given":"x(t)=t^{2}-4t,\\qquad y(t)=t^{3}-3t","givenNote":"A curve C is defined by the parametric equations x(t) = t² − 4t and y(t) = t³ − 3t. No calculator is allowed on this problem.","givenFall":"x(t) = t² − 4t, y(t) = t³ − 3t (no calculator).","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\) as a function of \\(t\\), and write an equation for the line tangent to \\(C\\) at the point where \\(t=0\\).","sol":"<b>Parametric slope is the ratio of the rates:</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}\\). With \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t-4\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=3t^2-3\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3t^2-3}{2t-4}\\).<br><b>At \\(t=0\\):</b> slope \\(=\\dfrac{3(0)-3}{2(0)-4}=\\dfrac{-3}{-4}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\). The point is \\(\\big(x(0),y(0)\\big)=(0,0)\\).<br><b>Tangent line:</b> \\(y-0=\\dfrac{3}{4}(x-0)\\Rightarrow y=\\dfrac{3}{4}x\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3t^2-3}{2t-4}\\), one for the tangent line (correct slope <i>and</i> point). A frequent error is using \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dy}\\) (the reciprocal) for the slope.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3t^2-3}{2t-4}\\)","At \\(t=0\\): slope \\(\\tfrac34\\), point \\((0,0)\\), so \\(y=\\tfrac34 x\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\left.\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{t=0}=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{tangent: }y=","hint":"e.g. (3/4)x"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find \\(\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\\) at \\(t=0\\). Is the curve concave up or concave down at this point?","sol":"<b>The second derivative uses the parametric rule on the slope:</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right]}{dx/dt}\\) — <i>not</i> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y/dt^2}{d^2x/dt^2}\\).<br><b>Differentiate the slope</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3t^2-3}{2t-4}\\) with the quotient rule: \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\dfrac{3t^2-3}{2t-4}\\right]=\\dfrac{(6t)(2t-4)-(3t^2-3)(2)}{(2t-4)^2}\\). At \\(t=0\\): \\(\\dfrac{0-(-6)}{16}=\\dfrac{6}{16}=\\dfrac{3}{8}\\).<br><b>Divide by \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\big|_{0}=-4\\):</b> \\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{3/8}{-4}=-\\dfrac{3}{32}\\).<br>Since \\(-\\tfrac{3}{32}\\lt 0\\), the curve is <b>concave down</b> at \\(t=0\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right]=\\tfrac38\\) at \\(t=0\\), one for dividing by \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\) and concluding concave down. Using \\(\\dfrac{d^2y/dt^2}{d^2x/dt^2}\\) is the classic mistake.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\!\\left[\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right]=\\tfrac38\\) at \\(t=0\\)","\\(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{3/8}{-4}=-\\tfrac{3}{32}\\lt 0\\Rightarrow\\) concave down"],"fields":[{"label":"\\left.\\dfrac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\\right|_{t=0}=","hint":"e.g. -3/32"},{"label":"\\text{concavity:}","hint":"up or down"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the \\(t\\)-value at which \\(C\\) has a vertical tangent line and give the point \\((x,y)\\); then find the \\(t\\)-values at which \\(C\\) has a horizontal tangent line.","sol":"<b>Vertical tangent</b> ⇔ \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=0\\) while \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\neq 0\\). Solve \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t-4=0\\Rightarrow t=2\\). Check \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\big|_{2}=3(4)-3=9\\neq 0\\) ✓. The point is \\(\\big(x(2),y(2)\\big)=(4-8,\\ 8-6)=(-4,2)\\).<br><b>Horizontal tangent</b> ⇔ \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=0\\) while \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\neq 0\\). Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=3t^2-3=0\\Rightarrow t=\\pm 1\\). Check \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\big|_{1}=-2\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\big|_{-1}=-6\\neq 0\\) ✓.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the vertical tangent \\(t=2\\) (with \\(\\tfrac{dy}{dt}\\neq 0\\)) and its point, one for the horizontal \\(t=\\pm 1\\), one for verifying \\(\\tfrac{dx}{dt}\\neq 0\\) there so they are genuine horizontal tangents. The subtle point this curve avoids: \\(\\tfrac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\tfrac{dy}{dt}\\) never vanish at the same \\(t\\), so every candidate is a true tangent.","rubric":["Vertical: \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=0\\) at \\(t=2\\); point \\((-4,2)\\)","Horizontal: \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=0\\) at \\(t=1\\) and \\(t=-1\\)","Verified \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\neq 0\\) at \\(t=\\pm1\\) (genuine horizontal tangents)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{vertical at }t=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"\\text{point }(x,y)=","hint":"e.g. (-4,2)"},{"label":"\\text{horizontal at }t=","hint":"e.g. 1, -1"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Write, but do not evaluate, an integral expression that gives the length of the curve \\(C\\) from \\(t=0\\) to \\(t=3\\).","sol":"<b>Arc length for a parametric curve</b> integrates speed: \\(L=\\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2}\\,dt\\).<br><b>Substitute</b> \\(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2t-4\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}=3t^2-3\\), and limits \\(0\\) to \\(3\\):<br>\\[L=\\int_0^3\\sqrt{(2t-4)^2+(3t^2-3)^2}\\,dt.\\]<br>(The problem says <i>do not evaluate</i>; for reference this is \\(\\approx 24.07\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the correct arc-length form with limits \\(0\\) to \\(3\\), one for the correct integrand \\(\\sqrt{(2t-4)^2+(3t^2-3)^2}\\). Squaring the functions instead of their derivatives, or dropping the square root, loses the integrand point.","rubric":["Arc-length form \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^3\\sqrt{(dx/dt)^2+(dy/dt)^2}\\,dt\\) with limits \\(0\\) to \\(3\\)","Correct integrand \\(\\sqrt{(2t-4)^2+(3t^2-3)^2}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"L=","hint":"e.g. integral_0^3 sqrt(...) dt"}]}]},{"unit":9,"id":"u9f2","title":"Polar Curve","intro":"<b>Area enclosed → tangent slope → \\(dr/d\\theta\\) interpretation → maximum distance.</b> A calculator-active single-polar-curve problem worth 9 points, including a 'find and interpret' part where you explain a rate in a complete sentence — exactly the kind of justification AP readers look for.","given":"r=3+2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta\\le 2\\pi","givenNote":"The polar curve C is given by r = 3 + 2 sin θ for 0 ≤ θ ≤ 2π. A graphing calculator is required.","givenFall":"r = 3 + 2 sin θ for 0 ≤ θ ≤ 2π.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the area of the region enclosed by \\(C\\).","sol":"<b>Polar area is</b> \\(A=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\displaystyle\\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} r^2\\,d\\theta\\). The full curve is swept once over \\([0,2\\pi]\\), so \\(A=\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}(3+2\\sin\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta\\).<br><b>Evaluate</b> (by hand or calculator): expand \\((3+2\\sin\\theta)^2=9+12\\sin\\theta+4\\sin^2\\theta\\). Over \\([0,2\\pi]\\), \\(\\int 9\\,d\\theta=18\\pi\\), \\(\\int 12\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=0\\), and \\(\\int 4\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\theta=4\\pi\\) (using \\(\\sin^2\\theta=\\tfrac{1-\\cos2\\theta}{2}\\)). So \\(A=\\dfrac12(18\\pi+4\\pi)=11\\pi\\approx 34.558\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the area integral with the \\(\\tfrac12\\) and correct limits, one for the correct integrand \\((3+2\\sin\\theta)^2\\), one for the value. Forgetting the \\(\\tfrac12\\) (giving \\(22\\pi\\)) is the most common error.","rubric":["\\(A=\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}(3+2\\sin\\theta)^2\\,d\\theta\\)","Expanded and integrated correctly","\\(A=11\\pi\\approx 34.558\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{area}=","hint":"e.g. 34.558"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the slope of the line tangent to \\(C\\) at \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\).","sol":"<b>Convert to \\(x,y\\) and use the parametric slope.</b> With \\(x=r\\cos\\theta\\), \\(y=r\\sin\\theta\\), \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{r'\\sin\\theta+r\\cos\\theta}{r'\\cos\\theta-r\\sin\\theta}\\), where \\(r'=\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=2\\cos\\theta\\).<br><b>At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\):</b> \\(r=3+2\\sin\\tfrac\\pi6=4\\) and \\(r'=2\\cos\\tfrac\\pi6=\\sqrt3\\).<br>Numerator \\(=\\sqrt3\\cdot\\tfrac12+4\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}{2}\\); denominator \\(=\\sqrt3\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}-4\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac32-2=-\\tfrac12\\). So \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3/2}{-1/2}=-5\\sqrt3\\approx-8.660\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the polar slope formula with \\(r'=2\\cos\\theta\\), one for the value. Using \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) as the slope (a frequent confusion) earns neither point.","rubric":["Slope \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{r'\\sin\\theta+r\\cos\\theta}{r'\\cos\\theta-r\\sin\\theta}\\) with \\(r'=2\\cos\\theta\\)","At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi6\\): slope \\(=-5\\sqrt3\\approx-8.660\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{slope}=","hint":"e.g. -8.660"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) at \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\). Using a complete sentence, interpret the meaning of this value in the context of the curve.","sol":"<b>Differentiate \\(r\\) with respect to \\(\\theta\\):</b> \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=2\\cos\\theta\\). At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\): \\(2\\cos\\tfrac\\pi3=2\\cdot\\tfrac12=1\\).<br><b>Interpretation:</b> Since \\(r\\) is the distance from the origin to the curve, \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=1\\) at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi3\\) means that at that angle the distance from the origin is <b>increasing at a rate of \\(1\\) unit per radian</b> as \\(\\theta\\) increases.<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the value \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=1\\), one for an interpretation in context that mentions the <i>distance from the origin increasing</i> (a complete sentence). Calling \\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) the slope of the curve loses the interpretation point — it is the rate of change of the radius, not the tangent slope.","rubric":["\\(\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=2\\cos\\theta\\); at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi3\\) it equals \\(1\\)","Interpreted in a sentence: the distance from the origin is increasing at \\(1\\) unit per radian"],"fields":[{"label":"\\left.\\dfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\right|_{\\pi/3}=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the maximum distance from the origin to a point on \\(C\\) and the value of \\(\\theta\\) at which it occurs, then give the rectangular coordinates of that point.","sol":"<b>The distance from the origin is \\(r\\) itself, so maximize \\(r=3+2\\sin\\theta\\).</b> Since \\(\\sin\\theta\\) has maximum value \\(1\\) at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\), the maximum distance is \\(r=3+2(1)=5\\), occurring at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\).<br><b>Rectangular coordinates:</b> \\(x=r\\cos\\theta=5\\cos\\tfrac\\pi2=0\\), \\(y=r\\sin\\theta=5\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2=5\\). The point is \\((0,5)\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for \\(r_{\\max}=5\\) at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\), one for the rectangular point \\((0,5)\\). Maximizing the <i>area integrand</i> or solving \\(\\tfrac{dr}{d\\theta}=0\\) without checking it is a maximum are common missteps.","rubric":["Max \\(r\\) when \\(\\sin\\theta=1\\): \\(r=5\\) at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi2\\)","Rectangular point \\((0,5)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"r_{\\max}=","hint":"a number"},{"label":"(x,y)=","hint":"e.g. (0,5)"}]}]},{"unit":9,"id":"u9f3","title":"Two Polar Curves","intro":"<b>Intersection → area inside one/outside the other → area-inside-both setup → tangent slope.</b> A calculator-active two-curve polar problem worth 9 points. Part C asks you to <i>set up but not evaluate</i> the inside-both area — the hardest setup on the polar exam, because the bounding curve changes.","given":"r_1=3\\sin\\theta\\quad(\\text{circle}),\\qquad r_2=1+\\sin\\theta\\quad(\\text{cardioid})","givenNote":"The circle r = 3 sin θ and the cardioid r = 1 + sin θ intersect in the upper half-plane. A graphing calculator is required for parts B and D.","givenFall":"Circle r = 3 sin θ and cardioid r = 1 + sin θ.","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the values of \\(\\theta\\) in \\([0,\\pi]\\) at which the two curves intersect away from the pole, and the value of \\(r\\) at those points.","sol":"<b>Set the radii equal</b> to find the intersection angles: \\(3\\sin\\theta=1+\\sin\\theta\\Rightarrow 2\\sin\\theta=1\\Rightarrow\\sin\\theta=\\tfrac12\\). In \\([0,\\pi]\\) this gives \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\) and \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\).<br><b>Value of \\(r\\):</b> at either angle, \\(r=3\\sin\\theta=3\\cdot\\tfrac12=1.5\\) (equivalently \\(1+\\sin\\theta=1.5\\)).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the equation \\(3\\sin\\theta=1+\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta=\\tfrac12\\), one for both angles with \\(r=1.5\\). Both curves also pass through the pole, but at different \\(\\theta\\)-values, so the pole is found graphically, not by setting the radii equal.","rubric":["Set \\(3\\sin\\theta=1+\\sin\\theta\\Rightarrow\\sin\\theta=\\tfrac12\\)","\\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi6,\\ \\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\) with \\(r=1.5\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\theta=","hint":"e.g. pi/6, 5pi/6"},{"label":"r=","hint":"a number"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the area of the region that is inside the circle \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) and outside the cardioid \\(r=1+\\sin\\theta\\).","sol":"<b>Use</b> \\(A=\\dfrac12\\displaystyle\\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta}\\big(r_{\\text{out}}^2-r_{\\text{in}}^2\\big)\\,d\\theta\\), with limits from the intersection angles found in Part A.<br>Between \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\) and \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\), the circle \\(3\\sin\\theta\\) is the outer curve and the cardioid \\(1+\\sin\\theta\\) is the inner, so<br>\\[A=\\dfrac12\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}\\Big[(3\\sin\\theta)^2-(1+\\sin\\theta)^2\\Big]\\,d\\theta\\approx 3.142.\\]<br>(Exactly \\(\\pi\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the limits \\(\\tfrac\\pi6\\) to \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\), one for the integrand \\((\\text{outer})^2-(\\text{inner})^2\\) in the right order, one for the value. Squaring the <i>difference</i> \\((r_{\\text{out}}-r_{\\text{in}})^2\\) instead of the difference of squares is the signature polar-area error.","rubric":["Limits \\(\\tfrac\\pi6\\) to \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\) from the intersection","Integrand \\(\\tfrac12\\big[(3\\sin\\theta)^2-(1+\\sin\\theta)^2\\big]\\)","Evaluated \\(\\approx 3.142\\) (i.e. \\(\\pi\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{area}=","hint":"e.g. 3.142"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Write, but do not evaluate, an integral expression that gives the area of the region inside both curves.","sol":"<b>The region inside both is bounded by whichever curve is closer to the pole, and that switches at the intersection angles.</b><br>• For \\(0\\le\\theta\\le\\tfrac\\pi6\\) and \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\\), the circle \\(3\\sin\\theta\\) is the inner (smaller) curve.<br>• For \\(\\tfrac\\pi6\\le\\theta\\le\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\), the cardioid \\(1+\\sin\\theta\\) is the inner curve.<br>So the area inside both is the three-piece sum<br>\\[A=\\dfrac12\\int_0^{\\pi/6}(3\\sin\\theta)^2 d\\theta+\\dfrac12\\int_{\\pi/6}^{5\\pi/6}(1+\\sin\\theta)^2 d\\theta+\\dfrac12\\int_{5\\pi/6}^{\\pi}(3\\sin\\theta)^2 d\\theta.\\]<br>(Do not evaluate; for reference this equals \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}\\approx 3.927\\).)<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for recognizing the boundary switches (circle near \\(\\theta=0\\) and \\(\\theta=\\pi\\), cardioid in the middle), one for the correct three-piece integral with \\(\\tfrac12\\) and proper limits. Writing a single integral of one curve over \\([0,\\pi]\\) misses the switch and loses both points.","rubric":["Recognized the inner curve switches at \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi6,\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\) (circle near \\(0\\) and \\(\\pi\\), cardioid between)","Correct three-piece sum with \\(\\tfrac12\\) and limits \\(0\\to\\tfrac\\pi6\\), \\(\\tfrac\\pi6\\to\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\), \\(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\to\\pi\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{area}=","hint":"a sum of integrals"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the slope of the line tangent to the circle \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) at \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\).","sol":"<b>Use the polar slope formula</b> \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{r'\\sin\\theta+r\\cos\\theta}{r'\\cos\\theta-r\\sin\\theta}\\) with \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(r'=3\\cos\\theta\\).<br><b>At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\):</b> \\(r=3\\sin\\tfrac\\pi3=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\), \\(r'=3\\cos\\tfrac\\pi3=\\tfrac32\\).<br>Numerator \\(=\\tfrac32\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}+\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4}+\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4}=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\); denominator \\(=\\tfrac32\\cdot\\tfrac12-\\tfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}=\\tfrac34-\\tfrac94=-\\tfrac64=-\\tfrac32\\). So \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3/2}{-3/2}=-\\sqrt3\\approx-1.732\\).<br><br><b>Scoring:</b> one point for the slope formula with \\(r'=3\\cos\\theta\\), one for the value \\(-\\sqrt3\\). (Note: \\(r=3\\sin\\theta\\) is a circle through the origin; the tangent slope still requires the full polar formula, not \\(\\tfrac{dr}{d\\theta}\\).)","rubric":["Polar slope formula with \\(r'=3\\cos\\theta\\)","At \\(\\theta=\\tfrac\\pi3\\): slope \\(=-\\sqrt3\\approx-1.732\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{slope}=","hint":"e.g. -1.732"}]}]},{"unit":10,"id":"u10f0","title":"Ratio test & geometric","intro":"<b>Ratio test → interval → derivative series → geometric closed form → alternating error.</b> The most common Q6 shape.","given":"f(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-3)^{n}}{n\\cdot 2^{n}}","givenNote":"This Taylor series about \\(x=3\\) converges to \\(f(x)\\) on its interval of convergence.","givenFall":"f(x) = Σ (x−3)ⁿ / (n·2ⁿ),  about x = 3","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Use the ratio test to find the <b>interval of convergence</b> of the series for \\(f\\).","sol":"<b>Ratio test:</b> \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{(x-3)^{n+1}}{(n+1)2^{n+1}}\\cdot\\frac{n\\,2^{n}}{(x-3)^{n}}\\right|=\\frac{|x-3|}{2}\\). Setting \\(\\frac{|x-3|}{2}\\lt 1\\) gives \\(|x-3|\\lt 2\\), so \\(R=2\\) and the interior is \\((1,5)\\).<br><b>Endpoints:</b> at \\(x=1\\), the series is \\(\\sum\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\), which converges by the alternating series test. At \\(x=5\\), it is \\(\\sum\\frac{1}{n}\\), the harmonic series, which diverges. Interval of convergence: \\([1,5)\\).","rubric":["Set up the ratio test and found \\(R=2\\) (interior \\((1,5)\\))","Justified \\(x=1\\) converges (alternating series test)","Justified \\(x=5\\) diverges (harmonic) and gave \\([1,5)\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"R=","hint":"radius, e.g. 2"},{"label":"\\text{Interval}=","hint":"e.g. [1,5)"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the general term of the Taylor series for \\(f'(x)\\), the derivative of \\(f\\), about \\(x=3\\).","sol":"Differentiate term by term: \\(f(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-3)^{n}}{n\\,2^{n}}\\Rightarrow f'(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{2^{n}}\\). The first three nonzero terms are \\(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{x-3}{4}+\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\cdots\\), with general term \\(\\dfrac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{2^{n}}\\).","rubric":["Differentiated term by term to get general term \\(\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{2^{n}}\\)","Gave correct first three terms \\(\\frac12+\\frac{x-3}{4}+\\frac{(x-3)^2}{8}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(x)=\\sum\\,","hint":"general term, e.g. (x-3)^(n-1)/2^n"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"The series for \\(f'\\) is geometric. Find a <b>closed form</b> for \\(f'(x)\\) on the interval of convergence.","sol":"The series \\(f'(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{2^{n}}\\) is geometric with first term \\(a=\\frac{1}{2}\\) and ratio \\(r=\\frac{x-3}{2}\\). So \\(f'(x)=\\dfrac{a}{1-r}=\\dfrac{\\frac12}{1-\\frac{x-3}{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{5-x}\\), valid where \\(|r|\\lt 1\\).","rubric":["Identified \\(a=\\frac12\\) and \\(r=\\frac{x-3}{2}\\)","Used \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) to get \\(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(x)=","hint":"e.g. 1/(5-x)"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"At \\(x=1\\), \\(f(1)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\). Approximating \\(f(1)\\) with the first three terms, give an <b>upper bound</b> on the error.","sol":"At \\(x=1\\) the series is alternating with terms \\(\\frac{1}{n}\\) decreasing to 0, so the <b>alternating series error bound</b> applies: the error is at most the first omitted term. After three terms, the next term is \\(n=4\\): \\(\\left|\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4}\\right|=\\frac{1}{4}\\). (The true error \\(\\approx 0.140\\lt \\frac14\\).)","rubric":["Recognized the alternating series error bound applies","Bound = first omitted term \\(=\\frac14\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{error}\\le","hint":"e.g. 1/4"}]}]},{"unit":10,"id":"u10f1","title":"Lagrange error bound","intro":"<b>Build a Maclaurin series → general term → polynomial approximation → Lagrange error bound.</b> The harder error type.","given":"f(x)=e^{2x}","givenNote":"Consider the Maclaurin series for \\(f(x)=e^{2x}\\).","givenFall":"f(x) = e^(2x)","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the first four nonzero terms of the Maclaurin series for \\(f(x)=e^{2x}\\).","sol":"Use \\(e^{u}=\\sum\\frac{u^n}{n!}\\) with \\(u=2x\\): \\(e^{2x}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(2x)^n}{n!}=1+2x+2x^2+\\frac{4}{3}x^3+\\cdots\\). The \\(x^3\\) coefficient is \\(\\frac{2^3}{3!}=\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{4}{3}\\).","rubric":["First three terms \\(1+2x+2x^2\\) correct","Fourth term \\(\\frac{4}{3}x^3\\) correct"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{coeff of }x^{3}=","hint":"e.g. 4/3"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the general term of the Maclaurin series for \\(e^{2x}\\).","sol":"From \\(e^{2x}=\\sum\\frac{(2x)^n}{n!}\\), the general term is \\(\\dfrac{(2x)^n}{n!}=\\dfrac{2^n x^n}{n!}\\).","rubric":["Recognized substitution \\(u=2x\\) into \\(e^u\\) series","General term \\(\\frac{2^n x^n}{n!}\\) correct"],"fields":[{"label":"\\sum\\,","hint":"e.g. 2^n x^n / n!"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Let \\(P_3\\) be the third-degree Maclaurin polynomial. Use it to approximate \\(f(0.5)=e^{1}\\). (Enter as a fraction.)","sol":"\\(P_3(x)=1+2x+2x^2+\\frac43 x^3\\). At \\(x=0.5\\): \\(1+1+0.5+\\frac43\\cdot\\frac18=1+1+\\frac12+\\frac16=\\frac{8}{3}\\approx 2.667\\). (True value \\(e\\approx 2.718\\).)","rubric":["Substituted \\(x=0.5\\) into \\(P_3\\)","Got \\(P_3(0.5)=\\frac{8}{3}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"P_3(0.5)=","hint":"e.g. 8/3"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":3,"prompt":"Use the Lagrange error bound to bound the error in approximating \\(f(0.5)\\) by \\(P_3\\). (\\(f^{(4)}(x)=16e^{2x}\\); use its max on \\([0,0.5]\\).)","sol":"Lagrange: \\(R_3\\le\\frac{M}{4!}|x|^4\\) where \\(M=\\max|f^{(4)}|\\) on \\([0,0.5]\\). Since \\(f^{(4)}(x)=16e^{2x}\\) is increasing, \\(M=16e^{1}=16e\\). So \\(R_3\\le\\frac{16e}{4!}(0.5)^4=\\frac{16e}{24}\\cdot\\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{e}{24}\\approx 0.113\\). (Actual error \\(\\approx 0.052\\).)","rubric":["Identified \\(M=16e\\) (max of \\(f^{(4)}\\) on the interval)","Set up \\(\\frac{M}{4!}(0.5)^4\\)","Simplified to \\(\\frac{e}{24}\\approx 0.113\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{error}\\le","hint":"e.g. e/24"}]}]},{"unit":10,"id":"u10f2","title":"Taylor from a data table","intro":"<b>Build a Taylor polynomial from given derivative values → approximate f and f′ → bound the error.</b> Series from data, not handed to you.","given":"f(2)=3,\\quad f'(2)=-1,\\quad f''(2)=4,\\quad f'''(2)=12","givenNote":"\\(f\\) and its first three derivatives have the values shown at \\(x=2\\).","givenFall":"At x=2:  f=3, f'=-1, f''=4, f'''=12","givenHTML":"<table class=\"dtable\"><thead><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>\\(f(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f''(x)\\)</th><th>\\(f'''(x)\\)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(2\\)</td><td>\\(3\\)</td><td>\\(-1\\)</td><td>\\(4\\)</td><td>\\(12\\)</td></tr></tbody></table>","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Write the third-degree Taylor polynomial \\(T_3(x)\\) for \\(f\\) about \\(x=2\\). (Enter the coefficient of \\((x-2)^2\\).)","sol":"\\(T_3(x)=f(2)+f'(2)(x-2)+\\frac{f''(2)}{2!}(x-2)^2+\\frac{f'''(2)}{3!}(x-2)^3\\) \\(=3-(x-2)+\\frac{4}{2}(x-2)^2+\\frac{12}{6}(x-2)^3=3-(x-2)+2(x-2)^2+2(x-2)^3\\). The \\((x-2)^2\\) coefficient is \\(\\frac{f''(2)}{2!}=\\frac{4}{2}=2\\).","rubric":["Used the Taylor formula with \\(2!\\) and \\(3!\\) in denominators","Coefficients \\(3,-1\\) for the constant and linear terms","Coefficient of \\((x-2)^2\\) is \\(2\\) (and \\((x-2)^3\\) is \\(2\\))"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{coeff of }(x-2)^2=","hint":"e.g. 2"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Use \\(T_3\\) to approximate \\(f(2.1)\\).","sol":"With \\(x-2=0.1\\): \\(T_3(2.1)=3-0.1+2(0.1)^2+2(0.1)^3=3-0.1+0.02+0.002=2.922\\).","rubric":["Substituted \\(x-2=0.1\\) into \\(T_3\\)","Got \\(f(2.1)\\approx 2.922\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f(2.1)\\approx","hint":"e.g. 2.922"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Using \\(T_3'(x)\\), approximate \\(f'(2.1)\\).","sol":"\\(T_3'(x)=-1+4(x-2)+6(x-2)^2\\). At \\(x=2.1\\): \\(-1+4(0.1)+6(0.1)^2=-1+0.4+0.06=-0.54\\).","rubric":["Differentiated \\(T_3\\) correctly","Got \\(f'(2.1)\\approx -0.54\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"f'(2.1)\\approx","hint":"e.g. -0.54"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"Given \\(|f^{(4)}(x)|\\le 24\\) on \\([2,2.1]\\), bound the error of the \\(T_3\\) approximation of \\(f(2.1)\\).","sol":"Lagrange: \\(R_3\\le\\frac{M}{4!}|x-2|^4=\\frac{24}{24}(0.1)^4=(0.1)^4=0.0001\\). The bound is \\(\\frac{1}{10000}\\).","rubric":["Used \\(\\frac{M}{4!}|x-2|^4\\) with \\(M=24\\)","Simplified to \\(0.0001\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{error}\\le","hint":"e.g. 0.0001"}]}]},{"unit":10,"id":"u10f3","title":"Substitution & integration","intro":"<b>Build a series by substitution → radius → integrate term by term → alternating error.</b> Manipulating known series.","given":"g(x)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^{3}}","givenNote":"Build the Maclaurin series for \\(g\\) from the geometric series.","givenFall":"g(x) = 1/(1+x^3)","givenHTML":"","parts":[{"label":"Part A","pts":3,"prompt":"Find the general term of the Maclaurin series for \\(g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+x^{3}}\\).","sol":"Use \\(\\frac{1}{1-u}=\\sum u^n\\) with \\(u=-x^3\\): \\(\\frac{1}{1+x^3}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-x^3)^n=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n x^{3n}=1-x^3+x^6-\\cdots\\). General term \\((-1)^n x^{3n}\\).","rubric":["Recognized the geometric template \\(\\frac{1}{1-u}=\\sum u^n\\)","Substituted \\(u=-x^3\\) correctly","General term \\((-1)^n x^{3n}\\) correct"],"fields":[{"label":"\\sum\\,","hint":"e.g. (-1)^n x^(3n)"}]},{"label":"Part B","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the radius of convergence of this series.","sol":"The geometric series converges when \\(|u|\\lt 1\\), i.e. \\(|-x^3|\\lt 1\\Rightarrow|x|^3\\lt 1\\Rightarrow|x|\\lt 1\\). So \\(R=1\\).","rubric":["Set \\(|-x^3|\\lt 1\\)","Concluded \\(R=1\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"R=","hint":"e.g. 1"}]},{"label":"Part C","pts":2,"prompt":"Find the first three nonzero terms of the series for \\(\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt\\).","sol":"Integrate term by term: \\(\\int_0^x(1-t^3+t^6-\\cdots)\\,dt=x-\\frac{x^4}{4}+\\frac{x^7}{7}-\\cdots\\) (general term \\(\\frac{(-1)^n x^{3n+1}}{3n+1}\\)).","rubric":["Integrated the series term by term","First three terms \\(x-\\frac{x^4}{4}+\\frac{x^7}{7}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\int_0^x g\\,dt=","hint":"e.g. x - x^4/4 + x^7/7"}]},{"label":"Part D","pts":2,"prompt":"At \\(x=1\\), the integral series is \\(\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^n}{3n+1}\\). Approximating with the first three terms, bound the error.","sol":"The series \\(1-\\frac14+\\frac17-\\frac{1}{10}+\\cdots\\) is alternating with terms decreasing to 0. The error after three terms is at most the fourth term (\\(n=3\\)): \\(\\frac{1}{3(3)+1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\).","rubric":["Recognized alternating series error bound","Bound = fourth term \\(=\\frac{1}{10}\\)"],"fields":[{"label":"\\text{error}\\le","hint":"e.g. 1/10"}]}]}]};
